ADDART({id:"originoflife",t:"The Origin of Life",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🌋",w:"Abiogenesis",r:["geotime","earthplanet","cell","dna","bacteria","astrobio"],
p:[["A great mystery","No one knows exactly how life began on Earth."],["Very early","Life appeared within about a billion years of Earth forming."],["Clues from chemistry","Experiments show life’s building blocks can form naturally."]],
l:"The origin of life is the question of how living things first arose from non-living chemicals on the early Earth, a process scientists call abiogenesis. Earth formed about 4.54 billion years ago. The oldest widely accepted fossils, layered mounds built by microbes called stromatolites, are about 3.5 billion years old, and chemical traces may be older still, so life appeared surprisingly early. Scientists do not yet know exactly how it happened, but experiments show that amino acids and other building blocks of life can form naturally. Leading ideas suggest life began in warm pools on land or around hot springs on the deep seafloor, perhaps starting with self-copying molecules of RNA.",
s:[["The young Earth","The early Earth was very different from today. There was no oxygen in the air, volcanoes were everywhere, and asteroids and comets struck often. Oceans formed early, perhaps by 4.4 billion years ago. Comets and meteorites may have delivered water and organic molecules, the carbon-based chemicals life is made of."],["The Miller–Urey experiment","In 1952 Stanley Miller, a student of Harold Urey, filled glass flasks with water and gases thought to resemble the early atmosphere and zapped them with sparks like lightning. Within days, amino acids, the building blocks of proteins, had formed. The experiment showed that life’s ingredients could arise naturally."],["The RNA world","Living cells need DNA to store instructions and proteins to do work, but each needs the other to be made. RNA can do both jobs: it stores information and can speed up reactions. Many scientists think early life used RNA alone, an idea called the RNA world, before DNA and proteins took over."],["Where did it start?","Some scientists favor hydrothermal vents on the deep seafloor, where hot, mineral-rich water provides energy and chemicals. Others favor shallow pools on land near volcanoes, where repeated drying and wetting could join small molecules into long chains. A few suggest life’s ingredients, or even life itself, came from space."]],
k:[["Age of Earth","About 4.54 billion years"],["Oldest accepted fossils","Stromatolites, about 3.5 billion years old"],["Scientific name","Abiogenesis"],["Famous experiment","Miller–Urey, 1952"],["Leading idea","An RNA world"]],
d:[["c. 4.54 billion years ago","Earth forms"],["c. 4.4 billion years ago","The first oceans may form"],["c. 3.5 billion years ago","Microbes build stromatolites"],["1952","The Miller–Urey experiment makes amino acids"],["2025","Scientists report amino acids and DNA bases in samples from asteroid Bennu"]],
s2:[["Our last universal ancestor","All living things share the same genetic code, which suggests they descend from one ancient population of cells, called LUCA, the last universal common ancestor. A 2024 study estimated that LUCA lived about 4.2 billion years ago and was already a fairly complex microbe."],["Life elsewhere?","If life began easily on Earth, it might have begun on other worlds too. Space missions search for signs of past or present life on Mars, Jupiter’s moon Europa and Saturn’s moon Enceladus. Samples from asteroids such as Bennu and Ryugu contain amino acids and other ingredients of life."]],
sp:["Living stromatolites","Stromatolites still form today in a few salty, sheltered places, such as Shark Bay in Western Australia. They are built slowly by mats of cyanobacteria that trap sand and minerals, layer by layer. Seeing them is like looking back more than 3 billion years."],
g:[["Abiogenesis","Life arising from non-living chemicals"],["Stromatolite","A layered rock mound built by microbes"],["RNA","A molecule related to DNA that can store information and speed up reactions"]],
f:["Life appeared within about a billion years of Earth forming.","The Miller–Urey experiment made amino acids from simple gases.","All living things share the same genetic code."],
fun:"Every living thing on Earth, from bacteria to blue whales, may be descended from a single population of microbes that lived more than 4 billion years ago."});

ADDART({id:"snowballearth",t:"Snowball Earth",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"❄️",w:"Snowball Earth",r:["iceage","glaciers","geotime","ediacaran","climate","volc"],
p:[["A frozen planet","At times, Earth may have frozen almost from pole to pole."],["Long ago","The biggest freezes happened between about 720 and 635 million years ago."],["Volcanoes to the rescue","Gases from volcanoes slowly warmed the planet again."]],
l:"Snowball Earth is the idea that Earth’s surface froze over completely, or almost completely, during a few extreme ice ages long before animals evolved. The best-known episodes came during the Cryogenian Period, which means cold birth: the Sturtian glaciation, from about 717 to 660 million years ago, and the Marinoan glaciation, which ended about 635 million years ago. Rocks left by glaciers have been found in places that were then near the equator, suggesting ice reached the tropics. Life survived, probably in pockets of open water, under thin ice or near hot springs. Soon after the ice melted, the first large, complex living things appeared.",
s:[["Clues in the rocks","Glaciers leave behind jumbled rocks called tillites and dropstones, boulders that fell from floating ice into soft seafloor mud. Rocks of this age contain such deposits from every continent, including places that magnetic studies show were near the equator. On top of them often lie thick layers of carbonate rock that formed in warm water."],["How it froze","Ice and snow reflect sunlight back to space. Once ice spreads far enough from the poles, the planet reflects so much sunlight that it cools further, and more ice forms. Climate models show that past a certain point, this runaway freeze could cover the whole globe. Changes in continents and falling carbon dioxide may have started it."],["How it thawed","With the oceans frozen, carbon dioxide from volcanoes could not dissolve in seawater or be removed by weathering rocks. Over millions of years it built up in the air until the greenhouse effect melted the ice, perhaps quickly. The hot, stormy aftermath may explain the carbonate rocks found above the glacial deposits."],["Snowball or slushball?","Some scientists think the oceans never froze completely and that a band of open water stayed near the equator, an idea nicknamed Slushball Earth. Fossils of algae that needed light and the survival of many kinds of life support some open water. The debate continues."]],
k:[["Main period","Cryogenian, about 720–635 million years ago"],["Major freezes","Sturtian and Marinoan glaciations"],["Earlier freeze","Huronian glaciation, about 2.4–2.1 billion years ago"],["Term coined","1992, by Joseph Kirschvink"],["What ended it","Carbon dioxide from volcanoes"]],
d:[["c. 2.4 billion years ago","The Huronian glaciation begins as oxygen rises"],["c. 717 Ma","The Sturtian glaciation begins"],["c. 635 Ma","The Marinoan glaciation ends"],["1992","Joseph Kirschvink coins the term Snowball Earth"],["1998","Paul Hoffman and colleagues publish evidence from Namibia"]],
s2:[["A trigger for animals?","Shortly after the last big freeze, the Ediacaran Period began, and the first large, complex organisms appeared. Some scientists think the melting of the ice released nutrients into the oceans and raised oxygen levels, helping life grow larger and more complex. Others think the timing may be a coincidence."],["Could it happen again?","Today’s Sun is brighter than it was 700 million years ago, and continents and the carbon cycle are arranged differently, so a full Snowball Earth is very unlikely in the near future. The idea, however, shows how powerful the feedback between ice, sunlight and carbon dioxide can be."]],
sp:["Ice on a tropical island","Some Snowball Earth glacial rocks are now found in hot places such as Namibia, Australia and the Middle East. At the time, continents were arranged differently, and some of these rocks formed near the equator, which is why scientists think ice reached the tropics."],
g:[["Glaciation","A time when ice sheets cover large areas"],["Dropstone","A rock dropped from floating ice into seafloor mud"],["Albedo","How much sunlight a surface reflects"]],
f:["Glaciers may once have reached the equator.","The Cryogenian Period’s name means cold birth.","Volcanic carbon dioxide ended the deep freeze."],
fun:"During Snowball Earth, the ice over the oceans may have been hundreds of meters thick, yet some algae survived, including relatives of the green algae from which land plants later evolved."});

ADDART({id:"pangaea",t:"Pangaea",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🗺️",w:"Pangaea",r:["continents","earthlayers","geotime","volc","dinosaurs","massext","nothosaurus"],
p:[["One giant continent","Pangaea was a supercontinent that joined almost all of Earth’s land."],["Age of the first dinosaurs","It existed from about 335 to 175 million years ago."],["Still moving","The continents keep drifting today, a few centimeters a year."]],
l:"Pangaea was a supercontinent that brought together almost all of Earth’s land into a single huge landmass. It formed about 335 million years ago, as earlier continents collided, and began breaking apart about 200 million years ago, splitting into the continents we know today. Its name means all lands, and the vast ocean around it is called Panthalassa, all sea. Pangaea existed when early reptiles spread and the first dinosaurs appeared. The German scientist Alfred Wegener proposed in 1912 that the continents had once been joined and had drifted apart. His idea was doubted for decades, until the discovery of plate tectonics in the 1960s proved him right.",
s:[["Wegener’s clues","Wegener noticed that the coasts of South America and Africa fit together like puzzle pieces. Fossils of the same land animals and plants, such as the reptile Mesosaurus and the fern-like plant Glossopteris, are found on continents now separated by oceans. Matching rock layers and signs of ancient glaciers in today’s tropics added to his evidence."],["Why it was doubted","Wegener could not explain what force could move whole continents, so most geologists rejected his idea of continental drift. He died in 1930 on an expedition across the Greenland ice sheet, long before he was proved right."],["Plate tectonics","In the 1950s and 1960s, maps of the seafloor revealed long underwater mountain ranges where new crust forms and spreads apart. Earth’s outer shell is broken into plates that move slowly over hotter rock below. This theory, plate tectonics, explained how Pangaea formed and broke up."],["Breaking apart","Pangaea first split into a northern part, Laurasia, and a southern part, Gondwana, as the Atlantic Ocean began to open. Volcanic eruptions along the rifts poured out enormous lava flows and may have contributed to a mass extinction about 201 million years ago. Over time India raced north and crashed into Asia, raising the Himalayas."]],
k:[["Formed","About 335 million years ago"],["Began breaking up","About 200 million years ago"],["Name meaning","All lands"],["Surrounding ocean","Panthalassa"],["Proposed by","Alfred Wegener, 1912"]],
d:[["c. 335 Ma","Pangaea comes together"],["c. 252 Ma","The Great Dying strikes life on Pangaea"],["c. 200 Ma","Pangaea begins to break apart"],["1912","Alfred Wegener proposes continental drift"],["1960s","Plate tectonics is accepted"]],
s2:[["Life on a supercontinent","Pangaea’s interior was far from the sea, so much of it was hot and dry desert, with seasonal monsoon rains near the coasts. Because land was connected, animals could walk from what is now Antarctica to North America. This is why fossils of the same dinosaurs are found on many continents."],["The next supercontinent","Continents keep moving at about the speed fingernails grow. Supercontinents seem to form and break apart in cycles every few hundred million years. Scientists predict that in about 200 to 250 million years, the continents may join again in a new supercontinent, sometimes called Pangaea Ultima or Amasia."]],
sp:["The Atlantic is still growing","The Atlantic Ocean, which opened as Pangaea split, is still widening by about 2 to 5 centimeters a year along the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, an underwater mountain chain where new seafloor forms. In Iceland, the ridge rises above the sea, and visitors can walk in a rift valley between the North American and Eurasian plates."],
g:[["Supercontinent","A landmass made of most or all of Earth’s continents"],["Continental drift","The slow movement of continents across Earth’s surface"],["Plate tectonics","The theory that Earth’s outer shell is broken into moving plates"]],
f:["Pangaea means all lands.","Mesosaurus fossils are found in both South America and Africa.","Continents move about as fast as fingernails grow."],
fun:"Because the land was all connected, a dinosaur could, in theory, have walked from present-day Brazil to present-day China without crossing an ocean."});

ADDART({id:"srivijaya",t:"Srivijaya",c:"History",i:"⛵",w:"Srivijaya",r:["indonesiacountry","malaysiacountry","buddh","trade","seaempires","majapahit"],
p:[["A sea empire","Srivijaya was a trading kingdom based on the island of Sumatra."],["Controlled the straits","It guarded the sea routes between India and China."],["Buddhist center","It was a famous place of Buddhist learning."]],
l:"Srivijaya was a powerful Buddhist kingdom based at Palembang on the island of Sumatra, in today’s Indonesia, that flourished from the 7th to about the 11th century. By controlling the Strait of Malacca and the Sunda Strait, the busy sea lanes between India and China, it grew rich from trade, taxing ships and selling forest products, spices and gold. Its influence spread across Sumatra, the Malay Peninsula and parts of Java and Borneo. Srivijaya was also a renowned center of Buddhist learning, visited by monks on their way to India. Its power declined after a raid by the Chola kingdom of southern India in 1025. Its very existence was largely forgotten until historians pieced it together in the 20th century.",
s:[["Masters of the straits","Ships sailing between India and China had to pass through narrow straits and wait for the monsoon winds to change. Srivijaya offered safe ports, supplies and protection from pirates, and it made ships stop and pay. Its navy and alliances with local sea peoples helped it control the routes."],["Monks and learning","The Chinese monk Yijing stopped at Srivijaya in 671 and later stayed for years, copying and translating Buddhist texts. He advised other Chinese monks to study Sanskrit there before going on to India. Srivijaya supported famous teachers and sent gifts to Buddhist centers in India."],["Inscriptions in Old Malay","Stone inscriptions found around Palembang, such as the Kedukan Bukit inscription of 682, are among the oldest known texts in the Malay language. They record a ruler’s journey, a victory and the founding of the kingdom, and some contain curses against anyone who betrayed the king."],["Decline","In 1025 the Chola king Rajendra I of southern India sent a fleet that raided Srivijaya’s ports and captured its king. Srivijaya survived but weakened, and its center may have moved to Jambi. Over the next centuries other powers, including Majapahit on Java, took over the trade routes."]],
k:[["Center","Palembang, Sumatra"],["Height","7th to 11th centuries"],["Religion","Buddhism"],["Wealth from","Controlling sea trade"],["Oldest inscription","Kedukan Bukit, 682"]],
d:[["671","The Chinese monk Yijing visits Srivijaya"],["682","The Kedukan Bukit inscription records the kingdom’s founding"],["1025","The Chola kingdom of southern India raids Srivijaya"],["1918","Historian George Cœdès identifies Srivijaya as a great kingdom"]],
s2:[["A forgotten empire","Srivijaya left few buildings, because much was built of wood, and its name was rarely remembered. Chinese records described a kingdom called Shilifoshi, and Arab writers spoke of the rich land of Zabaj. In 1918 the French historian George Cœdès connected these records with inscriptions in Sumatra and revealed a great forgotten empire."],["Legacy","Srivijaya spread the Malay language as a language of trade across the islands, which helped lay the foundation for modern Malay and Indonesian. Indonesians today remember Srivijaya, together with Majapahit, as an early example of a great kingdom uniting the archipelago."]],
sp:["A monk’s voyage","In 671 the Chinese monk Yijing sailed from Guangzhou and reached Srivijaya in less than 20 days. He found more than a thousand Buddhist monks there studying as in India. His writings are one of the main sources historians use to learn about the kingdom."],
g:[["Strait","A narrow sea passage between two larger seas"],["Monsoon","Seasonal winds that change direction"],["Inscription","Writing carved into stone or metal"]],
f:["Srivijaya controlled the Strait of Malacca.","Its inscriptions are among the oldest texts in Malay.","Its name was nearly forgotten until 1918."],
fun:"Some Srivijaya inscriptions include long curses, warning that anyone who disobeyed or betrayed the king would be destroyed by magic."});

ADDART({id:"majapahit",t:"Majapahit",c:"History",i:"🏯",w:"Majapahit",r:["indonesiacountry","srivijaya","hindu","seaempires","mongol","trade"],
p:[["Java’s great empire","Majapahit was a Hindu-Buddhist empire based in eastern Java."],["Islands united","At its height it claimed influence over much of today’s Indonesia."],["Golden age","It is remembered for its art, poetry and powerful leaders."]],
l:"Majapahit was a Hindu-Buddhist empire based in eastern Java, in today’s Indonesia, from 1293 to around 1527. It was one of the last and greatest of the Hindu-Buddhist kingdoms of maritime Southeast Asia. Under its famous prime minister Gajah Mada and King Hayam Wuruk in the 14th century, Majapahit grew rich from rice farming and trade and claimed influence over much of the Indonesian archipelago and beyond. Its capital, near today’s Trowulan, was a large city of brick temples, canals and markets. Majapahit declined in the 15th and 16th centuries as Muslim trading states rose along Java’s coast. Indonesians today remember it as a symbol of a united archipelago.",
s:[["Born from a Mongol invasion","In 1293 the Mongol ruler of China, Kublai Khan, sent a fleet to punish a Javanese king. A Javanese prince, Raden Wijaya, first allied with the Mongols to defeat his rival, then turned on them and drove them out. He founded Majapahit, named after a bitter maja fruit."],["Gajah Mada’s oath","Gajah Mada, who became prime minister in the 1330s, is said to have sworn the Palapa Oath: that he would not enjoy pleasures until he had united the islands under Majapahit. Through warfare and diplomacy, he expanded the empire’s power across the archipelago."],["The golden age","Under King Hayam Wuruk, who ruled from 1350 to 1389, Majapahit reached its height. A long poem written in 1365, the Nagarakretagama, describes his court, travels and the many places that paid tribute or traded with Majapahit, from Sumatra to New Guinea."],["Decline","After Hayam Wuruk’s death, disputes over the throne weakened Majapahit. Muslim port cities along Java’s north coast, such as Demak, grew powerful from trade. By the early 16th century Majapahit had faded away, and many Hindu nobles and artists moved to Bali, where Hindu culture survives today."]],
k:[["Center","Eastern Java, near Trowulan"],["Lasted","1293 to about 1527"],["Religions","Hinduism and Buddhism"],["Famous leaders","Gajah Mada, Hayam Wuruk"],["Key text","Nagarakretagama, 1365"]],
d:[["1293","Raden Wijaya founds Majapahit after defeating a Mongol force"],["c. 1336","Gajah Mada swears the Palapa Oath"],["1350","Hayam Wuruk becomes king"],["1365","The Nagarakretagama is written"],["c. 1527","Majapahit comes to an end"]],
s2:[["Unity in diversity","Indonesia’s national motto, Bhinneka Tunggal Ika, meaning unity in diversity, comes from a Majapahit-era poem, the Kakawin Sutasoma, by the poet Mpu Tantular. It originally described the unity of Hindu and Buddhist beliefs. The red and white of Indonesia’s flag are also linked to Majapahit’s colors."],["Art and brick","Majapahit artists made red brick temples, gateways and fine terracotta figures. Archaeologists at Trowulan have found the remains of canals, water tanks, houses and Chinese coins and pottery, showing the city’s wealth and trade connections."]],
sp:["A poem that survived","The only known copy of the Nagarakretagama, written on palm leaves, was found in 1894 on the island of Lombok, when Dutch colonial forces attacked a royal palace there and took it to the Netherlands. It was returned to Indonesia in 1973 and is now listed in UNESCO’s Memory of the World register."],
g:[["Tribute","Gifts paid by a smaller state to a stronger one"],["Archipelago","A large group of islands"],["Terracotta","Baked clay used for pottery and figures"]],
f:["Majapahit was founded after driving out a Mongol force.","Indonesia’s motto comes from a Majapahit-era poem.","Many Majapahit nobles moved to Bali."],
fun:"Majapahit is named after the maja fruit, which is bitter, from Javanese words meaning bitter maja."});

ADDART({id:"mauryaempire",t:"The Maurya Empire",c:"History",i:"🦁",w:"Maurya Empire",r:["ashoka","indiacountry","alexander","buddh","hindu","persia"],
p:[["India’s first great empire","The Maurya Empire united most of South Asia for the first time."],["Founded by Chandragupta","It began around 321 BCE."],["Ashoka’s empire","Its most famous ruler, Ashoka, spread Buddhism."]],
l:"The Maurya Empire was the first empire to unite most of the Indian subcontinent, lasting from about 321 to 185 BCE. It was founded by Chandragupta Maurya, who, with the help of his advisor Chanakya, overthrew the Nanda kingdom and expanded his rule soon after Alexander the Great’s armies left the region. At its height, under Chandragupta’s grandson Ashoka, the empire stretched from today’s Afghanistan to Bangladesh and far into southern India. Its capital, Pataliputra, near modern Patna, was one of the largest cities in the world. The Mauryas built roads, collected taxes and kept a large army, and Ashoka’s inscriptions spread messages of tolerance and nonviolence.",
s:[["Chandragupta and Chanakya","Chandragupta Maurya came to power around 321 BCE. Tradition says he was guided by Chanakya, a shrewd teacher also called Kautilya, who is credited with the Arthashastra, an ancient book about government, economics and war. Chandragupta defeated the Greek general Seleucus, who gave him lands in the northwest in exchange for 500 war elephants."],["A great capital","The Greek ambassador Megasthenes lived at Pataliputra and described it as a vast city surrounded by a wooden wall with hundreds of towers and gates. He wrote about officials who managed markets, roads, foreigners and births and deaths, showing how organized the empire was."],["Ashoka’s change of heart","Ashoka conquered the kingdom of Kalinga around 261 BCE, but the bloodshed horrified him. He turned to Buddhism and ruled by dhamma, a code of moral behavior. He carved edicts on rocks and pillars across the empire urging kindness, respect for all religions and care for people and animals."],["The end","After Ashoka’s death around 232 BCE, the empire weakened and broke apart. In 185 BCE the last Maurya king, Brihadratha, was killed by his general Pushyamitra Shunga, who founded a new dynasty."]],
k:[["Lasted","About 321 to 185 BCE"],["Founder","Chandragupta Maurya"],["Capital","Pataliputra (near Patna)"],["Most famous ruler","Ashoka"],["Famous book","Arthashastra"]],
d:[["c. 326 BCE","Alexander the Great reaches the Indus region"],["c. 321 BCE","Chandragupta Maurya founds the empire"],["c. 305 BCE","Chandragupta makes peace with Seleucus"],["c. 268 BCE","Ashoka becomes emperor"],["c. 261 BCE","The Kalinga War"],["185 BCE","The last Maurya king is killed"]],
s2:[["Symbols of modern India","The Lion Capital of Ashoka, a sculpture of four lions that once topped a pillar at Sarnath, is India’s national emblem. The 24-spoked wheel from Ashoka’s pillars, the Ashoka Chakra, appears at the center of India’s flag."],["Spreading Buddhism","Ashoka sent Buddhist missionaries far beyond his empire. Tradition says his son Mahinda and daughter Sanghamitta took Buddhism to Sri Lanka. From South Asia, Buddhism later spread across Southeast and East Asia."]],
sp:["Reading the edicts","For centuries no one could read Ashoka’s inscriptions, written in an ancient script called Brahmi. In 1837 the British scholar James Prinsep deciphered it, revealing that a king called Devanampiya Piyadasi, Beloved of the Gods, was Ashoka. Brahmi is the ancestor of many scripts used in South and Southeast Asia, including Devanagari, Bengali, Tamil, Thai and Tibetan."],
g:[["Edict","An official public order or message"],["Dhamma","Ashoka’s code of moral behavior"],["Dynasty","A series of rulers from the same family"]],
f:["The Maurya Empire was the first to unite most of South Asia.","India’s national emblem comes from Ashoka’s lion pillar.","Chandragupta traded land for 500 war elephants."],
fun:"Ashoka’s edicts include orders to plant shade trees and dig wells along roads, and to provide medical care for both people and animals."});

ADDART({id:"recalgorithms",t:"Recommendation Algorithms",c:"Science and Technology",i:"🎯",w:"Recommender system",r:["socialmedia","youtube","screentime","misinformation","influencers","digitalfootprint"],
p:[["Why you see what you see","Apps use algorithms to choose videos and posts for you."],["Learning from you","They learn from what you watch, like, skip and share."],["Helpful and tricky","They can show you great things but can also keep you scrolling."]],
l:"A recommendation algorithm is a computer program that decides which videos, posts, songs, products or games to show each person. Apps such as YouTube, TikTok, Instagram, Spotify and online shops use them to fill your feed. The algorithm studies what you watch, like, share, comment on, search for and even how long you pause on something, then predicts what you are likely to enjoy next. Recommendations help people discover things they love, but they are also designed to keep people using an app for as long as possible. Understanding how they work helps you stay in control of your time and of what you see.",
s:[["How a feed is made","When you open an app, the algorithm picks from millions of possible posts. It scores each one by how likely you are to watch, like or share it, using signals from your past behavior and from people with similar tastes. The highest-scoring posts appear first. Small actions, such as rewatching a video, quickly change what you see."],["Rabbit holes","Because algorithms show more of what holds your attention, you can fall into a rabbit hole, watching video after video on one topic. Sometimes recommendations drift toward more extreme or upsetting content, because it grabs attention. Researchers and governments study how to make feeds safer, especially for young people."],["Filter bubbles","If an algorithm only shows you things you already agree with or like, you may end up in a filter bubble, seeing a narrow slice of the world. This can make it harder to understand other points of view and easier for false information to spread. Following a variety of sources helps."],["Taking control","Most apps let you tell the algorithm what you do not want, using buttons such as Not interested, or by muting words and accounts. You can clear your watch history, turn off autoplay and set time limits. Searching for topics you care about also teaches the feed what you want to see."]],
k:[["What it does","Chooses what to show each user"],["Learns from","Watching, liking, sharing, pausing"],["Used by","Video apps, social media, music and shopping sites"],["Main goal for companies","Keep users engaged"],["Risk","Rabbit holes and filter bubbles"]],
d:[["1992","An early recommender system, Tapestry, filters email at Xerox PARC"],["2006","Netflix offers a $1 million prize for better movie recommendations"],["2011","Eli Pariser warns of filter bubbles"],["2016","TikTok, built around its For You feed, launches as Douyin in China"],["2023","EU rules start requiring very large platforms to offer a feed not based on profiling"]],
s2:[["Rules and laws","Governments are starting to regulate recommendation feeds. The European Union’s Digital Services Act requires the largest platforms to explain how their feeds work and to offer a version not based on personal profiling. Australia banned social media accounts for children under 16 from December 2025, and platforms removed about 4.7 million accounts in the first month."],["Not just social media","Recommendation algorithms also suggest products on shopping sites, routes in map apps, news stories, games and even people to follow or date. Streaming services say most of what people watch comes from recommendations rather than searches."]],
sp:["Why short videos learn fast","Short-video apps can learn a new user’s tastes within minutes. Each video lasts only seconds, so the app quickly collects many signals: which videos are watched to the end, replayed or swiped away. This is why such feeds can feel personal so quickly, and why they can be hard to put down."],
g:[["Algorithm","A set of step-by-step instructions a computer follows"],["Feed","A stream of posts or videos chosen for a user"],["Filter bubble","A situation where you mostly see views like your own"]],
f:["Algorithms notice even how long you pause on a post.","Netflix once offered $1 million to improve its recommendations.","Many apps have a Not interested button to train your feed."],
fun:"In 2009, a team that improved Netflix’s movie predictions by just over 10 percent won the company’s $1 million prize."});

ADDART({id:"copyrightonline",t:"Copyright and Sharing Online",c:"Science and Technology",i:"©️",w:"Copyright",r:["netiquette","digitalfootprint","youtube","memes","wikipedia","deepfakes"],
p:[["Who owns it?","Copyright protects the creators of songs, pictures, videos and writing."],["Ask or check first","You usually need permission to use someone else’s work."],["Free to share","Some works are in the public domain or use open licenses."]],
l:"Copyright is a kind of legal protection that gives creators control over how their original work is copied and shared. It covers books, songs, photos, drawings, films, video games, computer code and online posts. In most countries copyright begins automatically the moment a work is created, without needing to register it. Online, it is very easy to copy and repost things, but that does not make it legal or fair. Using someone’s work without permission can get videos taken down or accounts blocked. At the same time, the law allows some limited uses, such as quoting for school or review, and many creators share their work freely using open licenses.",
s:[["What copyright protects","Copyright protects the way an idea is expressed, not the idea itself. A story about a wizard school is an idea anyone can use, but copying another author’s actual sentences is not allowed. Names, short phrases and facts usually are not protected by copyright, though logos and brand names may be protected by trademark law."],["How long it lasts","Copyright lasts a long time: in many countries, for the creator’s life plus 50 or 70 years. After that, a work enters the public domain, and anyone can use it freely. Most music and writing from before 1900 is in the public domain."],["Fair use","Copyright law allows some uses without permission, called fair use or fair dealing. Quoting a short passage in a school report, using a clip to review a film, or making a parody may be allowed. Whether a use is fair depends on things like how much is used, why, and whether it harms the creator’s ability to earn money."],["Open licenses","Many creators want others to use their work. Creative Commons licenses, created in 2002, let creators say, for example, that anyone may share their work if they give credit. Wikipedia’s articles and many photos on Wikimedia Commons use such licenses, which is why they can be reused with proper credit."]],
k:[["Protects","Original creative works"],["Starts","Automatically when a work is created"],["Typical term","Creator’s life plus 50–70 years"],["Key treaty","Berne Convention, 1886"],["Open licenses","Creative Commons, from 2002"]],
d:[["1710","Britain’s Statute of Anne creates the first modern copyright law"],["1886","The Berne Convention sets international copyright rules"],["1996","The WIPO Copyright Treaty updates copyright for the digital age"],["2001","Creative Commons is founded"],["2002","The first Creative Commons licenses are released"]],
s2:[["Giving credit","Giving credit, saying who made something, is good manners and is required by many open licenses. But credit alone does not make it legal to use copyrighted work without permission. When in doubt, use your own photos and music, ask the creator, or choose works marked as free to use."],["Copyright and AI","Artificial intelligence tools that create pictures, text and music are trained on huge amounts of existing work. Courts and governments are deciding whether this training is fair and who owns what AI creates. Many countries say copyright protects only works made by humans."]],
sp:["The monkey selfie","In 2011 a crested macaque in Indonesia picked up a photographer’s camera and took photos of itself. The photos spread online, and a long argument followed over who owned them. The US Copyright Office stated that works created by animals cannot be copyrighted."],
g:[["Copyright","The legal right to control copying of an original work"],["Public domain","Works no longer protected by copyright, free for all to use"],["License","Permission to use something under certain conditions"]],
f:["Copyright starts automatically when you create something.","Ideas are not protected by copyright, only how they are expressed.","Creative Commons licenses began in 2002."],
fun:"Your own drawings, photos, stories and videos are protected by copyright the moment you make them, even if you are a child."});

ADDART({id:"onlinecommunities",t:"Online Communities",c:"Science and Technology",i:"💬",w:"Online community",r:["netiquette","onlinesafety","cyberbullying","onlinegaming","socialmedia","wikipedia"],
p:[["Groups on the internet","Online communities are groups of people who meet and talk online."],["Shared interests","They form around games, hobbies, school, fandoms and causes."],["Rules matter","Good communities have clear rules and moderators."]],
l:"An online community is a group of people who regularly gather and communicate on the internet around a shared interest, place or purpose. They include discussion forums, group chats, gaming servers, fan groups, open-source software projects and wikis. Online communities let people find friends who share their interests, ask for help, learn new skills and work together, even across the world. Many young people belong to communities on platforms such as Discord, Reddit, Roblox or Minecraft servers. Like any community, they work best with clear rules, kind members and good moderators, and they can also bring risks such as bullying, scams and contact with strangers.",
s:[["A long history","Online communities are older than the World Wide Web. In 1978 two hobbyists in Chicago created one of the first bulletin board systems, where people dialed in by phone to post messages. Usenet newsgroups started in 1980. Later came web forums, chat rooms, social networks, and in the 2010s platforms such as Discord."],["Building things together","Some online communities create amazing things. Thousands of volunteers write and edit Wikipedia. Programmers around the world build free software such as Linux. Players design levels, write guides and create mods for games, and fans translate shows and books into other languages."],["Moderation","Moderators are people who enforce a community’s rules, removing harmful posts and banning troublemakers. Many are volunteers. Clear rules about respect, no harassment and no sharing of personal information help keep communities welcoming. Many platforms also use automated tools to catch spam and harmful content."],["Staying safe","In online communities, people may not be who they say they are. Keep personal details private, never share passwords, be careful with links, and do not meet online friends in person without a trusted adult. If someone makes you uncomfortable, block and report them and tell an adult you trust."]],
k:[["Examples","Forums, servers, group chats, wikis"],["Early example","Bulletin board systems, 1978"],["Kept safe by","Rules and moderators"],["Benefits","Friendship, help, learning, teamwork"],["Risks","Bullying, scams, strangers"]],
d:[["1978","One of the first bulletin board systems opens in Chicago"],["1980","Usenet newsgroups begin"],["2001","Wikipedia is founded"],["2005","Reddit launches"],["2015","Discord launches"]],
s2:[["Finding your people","For young people who feel different or isolated, online communities can offer support and belonging, connecting them with others who share their interests, identities or experiences. Studies show these connections can be valuable, especially when they add to, rather than replace, friendships in daily life."],["Toxic communities","Some communities encourage hate, dangerous challenges or unhealthy behavior. Warning signs include pressure to keep secrets, cruelty toward outsiders and leaders who demand money or loyalty. Leaving a community that makes you feel bad is always okay."]],
sp:["Wikipedia’s volunteers","Wikipedia is written and checked by volunteers, not paid writers. Tens of thousands of people edit it regularly, discussing changes on talk pages and following shared rules about neutrality and citing sources. It shows how a large online community can build something useful for everyone."],
g:[["Moderator","A person who enforces a community’s rules"],["Forum","A website where people post and reply to messages"],["Server","In apps like Discord, a community space with channels"]],
f:["Online communities are older than the World Wide Web.","Wikipedia is written by volunteers in an online community.","Moderators help keep communities safe and friendly."],
fun:"The first bulletin board system was built in early 1978, partly because a Chicago blizzard kept its two creators indoors."});

;
/* data/thin6.js */
// Thin sections, part 6 (28 November 2026): signals, the Moon, space issues, money and pop culture.
ADDART({id:"morsecode",t:"Morse Code",c:"Science and Technology",i:"📡",w:"Morse code",r:["telegraph","radio","ships","titanic","mobilenetworks"],
p:[["Dots and dashes","Morse code spells letters with short and long signals."],["Sent by wire and radio","It carried messages by telegraph and radio for over a century."],["SOS","Its distress call, SOS, became famous worldwide."]],
l:"Morse code is a way of sending text using short and long signals, called dots and dashes, or dits and dahs. Each letter and number has its own pattern: E is a single dot and T a single dash, while SOS is three dots, three dashes and three dots. It was developed in the 1830s and 1840s by the American inventor Samuel Morse and his partner Alfred Vail for the electric telegraph. Morse code can be sent by clicks on a wire, radio beeps, flashes of light or even taps and blinks. For more than a century it carried news, business and emergency messages around the world, and amateur radio operators still use it today.",
s:[["How it works","Common letters get short codes, so messages are quicker to send: E is one dot, T one dash, A is dot dash. A dash lasts three times as long as a dot, and gaps separate letters and words. Skilled operators can send and understand more than 20 words a minute by ear."],["The telegraph","On 24 May 1844 Samuel Morse sent the first long-distance telegraph message in the United States, from Washington, D.C., to Baltimore: What hath God wrought. Telegraph lines soon crossed continents, and undersea cables later linked them, letting news travel in minutes instead of weeks."],["International Morse","The original American Morse code was revised in Europe in the mid-1800s into International Morse code, which became the worldwide standard. It added codes for letters with accents and is the version used today."],["Radio and the sea","With the invention of radio, ships could send Morse messages across the ocean. In 1906 an international conference chose SOS as the distress signal, because its pattern is easy to send and recognize. The Titanic used both the older CQD call and SOS when it sank in 1912."]],
k:[["Invented by","Samuel Morse and Alfred Vail"],["First long-distance US message","24 May 1844"],["Distress signal","SOS (· · · – – – · · ·)"],["Standard form","International Morse code"],["Used today by","Amateur radio operators, pilots’ navigation beacons"]],
d:[["1837","Morse and Vail demonstrate their telegraph"],["1844","What hath God wrought is sent from Washington to Baltimore"],["1906","SOS is adopted as an international distress signal"],["1912","The Titanic sends SOS messages"],["1999","Ships switch from Morse code to satellite distress systems"]],
s2:[["Blinking a message","In 1966 Jeremiah Denton, an American prisoner of war in North Vietnam, was forced to give a television interview. While speaking, he blinked the word TORTURE in Morse code, confirming to the world how prisoners were being treated."],["Still useful","Morse code can get through when voice signals are weak or noisy, so amateur radio fans still use it. Radio beacons that guide aircraft identify themselves in Morse, and people with severe disabilities can use Morse with a single switch or eye blinks to communicate with computers."]],
sp:["Light and sound","Morse code does not need electricity. Navies flash it between ships with signal lamps, and people have sent it by tapping on pipes or walls. In emergencies, three short, three long and three short flashes of a flashlight or blasts of a whistle are widely recognized as SOS."],
g:[["Telegraph","A system for sending messages along wires using electric signals"],["Dit and dah","The short and long signals of Morse code"],["Distress signal","A call for urgent help"]],
f:["E is a single dot in Morse code.","SOS became the international distress signal in 1906.","Ships stopped using Morse for distress calls in 1999."],
fun:"SOS does not stand for anything. It was chosen simply because three dots, three dashes and three dots are easy to send and hard to mistake."});

ADDART({id:"barcodes",t:"Barcodes",c:"Science and Technology",i:"🏷️",w:"Barcode",r:["qrcodes","rfid","ecommerce","computer","laser"],
p:[["Stripes with a message","A barcode stores a number in black bars and white spaces."],["Beep at the checkout","Scanners read barcodes in a fraction of a second."],["Everywhere","Barcodes track products, parcels, books and boarding passes."]],
l:"A barcode is a pattern of black bars and white spaces, or squares and dots, that a machine can read. The widths of the bars stand for numbers or letters, which link to information in a computer, such as a product’s name and price. Barcodes speed up shopping, help stores and warehouses keep track of stock, and are used on parcels, library books, hospital wristbands, tickets and boarding passes. The barcode was invented by Norman Woodland and Bernard Silver, who patented it in 1952, and the first product scanned at a supermarket checkout was a pack of chewing gum in 1974. Two-dimensional codes such as QR codes can hold much more information.",
s:[["How a barcode works","A scanner shines light, often a laser, across the bars. Black bars absorb light and white spaces reflect it, so the scanner sees a pattern of light and dark that it turns into numbers. A check digit at the end lets the scanner confirm it read the code correctly."],["Drawn in the sand","Norman Woodland, a student who knew Morse code, came up with the idea in 1948 while sitting on a beach in Miami. He drew dots and dashes in the sand with his fingers, then pulled them downward into thin and thick lines. He and Bernard Silver filed a patent, granted in 1952."],["The Universal Product Code","Barcodes became practical only after lasers and computers became cheap. In 1973 American grocers chose a design developed at IBM by George Laurer, the Universal Product Code, or UPC. On 26 June 1974, a pack of chewing gum was scanned at a supermarket in Troy, Ohio, the first product sold using a UPC barcode."],["Many kinds","The 12-digit UPC is used in North America, and the 13-digit EAN code in much of the rest of the world; the first digits identify the country where the code was issued. Books have ISBN barcodes. Two-dimensional codes such as QR codes and Data Matrix store information in squares and can be read by phone cameras."]],
k:[["Invented by","Norman Woodland and Bernard Silver"],["Patent granted","1952"],["First supermarket scan","26 June 1974"],["Common types","UPC, EAN, QR code"],["Read by","Laser or camera scanners"]],
d:[["1948","Norman Woodland sketches the idea in the sand"],["1952","Woodland and Silver receive a patent"],["1973","US grocers adopt the Universal Product Code"],["1974","The first product is scanned at a checkout"],["1994","The QR code is invented in Japan"]],
s2:[["Beyond the store","Barcodes help hospitals match patients to the right medicine, let airlines scan boarding passes, track parcels through delivery networks and help scientists tag samples. Some researchers have even glued tiny barcodes to bees to study their behavior."],["What comes next","Newer systems such as RFID tags use radio waves instead of light, so they can be read without a direct line of sight, even many at once. Some stores already use them for clothing and self-checkout, but printed barcodes remain cheap and common."]],
sp:["The gum in the museum","The pack of Wrigley’s Juicy Fruit gum scanned in Troy, Ohio, in 1974 was chosen for the first scan partly to show that even small products could carry a barcode. The pack and its receipt are now kept at the Smithsonian’s National Museum of American History."],
g:[["Barcode","A machine-readable pattern of bars or squares"],["Scanner","A device that reads barcodes"],["Check digit","An extra number used to catch reading errors"]],
f:["The barcode idea was first drawn in sand on a beach.","The first barcode scanned at a checkout was on a pack of gum.","QR codes are a kind of two-dimensional barcode."],
fun:"Norman Woodland based the first barcode on Morse code, stretching dots and dashes into thin and thick lines."});

ADDART({id:"antennas",t:"Antennas",c:"Science and Technology",i:"📶",w:"Antenna (radio)",r:["radio","mobilenetworks","wifi","radioastronomy","smartphone"],
p:[["Catching invisible waves","Antennas send and receive radio waves."],["Hidden everywhere","Phones, cars, routers and satellites all have antennas."],["Size matters","An antenna’s size is matched to the wavelength it uses."]],
l:"An antenna is a device that sends out or picks up radio waves, turning electric signals into invisible waves and back again. Antennas are used in radios, televisions, mobile phones, Wi-Fi routers, GPS receivers, radar, satellites and radio telescopes. They come in many shapes: rods, wires, flat panels, loops and large curved dishes. The size and shape of an antenna depend on the wavelength of the radio waves it is designed for, so a radio tower can be hundreds of meters tall while a phone antenna fits inside the case. Heinrich Hertz built the first antennas in the 1880s to prove that radio waves exist.",
s:[["How antennas work","When an alternating electric current flows back and forth in an antenna, it makes electromagnetic waves that spread outward at the speed of light. When radio waves reach another antenna, they push electrons back and forth inside it, creating a tiny current that a receiver can amplify and decode."],["Matching the wavelength","Antennas work best when their length matches the wavelength. A common design, the half-wave dipole, is half as long as the wave. Long waves used for AM radio need big towers, while the short waves used by Wi-Fi and phones need antennas only a few centimeters long."],["Many designs","A Yagi antenna, invented in Japan in the 1920s, has rows of metal rods and points at a distant station, like old rooftop TV aerials. Dish antennas focus waves like a mirror focuses light, letting satellite TV and deep-space antennas catch faint signals. Phones use tiny antennas printed on circuit boards or built into their frames."],["Phased arrays","Some antennas are made of many small elements working together. By shifting the timing of signals in each element, a phased array can steer its beam electronically without moving. Modern radar, 5G phone towers and satellite internet dishes use this technology."]],
k:[["Job","Send and receive radio waves"],["First built by","Heinrich Hertz, 1880s"],["Key rule","Size matches wavelength"],["Common types","Dipole, Yagi, dish, phased array"],["Largest single dish","FAST, China, 500 m"]],
d:[["1886–1888","Heinrich Hertz builds antennas and detects radio waves"],["1901","Guglielmo Marconi receives a radio signal across the Atlantic"],["1926","Hidetsugu Yagi and Shintaro Uda describe the Yagi antenna"],["1960s","Giant dish antennas begin tracking spacecraft"],["2016","China completes FAST, a 500-meter radio telescope dish"]],
s2:[["Listening to space","Radio telescopes are giant antennas that pick up radio waves from stars, galaxies and the gas between them. NASA’s Deep Space Network uses dishes 70 meters wide in California, Spain and Australia to talk with spacecraft billions of kilometers away, including the Voyager probes."],["Antennas in your body’s world","A modern smartphone may contain several antennas for mobile networks, Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, GPS and contactless payments. Engineers must squeeze them into a thin case and keep them working when a hand covers part of the phone."]],
sp:["Signals from Voyager","Voyager 1, launched in 1977, is more than 24 billion kilometers from Earth. Its radio transmitter has only about the power of a refrigerator light bulb, and by the time its signal reaches Earth it is incredibly faint. Only the huge dish antennas of the Deep Space Network can still pick it up."],
g:[["Antenna","A device that sends or receives radio waves"],["Wavelength","The distance between one wave peak and the next"],["Phased array","A group of antennas that steer a beam electronically"]],
f:["Heinrich Hertz built the first antennas in the 1880s.","Antenna size is matched to the wavelength it uses.","The Deep Space Network’s largest dishes are 70 meters wide."],
fun:"The radio meaning of the word antenna is credited to Guglielmo Marconi. In Italian, antenna means a pole, and he used it for the tall masts that held up his wires."});

ADDART({id:"apollo11",t:"Apollo 11",c:"Space",i:"👨‍🚀",w:"Apollo 11",r:["apollo","armstrong","apollo8","apollo13","moon","artemis"],
p:[["First Moon landing","Apollo 11 landed the first people on the Moon in July 1969."],["Three astronauts","Neil Armstrong, Buzz Aldrin and Michael Collins flew the mission."],["One small step","Armstrong’s first words on the Moon became famous."]],
l:"Apollo 11 was the American space mission that landed the first humans on the Moon. It launched from Florida on 16 July 1969 on a Saturn V rocket, carrying astronauts Neil Armstrong, Buzz Aldrin and Michael Collins. On 20 July, Armstrong and Aldrin landed the lunar module Eagle in the Sea of Tranquility, while Collins orbited above in the command module Columbia. Armstrong stepped onto the surface first, saying: That’s one small step for man, one giant leap for mankind. The two men spent about two and a half hours walking on the Moon, collected rocks, set up experiments and planted a US flag before returning safely to Earth on 24 July.",
s:[["The journey","The Saturn V, the most powerful rocket flown up to that time, sent the astronauts on a three-day trip to the Moon. After entering lunar orbit, Armstrong and Aldrin climbed into Eagle and separated from Columbia. Collins stayed behind, circling the Moon alone and losing radio contact with Earth each time he passed behind it."],["A tense landing","As Eagle descended, its computer flashed unfamiliar 1202 and 1201 alarms because it was overloaded, but mission controllers decided it was safe to continue. Armstrong saw the planned landing site was strewn with boulders and flew the lander manually to a safer spot, touching down with less than a minute of fuel left. He radioed: The Eagle has landed."],["On the surface","About six and a half hours later, Armstrong climbed down the ladder and stepped onto the Moon. Aldrin followed. They found it easy to move in the low gravity, collected about 21.5 kilograms of rock and soil, set up a laser reflector and a seismometer, and spoke by phone with US President Richard Nixon."],["Coming home","After about 21 hours on the Moon, Eagle lifted off and rejoined Columbia. The crew splashed down in the Pacific Ocean on 24 July 1969 and spent three weeks in quarantine in case they had brought back germs from the Moon. Around 650 million people are thought to have watched the first steps on television."]],
k:[["Launch","16 July 1969"],["Moon landing","20 July 1969"],["Crew","Neil Armstrong, Buzz Aldrin, Michael Collins"],["Landing site","Sea of Tranquility"],["Samples returned","About 21.5 kg"]],
d:[["16 July 1969","Apollo 11 launches from Kennedy Space Center"],["19 July 1969","The spacecraft enters orbit around the Moon"],["20 July 1969","Eagle lands in the Sea of Tranquility"],["21 July 1969","Armstrong steps onto the Moon (UTC)"],["24 July 1969","The crew splashes down in the Pacific"]],
s2:[["What they left behind","The astronauts left a plaque reading: Here men from the planet Earth first set foot upon the Moon, July 1969 A.D. We came in peace for all mankind. They also left a disk with goodwill messages from world leaders, medals honoring astronauts and cosmonauts who had died, and the laser reflector, which scientists still bounce lasers off to measure the Moon’s distance."],["A race won","Apollo 11 fulfilled President John F. Kennedy’s 1961 goal of landing a man on the Moon before the end of the decade and was a high point of the space race with the Soviet Union. Six Apollo missions landed in all, and twelve astronauts walked on the Moon by the end of 1972."]],
sp:["The forgotten astronaut","Michael Collins never walked on the Moon, but his job was vital: if anything went wrong, he had to bring Columbia home. Alone behind the Moon, cut off from all contact, he later wrote that he felt not lonely but aware, and even joyful. He is sometimes called the loneliest man in history."],
g:[["Lunar module","The spacecraft that landed on the Moon"],["Command module","The spacecraft that carried the crew to the Moon and back"],["Quarantine","Keeping people apart to stop the spread of possible germs"]],
f:["Eagle landed with less than a minute of fuel to spare.","About 650 million people watched the first steps.","A laser reflector left by Apollo 11 is still used today."],
fun:"The footprints left by Armstrong and Aldrin are probably still there, because the Moon has no wind or rain to wipe them away."});

ADDART({id:"moonrocks",t:"Moon Rocks",c:"Space",i:"🪨",w:"Moon rock",r:["apollo","apollo11","changeprogram","lunaprogram","moon","meteorites"],
p:[["Pieces of the Moon","Moon rocks are samples brought back from the Moon or fallen as meteorites."],["Clues to the past","They reveal how the Moon and Earth formed."],["Rare treasures","Only a few hundred kilograms have ever been collected."]],
l:"Moon rocks are pieces of the Moon studied by scientists on Earth. Most were collected by the six Apollo missions that landed astronauts on the Moon between 1969 and 1972, which brought back about 382 kilograms of rock, soil and core samples. Robotic Soviet Luna probes returned small amounts in the 1970s, and China’s Chang’e 5 and Chang’e 6 missions brought back samples in 2020 and 2024, the latter from the Moon’s far side for the first time. Other pieces arrive on Earth naturally as lunar meteorites. Moon rocks show that the Moon is about 4.5 billion years old and support the idea that it formed after a giant collision with the young Earth.",
s:[["Kinds of rock","The Moon’s dark plains, called maria, are made of basalt, volcanic rock from ancient lava flows. The bright highlands are made mostly of anorthosite, a pale rock that floated to the top of a global ocean of molten rock when the Moon was young. Many samples are breccias, broken rock fragments welded together by impacts."],["Where the Moon came from","Moon rocks are remarkably similar to Earth rocks in the types of oxygen they contain, but they have almost no water and fewer easily evaporated elements. This supports the giant-impact hypothesis: that a Mars-sized body struck the early Earth about 4.5 billion years ago, and the Moon formed from the debris."],["Robot collectors","The Soviet Luna 16, 20 and 24 missions returned about 300 grams of soil between 1970 and 1976. China’s Chang’e 5 brought back about 1.7 kilograms of young volcanic rock in 2020. In 2024 Chang’e 6 returned about 1.9 kilograms from a huge crater on the far side, the first samples from the side always facing away from Earth."],["Protected and shared","Apollo samples are stored in special nitrogen-filled cabinets at NASA’s Johnson Space Center in Houston to keep them clean. Scientists around the world can borrow small pieces for research. Some samples were sealed in 1972 and opened only in 2019 and later, so that newer tools could study them."]],
k:[["Apollo samples","About 382 kg"],["Luna samples","About 300 g"],["Chang’e 5 (2020)","About 1.7 kg"],["Chang’e 6 (2024)","About 1.9 kg, from the far side"],["Age of the Moon","About 4.5 billion years"]],
d:[["1969","Apollo 11 returns the first Moon rocks"],["1970","Luna 16 makes the first robotic sample return"],["1972","Apollo 17 returns the last Apollo samples"],["1982","Scientists identify the first lunar meteorite, found in Antarctica"],["2020","Chang’e 5 returns samples"],["2024","Chang’e 6 returns the first far-side samples"]],
s2:[["The Genesis Rock","Among the most famous Moon rocks is a piece of anorthosite collected by Apollo 15 astronauts in 1971, nicknamed the Genesis Rock. More than 4 billion years old, it is a piece of the Moon’s early crust and helped scientists understand how the Moon’s surface formed."],["Lost gifts","After the Apollo 11 and Apollo 17 missions, the United States gave small pieces of Moon rock, mounted in displays, to more than 130 countries and to every US state. Many have since gone missing, been stolen or sold illegally, and volunteers have tracked down some of them."]],
sp:["Moon rocks from the sky","Not all Moon rocks came back on spacecraft. When asteroids strike the Moon, some rock is blasted into space, and a few pieces eventually fall to Earth as meteorites. More than 500 lunar meteorites have been found, many in the deserts of North Africa and on the ice of Antarctica, where dark rocks stand out against the white."],
g:[["Maria","The dark plains on the Moon, made of ancient lava"],["Anorthosite","A pale rock that makes up the Moon’s highlands"],["Breccia","Rock made of broken pieces cemented together"]],
f:["Apollo astronauts brought back about 382 kilograms of Moon rock.","Chang’e 6 returned the first samples from the Moon’s far side in 2024.","Some Apollo samples stayed sealed for nearly 50 years."],
fun:"Moon dust is so sharp and clingy that it scratched Apollo spacesuits and smelled, according to the astronauts, like spent gunpowder."});

ADDART({id:"artemisii",t:"Artemis II",c:"Space",i:"🌘",w:"Artemis II",r:["artemis","apollo8","apollo11","nasa","csa","artemisaccords"],
p:[["Back around the Moon","Artemis II carried four astronauts around the Moon in April 2026."],["First since Apollo","It was the first crewed flight beyond low Earth orbit since 1972."],["Record breaker","The crew traveled farther from Earth than any humans before."]],
l:"Artemis II was the first crewed mission of NASA’s Artemis program and the first time people had traveled to the vicinity of the Moon since Apollo 17 in 1972. Four astronauts, NASA’s Reid Wiseman, Victor Glover and Christina Koch and the Canadian Space Agency’s Jeremy Hansen, launched from Florida on 1 April 2026 aboard an Orion spacecraft on top of the Space Launch System rocket. They looped around the far side of the Moon on a free-return path, setting a new record for the farthest distance humans have traveled from Earth, about 406,800 kilometers, and splashed down in the Pacific Ocean on 10 April 2026. The flight tested Orion’s systems with people on board before future landings.",
s:[["The crew","Commander Reid Wiseman led the mission, with pilot Victor Glover and mission specialists Christina Koch and Jeremy Hansen. Koch became the first woman, Glover the first Black astronaut and Hansen the first non-American to travel around the Moon."],["The flight","After launch, Orion first circled Earth to check its systems, then fired its engine to head for the Moon. It swung around the Moon’s far side, using the Moon’s gravity to sling it back toward Earth without needing a big engine burn, a free-return trajectory like the one used by Apollo 13."],["A delayed start","Artemis II faced years of delays. A launch attempt in early 2026 was called off after a leak, and the rocket was rolled back for repairs before the successful launch on 1 April. The mission followed Artemis I, an uncrewed test flight around the Moon in 2022."],["Testing for landings","The crew tested Orion’s life support, navigation and communication systems, practiced flying the spacecraft by hand and took photographs and observations of the Moon. Orion’s heat shield protected them as they returned through Earth’s atmosphere at about 40,000 kilometers per hour."]],
k:[["Launch","1 April 2026"],["Splashdown","10 April 2026"],["Crew","Reid Wiseman, Victor Glover, Christina Koch, Jeremy Hansen"],["Spacecraft","Orion on the Space Launch System"],["Distance record","About 406,800 km from Earth"]],
d:[["2019","NASA names the Artemis program"],["2022","Artemis I flies an uncrewed Orion around the Moon"],["2023","The Artemis II crew is announced"],["1 April 2026","Artemis II launches"],["10 April 2026","The crew splashes down in the Pacific"]],
s2:[["What comes next","In February 2026 NASA reshaped its plans. Artemis III, targeted for 2027, will test docking Orion with a commercial lunar lander in orbit around Earth, and the first crewed landing near the Moon’s south pole is planned for Artemis IV in 2028."],["International teamwork","Artemis is a partnership. Canada provided an astronaut seat in exchange for building a robotic arm for a future lunar space station. The European Space Agency builds Orion’s service module, which provides power, propulsion, air and water."]],
sp:["Breaking Apollo 13’s record","For 56 years, the record for the farthest humans had ever traveled from Earth belonged to the crew of Apollo 13, who in 1970 swung around the far side of the Moon after an explosion forced them to abandon a landing. Artemis II’s crew passed that distance on their flight around the Moon."],
g:[["Free-return trajectory","A path that uses the Moon’s gravity to send a spacecraft back to Earth"],["Orion","NASA’s spacecraft for carrying astronauts beyond low Earth orbit"],["Heat shield","A layer that protects a spacecraft from heat during reentry"]],
f:["Artemis II was the first crewed trip near the Moon since 1972.","Christina Koch became the first woman to travel around the Moon.","The crew set a record for the farthest humans have traveled from Earth."],
fun:"From the far side of the Moon, the Artemis II astronauts could see the whole Moon close up with Earth appearing small behind it, a view no humans had seen for more than 50 years."});

ADDART({id:"spacemedicine",t:"Space Medicine",c:"Space",i:"🩺",w:"Space medicine",r:["astronauts","iss","spacetourism","spaceweather","telemedicine","heart"],
p:[["Health beyond Earth","Space medicine studies how space affects the human body."],["Weightless bodies","Without gravity, bones and muscles weaken."],["Protecting astronauts","Exercise, diet and shielding keep crews healthy."]],
l:"Space medicine is the branch of medicine that studies how spaceflight affects the human body and how to keep astronauts healthy. In weightlessness, bones lose minerals, muscles shrink, fluids shift toward the head, and the heart and eyes change. Astronauts are also exposed to more radiation than on Earth, sleep in a noisy, confined place and live far from hospitals. Doctors and scientists use exercise, special diets, medicines and careful monitoring to protect them. Research on the International Space Station has shown what happens to the body over months in space, knowledge needed before longer missions to the Moon and Mars.",
s:[["Bones and muscles","On Earth, gravity constantly loads bones and muscles. In space, astronauts can lose roughly 1 to 1.5 percent of the bone mass in their hips and spine each month. Muscles in the legs and back weaken. To slow these losses, astronauts on the space station exercise about two hours a day on a treadmill, a bike and a resistance machine."],["Fluids, heart and eyes","Without gravity pulling fluids down, blood and other fluids shift toward the head, making faces puffy and legs thin. The heart does not have to work as hard and can shrink slightly. Some astronauts develop swelling at the back of the eye that blurs vision, a condition still being studied."],["Radiation","Outside Earth’s protective atmosphere and magnetic field, astronauts receive much higher doses of radiation from the Sun and cosmic rays, raising their lifetime risk of cancer. Spacecraft have shielded areas where crews shelter during solar storms. Protecting crews from radiation is one of the biggest challenges for a mission to Mars."],["Mind and sleep","Living in a small space with the same few people, far from family, can be stressful. Astronauts see about 16 sunrises a day on the space station, which confuses the body clock. Careful schedules, lighting that mimics day and night, private calls home and psychological support help."]],
k:[["Bone loss in space","About 1–1.5% per month in some bones"],["Daily exercise on the ISS","About 2 hours"],["Longest single spaceflight","437 days, Valeri Polyakov (1994–95)"],["Main dangers","Weightlessness, radiation, isolation"],["Famous study","NASA Twins Study, 2015–2016"]],
d:[["1961","Yuri Gagarin shows that humans can survive spaceflight"],["1995","Valeri Polyakov completes a record 437 days in space"],["2016","Scott Kelly returns after nearly a year in space for the Twins Study"],["2019","Results of the Twins Study are published"],["2023","Frank Rubio returns after 371 days, a US record"]],
s2:[["The Twins Study","Astronaut Scott Kelly spent nearly a year on the space station while his identical twin brother Mark, also an astronaut, stayed on Earth. Comparing the twins showed changes in Scott’s genes’ activity, immune system and eyes, though most returned to normal after he came home."],["Benefits on Earth","Space medicine has helped people on the ground. Research on bone loss in astronauts has improved understanding of osteoporosis, and techniques for remote medical care in space have helped doctors treat patients in isolated places through telemedicine."]],
sp:["Back on their feet","After months in space, returning astronauts often feel dizzy and heavy, and some cannot walk steadily at first. They are often helped out of their capsule and spend weeks doing physical therapy to rebuild balance, bone and muscle. Many say even holding up their head feels hard on the first day."],
g:[["Microgravity","The near-weightlessness felt in orbit"],["Cosmic rays","High-energy particles from space"],["Osteoporosis","A condition in which bones become weak and brittle"]],
f:["Astronauts on the space station exercise about two hours a day.","They see about 16 sunrises a day.","The longest single spaceflight lasted 437 days."],
fun:"Astronauts can grow up to about 3 percent taller in space because their spines stretch without gravity, but they shrink back after returning to Earth."});

ADDART({id:"spacemining",t:"Space Mining",c:"Space",i:"⛏️",w:"Space mining",r:["asteroids","spacelaw","artemisaccords","moonbases","spacex","moonrocks"],
p:[["Resources from space","Space mining means using materials from the Moon, asteroids or planets."],["Water is the prize","Ice could provide drinking water, air and rocket fuel."],["Not yet begun","No commercial mining in space has happened so far."]],
l:"Space mining is the idea of extracting useful materials from the Moon, asteroids, comets or other worlds. The most valuable early target is probably water ice, found in permanently shadowed craters near the Moon’s poles. Water can be split into hydrogen and oxygen for rocket fuel and breathing air, so using it in space would be far cheaper than launching it from Earth. Some asteroids contain metals such as iron, nickel and platinum. No commercial mining has yet taken place beyond Earth, but space agencies have returned samples from asteroids, and companies and governments are developing technologies and rules for future mining.",
s:[["Ice on the Moon","In 2009 NASA’s LCROSS mission crashed a rocket stage into a dark crater near the Moon’s south pole and detected water in the plume of debris. Later studies confirmed ice in shadowed craters at both poles. This is a major reason why many countries plan to send astronauts and robots to the lunar south pole."],["Living off the land","Using local materials in space is called in-situ resource utilization. Besides water, lunar soil contains oxygen bound in minerals, and it could be melted or pressed into bricks for building. Experiments have already produced oxygen from simulated lunar soil and from Mars air, on NASA’s Perseverance rover."],["Asteroids","Asteroids are leftovers from the formation of the solar system. Some are rocky, some icy and some metal-rich. Japan’s Hayabusa2 and NASA’s OSIRIS-REx brought back samples from the asteroids Ryugu and Bennu, and NASA’s Psyche spacecraft is traveling to a metal-rich asteroid, arriving in 2029."],["Who owns space?","The 1967 Outer Space Treaty says no country can claim the Moon or other celestial bodies. Some countries, including the United States and Luxembourg, have passed laws letting their companies own resources they extract. The Artemis Accords, signed by many countries, support resource use, while others argue for a shared international system."]],
k:[["Main early target","Water ice at the Moon’s poles"],["Water uses","Drinking, breathing air, rocket fuel"],["Asteroid samples returned","Itokawa, Ryugu, Bennu"],["Key treaty","Outer Space Treaty, 1967"],["Commercial mining so far","None"]],
d:[["1967","The Outer Space Treaty is signed"],["2009","LCROSS detects water at the Moon’s south pole"],["2015","A US law lets American companies own resources they mine in space"],["2020","Hayabusa2 returns samples from asteroid Ryugu"],["2023","OSIRIS-REx returns samples from asteroid Bennu"]],
s2:[["A one-dollar deal","In 2020 NASA agreed to buy small amounts of lunar soil from private companies for prices from $1 to $15,000, to set an example that companies can collect and sell resources in space. The companies would collect the soil on future missions and transfer ownership to NASA on the Moon."],["Worries and limits","Critics warn that mining could damage scientifically valuable sites, create space debris and benefit only rich countries and companies. Bringing large amounts of material back to Earth would be extremely expensive, so most experts expect space resources to be used in space, at least at first."]],
sp:["A sample from Bennu","In September 2023 a capsule from NASA’s OSIRIS-REx spacecraft landed in the Utah desert with about 121 grams of rock and dust from the asteroid Bennu, the largest asteroid sample ever returned. Scientists found water-bearing minerals and building blocks of life, but extracting such materials on a large scale remains a challenge for the future."],
g:[["In-situ resource utilization","Using materials found where you are in space"],["Permanently shadowed crater","A crater never reached by sunlight"],["Regolith","The loose rock and dust covering the Moon or an asteroid"]],
f:["Water ice exists in dark craters at the Moon’s poles.","OSIRIS-REx brought back 121 grams of asteroid Bennu.","No commercial mining has happened in space yet."],
fun:"Rocket fuel made from water on the Moon could make the Moon a refueling station for missions going deeper into the solar system."});

ADDART({id:"satellites",t:"Satellites",c:"Space",i:"🛰️",w:"Satellite",r:["sputnik","gps","weathersatellites","megaconstellations","spacejunk","antennas"],
p:[["Machines in orbit","Artificial satellites are machines that circle Earth."],["Part of daily life","They bring us weather forecasts, maps, TV and internet."],["Getting crowded","More than 15,000 working satellites now orbit Earth."]],
l:"An artificial satellite is a machine placed in orbit around Earth or another body. The first, the Soviet Union’s Sputnik 1, was launched on 4 October 1957. Today satellites provide navigation through systems like GPS, weather forecasts, television, phone and internet links, images of Earth for maps and disaster response, and scientific observations of space. They orbit at different heights: low Earth orbit, a few hundred kilometers up, and geostationary orbit, about 35,800 kilometers up, where a satellite stays over the same spot. As of November 2026, more than 15,800 working satellites orbit Earth, about 11,000 of them belonging to SpaceX’s Starlink internet network.",
s:[["Staying in orbit","A satellite stays up because it moves sideways extremely fast, about 28,000 kilometers per hour in low orbit. It is always falling toward Earth, but it moves forward so quickly that Earth’s surface curves away beneath it. Higher orbits need less speed, and satellites there take longer to go around."],["Kinds of orbit","Low Earth orbit is home to the International Space Station, Earth-imaging satellites and internet constellations. Navigation satellites such as GPS orbit at about 20,000 kilometers. Geostationary satellites, 35,786 kilometers above the equator, match Earth’s rotation, so TV dishes can point at them without moving."],["Jobs satellites do","Communications satellites relay phone calls, TV and internet. Weather satellites track storms. Navigation satellites send precise timing signals that phones use to find their location. Observation satellites map forests, crops, cities and ice. Science satellites, like space telescopes, look out into the universe."],["Crowded skies","The number of satellites has soared since 2019 as companies launched large constellations for internet service. More satellites bring more risk of collisions and space debris, and their reflected light can streak across astronomers’ images. Rules for removing old satellites and avoiding collisions are being developed."]],
k:[["First satellite","Sputnik 1, 4 October 1957"],["Working satellites","More than 15,800 (November 2026)"],["Largest network","Starlink, about 11,000 satellites"],["Low orbit speed","About 28,000 km/h"],["Geostationary height","About 35,786 km"]],
d:[["1945","Arthur C. Clarke proposes geostationary communication satellites"],["1957","Sputnik 1 becomes the first artificial satellite"],["1962","Telstar relays the first live transatlantic TV"],["1978","The first GPS satellite is launched"],["2019","SpaceX begins launching Starlink satellites in large batches"]],
s2:[["Satellites in disasters","After earthquakes, floods and fires, satellites quickly photograph damaged areas, helping rescuers plan where to go. An international agreement, the International Charter on Space and Major Disasters, lets countries hit by disasters request free satellite images."],["Small satellites","Not all satellites are big. CubeSats, built from cubes 10 centimeters on a side, are cheap enough for universities and even schools to build. Hundreds have been launched to test new technology and study Earth and space."]],
sp:["Spotting satellites yourself","Many satellites can be seen with the naked eye on a clear night, shortly after sunset or before sunrise, as steady points of light moving across the sky. The International Space Station is often the brightest. Free apps and websites predict when satellites will pass overhead."],
g:[["Orbit","The curved path of an object around a planet or star"],["Geostationary orbit","An orbit where a satellite stays above one spot on Earth"],["Constellation","In spaceflight, a group of satellites working together"]],
f:["Sputnik 1 was launched in 1957.","More than 15,800 working satellites orbit Earth.","Geostationary satellites orbit about 35,786 kilometers up."],
fun:"Sputnik 1 was a metal ball about the size of a beach ball, and its simple beeping radio signal could be picked up by amateur radio operators around the world."});

ADDART({id:"banks",t:"Banks",c:"Society",i:"🏦",w:"Bank",r:["budgeting","interestdebt","digitalpay","creditcards","inflation","financialliteracy","imfworldbank"],
p:[["Keeping money safe","Banks hold people’s money and lend it to others."],["Interest","Banks pay interest on savings and charge interest on loans."],["Central banks","A country’s central bank manages its money supply."]],
l:"A bank is a business that keeps people’s money safe, lends money and helps move money from place to place. When you deposit money in a bank, it can pay you interest, and it lends much of that money to people and businesses who need loans for homes, cars, schools or new companies, charging them higher interest. Banks also provide payment cards, transfers and online banking. A country’s central bank, such as the US Federal Reserve or the European Central Bank, manages the money supply, sets key interest rates and oversees other banks. Modern banking grew from moneylenders and merchants in medieval Italy, and the word bank comes from the Italian banco, meaning bench.",
s:[["How banks make money","Banks earn money mainly from the difference between the interest they charge borrowers and the interest they pay savers. They also charge fees for services such as transfers, cards and accounts. Because only some depositors want their money at the same time, banks can lend out most of what they hold, keeping a portion in reserve."],["From benches to branches","In medieval Italy, moneychangers sat at benches in markets to exchange coins and lend money. Wealthy families such as the Medici of Florence built banking networks across Europe. Banca Monte dei Paschi di Siena, founded in 1472, is often called the oldest bank still operating."],["Central banks","The Swedish Riksbank, founded in 1668, and the Bank of England, founded in 1694, are among the oldest central banks. Central banks issue banknotes, act as lenders of last resort when banks are in trouble and adjust interest rates to keep prices stable and support jobs."],["Bank runs and safety","If many depositors fear a bank will fail and rush to withdraw money at once, it can collapse, a bank run. After waves of bank failures in the Great Depression, many countries created deposit insurance, which guarantees savers’ money up to a limit. Governments also regulate how much banks must hold in reserve."]],
k:[["Main jobs","Holding deposits, lending, payments"],["Earn money from","Interest differences and fees"],["Oldest operating bank","Monte dei Paschi di Siena, 1472"],["Oldest central bank","Sveriges Riksbank, 1668"],["Word origin","Italian banco, bench"]],
d:[["1472","Monte dei Paschi di Siena is founded"],["1668","Sweden’s Riksbank is founded"],["1694","The Bank of England is founded"],["1933","The United States creates federal deposit insurance"],["1967","The first cash machine (ATM) opens in London"]],
s2:[["Banking for everyone","More than a billion adults around the world still do not have a bank account. Mobile money services, which let people store and send money with a basic phone, have spread banking to millions, especially in Africa. Microfinance banks such as Grameen Bank, founded in Bangladesh by Muhammad Yunus, make small loans to people without collateral."],["Digital banks","Many people now bank mostly through apps, checking balances, paying bills and sending money instantly. Some banks have no branches at all. This convenience brings new risks, such as scams and phishing, so banks use passwords, one-time codes and fraud alerts to protect customers."]],
sp:["The first cash machine","On 27 June 1967, the world’s first cash machine opened at a Barclays bank branch in Enfield, north London. Customers used special paper vouchers, not plastic cards, to withdraw cash. Its inventor, John Shepherd-Barron, said he came up with the idea after arriving at his bank just after it closed."],
g:[["Deposit","Money placed in a bank account"],["Interest","Money paid for borrowing or earned for saving"],["Central bank","A country’s main bank, which manages its money"]],
f:["The word bank comes from the Italian word for bench.","The world’s first cash machine opened in London in 1967.","Sweden’s Riksbank is the oldest central bank."],
fun:"The word bankrupt comes from the Italian banca rotta, broken bench, from the custom of breaking the bench of a moneylender who could not pay his debts."});

ADDART({id:"retirement",t:"Pensions and Retirement",c:"Society",i:"👵",w:"Pension",r:["aging","budgeting","banks","interestdebt","stockm","financialliteracy"],
p:[["Life after work","Retirement is when people stop working, usually in later life."],["Paying for it","Pensions and savings provide income after work ends."],["An aging world","As people live longer, countries are rethinking pensions."]],
l:"Retirement is the stage of life when people stop working full time, usually in their 60s. Retired people often live on a pension, regular payments from the government, a former employer or their own savings. Most countries have a state pension system paid for by taxes or contributions from workers, and many people also save in workplace or personal retirement plans that invest money over decades. Germany created one of the first national old-age pensions in 1889. Today, as people live longer and fewer babies are born, many countries face the challenge of paying for more retirees with fewer workers, and some are raising the age at which pensions begin.",
s:[["Kinds of pensions","State pensions are run by governments and often paid from the contributions of today’s workers. Workplace pensions are run by employers, sometimes promising a set income for life and sometimes building up a savings pot. Personal pensions and savings accounts let individuals put money aside themselves, often with tax benefits."],["The power of starting early","Money invested for retirement can grow through compound interest, earning returns on past returns. Someone who starts saving small amounts in their 20s may end up with more than someone who saves larger amounts starting in their 40s, because the money has longer to grow."],["Retirement ages","Pension ages vary by country, typically between about 60 and 67. Many countries are gradually raising them as life expectancy rises. Such changes are often controversial, and proposals to raise the retirement age have led to large protests in some countries."],["An aging world","In 1950 there were many working-age people for every person over 65. Today that ratio is much lower in countries such as Japan, Italy and South Korea, where more than one in five people is over 65. Countries are responding by raising pension ages, encouraging people to work longer, increasing contributions and attracting younger workers."]],
k:[["Usual age","About 60 to 67, varying by country"],["First national old-age pension","Germany, 1889"],["Main kinds","State, workplace and personal pensions"],["Key idea","Compound interest rewards saving early"],["Big challenge","Aging populations"]],
d:[["1889","Germany introduces old-age pensions under Otto von Bismarck"],["1908","Britain introduces a state pension"],["1935","The United States creates Social Security"],["1981","Chile introduces a system of private individual pension accounts"]],
s2:[["Life in retirement","Retirement can last 20 or 30 years. Many retired people travel, volunteer, care for grandchildren, learn new skills or start new projects. Staying active, connected to friends and engaged with interests is linked to better health and happiness in later life."],["Not everyone can retire","Hundreds of millions of older people around the world have no pension, especially those who worked in informal jobs without contracts. They may rely on family or keep working into old age. Some countries have created small universal pensions paid to all older people to reduce poverty."]],
sp:["Why 65?","The idea that retirement begins around 65 dates back more than a century. Germany’s first pensions in 1889 started at age 70, later lowered to 65, at a time when few people lived that long. Today many people live 20 years or more beyond 65, which is why governments are debating whether the old age limits still make sense."],
g:[["Pension","Regular payments made to a person after they retire"],["Compound interest","Interest earned on both savings and past interest"],["Life expectancy","The average number of years a person is expected to live"]],
f:["Germany introduced old-age pensions in 1889.","Pension ages usually range from about 60 to 67.","Saving early lets compound interest do more work."],
fun:"When Germany introduced old-age pensions in 1889, the starting age was 70, older than most workers of that time ever lived to."});

ADDART({id:"consumerrights",t:"Consumer Rights",c:"Society",i:"🛒",w:"Consumer protection",r:["ecommerce","onlinescams","advertising","costliving","budgeting","digitalpay"],
p:[["Fair deals for shoppers","Consumer rights protect people who buy goods and services."],["Safety and honesty","Products should be safe and described truthfully."],["Refunds and repairs","Faulty products can be returned, repaired or replaced."]],
l:"Consumer rights are protections that help people buying goods and services get safe products, honest information and fair treatment. They include the right to products that are safe and work as described, to clear prices and information, to choose between competing products, to complain and be heard, and to a repair, replacement or refund when something is faulty. Governments enforce these rights through laws and consumer protection agencies, and consumer groups test products and campaign for change. In 1962 US President John F. Kennedy set out four basic consumer rights, and 15 March is celebrated as World Consumer Rights Day.",
s:[["The basic rights","Kennedy named the right to safety, the right to be informed, the right to choose and the right to be heard. Consumer groups later added the right to redress, which means fixing problems; the right to consumer education; the right to a healthy environment; and the right to basic needs such as food, water and housing."],["When something goes wrong","In many countries, goods must be of reasonable quality, fit for their purpose and as described. If a new product breaks soon after purchase, buyers can usually ask for a repair, replacement or refund. Keeping receipts, checking warranties and contacting the seller first are useful steps."],["Shopping online","Online shopping has its own protections. In the European Union, buyers generally have 14 days to change their minds about most online purchases. Paying with methods that offer buyer protection, checking seller reviews and avoiding deals that seem too good to be true help shoppers avoid scams."],["Product safety","Governments set safety standards for things like toys, cars, food, electrical goods and medicines. When dangerous faults are found, companies must issue recalls and fix or replace products. Investigations by journalists and campaigners, such as Ralph Nader’s 1965 book on car safety, Unsafe at Any Speed, led to stronger safety laws."]],
k:[["Kennedy’s four rights","Safety, information, choice, being heard"],["World Consumer Rights Day","15 March"],["UN guidelines","1985"],["Common remedies","Repair, replacement or refund"],["EU online cooling-off period","Generally 14 days"]],
d:[["1906","The United States passes its first federal food and drug safety laws"],["1962","President Kennedy sets out four consumer rights"],["1965","Ralph Nader publishes Unsafe at Any Speed"],["1983","World Consumer Rights Day is first celebrated"],["1985","The United Nations adopts guidelines for consumer protection"]],
s2:[["Tricky designs","Some websites use dark patterns, designs that trick people into buying more, signing up for subscriptions or sharing data, such as hidden fees revealed only at checkout or making it very hard to cancel. Regulators in several countries have started fining companies for such tricks."],["The right to repair","A growing movement argues that people should be able to fix their own phones, cars and appliances, or take them to independent repair shops, instead of throwing them away. Laws in the European Union and several US states now require some manufacturers to make spare parts and repair information available."]],
sp:["Testing products","Independent consumer organizations, such as Consumer Reports in the United States and Which? in the United Kingdom, buy products anonymously and test them in laboratories for safety, quality and value. Their results help shoppers choose wisely and sometimes reveal dangers that lead to recalls."],
g:[["Consumer","A person who buys goods or services for their own use"],["Warranty","A promise to repair or replace a product if it fails"],["Recall","A request to return a product because of a safety problem"]],
f:["15 March is World Consumer Rights Day.","President Kennedy named four consumer rights in 1962.","EU shoppers generally have 14 days to cancel most online purchases."],
fun:"World Consumer Rights Day falls on 15 March because that is the date in 1962 when President Kennedy gave his speech on consumer rights to the US Congress."});

ADDART({id:"fanfiction",t:"Fan Fiction",c:"Arts and Culture",i:"✍️",w:"Fan fiction",r:["fandom","anime","superhero","cosplay","copyrightonline","onlinecommunities"],
p:[["Stories by fans","Fan fiction is writing by fans using characters from books, films or games."],["A creative hobby","Millions of people write and read it online."],["A path for writers","Many professional authors began by writing fan fiction."]],
l:"Fan fiction, often called fanfic, is writing by fans that uses characters, settings or ideas from existing books, films, TV shows, comics, anime or video games. Fans write new adventures, imagine what happened between scenes, explore minor characters, change endings or combine different stories. Fan fiction has a long history, from retellings of myths to fan magazines of the 1960s, but the internet made it hugely popular. Websites such as FanFiction.Net and Archive of Our Own host millions of stories. Writing fan fiction helps people practice storytelling and connect with others, though it raises questions about copyright.",
s:[["Old roots","Writers have always borrowed from earlier stories. Ancient poets retold the myths of Troy, and Shakespeare reworked older plays. In the 1890s fans wrote their own Sherlock Holmes stories. Modern fan fiction grew in the 1960s, when Star Trek fans began sharing stories in homemade magazines called fanzines."],["Going online","With the internet, fans could share stories instantly with readers worldwide. FanFiction.Net opened in 1998, Wattpad in 2006 and Archive of Our Own, run by volunteers of the Organization for Transformative Works, in 2008. Popular sources include Harry Potter, anime, superhero films and K-pop."],["The language of fanfic","Fan fiction has its own terms. Canon means the original story, while AU, or alternate universe, puts characters in a different world, such as a coffee shop or a school. A one-shot is a single complete story, and tags help readers find stories by characters, themes and content warnings."],["Copyright questions","Fan fiction uses characters owned by others, so its legal status is unclear in many places. Most fan writers do not charge money, which may help their work count as fair use. Some authors welcome fan fiction, while others ask fans not to write it."]],
k:[["Also called","Fanfic"],["Early modern form","Star Trek fanzines, 1960s"],["Major sites","FanFiction.Net, Archive of Our Own, Wattpad"],["Key term","Canon, the original story"],["Award","Archive of Our Own won a Hugo Award in 2019"]],
d:[["1967","Star Trek fans publish Spockanalia, an early fanzine with fan stories"],["1998","FanFiction.Net is launched"],["2006","Wattpad is launched"],["2008","Archive of Our Own opens"],["2019","Archive of Our Own wins a Hugo Award"]],
s2:[["From fanfic to bestsellers","Several professional writers began with fan fiction, and some published novels started as fan stories with the names changed. Writing for an audience of fans gives new writers practice, feedback and confidence."],["A welcoming community","Fan fiction communities are known for encouraging beginners, with readers leaving comments and kudos. Many writers are teenagers and young adults, and many are women, who have long been central to fan writing communities."]],
sp:["Writing your own","Anyone can try writing fan fiction. Many writers start by asking a what if question: What if a minor character were the hero? What happened after the last chapter? Reading widely, respecting the original story’s characters and asking friends for feedback are good first steps."],
g:[["Canon","The official original story and its facts"],["Fandom","The community of fans of a book, show or other work"],["Alternate universe (AU)","A fan story set in a different world from the original"]],
f:["Fan fiction grew from Star Trek fanzines in the 1960s.","Archive of Our Own won a Hugo Award in 2019.","Canon means the original story."],
fun:"Some of the earliest fan fiction was written about Sherlock Holmes, after fans were upset when Arthur Conan Doyle killed off the detective in 1893."});

ADDART({id:"livestreaming",t:"Livestreaming",c:"Arts and Culture",i:"🔴",w:"Livestreaming",r:["streaming","esports","influencers","youtube","onlinesafety","videogames","creatoreconomy"],
p:[["Live on the internet","Livestreaming means broadcasting video online as it happens."],["Games, music and chat","People stream games, concerts, cooking, sports and conversations."],["Talking back","Viewers can chat with streamers in real time."]],
l:"Livestreaming is broadcasting live video over the internet so that people can watch as events happen. Unlike regular videos that are recorded and uploaded, a livestream lets viewers interact in real time, sending messages in a chat that the streamer can read and answer. People livestream video games, music, concerts, sports, classes, news events, cooking and everyday conversations. Platforms such as Twitch, YouTube, TikTok, Kick and Facebook host millions of streams. Popular streamers can earn money from subscriptions, donations, advertising and sponsors. Livestreaming also brings risks, such as sharing personal information by mistake or meeting harmful behavior in chats.",
s:[["Early streams","In 1991 computer scientists at the University of Cambridge pointed a camera at their coffee pot so they could see from their desks whether it was empty; from 1993 anyone online could watch. In 1994 the Rolling Stones broadcast part of a concert live over the internet. Faster connections in the 2000s made live video practical for everyone."],["The rise of game streaming","Justin.tv, launched in 2007, let anyone broadcast live. Its gaming section became so popular that it was relaunched as Twitch in 2011, and Amazon bought it in 2014. Watching people play games, compete in esports and chat became a new kind of entertainment."],["Interaction","What makes livestreaming different is interaction. Viewers chat, ask questions, send emojis and support streamers with paid subscriptions or donations. Some streams let viewers vote on what happens next. Moderators help remove spam and rude messages."],["Streaming safely","Streamers can accidentally reveal where they live through things seen or heard on camera. Safety advice includes hiding personal details, using a nickname, setting chat rules, using moderation tools and never streaming without a parent’s permission for younger users. Platforms have minimum age rules."]],
k:[["What it is","Broadcasting live video online"],["Popular platforms","Twitch, YouTube, TikTok, Kick, Facebook"],["Twitch launched","2011"],["Key feature","Real-time chat with viewers"],["Ways to earn","Subscriptions, donations, ads, sponsors"]],
d:[["1993","Cambridge’s coffee pot camera goes on the World Wide Web"],["1994","The Rolling Stones stream part of a concert online"],["2007","Justin.tv launches"],["2011","Twitch launches"],["2014","Amazon buys Twitch"]],
s2:[["More than games","Livestreaming is used by teachers for online classes, by museums and zoos for virtual tours and animal cams, by scientists for rocket launches and eclipses, and by musicians for concerts. During the COVID-19 pandemic, livestreams let people attend religious services, weddings and events from home."],["The pressure of streaming live","Successful streamers often broadcast for many hours a day to keep their audience, which can lead to stress and burnout. Some have spoken about the pressure to always be online and entertaining, and the importance of taking breaks."]],
sp:["Animal cams","Some of the most watched livestreams show animals, not people. Webcams broadcast eagles nesting, bears catching salmon at Katmai National Park in Alaska, giant pandas in zoos and wildlife at African watering holes, letting millions of viewers watch nature live from home."],
g:[["Livestream","A video broadcast live over the internet"],["Streamer","A person who broadcasts livestreams"],["Moderator","A person who manages a stream’s chat"]],
f:["The first webcam watched a coffee pot at Cambridge.","Twitch grew out of Justin.tv.","Livestreams let viewers chat in real time."],
fun:"The Cambridge coffee pot that inspired one of the first webcams was later sold at auction for about £3,350."});

ADDART({id:"tradingcards",t:"Trading Cards",c:"Arts and Culture",i:"🃏",w:"Trading card",r:["boardgames","anime","fandom","videogames","baseball","collect"],
p:[["Collect and trade","Trading cards are cards made to be collected, swapped and played."],["Sports to monsters","They feature athletes, game characters, films and more."],["Big business","Rare cards can sell for millions of dollars."]],
l:"Trading cards are small printed cards designed to be collected, traded and, in some cases, used to play games. The first popular trading cards showed baseball players and were packed with cigarettes in the United States in the 1880s. Sports cards remain popular, but the biggest sellers today are trading card games, such as Magic: The Gathering, launched in 1993, the Pokémon Trading Card Game, launched in Japan in 1996, and Yu-Gi-Oh!. Players build decks of cards with different powers and battle each other. Cards are sold in random packs, so collecting rare ones is part of the fun. More than 85 billion Pokémon cards had been produced by March 2026.",
s:[["Sports cards","Tobacco companies put picture cards of baseball players in cigarette packs in the late 1800s, partly to stiffen the packs. Later, gum and candy companies took over, and Topps sold baseball cards with bubble gum from the 1950s. Collectors still value cards of famous players, especially rookie cards from a player’s first year."],["Trading card games","In 1993 mathematician Richard Garfield created Magic: The Gathering, the first modern trading card game, in which players act as wizards casting spells. Its success inspired many others. In the Pokémon Trading Card Game, players use Pokémon cards and energy cards to battle, and Yu-Gi-Oh! grew from a manga and anime."],["Rarity and value","Cards are printed in different quantities: common, uncommon, rare and special versions with shiny holographic designs. Professional grading companies examine cards and give them a score for condition, sealing them in plastic cases. A perfect grade can multiply a card’s value."],["Record prices","In 2022 a 1952 Topps Mickey Mantle baseball card sold for about $12.6 million, a record for a sports card. A rare 1909–1911 Honus Wagner card sold for about $7.25 million the same year. Rare Pokémon cards have also sold for millions of dollars."]],
k:[["First popular cards","Baseball cards with tobacco, 1880s"],["First modern trading card game","Magic: The Gathering, 1993"],["Pokémon TCG","Launched in Japan, 1996"],["Pokémon cards produced","More than 85 billion (March 2026)"],["Record sports card sale","About $12.6 million (2022)"]],
d:[["1880s","Baseball cards are packed with cigarettes"],["1952","Topps releases a famous baseball card set"],["1993","Magic: The Gathering is released"],["1996","The Pokémon Trading Card Game launches in Japan"],["1999","The Yu-Gi-Oh! card game launches"]],
s2:[["Tournaments","Trading card games have competitive tournaments around the world, from local game shops to world championships with large prizes. Players study strategy, build decks carefully and adapt to new card sets that are released several times a year."],["Collecting wisely","High demand for some cards has led to shortages and resellers buying up products, which frustrates young collectors. Fake cards are also common. Collectors are advised to buy from trusted sellers, learn to spot fakes and collect mainly for enjoyment rather than profit."]],
sp:["Digital cards","Many trading card games now have digital versions, such as Magic: The Gathering Arena and Pokémon TCG Live, where players collect and battle online. Digital cards cannot be lost or damaged, but they also cannot be held, traded in person or resold in the same way as physical cards."],
g:[["Deck","The set of cards a player uses in a game"],["Booster pack","A sealed pack of random cards"],["Grading","Professionally rating a card’s condition"]],
f:["The first popular trading cards came with cigarettes.","Magic: The Gathering was the first modern trading card game.","A Mickey Mantle card sold for about $12.6 million."],
fun:"Early tobacco cards were partly added to cigarette packs to stiffen them, protecting the cigarettes from being crushed."});

