ADDART({id:"wormholes",t:"Wormholes",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🕳️",w:"Wormhole",r:["bh","relat","einstein","scifi","lensing","hawking"],
p:[["Shortcuts through space","A wormhole would be a tunnel joining two distant places."],["Allowed by Einstein","General relativity permits them on paper."],["Never found","No wormhole has ever been observed."]],
l:"A wormhole is a hypothetical tunnel through space and time that would connect two distant places, or even two different times, by a shortcut. The idea comes from Albert Einstein’s general theory of relativity, which describes gravity as the bending of space and time. In 1935 Einstein and Nathan Rosen found solutions to his equations that linked two regions of space by a ‘bridge’, now called an Einstein–Rosen bridge. The name wormhole was coined by the physicist John Wheeler in 1957, comparing it to a worm boring through an apple instead of crawling around its surface. Wormholes appear often in science fiction as a way to travel between stars. In reality, the simplest wormholes would collapse too quickly for anything, even light, to pass through. In 1988 Kip Thorne and colleagues showed that a wormhole could stay open only if it were propped up by ‘exotic matter’ with negative energy, which may not exist in the amounts needed. No wormhole has ever been detected.",
s:[["Einstein–Rosen bridges","The 1935 bridges were found inside the mathematics of black holes. They connect two separate regions but pinch off so fast that nothing can cross. Physicists now think they would not form in real black holes made by collapsing stars."],["Traversable wormholes","Thorne and his student Michael Morris explored what a wormhole humans could travel through would need. Its throat would have to be held open by matter with negative energy density. Quantum physics allows small amounts of negative energy, as in the Casimir effect between metal plates, but probably not enough."],["Time machines?","If one end of a wormhole were moved at high speed or placed near a strong gravity field, time would pass differently at the two ends, possibly turning it into a time machine. Stephen Hawking proposed a ‘chronology protection conjecture’, suggesting that the laws of physics prevent this."],["In stories and films","Wormholes carry travelers in many science fiction stories. Thorne advised on the 2014 film Interstellar, whose wormhole was drawn using real equations to show how it would bend light around it."]],
k:[["Also called","Einstein–Rosen bridge"],["First described","1935"],["Named by","John Wheeler, 1957"],["Needed to stay open","Exotic matter with negative energy"],["Observed?","No"]],
d:[["1916","Ludwig Flamm describes an early wormhole-like solution"],["1935","Einstein and Rosen publish their bridge"],["1957","John Wheeler coins the word wormhole"],["1988","Morris and Thorne study traversable wormholes"]],
s2:[["ER = EPR","Some physicists have suggested that wormholes and quantum entanglement, the link between particles that Einstein called ‘spooky action at a distance’, may be two descriptions of the same thing."],["How to spot one","If wormholes existed, they might bend light from stars behind them in a recognizable way, and astronomers have proposed searching for such patterns."]],
g:[["Hypothetical","Proposed but not proven"],["Spacetime","Space and time combined into one fabric"],["Exotic matter","Matter with negative energy, not known to exist in bulk"],["Traversable","Able to be passed through"]],
f:["No wormhole has ever been found.","Wormholes come from general relativity.","John Wheeler named wormholes."],
fun:"John Wheeler also popularized the term ‘black hole’ in 1967."});

ADDART({id:"starlifecycle",t:"The Life Cycle of Stars",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🌟",w:"Stellar evolution",r:["stars","starformation","nebula","redgiants","whitedwarfs","supernova","hrdiagram","nucleosynthesis","wolfrayet","planetarynebulae"],
p:[["Born in clouds","Stars form in clouds of gas and dust."],["Mass decides fate","A star’s mass sets how long it lives and how it dies."],["Recycled stardust","Dying stars spread the elements that make planets and life."]],
l:"Stars are born, live for millions to trillions of years and then die, and the path each one follows depends mainly on how massive it is. Every star begins in a cold cloud of gas and dust, a nebula, where a clump collapses under gravity and heats up until hydrogen nuclei in its core start fusing into helium. The star then settles into the main sequence, the long, steady stage in which it spends about 90 percent of its life. Small stars burn their fuel slowly and can last for trillions of years, while the most massive stars blaze for only a few million. When a Sun-like star runs low on hydrogen in its core, it swells into a red giant, then puffs off its outer layers as a glowing planetary nebula and leaves behind a small, hot core called a white dwarf. A star more than about eight times the Sun’s mass ends with a supernova explosion, leaving a neutron star or a black hole. The carbon, oxygen, iron and other elements forged in stars and spread by their deaths become part of new stars, planets and living things.",
s:[["Birth","Inside a collapsing cloud, a protostar forms, wrapped in a spinning disk of gas and dust from which planets may later form. When its core reaches about 10 million degrees Celsius, hydrogen fusion begins and the star is born. Objects too small to fuse hydrogen become brown dwarfs."],["The long middle","On the main sequence, the outward push of energy from fusion balances the inward pull of gravity. The Sun has been on the main sequence for about 4.6 billion years and has roughly 5 billion years to go. Red dwarfs, the smallest stars, mix their fuel so thoroughly that none has yet finished its main sequence life."],["Death of a Sun-like star","As its core runs out of hydrogen, a Sun-like star expands into a red giant, perhaps growing large enough to swallow the inner planets. It then fuses helium into carbon and oxygen before shedding its outer layers. The exposed core, a white dwarf about the size of Earth, slowly cools over billions of years."],["Death of a massive star","Massive stars fuse heavier and heavier elements in layers, like an onion, until the core is iron. The core then collapses in a fraction of a second, rebounding into a supernova that can briefly outshine a whole galaxy. Elements heavier than iron are made in these explosions and in collisions of neutron stars."]],
k:[["Birthplace","Clouds of gas and dust (nebulae)"],["Main power source","Nuclear fusion of hydrogen"],["Main sequence share of life","About 90 percent"],["Sun-like star ends as","A white dwarf"],["Massive star ends as","A neutron star or black hole"],["Key factor","Mass"]],
s2:[["We are star stuff","Almost all the atoms in our bodies other than hydrogen were made inside stars. The astronomer Carl Sagan popularized the phrase ‘we are made of star stuff’."],["The Sun’s future","In about 5 billion years the Sun will become a red giant and later a white dwarf. Long before that, in about a billion years, its growing brightness may make Earth too hot for oceans."]],
g:[["Main sequence","The stable, hydrogen-fusing stage of a star’s life"],["Protostar","A star still forming"],["Planetary nebula","A glowing shell of gas shed by a dying star"],["Fusion","Joining light atomic nuclei, releasing energy"]],
f:["A star’s mass decides its fate.","Massive stars live shorter lives.","The Sun will become a white dwarf."],
fun:"The smallest red dwarfs may shine for over 10 trillion years, about 700 times the current age of the universe."});

ADDART({id:"hrdiagram",t:"The Hertzsprung–Russell Diagram",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"📈",w:"Hertzsprung–Russell diagram",r:["stars","starlifecycle","redgiants","whitedwarfs","sun","distances"],
p:[["A map of stars","It plots stars by brightness and temperature."],["Patterns appear","Most stars fall along a band called the main sequence."],["Shows life stages","Giants and white dwarfs sit in their own regions."]],
l:"The Hertzsprung–Russell diagram, or H–R diagram, is a chart that plots stars by their true brightness, called luminosity, against their surface temperature or color. It was developed independently around 1910 to 1913 by the Danish astronomer Ejnar Hertzsprung and the American astronomer Henry Norris Russell, and it became one of the most important tools in astronomy. By tradition, hot blue stars are placed on the left and cool red stars on the right, with bright stars at the top and dim ones at the bottom. When many stars are plotted, they do not scatter randomly. About 90 percent fall along a diagonal band called the main sequence, running from hot, bright stars at the top left to cool, faint red dwarfs at the bottom right. Red giants and supergiants form groups at the upper right, being cool but very bright because they are huge, while white dwarfs sit at the lower left, being hot but faint because they are tiny. Because stars move across the diagram as they age, it reveals how stars evolve.",
s:[["Reading the diagram","A star’s position tells its size. Two stars with the same temperature but different brightness must differ in size, since a larger star has more glowing surface. So stars at the top right are giants and those at the bottom left are dwarfs. The Sun lies near the middle of the main sequence."],["Spectral types","Stars are sorted by temperature into classes O, B, A, F, G, K and M, from hottest to coolest. Generations of students have remembered them with the phrase ‘Oh Be A Fine Girl/Guy, Kiss Me’. The system was developed at Harvard by Annie Jump Cannon and others around 1900."],["Ages of clusters","Stars in a cluster formed at about the same time. As a cluster ages, its most massive stars leave the main sequence first. The point where the main sequence bends off, called the turnoff, shows the cluster’s age. This method shows that the oldest globular clusters are around 12 to 13 billion years old."],["Gaia’s diagram","The Gaia spacecraft measured precise distances to more than a billion stars, allowing astronomers to make the most detailed H–R diagrams ever, revealing fine features such as a pile-up of white dwarfs that are freezing solid as they cool."]],
k:[["Named after","Ejnar Hertzsprung and Henry Norris Russell"],["Developed","About 1910–1913"],["Axes","Luminosity and temperature"],["Main band","The main sequence"],["Spectral classes","O, B, A, F, G, K, M"]],
d:[["c. 1901","Annie Jump Cannon’s spectral classes take shape"],["1911","Hertzsprung publishes star color–brightness plots"],["1913","Russell presents his diagram"],["2018","Gaia data produce a very detailed H–R diagram"]],
s2:[["Backwards temperature","The H–R diagram’s temperature axis runs backwards, with hotter stars on the left, a tradition dating from when it was plotted by spectral type."],["Harvard computers","Annie Jump Cannon classified around 350,000 stars by hand, part of a team of women at Harvard known as the Harvard Computers."]],
g:[["Luminosity","The total light a star gives off"],["Spectral type","A class of star based on its temperature"],["Turnoff","Where a cluster’s stars leave the main sequence"],["Supergiant","An extremely large, bright star"]],
f:["Most stars lie on the main sequence.","Hot stars are on the left of the diagram.","White dwarfs are hot but faint."],
fun:"The Sun is a G-type star, right in the middle of the spectral sequence."});

ADDART({id:"variablestars",t:"Variable Stars",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"💫",w:"Variable star",r:["stars","distances","hubble","betelgeuse","binarystars","galaxies","novae"],
p:[["Changing brightness","Variable stars brighten and dim over time."],["Cosmic yardsticks","Cepheid variables are used to measure distances."],["Many causes","Pulsations, eclipses and outbursts make them vary."]],
l:"Variable stars are stars whose brightness, as seen from Earth, changes over time, from minutes to years. Some vary because they pulsate, swelling and shrinking in a regular rhythm; others because a companion star passes in front of them, because of outbursts and flares, or because dark spots rotate across their surface. Astronomers know of millions of variable stars. The most important kind is the Cepheid variable, a yellow supergiant that pulsates every few days to months. In 1908 and 1912 the American astronomer Henrietta Swan Leavitt discovered that the brighter a Cepheid is, the longer its cycle. Measuring a Cepheid’s period therefore reveals its true brightness, and comparing that with how bright it appears tells its distance. Edwin Hubble used Cepheids in the 1920s to show that the Andromeda Nebula is a separate galaxy far beyond the Milky Way, and later to show that the universe is expanding. Other famous variables include Mira, Algol and Betelgeuse.",
s:[["Pulsating stars","Cepheids, RR Lyrae stars and Mira variables expand and contract because layers of gas in them trap and release heat like a valve. RR Lyrae stars, found in old star clusters, all have about the same brightness and are also used to measure distances."],["Eclipsing binaries","Algol, the ‘Demon Star’ in Perseus, dims every 2.87 days when its fainter companion passes in front of it. Eclipsing binaries let astronomers measure the sizes and masses of stars directly."],["Explosive variables","Some stars brighten suddenly. Novae flare up when a white dwarf gathers gas from a companion until it ignites in a thermonuclear blast. Young stars and red dwarfs can produce powerful flares."],["Amateur astronomers","Watching variable stars is a popular hobby. The American Association of Variable Star Observers, founded in 1911, has collected more than 50 million observations, many by amateurs, that professional astronomers use."]],
k:[["Main types","Pulsating, eclipsing and eruptive"],["Key discovery","Leavitt’s period–luminosity law (1912)"],["Distance tool","Cepheid variables"],["Famous examples","Mira, Algol, Delta Cephei, Betelgeuse"]],
d:[["1596","David Fabricius notices Mira changing"],["1784","John Goodricke finds Delta Cephei varies"],["1912","Henrietta Leavitt publishes the period–luminosity law"],["1923","Hubble finds a Cepheid in Andromeda"]],
s2:[["Polaris","The North Star is a Cepheid variable, though its brightness changes only slightly, over about four days."],["Great dimming","In late 2019 and early 2020 Betelgeuse dimmed dramatically, partly because it puffed out a cloud of dust that blocked some of its light."]],
g:[["Cepheid","A pulsating star used to measure distances"],["Period","The time for one full cycle"],["Eclipsing binary","Two stars that pass in front of each other"],["Nova","A sudden brightening of a star"]],
f:["Cepheids are used to measure distances.","Henrietta Leavitt discovered the period–luminosity law.","Algol dims when its companion passes in front."],
fun:"Mira’s name means ‘wonderful’ in Latin, because it fades from easy visibility to invisible to the naked eye and back again over about 11 months."});

ADDART({id:"kilonovae",t:"Kilonovae and Neutron Star Collisions",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"💥",w:"Kilonova",r:["pulsar","gravwaves","supernova","fusion","gammaraybursts","stellarbh","nucleosynthesis"],
p:[["Stars colliding","A kilonova is the blast when two neutron stars merge."],["Gold factories","These collisions make gold, platinum and other heavy elements."],["Seen and heard","In 2017 one was detected by both light and gravitational waves."]],
l:"A kilonova is a bright flash of light produced when two neutron stars, or a neutron star and a black hole, collide and merge. Neutron stars are the incredibly dense remains of exploded stars: a teaspoon of their matter would weigh billions of tonnes. When two orbit each other, they slowly lose energy as gravitational waves and spiral together over millions of years until they smash into one another at a large fraction of the speed of light. The collision throws out clouds of neutron-rich matter, in which rapid nuclear reactions build heavy elements such as gold, platinum and uranium. The radioactive decay of these new elements makes the debris glow about a thousand times brighter than an ordinary nova, giving the kilonova its name. On 17 August 2017 the LIGO and Virgo detectors recorded gravitational waves from a neutron star merger, GW170817, and about 70 telescopes then watched the kilonova in the galaxy NGC 4993, 130 million light-years away. It was the first cosmic event observed in both gravitational waves and light.",
s:[["GW170817","The gravitational wave signal lasted about 100 seconds as the stars spiraled together. Less than two seconds after the merger, the Fermi space telescope detected a short burst of gamma rays. Within hours, telescopes found the kilonova, which faded from blue to red over days as heavy elements formed. The event marked the start of multi-messenger astronomy."],["Where gold comes from","Elements heavier than iron are made mainly by the rapid neutron-capture process, or r-process. Kilonovae appear to be one of its main sites. The 2017 kilonova was estimated to have produced several Earth masses of gold and platinum, though the exact amounts are uncertain."],["Short gamma-ray bursts","Short bursts of gamma rays lasting less than about two seconds were long suspected to come from neutron star mergers. GW170817 confirmed the link. Some long bursts have also been followed by kilonovae, showing that the picture is more complicated."],["Testing physics","GW170817’s gravitational waves and gamma rays arrived within two seconds of each other after a journey of 130 million years, showing that gravity travels at the speed of light to extremely high precision and ruling out several alternative theories of gravity."]],
k:[["Caused by","Merging neutron stars (or neutron star and black hole)"],["Brightness","About 1,000 times an ordinary nova"],["Produces","Gold, platinum, uranium and other heavy elements"],["Best studied","GW170817, 17 August 2017"],["Host galaxy of GW170817","NGC 4993, about 130 million light-years away"]],
d:[["1974","First binary pulsar discovered"],["2013","Possible kilonova seen after a gamma-ray burst"],["2017","GW170817: first neutron star merger detected"],["2023","Webb finds heavy element tellurium in a kilonova"]],
s2:[["Name","The name kilonova was proposed in 2010 because these events were predicted to be about a thousand times brighter than a classical nova, though far fainter than supernovae."],["Gold in your jewelry","Some of the gold in jewelry on Earth may have been made in neutron star collisions that took place before the Solar System formed."]],
g:[["Neutron star","The dense collapsed core of a massive star"],["r-process","Rapid neutron capture that builds heavy elements"],["Multi-messenger astronomy","Studying events with different signals, such as light and gravitational waves"],["Gamma-ray burst","A brief, intense flash of gamma rays"]],
f:["Kilonovae make heavy elements like gold.","GW170817 was seen in light and gravitational waves.","Kilonovae come from neutron star collisions."],
fun:"A sugar-cube-sized piece of neutron star would weigh about as much as all the people on Earth combined."});

ADDART({id:"hypergiants",t:"The Largest Stars",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🔴",w:"List of largest stars",r:["redgiants","betelgeuse","stars","supernova","magellanic","hrdiagram","wolfrayet","etacarinae"],
p:[["Giant suns","The largest stars are over 1,000 times wider than the Sun."],["Hard to measure","Their sizes are uncertain and often revised."],["Short, wild lives","They burn out in a few million years."]],
l:"The largest known stars are red supergiants and hypergiants more than a thousand times wider than the Sun. Placed where the Sun is, the biggest would swallow the orbits of Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars and Jupiter, and perhaps reach toward Saturn. Leading candidates include WOH G64 in the Large Magellanic Cloud, about 1,540 times the Sun’s radius; VY Canis Majoris, about 1,400; and VX Sagittarii, among others. Some stars once listed as the largest, such as UY Scuti, have been shrunk by newer measurements, and others, such as Stephenson 2-18, have very uncertain estimates. Measuring these stars is difficult because they have no sharp surface, are wrapped in dust, pulsate and are very far away, so their sizes depend on uncertain distances. Despite their size, these stars are not the most massive: a red supergiant may be ‘only’ 10 to 40 times the Sun’s mass, its outer layers spread extremely thin. The most massive known star, R136a1, is much smaller but about 200 times the Sun’s mass.",
s:[["Why the size changes","Most sizes are calculated from a star’s brightness and temperature, which depend on its distance. When Gaia and other surveys revised distances, some stars shrank. UY Scuti, once thought to be about 1,700 times the Sun’s radius, is now estimated at roughly 900."],["Thin and puffy","A red supergiant’s outer layers are so thin that they are less dense than the air we breathe, almost a vacuum. Huge bubbles of hot gas rise and fall across its surface, and the star loses mass in strong winds that build clouds of dust around it."],["Changing star","In 2024 astronomers used the Very Large Telescope Interferometer to capture the first close-up image of a star in another galaxy, WOH G64, showing it surrounded by an egg-shaped cocoon of dust. The star has dimmed in recent years and may be changing as it nears the end of its life."],["Most massive versus largest","The most massive stars are hot, blue and relatively compact. R136a1, in the Tarantula Nebula, is the most massive known, at perhaps around 200 solar masses, though estimates vary. Such stars shine millions of times brighter than the Sun."]],
k:[["Largest candidates","WOH G64, VY Canis Majoris, VX Sagittarii"],["Typical size","Over 1,000 times the Sun’s radius"],["Typical mass","About 10–40 Suns"],["Most massive known star","R136a1, roughly 200 Suns"],["Lifespan","A few million years"]],
d:[["1801","VY Canis Majoris first cataloged by Jérôme Lalande"],["2010","R136a1 identified as the most massive known star"],["2024","First close-up image of a star in another galaxy (WOH G64)"]],
s2:[["Theoretical limit","Astronomers think cool supergiants cannot grow much beyond about 1,500 to 2,000 times the Sun’s radius, because they would become unstable."],["Betelgeuse","The familiar red supergiant Betelgeuse in Orion is about 700 to 1,000 times the Sun’s radius, smaller than the largest stars but close enough to appear as a disk in large telescopes."]],
g:[["Red supergiant","A huge, cool, bright star near the end of its life"],["Hypergiant","An extremely bright, massive supergiant"],["Solar radius","The radius of the Sun, used as a unit"],["Interferometer","Telescopes combined to see fine detail"]],
f:["The largest stars are not the most massive.","Star sizes are often revised.","WOH G64 is in the Large Magellanic Cloud."],
fun:"Light takes about 4.6 seconds to cross the Sun but would need nearly two hours to cross the largest known stars."});

ADDART({id:"localgroup",t:"The Local Group",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🌌",w:"Local Group",r:["milky","andromeda","magellanic","galaxies","galaxymergers","superclusters","dwarfgalaxies","virgocluster"],
p:[["Our galactic neighborhood","The Milky Way belongs to a group of galaxies called the Local Group."],["Two giants","It is dominated by the Milky Way and Andromeda."],["Dozens of dwarfs","Most members are small dwarf galaxies."]],
l:"The Local Group is the group of galaxies that includes the Milky Way. It stretches about 10 million light-years across and contains more than 100 known galaxies, most of them small dwarf galaxies, held together by their mutual gravity. Three large spiral galaxies dominate it: the Andromeda Galaxy, which is the largest; the Milky Way; and the smaller Triangulum Galaxy. The Milky Way and Andromeda, about 2.5 million light-years apart, are each surrounded by their own swarm of satellite galaxies, including the Large and Small Magellanic Clouds, which orbit the Milky Way and can be seen with the naked eye from the Southern Hemisphere. New faint dwarf galaxies are still being discovered by sky surveys. The Local Group is on the outskirts of the Virgo Supercluster, and both are part of an even larger structure called Laniakea. The term Local Group was introduced by Edwin Hubble in 1936.",
s:[["The big three","Andromeda has about a trillion stars, and the Milky Way perhaps 100 to 400 billion. Recent studies suggest the two have broadly similar total masses, including dark matter. Triangulum, about 3 million light-years away, is the most distant object some people can see with the naked eye under very dark skies."],["Dwarf galaxies","Dwarf galaxies range from the Magellanic Clouds, with billions of stars, to ultra-faint dwarfs with only a few thousand. Ultra-faint dwarfs contain enormous amounts of dark matter compared with their stars, making them natural laboratories for studying it."],["Galaxy cannibalism","The Milky Way has torn apart and absorbed many smaller galaxies. The Sagittarius Dwarf Galaxy is being stretched into long streams of stars that wrap around our galaxy. Gaia data revealed that about 8 to 11 billion years ago the Milky Way merged with a galaxy now called Gaia–Enceladus."],["The future","Andromeda and the Milky Way are moving toward each other at about 110 kilometers per second. They may collide in billions of years, though a 2025 study found roughly even odds of a merger within 10 billion years. Eventually, the Local Group’s large galaxies are expected to merge into one giant elliptical galaxy."]],
k:[["Size","About 10 million light-years across"],["Known galaxies","More than 100, mostly dwarfs"],["Largest member","Andromeda Galaxy"],["Big three","Andromeda, Milky Way, Triangulum"],["Part of","Virgo Supercluster and Laniakea"],["Named by","Edwin Hubble, 1936"]],
d:[["1923","Hubble shows Andromeda is a separate galaxy"],["1936","Hubble names the Local Group"],["1994","Sagittarius Dwarf Galaxy discovered"],["2018","Gaia reveals the Gaia–Enceladus merger"]],
s2:[["Lonely neighbors","Beyond the Local Group lie other small groups, such as the Sculptor Group and the M81 Group, separated by largely empty space."],["Moving through space","The Local Group is moving at about 600 kilometers per second relative to the cosmic microwave background, pulled by the mass of distant superclusters."]],
g:[["Dwarf galaxy","A small galaxy with up to a few billion stars"],["Satellite galaxy","A small galaxy orbiting a larger one"],["Galaxy group","A small collection of galaxies bound by gravity"],["Dark matter","Invisible matter detected by its gravity"]],
f:["The Milky Way is in the Local Group.","Andromeda is the largest Local Group galaxy.","Most Local Group galaxies are dwarfs."],
fun:"Light from the Andromeda Galaxy that reaches your eyes tonight left it about 2.5 million years ago, before modern humans existed."});

ADDART({id:"galaxymergers",t:"Galaxy Collisions",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🌪️",w:"Galaxy merger",r:["galaxies","andromeda","milky","localgroup","supermassivebh","starformation","galaxytypes","starburstgalaxies","whirlpool","centaurusa"],
p:[["Cosmic crashes","Galaxies often collide and merge over hundreds of millions of years."],["Stars rarely hit","The stars are so far apart that they almost never collide."],["Starbursts","Collisions squeeze gas and set off bursts of star birth."]],
l:"Galaxy collisions happen when two or more galaxies are pulled together by gravity and pass through or merge with each other. Because galaxies are huge and move slowly by cosmic standards, a collision unfolds over hundreds of millions to billions of years. Although galaxies contain billions of stars, the stars are separated by such vast distances that they almost never hit each other. Instead, gravity flings stars into long curving tails and bridges, while clouds of gas crash together and are compressed, triggering bursts of new star formation. When two large galaxies of similar size merge, their spiral shapes are usually destroyed and they settle into a single rounded elliptical galaxy, and their central supermassive black holes eventually sink together. Mergers were more common in the early universe, when galaxies were closer together, and they are one of the main ways galaxies grow. Famous colliding galaxies include the Antennae Galaxies, the Mice and Stephan’s Quintet. The Milky Way and the Andromeda Galaxy are heading toward each other.",
s:[["Tidal tails","As galaxies pass close by, gravity pulls stars and gas from their outer parts into long streamers called tidal tails. The Antennae Galaxies, about 45 to 65 million light-years away, have two curving tails that look like an insect’s antennae."],["Starbursts and quasars","Gas driven to the center of merging galaxies can produce stars at hundreds of times the normal rate and feed the central black holes, sometimes switching on a quasar. Many of the brightest infrared galaxies in the sky are mergers."],["Milkomeda","The Milky Way and Andromeda are approaching at about 110 kilometers per second. Earlier studies predicted a collision in about 4.5 billion years. A 2025 study that included the Large Magellanic Cloud and other neighbors found only about a 50 percent chance of a merger within the next 10 billion years. The merged galaxy has been nicknamed Milkomeda."],["What would we see?","If the Milky Way and Andromeda do collide, the Sun and Earth would almost certainly not hit another star. The night sky would change dramatically over millions of years as Andromeda grew to fill the sky and new stars formed."]],
k:[["Timescale","Hundreds of millions to billions of years"],["Star collisions","Extremely rare"],["Effects","Tidal tails, starbursts, feeding black holes"],["Result of major mergers","Elliptical galaxies"],["Famous examples","Antennae, the Mice, Stephan’s Quintet"]],
d:[["1966","Halton Arp publishes his Atlas of Peculiar Galaxies"],["1972","Toomre brothers simulate galaxy collisions"],["2012","Hubble data predict a Milky Way–Andromeda collision"],["2025","New study puts the odds of that merger near 50 percent"]],
s2:[["Computer pioneers","In 1972 the brothers Alar and Juri Toomre used early computer simulations to show that tidal tails are made by colliding galaxies."],["Minor mergers","Big galaxies more often swallow small ones. The Milky Way is currently absorbing the Sagittarius Dwarf Galaxy."]],
g:[["Merger","Two galaxies combining into one"],["Tidal tail","A stream of stars pulled out by gravity"],["Starburst","A period of rapid star formation"],["Elliptical galaxy","A rounded galaxy of mostly old stars"]],
f:["Stars almost never collide when galaxies merge.","Mergers trigger star formation.","The Milky Way may merge with Andromeda."],
fun:"In the Antennae Galaxies, the collision has created thousands of young star clusters."});

ADDART({id:"galaxyclusters",t:"Galaxy Clusters",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"✴️",w:"Galaxy cluster",r:["galaxies","darkmatter","lensing","bulletcluster","superclusters","chandra","virgocluster","comacluster","perseuscluster"],
p:[["Cities of galaxies","Clusters hold hundreds to thousands of galaxies."],["Mostly invisible","Dark matter makes up most of a cluster’s mass."],["Hot gas","The space between galaxies is filled with gas at millions of degrees."]],
l:"Galaxy clusters are the largest structures in the universe held together by their own gravity. A cluster contains hundreds to thousands of galaxies spread across a few million to about ten million light-years, along with enormous amounts of hot gas and dark matter. The galaxies themselves make up only a few percent of a cluster’s mass. About 10 to 15 percent is gas between the galaxies, heated to tens of millions of degrees so that it glows in X-rays, and the rest, roughly 80 to 85 percent, is dark matter. The nearest large cluster is the Virgo Cluster, about 55 million light-years away, with over a thousand galaxies; another well-studied example is the Coma Cluster. In 1933 the Swiss astronomer Fritz Zwicky found that galaxies in the Coma Cluster were moving too fast to be held together by the visible matter alone, the first strong evidence for dark matter. Clusters also bend light from galaxies far behind them, acting as natural magnifying glasses through gravitational lensing.",
s:[["Counting the mass","Astronomers weigh clusters in three ways: by how fast the galaxies move, by the temperature of the hot gas, and by how much the cluster bends light from more distant galaxies. All three show far more mass than can be seen, pointing to dark matter."],["Giant ellipticals","At the centers of many clusters sit the biggest galaxies in the universe, giant elliptical galaxies that have grown by swallowing their neighbors. Many host supermassive black holes whose jets blow bubbles in the surrounding hot gas."],["Natural telescopes","The gravity of massive clusters such as Abell 1689 and SMACS 0723 magnifies and distorts the light of galaxies behind them into arcs and multiple images. The James Webb Space Telescope’s first deep image in 2022 showed SMACS 0723 and thousands of lensed galaxies."],["Measuring the universe","Because clusters grow over cosmic time, counting how many there are at different distances tests ideas about dark matter and dark energy. Surveys by the Euclid and eROSITA telescopes have mapped hundreds of thousands of clusters."]],
k:[["Galaxies per cluster","Hundreds to thousands"],["Size","A few million to about 10 million light-years"],["Mass makeup","About 80–85% dark matter, 10–15% hot gas, a few percent stars"],["Nearest large cluster","Virgo Cluster, about 55 million light-years"],["Dark matter first noticed","Coma Cluster, 1933"]],
d:[["1781","Charles Messier notes many nebulae in Virgo"],["1933","Fritz Zwicky proposes dark matter in the Coma Cluster"],["1958","George Abell publishes his cluster catalog"],["2022","Webb’s first deep field shows SMACS 0723"]],
s2:[["Galaxy groups","Smaller gatherings of up to about 50 galaxies, like our Local Group, are called groups rather than clusters."],["El Gordo","The cluster nicknamed El Gordo, ‘the fat one’ in Spanish, is one of the most massive known clusters in the distant universe, seen as it was about 7 billion years ago."]],
g:[["Galaxy cluster","A large group of galaxies bound by gravity"],["Intracluster gas","Hot gas between the galaxies in a cluster"],["Gravitational lensing","The bending of light by mass"],["Dark matter","Invisible matter detected by its gravity"]],
f:["Most of a cluster’s mass is dark matter.","Clusters contain hot X-ray gas.","Zwicky found evidence of dark matter in a cluster."],
fun:"The hot gas in a galaxy cluster can weigh more than all the cluster’s galaxies put together."});

ADDART({id:"bulletcluster",t:"The Bullet Cluster",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🎯",w:"Bullet Cluster",r:["galaxyclusters","darkmatter","lensing","chandra","galaxymergers","hubblest"],
p:[["A cosmic smash-up","Two galaxy clusters collided at high speed."],["Dark matter split off","The collision separated dark matter from ordinary gas."],["Strong evidence","It is one of the best pieces of evidence for dark matter."]],
l:"The Bullet Cluster, also known as 1E 0657-56, is a pair of galaxy clusters that collided about 150 million years before the light we see left them, around 3.8 billion light-years away. A smaller cluster plowed through a larger one at several thousand kilometers per second, and the shock wave at its front gives the cluster its bullet-like shape in X-ray images. During the collision, the galaxies in each cluster passed through each other mostly unharmed, because they are widely spaced. The hot gas between the galaxies, which makes up most of the ordinary matter, collided and slowed down, piling up in the middle. But when astronomers mapped where most of the mass is by studying how it bends light from background galaxies, they found that it had moved on with the galaxies, separated from the gas. This showed that most of the mass is invisible and does not collide like gas, as expected for dark matter. The result, published in 2006 using the Chandra X-ray Observatory, the Hubble Space Telescope and ground telescopes, is often called direct evidence that dark matter exists.",
s:[["Seeing the invisible","Astronomers combined two maps: hot gas, shown in pink in famous images, from Chandra’s X-ray observations, and the total mass, shown in blue, from gravitational lensing. The two do not line up, which is hard to explain unless most of the mass is dark matter."],["Why it matters","Some scientists had proposed that dark matter does not exist and that gravity instead works differently over large distances. In those theories, the mass should follow the visible gas. The Bullet Cluster’s separation of mass and gas was a strong challenge to them."],["How fast?","Early measurements suggested the bullet was moving at about 4,700 kilometers per second, a speed some thought too high for standard models. Later simulations showed that the collision could be explained at lower speeds."],["Other bullets","Astronomers have since found other colliding clusters that show similar separations of dark matter and gas, such as the ‘Baby Bullet’ and the Musket Ball Cluster, strengthening the case. In 2025 the Webb telescope produced an even more detailed mass map of the Bullet Cluster."]],
k:[["Catalog name","1E 0657-56"],["Distance","About 3.8 billion light-years"],["Event","Collision of two galaxy clusters"],["Key finding","Dark matter separated from ordinary gas"],["Published","2006"],["Telescopes","Chandra, Hubble, ground-based telescopes"]],
d:[["1995","Cluster identified as an X-ray source"],["2006","Separation of dark matter and gas announced"],["2008","Similar ‘Baby Bullet’ cluster reported"],["2025","Webb maps the Bullet Cluster’s mass in detail"]],
s2:[["Shock wave","The bow shock in front of the smaller cluster is like the shock wave in front of a supersonic jet, heating gas to around 100 million degrees."],["A favorite image","The Bullet Cluster picture, with its pink gas and blue dark matter, has become one of the best-known images in astronomy textbooks."]],
g:[["Bow shock","A shock wave in front of a fast-moving object"],["Mass map","A chart showing where mass is"],["Modified gravity","Ideas that change the law of gravity instead of adding dark matter"],["Weak lensing","Slight distortions of background galaxies by mass"]],
f:["The Bullet Cluster is evidence for dark matter.","The gas and the mass were separated.","The clusters collided at high speed."],
fun:"Despite the violent collision, almost no individual stars or galaxies in the Bullet Cluster are thought to have hit each other."});

ADDART({id:"superclusters",t:"Superclusters and Laniakea",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🌐",w:"Laniakea Supercluster",r:["galaxyclusters","localgroup","cosmicweb","milky","darkmatter","bigbang","virgocluster","comacluster"],
p:[["Clusters of clusters","Superclusters are vast groupings of galaxy groups and clusters."],["Our home supercluster","The Milky Way lies in Laniakea, defined in 2014."],["Not bound together","Most superclusters are being pulled apart by cosmic expansion."]],
l:"Superclusters are among the largest structures in the universe: enormous regions, hundreds of millions of light-years across, where groups and clusters of galaxies are more crowded than average. Unlike galaxy clusters, most superclusters are not held together by gravity; the expansion of the universe will gradually pull them apart. Our Local Group lies on the outskirts of the Virgo Supercluster, which in turn is part of a much larger structure called Laniakea. In 2014 a team led by the astronomer R. Brent Tully mapped the motions of thousands of galaxies and defined Laniakea as the region in which galaxies flow toward a common center of gravity, like water flowing within a river basin. Laniakea is about 500 million light-years across and contains around 100,000 galaxies. Its name comes from Hawaiian words meaning ‘immeasurable heaven’. Other huge structures include the Shapley Supercluster, the Hercules–Corona Borealis Great Wall and the Sloan Great Wall.",
s:[["Mapping flows","Galaxies move apart with the expansion of the universe, but they also have their own motions pulled by nearby mass. By subtracting the expansion, Tully’s team found where galaxies are flowing and drew Laniakea’s boundary where the flows divide."],["The Great Attractor","Galaxies in our region are pulled toward a concentration of mass called the Great Attractor, near the center of Laniakea. It lies behind the dusty disk of the Milky Way, which makes it hard to see in visible light, but X-ray and radio surveys reveal clusters there, including the Norma Cluster."],["How big can structures be?","Cosmologists expect the universe to look smooth on scales above several hundred million light-years. Some claimed giant structures, such as the Hercules–Corona Borealis Great Wall, may be billions of light-years long, which would be hard to explain. Not all astronomers agree they are real single structures."],["A changing map","Later studies suggested that Laniakea may be part of an even larger flow pattern, and a later study proposed that our region could belong to a bigger basin of attraction around the Shapley Supercluster. Mapping is limited by how accurately distances can be measured."]],
k:[["Our supercluster","Laniakea"],["Laniakea’s size","About 500 million light-years"],["Galaxies in Laniakea","Around 100,000"],["Defined","2014, by R. Brent Tully and colleagues"],["Name meaning","Immeasurable heaven (Hawaiian)"],["Gravitational focus","The Great Attractor"]],
d:[["1953","Gérard de Vaucouleurs describes the Local Supercluster"],["1986","Great Attractor proposed"],["1989","‘Great Wall’ of galaxies discovered"],["2014","Laniakea defined"]],
s2:[["Your cosmic address","A full cosmic address might read: Earth, Solar System, Milky Way, Local Group, Virgo Supercluster, Laniakea Supercluster, observable universe."],["Not forever","Because dark energy is speeding up the expansion of the universe, most of Laniakea will spread apart in the distant future, leaving only bound groups like the Local Group together."]],
g:[["Supercluster","A large region of crowded galaxy groups and clusters"],["Basin of attraction","A region where galaxies flow toward a common center"],["Great Attractor","A concentration of mass pulling nearby galaxies"],["Peculiar velocity","A galaxy’s motion apart from cosmic expansion"]],
f:["The Milky Way is in the Laniakea Supercluster.","Most superclusters are not bound by gravity.","Laniakea was defined in 2014."],
fun:"Laniakea’s name was suggested by a Hawaiian language teacher to honor Polynesian navigators who used the stars to cross the Pacific."});

ADDART({id:"cosmicweb",t:"The Cosmic Web",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🕸️",w:"Observable universe#Large-scale structure",r:["superclusters","galaxyclusters","darkmatter","bigbang","cmb","galaxies","cosmicinflation","observableuniverse"],
p:[["The universe’s skeleton","Galaxies are strung along filaments in a giant web."],["Huge empty voids","Between the filaments lie vast, nearly empty regions."],["Shaped by dark matter","Dark matter’s gravity built the web over billions of years."]],
l:"The cosmic web is the name for the pattern in which matter is arranged across the universe on the largest scales. Galaxies are not spread out evenly but are gathered into long filaments and flat walls, with galaxy clusters at the places where filaments meet, like knots in a net. Between them lie voids, enormous regions tens to hundreds of millions of light-years across that contain very few galaxies. The pattern began with tiny ripples in the density of matter shortly after the Big Bang, which can be seen today in the cosmic microwave background. Over billions of years, gravity, mainly from dark matter, pulled matter from the less dense regions into the denser ones, emptying the voids and building the filaments. Large surveys that measure the positions of millions of galaxies, such as the Sloan Digital Sky Survey and DESI, have mapped the web in three dimensions, and computer simulations of dark matter reproduce its sponge-like structure.",
s:[["Filaments","Filaments can stretch for hundreds of millions of light-years. Galaxies and gas flow along them toward the clusters at their ends. Much of the universe’s ordinary matter is thought to be hidden in filaments as thin, hot gas, and astronomers have reported directly imaging a filament of gas linking galaxies in the early universe."],["Voids","Voids make up most of the volume of the universe. The Boötes Void, discovered in 1981, is about 330 million light-years across and contains only a few dozen known galaxies. Some astronomers think the Milky Way itself may sit in a large region of lower than average density, sometimes called the KBC Void."],["Mapping the web","The first maps came from redshift surveys in the 1980s, such as the CfA survey, which showed a ‘stick figure’ of galaxies and the Great Wall. Today the Dark Energy Spectroscopic Instrument has measured distances to tens of millions of galaxies, making the largest 3D map yet."],["Simulations","Supercomputer simulations such as the Millennium Simulation follow billions of particles of dark matter from the early universe to today. The webs they produce closely match real maps, supporting the idea that cold dark matter shaped cosmic structure."]],
k:[["Main parts","Filaments, walls, clusters and voids"],["Built by","Gravity, mainly from dark matter"],["Seeds","Tiny ripples after the Big Bang"],["Famous void","Boötes Void, about 330 million light-years across"],["Mapped by","Galaxy surveys such as SDSS and DESI"]],
d:[["1981","Boötes Void discovered"],["1986","CfA survey reveals a ‘stick figure’ of galaxies"],["2005","Millennium Simulation published"],["2024","DESI releases its first big results"]],
s2:[["Like a sponge","Astronomers sometimes compare the universe to a sponge: the voids are the holes, and the galaxies lie in the material between them."],["Brain lookalike","Some researchers have noted that maps of the cosmic web look strikingly similar to networks of nerve cells, though the resemblance is a coincidence of how networks form."]],
g:[["Filament","A long thread of galaxies and gas"],["Void","A vast region with very few galaxies"],["Redshift survey","A survey that measures distances to many galaxies"],["Simulation","A computer model of how something changes"]],
f:["Galaxies lie along filaments.","Voids are nearly empty regions.","Dark matter shaped the cosmic web."],
fun:"If the Milky Way were in the middle of the Boötes Void, we would not have known other galaxies existed until the 1960s."});

ADDART({id:"earlygalaxies",t:"The First Galaxies",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🔭",w:"Galaxy formation and evolution",r:["jwst","bigbang","galaxies","cmb","starformation","supermassivebh","observableuniverse"],
p:[["Looking back in time","Distant galaxies are seen as they were long ago."],["Webb’s discoveries","JWST has found galaxies less than 300 million years after the Big Bang."],["Surprisingly bright","Early galaxies are brighter and more mature than expected."]],
l:"The first galaxies formed within a few hundred million years after the Big Bang, about 13.8 billion years ago, as gas collected in clumps of dark matter and began to form stars. Because light takes time to travel, astronomers looking at very distant galaxies see them as they were when the light left, so the most distant galaxies show the universe in its infancy. As the universe expands, the light from these galaxies is stretched to longer, redder wavelengths, a change called redshift, until it reaches us as infrared light. That is why the James Webb Space Telescope, which sees in the infrared, has transformed the search. Since 2022 it has found many galaxies from the first 500 million years. In 2025 astronomers confirmed MoM-z14, with a redshift of 14.44, seen about 280 million years after the Big Bang, as the most distant galaxy known. Many early galaxies are brighter, more numerous and richer in elements such as nitrogen than astronomers predicted, prompting new ideas about how quickly stars and black holes formed.",
s:[["The dark ages and cosmic dawn","After the Big Bang the universe cooled and became dark, filled with neutral hydrogen gas. When the first stars and galaxies switched on, their ultraviolet light gradually ionized the gas, a period called the epoch of reionization, which was largely complete about a billion years after the Big Bang."],["Breaking records","Before Webb, the most distant confirmed galaxy was GN-z11, found by Hubble at a redshift of about 11. Webb confirmed JADES-GS-z14-0 at a redshift of about 14.2 in 2024 and then MoM-z14 at 14.44 in 2025. Candidates at even greater distances await confirmation by spectroscopy."],["Too big, too soon?","Some early Webb galaxies appeared so massive that they seemed to challenge models of galaxy formation. Further study showed many were less massive than first thought, often boosted by bright young stars or growing black holes, but early galaxies still formed stars very efficiently."],["Little red dots","Webb found many compact, very red objects in the early universe nicknamed ‘little red dots’. Astronomers are debating whether they are galaxies packed with stars, growing black holes hidden by gas, or something new."]],
k:[["Most distant confirmed galaxy","MoM-z14 (redshift 14.44)"],["Seen at","About 280 million years after the Big Bang"],["Key telescope","James Webb Space Telescope"],["Age of the universe","About 13.8 billion years"],["Reionization ended","About 1 billion years after the Big Bang"]],
d:[["1995","Hubble Deep Field shows thousands of distant galaxies"],["2016","GN-z11 measured at redshift about 11"],["2022","Webb begins science observations"],["2024","JADES-GS-z14-0 confirmed"],["2025","MoM-z14 confirmed as the most distant galaxy"]],
s2:[["Population III stars","The very first stars, called Population III, are thought to have been made only of hydrogen and helium and to have been huge and short-lived. None has been seen directly yet."],["Deep fields","Long exposures of tiny patches of sky, such as the Hubble Ultra Deep Field and Webb’s JADES survey, reveal thousands of faint, distant galaxies in an area smaller than a grain of sand held at arm’s length."]],
g:[["Redshift","The stretching of light to longer wavelengths"],["Reionization","When early starlight ionized the universe’s hydrogen"],["Spectroscopy","Splitting light to measure its properties"],["Deep field","A long exposure of a small patch of sky"]],
f:["Distant galaxies are seen as they were in the past.","Webb sees in infrared light.","MoM-z14 is the most distant confirmed galaxy."],
fun:"The light from MoM-z14 has been traveling for more than 13.5 billion years, about three times longer than Earth has existed."});

ADDART({id:"exodetect",t:"How Exoplanets Are Found",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🔎",w:"Methods of detecting exoplanets",r:["exoplanet","keplertelescope","tess","jwst","lensing","hotjupiters"],
p:[["Hard to see","Planets are faint and lost in their stars’ glare."],["Tiny clues","Most are found by dips in starlight or wobbles of their stars."],["Thousands found","More than 6,400 exoplanets have been confirmed."]],
l:"Exoplanets, planets orbiting stars other than the Sun, are extremely hard to see directly because they are tiny, faint and lost in the glare of their stars, which can be billions of times brighter. Instead, astronomers usually find them indirectly, by their effects on their stars. The transit method watches for a tiny, regular dip in a star’s brightness as a planet passes in front of it; NASA’s Kepler and TESS missions have found thousands of planets this way. The radial velocity, or wobble, method measures how a planet’s gravity makes its star move slightly toward and away from us, shifting the color of its light; it found the first planet around a Sun-like star, 51 Pegasi b, in 1995. Other methods include gravitational microlensing, in which a planet briefly magnifies the light of a more distant star; timing changes in pulsars and eclipsing stars; tracking tiny shifts in a star’s position; and direct imaging, which blocks out the starlight. As of October 2026, NASA counted more than 6,400 confirmed exoplanets.",
s:[["Transits","A planet the size of Jupiter crossing a Sun-like star dims it by about 1 percent; an Earth-sized planet, by only about 0.01 percent. The depth of the dip gives the planet’s size, and the time between dips gives its year. During a transit, starlight filtering through a planet’s atmosphere can reveal its gases."],["Wobbles","A star and its planet orbit their shared center of mass. The star’s tiny motion, as little as about a meter per second, shifts its light toward blue and then red. The size of the wobble gives the planet’s minimum mass. Combining transits and wobbles gives a planet’s density, showing whether it is rocky or gassy."],["Microlensing and imaging","Microlensing can find planets far from their stars and even free-floating planets, and NASA’s Roman Space Telescope, launched in 2026, will search for thousands this way. Direct imaging works best for young, hot giant planets far from their stars; instruments called coronagraphs block the starlight."],["Firsts","The first confirmed exoplanets, found in 1992 by Aleksander Wolszczan and Dale Frail, orbit a pulsar. In 1995 Michel Mayor and Didier Queloz found 51 Pegasi b around a Sun-like star, winning the 2019 Nobel Prize in Physics."]],
k:[["Confirmed exoplanets","More than 6,400 (October 2026)"],["Most productive method","Transits"],["First around a Sun-like star","51 Pegasi b, 1995"],["First exoplanets","Pulsar planets, 1992"],["Key missions","Kepler, TESS, Webb, Roman"]],
d:[["1992","First exoplanets found, around a pulsar"],["1995","51 Pegasi b discovered"],["2009","Kepler launched"],["2018","TESS launched"],["2025","Confirmed exoplanets pass 6,000"],["2026","TESS reaches 1,000 confirmed planets"]],
s2:[["Citizen scientists","Volunteers in projects such as Planet Hunters have spotted transiting planets that computer searches missed."],["Earth seen from afar","An alien astronomer watching the Sun would see Earth dim it by about 0.008 percent for about 13 hours once a year."]],
g:[["Transit","A planet passing in front of its star"],["Radial velocity","Motion toward or away from us"],["Microlensing","Brightening of a star by the gravity of an object in front"],["Coronagraph","A device that blocks a star’s light"]],
f:["Most exoplanets are found by transits.","The first exoplanets orbit a pulsar.","51 Pegasi b was found in 1995."],
fun:"Kepler stared at the same patch of sky, about 150,000 stars, for four years without blinking."});

ADDART({id:"hotjupiters",t:"Hot Jupiters",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🔥",w:"Hot Jupiter",r:["exoplanet","exodetect","jupiter","solarform","superearths","jwst"],
p:[["Scorching giants","Hot Jupiters are gas giants that orbit very close to their stars."],["Years of a few days","They circle their stars in just a few days."],["A surprise","Their discovery in 1995 overturned ideas about planet formation."]],
l:"Hot Jupiters are giant gas planets similar in size to Jupiter that orbit extremely close to their stars, often in less than ten days. Because they are so close, their daytime temperatures reach about 1,000 to over 2,000 degrees Celsius. The first exoplanet found around a Sun-like star, 51 Pegasi b, discovered in 1995, is a hot Jupiter that orbits its star in just over four days, much closer than Mercury orbits the Sun. The discovery was a shock, because astronomers had expected giant planets to form and stay far from their stars, as in our Solar System. Hot Jupiters are now thought to have formed farther out and migrated inward, either by moving through the disk of gas around the young star or by being thrown onto a tilted, stretched orbit by other planets and then pulled close. Because they are large and orbit often, hot Jupiters are easy to find, so many early exoplanets were of this kind, but only about 1 percent of Sun-like stars actually have one.",
s:[["Tidally locked","Most hot Jupiters are thought to always keep the same side facing their star, as the Moon does toward Earth. Fierce winds, measured at thousands of kilometers per hour, carry heat from the day side to the night side."],["Puffed up","Many hot Jupiters are much larger than expected for their mass, some with densities like cork. Heat from their stars and possibly magnetic effects may inflate them."],["Extreme weather","On HD 189733 b, a deep blue hot Jupiter, scientists think it may rain glass sideways in strong winds. WASP-76 b may have iron rain on its night side, and WASP-121 b’s atmosphere is so hot that metals become vapor."],["Atmosphere laboratories","Hot Jupiters are the easiest exoplanets whose atmospheres can be studied. In 2022 the Webb telescope detected carbon dioxide in the atmosphere of the hot Saturn WASP-39 b, the first clear detection in an exoplanet, and later mapped its chemistry."]],
k:[["Size","About Jupiter’s size"],["Orbit","Usually less than 10 days"],["Temperature","About 1,000–2,000+ °C"],["First discovered","51 Pegasi b, 1995"],["How common","About 1% of Sun-like stars"],["Origin","Formed farther out, then migrated inward"]],
d:[["1995","51 Pegasi b discovered"],["1999","First transiting hot Jupiter, HD 209458 b"],["2001","First exoplanet atmosphere detected (HD 209458 b)"],["2022","Webb detects carbon dioxide in WASP-39 b"]],
s2:[["Doomed planets","Some hot Jupiters are spiraling slowly into their stars. WASP-12 b’s orbit is shrinking, and it may be swallowed in a few million years."],["Ultra-hot Jupiters","Planets such as KELT-9 b reach nearly 4,000 degrees Celsius on their day sides, hotter than some stars."]],
g:[["Gas giant","A large planet made mostly of hydrogen and helium"],["Migration","A planet moving from where it formed"],["Tidally locked","Always showing the same face to its star"],["Transiting","Passing in front of its star from our view"]],
f:["Hot Jupiters orbit very close to their stars.","51 Pegasi b is a hot Jupiter.","Hot Jupiters probably migrated inward."],
fun:"A year on the hot Jupiter WASP-12 b lasts only about 26 hours."});

ADDART({id:"superearths",t:"Super-Earths and Mini-Neptunes",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🌍",w:"Super-Earth",r:["exoplanet","exodetect","earthplanet","habitablezone","trappist1","hotjupiters"],
p:[["The most common planets","Planets between Earth and Neptune in size are the most common kind known."],["None in our Solar System","Our Solar System has no planet of this size."],["Rock or gas?","Some are rocky; others have thick gas envelopes."]],
l:"Super-Earths and mini-Neptunes are planets larger than Earth but smaller than Neptune, roughly one to four times Earth’s width. Planets in this range are the most common type found around other stars, yet none exists in our Solar System, which makes them puzzling. The name super-Earth refers only to size, not to whether a planet is Earth-like. Smaller ones, up to about one and a half times Earth’s width, are usually rocky, like larger versions of Earth. Bigger ones, often called mini-Neptunes or sub-Neptunes, seem to have rocky cores wrapped in thick layers of hydrogen and helium gas, or possibly deep layers of water and ice. The Kepler mission revealed a gap in the sizes of planets close to their stars, around 1.5 to 2 times Earth’s width, suggesting that many planets lost their gas atmospheres, perhaps stripped away by radiation from their young stars. Some super-Earths, such as those around TRAPPIST-1 and LHS 1140, orbit in their stars’ habitable zones.",
s:[["The radius gap","In 2017 astronomers found that planets about 1.5 to 2 times Earth’s width are surprisingly rare. Planets above the gap probably kept thick gas envelopes, while those below lost them and are bare rock. The gap helps explain why both rocky super-Earths and gassy mini-Neptunes are common."],["Water worlds?","Some mini-Neptunes may be ‘water worlds’ with deep global oceans or thick steamy atmospheres. The planet K2-18 b, about 124 light-years away, has methane and carbon dioxide in its atmosphere. A 2025 claim of a possible sign of life there was widely disputed by other scientists."],["Lava worlds","Super-Earths very close to their stars, such as 55 Cancri e, are so hot that their surfaces may be covered in lava oceans. Webb has found hints of an atmosphere on 55 Cancri e, possibly fed by gases from its molten surface."],["Why not here?","Our Solar System may lack a super-Earth because Jupiter’s early movements blocked material from flowing inward or swept away planets that formed near the Sun. Scientists are still debating how typical our Solar System is."]],
k:[["Size","About 1–4 times Earth’s width"],["Frequency","The most common known planet type"],["In our Solar System","None"],["Rocky below","About 1.5 Earth widths"],["Famous examples","55 Cancri e, LHS 1140 b, K2-18 b"]],
d:[["2004","55 Cancri e discovered"],["2017","Radius gap described"],["2019","Water vapor reported in K2-18 b’s atmosphere"],["2023","Webb detects methane and carbon dioxide in K2-18 b"]],
s2:[["Not necessarily habitable","A super-Earth with a crushing hydrogen atmosphere could have surface pressures and temperatures far too high for life as we know it."],["Heavy gravity","On a rocky planet twice Earth’s width, gravity at the surface could be more than twice as strong as on Earth."]],
g:[["Super-Earth","A planet larger than Earth but smaller than Neptune"],["Mini-Neptune","A small planet with a thick gas envelope"],["Radius gap","A shortage of planets of certain sizes"],["Habitable zone","The region where liquid water could exist on a planet’s surface"]],
f:["Super-Earths are the most common known planets.","Our Solar System has no super-Earth.","Super-Earth refers only to size."],
fun:"The word super-Earth says nothing about life: a super-Earth could be a lava world or a steamy gas planet."});

ADDART({id:"rogueplanets",t:"Rogue Planets",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🪐",w:"Rogue planet",r:["exoplanet","exodetect","lensing","browndwarfs","milky","solarform"],
p:[["Planets without stars","Rogue planets drift through space alone."],["Very common","There may be more rogue planets than stars in the Milky Way."],["Found by lensing","Most are detected when they briefly magnify a distant star."]],
l:"Rogue planets, also called free-floating or interstellar planets, are planets that do not orbit any star but wander through the galaxy on their own. Most are probably born around stars and then flung out by gravitational encounters with other planets, while some may form on their own from collapsing clouds of gas, like small failed stars. Because they give off almost no light, rogue planets are very hard to find. Astronomers detect them mainly by gravitational microlensing: when a rogue planet passes in front of a more distant star, its gravity briefly bends and magnifies the star’s light, causing a brightening that lasts hours to days. Young, warm rogue planets can also be seen directly in infrared light in star-forming regions, and in 2023 the Webb telescope found dozens of pairs of Jupiter-sized objects floating freely in the Orion Nebula. Studies suggest that the Milky Way may contain billions of rogue planets, possibly more than it has stars. NASA’s Roman Space Telescope, launched in 2026, is expected to find hundreds of them.",
s:[["How planets are ejected","In young planetary systems, planets can pass close to each other. A giant planet like Jupiter can fling a smaller planet out of the system entirely. Passing stars in crowded clusters can also pull planets away."],["Microlensing surveys","Ground-based surveys such as OGLE and KMTNet watch millions of stars toward the center of the galaxy for brief brightenings. Very short events, lasting less than a day, are made by planet-mass objects with no star nearby. Some Earth-mass rogue planets have been found this way."],["Could they hold life?","A rogue planet would be extremely cold on its surface, but a large one with a thick hydrogen atmosphere might trap heat from its interior, and moons heated by tides could hold oceans under ice. These ideas are speculative."],["Orion’s puzzle","Webb found about 40 pairs of Jupiter-mass objects orbiting each other without a star in the Orion Nebula, nicknamed JuMBOs. They are hard to explain, and some astronomers have questioned whether all of them are real."]],
k:[["Also called","Free-floating planets"],["Light","Almost none"],["Main detection method","Gravitational microlensing"],["Estimated number in the Milky Way","Billions, possibly more than the number of stars"],["Upcoming survey","Roman Space Telescope (launched 2026)"]],
d:[["2000","Free-floating planet-mass objects seen in the Orion Nebula"],["2011","Microlensing suggests many Jupiter-mass rogue planets"],["2021","Dozens of rogue planets imaged in Upper Scorpius"],["2023","Webb finds JuMBOs in Orion"],["2026","Roman Space Telescope launched"]],
s2:[["Rogue planets passing by","If a rogue planet passed through the Solar System, it could disturb the orbits of comets and planets, but such close passes are extremely rare."],["Planets or brown dwarfs?","The line between a large rogue planet and a small brown dwarf is blurry. Some astronomers call free-floating objects of a few Jupiter masses planetary-mass objects."]],
g:[["Rogue planet","A planet not orbiting any star"],["Microlensing","Brightening of a star by an object passing in front"],["Ejection","Being thrown out of a planetary system"],["Brown dwarf","An object between a planet and a star in mass"]],
f:["Rogue planets do not orbit stars.","Most rogue planets are found by microlensing.","There may be billions in the Milky Way."],
fun:"A rogue planet’s ‘night’ never ends, because it has no sun to rise."});

ADDART({id:"proximab",t:"Proxima Centauri b",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🌑",w:"Proxima Centauri b",r:["alphacentauri","exoplanet","habitablezone","reddwarfs","exodetect","fermiparadox"],
p:[["The nearest exoplanet","It orbits the closest star to the Sun."],["In the habitable zone","Liquid water could exist there, under the right conditions."],["Harsh star","Its red dwarf star blasts it with flares."]],
l:"Proxima Centauri b is a planet orbiting Proxima Centauri, the closest star to the Sun, about 4.24 light-years away. Announced in August 2016 by a team led by Guillem Anglada-Escudé, it is the nearest known exoplanet. It was found by the radial velocity method, from tiny wobbles in its star’s motion. The planet has at least about 1.07 times Earth’s mass and orbits its star every 11.2 days, at only about a twentieth of the Earth–Sun distance. Because Proxima Centauri is a small, dim red dwarf, that close orbit places the planet in its habitable zone, where it receives about two-thirds of the energy Earth gets from the Sun and liquid water could in principle exist on its surface. Whether it can actually support life is uncertain. Red dwarfs like Proxima produce powerful flares and strong X-rays that could strip away a planet’s atmosphere, and the planet is probably tidally locked, with one side always facing its star. Two other planets have been reported around Proxima, including Proxima d, confirmed in 2022.",
s:[["Finding it","Astronomers in the Pale Red Dot campaign watched Proxima Centauri closely in 2016 and combined their data with older measurements. They found the star moving toward and away from us at about 1.4 meters per second, slower than a person walking, every 11.2 days."],["Living with a flaring star","In 2019 Proxima unleashed a flare that made it about 14,000 times brighter in ultraviolet light for a few seconds. Repeated flares could erode the planet’s atmosphere and make the surface hostile, though a thick atmosphere or magnetic field might offer protection."],["Its siblings","Proxima d, about a quarter of Earth’s mass and orbiting every five days, was confirmed in 2022, one of the lightest exoplanets known. A third planet, Proxima c, a possible super-Earth far from the star, has been proposed but is not confirmed."],["Visiting it","Proxima b is the most likely target for any first interstellar probe. The Breakthrough Starshot project, announced in 2016, proposed sending tiny laser-driven light sails at a fifth of the speed of light, which would reach it in about 20 years, but the technology does not yet exist."]],
k:[["Star","Proxima Centauri (a red dwarf)"],["Distance","About 4.24 light-years"],["Minimum mass","About 1.07 Earths"],["Year","11.2 Earth days"],["Discovered","2016"],["Habitable zone","Yes"]],
d:[["1915","Proxima Centauri discovered by Robert Innes"],["2016","Proxima b announced"],["2016","Breakthrough Starshot announced"],["2019","Huge flare seen on Proxima"],["2022","Proxima d confirmed"]],
s2:[["Part of a triple","Proxima Centauri orbits the bright pair Alpha Centauri A and B at a vast distance, taking roughly 550,000 years for one orbit."],["Not visible to the naked eye","Despite being the closest star, Proxima Centauri is too dim to see without a telescope."]],
g:[["Red dwarf","A small, cool, dim star"],["Radial velocity","Motion toward or away from the observer"],["Tidally locked","Always facing its star with the same side"],["Light sail","A thin sail pushed by light"]],
f:["Proxima b is the nearest known exoplanet.","Proxima b is in its star’s habitable zone.","Proxima Centauri is a red dwarf."],
fun:"From Proxima b, the Sun would appear as a fairly bright star in the constellation Cassiopeia."});

