// Space, part 16 (28 November 2026): more black holes, stars, galaxies, galaxy clusters and cosmology.
ADDART({id:"insidebh",t:"Inside a Black Hole",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🌌",w:"Event horizon",r:["bh","stellarbh","supermassivebh","hawkingrad","relat","wormholes"],
p:[["The point of no return","The event horizon is the boundary beyond which nothing can escape."],["Time slows down","Near a black hole, time passes more slowly than far away."],["Spaghettification","Tides could stretch a falling object like a noodle."]],
l:"A black hole is a region where gravity is so strong that nothing, not even light, can escape once it crosses a boundary called the event horizon. The horizon is not a solid surface but an invisible sphere; its size depends on the black hole’s mass, about 3 kilometers across for every solar mass, so a black hole as heavy as the Sun would be about 6 kilometers wide. Einstein’s general theory of relativity predicts strange effects near the horizon. To a distant observer, a clock falling toward a black hole ticks ever more slowly and its light grows redder and dimmer, so it never seems to cross. Just outside the horizon, light can orbit the black hole in a ring called the photon sphere. Close to a small black hole, the difference in gravity between your head and feet would stretch you into a long thin strand, a process nicknamed spaghettification. At the center, relativity predicts a singularity, a point of infinite density, but most physicists think a future theory combining gravity and quantum physics will replace it.",
s:[["The event horizon","The radius of the horizon was first calculated in 1916 by Karl Schwarzschild, only weeks after Einstein published his theory. It is called the Schwarzschild radius. Inside it, every possible path through space and time leads inward, so escape is impossible however fast you move."],["Time and light","Gravity slows time. A clock near a black hole runs slow compared with one far away, an effect that GPS satellites must correct for even in Earth’s weak gravity. Light climbing away from a black hole loses energy and shifts to longer, redder wavelengths, called gravitational redshift."],["Spaghettification","The pull on the near side of a falling object is stronger than on the far side. For a stellar black hole, this tidal stretching would tear a person apart well before the horizon. For a supermassive black hole, the horizon is so large that a traveler could cross it without feeling anything unusual at first. Astronomers have watched stars being torn apart in ‘tidal disruption events’, which flare brightly for months."],["Spinning black holes","Real black holes spin. A spinning black hole drags space around with it, and outside its horizon lies a region called the ergosphere where nothing can stay still. The mathematics of spinning black holes was worked out by Roy Kerr in 1963."]],
k:[["Boundary","Event horizon"],["Horizon size","About 3 km per solar mass"],["First calculated","Karl Schwarzschild, 1916"],["Center","Predicted singularity"],["Spinning black holes described","Roy Kerr, 1963"]],
d:[["1916","Schwarzschild solves Einstein’s equations for a black hole"],["1963","Roy Kerr describes spinning black holes"],["1967","John Wheeler popularizes the name black hole"],["2019","First image of a black hole’s shadow"]],
s2:[["No hair","Physicists say black holes have ‘no hair’: from outside, a black hole can be described by only its mass, spin and electric charge, whatever fell into it."],["Movies","The black hole Gargantua in the 2014 film Interstellar was drawn with the help of physicist Kip Thorne using real equations for how light bends."]],
g:[["Event horizon","The boundary of no return around a black hole"],["Singularity","A point where the theory predicts infinite density"],["Spaghettification","Stretching of objects by strong tidal forces"],["Ergosphere","The region around a spinning black hole where space is dragged around"]],
f:["Nothing can escape from inside the event horizon.","Time slows near a black hole.","Black holes can spin."],
fun:"Earth would have to be squeezed to the size of a marble, about 1.8 centimeters across, to become a black hole."});

ADDART({id:"primordialbh",t:"Primordial Black Holes",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"⚫",w:"Primordial black hole",r:["bh","bigbang","darkmatter","hawkingrad","gravwaves","stellarbh"],
p:[["Born in the Big Bang","They might have formed in the first second of the universe."],["Any size","They could be as small as an atom or as big as thousands of Suns."],["Still hypothetical","None has been confirmed."]],
l:"Primordial black holes are hypothetical black holes that may have formed in the very early universe, within the first second after the Big Bang, rather than from collapsing stars. If some regions of the hot, dense young universe were slightly denser than their surroundings, gravity could have squeezed them straight into black holes. Because they did not need a star, they could have almost any mass, from much less than a mountain to many thousands of times the Sun’s mass. The idea was proposed in 1966 by Yakov Zeldovich and Igor Novikov and developed by Stephen Hawking in 1971. The smallest ones would have evaporated by now through Hawking radiation, possibly in bursts of gamma rays, but none has been seen. Some scientists have suggested that primordial black holes could make up part or all of the mysterious dark matter, or help explain the heavy black holes found by gravitational wave detectors and the surprisingly early supermassive black holes found by the James Webb Space Telescope. Observations have ruled out many mass ranges, but some remain open.",
s:[["How they might form","In the first fraction of a second, the universe may have had small ripples in density. Where a ripple was strong enough, the region could collapse into a black hole as soon as light could cross it. Some theories of the early universe, including some versions of cosmic inflation, predict this."],["Dark matter candidate","If enough primordial black holes exist, they could make up dark matter. Searches using microlensing, the cosmic microwave background and the effects on stars and galaxies have ruled out most masses, but asteroid-mass black holes, roughly 10^17 to 10^22 grams, remain a possible window."],["Evaporating black holes","A primordial black hole with the mass of a large mountain would be finishing its evaporation today, ending in a burst of high-energy radiation. Gamma-ray telescopes have searched for such bursts without success so far."],["Clues from gravitational waves","Some black hole pairs detected by LIGO and Virgo are heavier than expected from ordinary stars. A few researchers argue that some could be primordial, but most astronomers think ordinary explanations are more likely."]],
k:[["Formed","In the first second after the Big Bang"],["Possible masses","From tiny to thousands of Suns"],["Proposed","1966 (Zeldovich and Novikov), 1971 (Hawking)"],["Status","Hypothetical, not confirmed"],["Possible role","Part of dark matter"]],
d:[["1966","Zeldovich and Novikov suggest early black holes"],["1971","Hawking develops the idea"],["1974","Hawking predicts that small black holes evaporate"],["2016","Gravitational wave discoveries revive interest"]],
s2:[["Passing through","An asteroid-mass primordial black hole would be smaller than an atom. If one passed through the Solar System, it might be detected by tiny changes in planets’ orbits."],["Planet Nine?","Some scientists have suggested that the proposed ‘Planet Nine’ in the outer Solar System could be a primordial black hole the size of a grapefruit, though this is speculative."]],
g:[["Primordial","Existing from the very beginning"],["Dark matter","Invisible matter detected by its gravity"],["Microlensing","Brightening of a star by an object passing in front"],["Evaporation","A black hole losing mass through Hawking radiation"]],
f:["Primordial black holes would not come from stars.","No primordial black hole has been confirmed.","They might be part of dark matter."],
fun:"A primordial black hole as massive as Mount Everest would be far smaller than a single atom."});

ADDART({id:"imbh",t:"Intermediate-Mass Black Holes",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🕳️",w:"Intermediate-mass black hole",r:["bh","stellarbh","supermassivebh","starclusters","gravwaves","hubblest"],
p:[["The missing link","They sit between stellar and supermassive black holes."],["Hard to find","Few strong candidates are known."],["Omega Centauri","Fast stars point to one in this star cluster."]],
l:"Intermediate-mass black holes are black holes with masses of roughly a hundred to a hundred thousand times the Sun’s, between the stellar black holes formed by dying stars and the supermassive black holes in the centers of galaxies. They are thought to be the missing link that could explain how supermassive black holes grew so large: perhaps from medium-sized seeds that merged and swallowed gas. Yet they have been surprisingly hard to find, because most are probably quiet, not feeding on enough gas to shine. Astronomers search for them in the centers of dense star clusters and small galaxies, in bright X-ray sources away from galaxy centers, and through gravitational waves. In 2020 LIGO and Virgo detected the merger GW190521, which produced a black hole of about 140 solar masses, the first clear intermediate-mass black hole. In 2024 a team using two decades of Hubble Space Telescope images found seven stars moving too fast at the center of Omega Centauri, the largest star cluster in the Milky Way, revealing a black hole of at least about 8,200 solar masses.",
s:[["Omega Centauri","Omega Centauri, about 17,000 light-years away, contains about 10 million stars and may be the leftover core of a small galaxy swallowed by the Milky Way. Researchers measured the motions of 1.4 million stars and found seven moving faster than the cluster’s escape speed. Only the pull of a hidden black hole can keep them there."],["GW190521","On 21 May 2019, gravitational wave detectors recorded two black holes of about 85 and 66 solar masses merging. The result, announced in 2020, was a black hole of about 142 solar masses. The heavier partner was itself too big to come from a single dying star, suggesting it formed from an earlier merger."],["Other candidates","HLX-1, an X-ray source at the edge of a galaxy about 290 million light-years away, is thought to hold a black hole of about 20,000 solar masses. Some dwarf galaxies also show signs of medium-sized central black holes."],["Why they matter","Finding many intermediate-mass black holes would help explain how the first supermassive black holes appeared so early in the universe. Future space-based gravitational wave observatories such as LISA, planned for the 2030s, should detect mergers of black holes in this range."]],
k:[["Mass range","About 100 to 100,000 Suns"],["First clear example","GW190521 merger result, about 142 Suns"],["Omega Centauri black hole","At least about 8,200 Suns"],["Omega Centauri distance","About 17,000 light-years"],["Why hard to find","Most are quiet and do not shine"]],
d:[["2009","HLX-1 identified as a strong candidate"],["2019","GW190521 detected"],["2020","GW190521 announced"],["2024","Black hole revealed in Omega Centauri"]],
s2:[["Escape speed","A star moving faster than a cluster’s escape speed should fly away. Fast stars that stay put reveal hidden mass."],["Wandering black holes","Some intermediate-mass black holes may wander through galaxies after their home star clusters or dwarf galaxies were torn apart."]],
g:[["Intermediate","In between"],["Globular cluster","A dense ball of old stars"],["Escape speed","The speed needed to break free of gravity"],["Seed black hole","A smaller black hole that grows into a larger one"]],
f:["Intermediate-mass black holes are between stellar and supermassive.","Omega Centauri holds one.","GW190521 made one."],
fun:"The fast stars at the center of Omega Centauri took 20 years of Hubble photos to catch moving."});

ADDART({id:"agn",t:"Active Galaxies, Blazars and Jets",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🔦",w:"Active galactic nucleus",r:["quasars","supermassivebh","galaxies","m87bh","radioastronomy","gammaraybursts"],
p:[["Powered by black holes","Gas falling into a supermassive black hole makes the galaxy’s center blaze."],["Cosmic jets","Some shoot beams of particles across thousands of light-years."],["Blazars","When a jet points at Earth, we see a blazar."]],
l:"An active galaxy is a galaxy whose center, called an active galactic nucleus, shines far more brightly than its stars can explain. The power comes from a supermassive black hole swallowing gas: as the gas spirals inward through a hot accretion disk, it heats up to millions of degrees and gives off radiation across the spectrum, from radio waves to X-rays and gamma rays. Many active galaxies also launch twin jets of particles moving at nearly the speed of light, driven by the black hole’s spin and magnetic fields. These jets can stretch for hundreds of thousands of light-years and inflate giant lobes that glow in radio waves. Astronomers once thought quasars, Seyfert galaxies, radio galaxies and blazars were different things, but the unified model suggests they are mostly the same kind of object seen from different angles and feeding at different rates. When a jet happens to point almost straight at Earth, it appears extremely bright and rapidly changing: a blazar. Active galaxies were much more common in the early universe.",
s:[["The unified model","Around the disk sits a thick ring of dust. If we view the system from the side, the dust hides the bright center; if we look from above, we see it directly. If we look straight down a jet, we see a blazar. Differences in how much gas is falling in also matter."],["Jets","Jets are among the fastest things known. Some blobs in jets appear to move faster than light, an illusion caused by the jet moving almost directly toward us at nearly light speed. M87’s jet and Centaurus A’s jets are famous examples."],["Blazars and neutrinos","In 2017 the IceCube detector at the South Pole caught a high-energy neutrino that was traced to the blazar TXS 0506+056, about 4 billion light-years away. It was the first time a source of such cosmic neutrinos was identified."],["Seyfert galaxies","Carl Seyfert described spiral galaxies with unusually bright centers in 1943. They are milder cousins of quasars, often close enough to study in detail."]],
k:[["Power source","A supermassive black hole swallowing gas"],["Types","Quasars, Seyferts, radio galaxies, blazars"],["Jet speed","Close to the speed of light"],["Seyfert galaxies described","1943"],["Neutrino source found","Blazar TXS 0506+056, 2017"]],
d:[["1918","Heber Curtis sees M87’s jet"],["1943","Carl Seyfert describes active spiral galaxies"],["1963","First quasar identified"],["2017","Neutrino traced to a blazar"]],
s2:[["Radio lobes","Some radio galaxies are among the largest single objects in the universe. Porphyrion, reported in 2024, has jets spanning about 23 million light-years."],["Changing looks","Some active galaxies switch between bright and dim states within months, as the black hole’s food supply changes."]],
g:[["Active galactic nucleus","A very bright galaxy center powered by a black hole"],["Accretion disk","A disk of gas spiraling into an object"],["Blazar","An active galaxy whose jet points toward Earth"],["Unified model","The idea that many active galaxies are the same thing seen from different angles"]],
f:["Active galaxies are powered by supermassive black holes.","A blazar’s jet points toward Earth.","Jets can travel at nearly light speed."],
fun:"Porphyrion’s jets are so long that more than 200 Milky Way galaxies could line up end to end along them."});

ADDART({id:"nucleosynthesis",t:"How Stars Make the Elements",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"⚛️",w:"Nucleosynthesis",r:["fusion","starlifecycle","supernova","kilonovae","bigbang","periodictable"],
p:[["Cosmic kitchens","Almost every element heavier than helium was made in stars."],["Fusion and explosions","Stars fuse light elements; explosions and collisions make heavier ones."],["We are stardust","The carbon in your body was made inside stars."]],
l:"Nucleosynthesis is the making of new atomic nuclei, the process that created the chemical elements. In the first few minutes after the Big Bang, the universe was hot enough to fuse protons and neutrons into hydrogen, helium and a little lithium, but nothing heavier. All the other elements were made later, mostly inside stars. Stars shine by fusing light nuclei into heavier ones: hydrogen into helium, then, in older and bigger stars, helium into carbon and oxygen, and so on up to iron in the cores of the most massive stars. Elements heavier than iron cannot be made by ordinary fusion, because building them uses up energy rather than releasing it. Instead they are made when nuclei capture neutrons, slowly in the outer layers of aging giant stars (the s-process) or very rapidly in explosive events such as neutron star collisions (the r-process). Supernovae, winds from dying stars and planetary nebulae scatter these elements into space, where they become part of new stars, planets and living things.",
s:[["The Big Bang’s share","Within about 20 minutes of the Big Bang, the universe produced roughly 75 percent hydrogen and 25 percent helium by mass, with traces of lithium. Measurements of these amounts in old stars and gas clouds match predictions, strong evidence for the Big Bang."],["Burning in layers","A massive star near the end of its life is built like an onion, with shells fusing different elements: hydrogen on the outside, then helium, carbon, neon, oxygen and silicon, with iron in the core. When the iron core collapses, the star explodes as a supernova."],["Neutron capture","In the s-process, which takes thousands of years in red giants, nuclei catch neutrons one at a time, making elements such as barium and lead. In the r-process, which takes seconds, nuclei are flooded with neutrons, making gold, platinum and uranium. The kilonova seen in 2017 confirmed neutron star mergers as an r-process site."],["B2FH","In 1957 Margaret Burbidge, Geoffrey Burbidge, William Fowler and Fred Hoyle published a famous paper, known as B2FH from their initials, explaining how stars make nearly all the elements. Fowler shared the 1983 Nobel Prize in Physics partly for this work."]],
k:[["Big Bang made","Hydrogen, helium, a little lithium"],["Stars fuse up to","Iron"],["Heavier elements","Made by neutron capture"],["Gold and platinum","Mostly from neutron star collisions and rare supernovae"],["Key paper","B2FH, 1957"]],
d:[["1920","Arthur Eddington suggests stars are powered by fusing hydrogen"],["1939","Hans Bethe explains how the Sun fuses hydrogen"],["1957","B2FH paper published"],["2017","Kilonova confirms neutron star mergers make heavy elements"]],
s2:[["Your body","About 65 percent of your body by mass is oxygen and 18 percent carbon, both made in stars. The hydrogen in your body’s water dates back to the Big Bang."],["Periodic table of origins","Astronomers have made periodic tables colored by where each element was made: the Big Bang, dying low-mass stars, exploding massive stars, white dwarf explosions or merging neutron stars."]],
g:[["Nucleosynthesis","The making of new atomic nuclei"],["Fusion","Joining light nuclei into heavier ones"],["Neutron capture","A nucleus absorbing a neutron"],["r-process","Rapid neutron capture that makes heavy elements"]],
f:["The Big Bang made mostly hydrogen and helium.","Stars fuse elements up to iron.","Gold is made by rapid neutron capture."],
fun:"The iron in your blood was forged in stars that exploded before the Sun was born."});

ADDART({id:"wolfrayet",t:"Wolf–Rayet Stars",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"💨",w:"Wolf–Rayet star",r:["stars","supernova","hypergiants","starlifecycle","jwst","gammaraybursts"],
p:[["Stripped giants","Wolf–Rayet stars have blown away their outer layers."],["Fierce winds","Their winds are millions of times stronger than the Sun’s."],["Doomed","They are expected to explode as supernovae."]],
l:"Wolf–Rayet stars are rare, extremely hot and massive stars that have lost most of their outer hydrogen through powerful winds, exposing the helium, carbon or nitrogen made in their interiors. They are named after Charles Wolf and Georges Rayet, French astronomers who in 1867 noticed three stars in the constellation Cygnus with unusual bright emission lines in their spectra. Wolf–Rayet stars have surface temperatures from about 30,000 to more than 200,000 degrees Celsius and shine hundreds of thousands of times brighter than the Sun. Their winds blow at up to several thousand kilometers per second and carry away about the Sun’s mass every 100,000 years or faster. Many live in pairs, and some binary Wolf–Rayet stars produce spiral or nested shells of dust as their winds collide. They represent a brief final stage of very massive stars, lasting only a few hundred thousand years, and are expected to end as supernovae, possibly some of the stripped kind or as long gamma-ray bursts, leaving neutron stars or black holes.",
s:[["Shells of dust","WR 140, about 5,000 light-years away, is a pair in which a Wolf–Rayet star and a massive companion swing close every eight years. Each time, their colliding winds make a shell of dust. In 2022 the James Webb Space Telescope captured more than 17 nested rings, like a fingerprint."],["Pinwheels","WR 104 makes a spiral ‘pinwheel’ of dust as the pair orbit. Some astronomers once worried it could produce a gamma-ray burst aimed at Earth, but later studies suggest its axis is tilted away."],["How they form","Very massive stars lose mass through their own strong winds. Others are stripped by a companion star in a close pair. Both routes expose the hot inner layers."],["WR 124","The James Webb Space Telescope’s 2023 image of WR 124, about 15,000 light-years away, showed the star surrounded by clumps of gas and dust it has shed, one of the first images Webb released of this rare stage."]],
k:[["Named after","Charles Wolf and Georges Rayet (1867)"],["Surface temperature","About 30,000 to over 200,000 °C"],["Brightness","Hundreds of thousands of times the Sun"],["Wind speed","Up to several thousand km/s"],["Fate","Supernova, leaving a neutron star or black hole"]],
d:[["1867","Wolf and Rayet describe the first examples"],["1999","WR 104’s pinwheel imaged"],["2022","Webb images WR 140’s nested dust rings"],["2023","Webb images WR 124"]],
s2:[["Gamma Velorum","The brightest Wolf–Rayet star in the sky, Gamma Velorum, can be seen with the naked eye from the Southern Hemisphere."],["Rare","About 670 Wolf–Rayet stars are known in the Milky Way, out of hundreds of billions of stars."]],
g:[["Emission line","A bright line in a spectrum from glowing gas"],["Stellar wind","A stream of gas blowing from a star"],["Binary","A pair of stars orbiting each other"],["Spectrum","Light spread out by wavelength"]],
f:["Wolf–Rayet stars have lost their outer hydrogen.","They have very strong winds.","Webb imaged WR 140’s dust rings."],
fun:"WR 140’s nested dust rings look so much like a fingerprint that astronomers call the star the ‘cosmic fingerprint’."});

ADDART({id:"novae",t:"Novae",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"✨",w:"Nova",r:["whitedwarfs","supernova","binarystars","variablestars","stars","kilonovae"],
p:[["A ‘new’ star","Nova means ‘new’; a faint star suddenly brightens."],["Not destroyed","The star survives and can erupt again."],["White dwarf blasts","Novae happen on white dwarfs that steal gas from a companion."]],
l:"A nova is a sudden, dramatic brightening of a star, which can become thousands to a million times brighter within days and then fade over weeks or months. The name comes from the Latin for ‘new’, because to early observers a star seemed to appear where none had been. A nova happens in a close pair of stars, in which a white dwarf, the dense core of a dead Sun-like star, pulls hydrogen gas from its companion. The gas piles up on the white dwarf’s surface, getting hotter and more compressed, until it ignites in a runaway thermonuclear explosion that blasts the outer layer into space. Unlike a supernova, a nova does not destroy the star, and the process can repeat. Most novae recur only after thousands of years or more, but recurrent novae such as T Coronae Borealis and RS Ophiuchi erupt every few decades. Astronomers find several novae in the Milky Way each year, though many more are hidden by dust; one or two a decade become bright enough to see with the naked eye.",
s:[["How it works","A white dwarf packs about the Sun’s mass into a ball the size of Earth. Gas falling onto it is crushed under enormous gravity. When the bottom of this layer reaches about 20 million degrees, hydrogen fusion starts suddenly and spreads, throwing off the layer at thousands of kilometers per second."],["T Coronae Borealis","T Coronae Borealis, about 3,000 light-years away, erupted in 1866 and 1946. Astronomers have been expecting its next eruption since 2024, when it should briefly become about as bright as the North Star. As of September 2026 it had not yet erupted."],["Nova, supernova, kilonova","A nova is a surface explosion on a surviving white dwarf. A supernova destroys a star, shining up to tens of thousands of times brighter than a nova. A kilonova comes from neutron stars colliding, about a thousand times brighter than a nova."],["Gamma rays","In 2010 the Fermi space telescope found that novae can produce gamma rays, the most energetic kind of light, as shock waves form in the ejected gas."]],
k:[["Meaning","New (Latin)"],["Cause","Explosion on the surface of a white dwarf"],["Brightening","Thousands to a million times"],["Star survives?","Yes"],["Famous recurrent nova","T Coronae Borealis (1866, 1946)"]],
d:[["1866","T Coronae Borealis erupts"],["1946","T Coronae Borealis erupts again"],["2010","Fermi detects gamma rays from a nova"],["2021","RS Ophiuchi erupts"]],
s2:[["Nova Persei","Nova Persei in 1901 became one of the brightest stars in the sky for a few days. Light echoes from its explosion were later seen spreading through nearby dust."],["Lithium makers","Novae are thought to produce much of the lithium in the galaxy, the element used in rechargeable batteries."]],
g:[["Nova","A sudden brightening caused by an explosion on a white dwarf"],["White dwarf","The dense leftover core of a Sun-like star"],["Recurrent nova","A nova that erupts again within decades"],["Thermonuclear","Powered by nuclear fusion at high temperature"]],
f:["A nova does not destroy its star.","Novae happen on white dwarfs.","T Coronae Borealis is a recurrent nova."],
fun:"When T Coronae Borealis erupts, it will jump from invisible to the naked eye to as bright as the North Star in a few hours."});

ADDART({id:"planetarynebulae",t:"Planetary Nebulae",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"💍",w:"Planetary nebula",r:["nebula","whitedwarfs","starlifecycle","redgiants","hubblest","jwst"],
p:[["Nothing to do with planets","They looked like planets through early telescopes."],["A dying star’s breath","A Sun-like star sheds its outer layers."],["Glowing shells","Ultraviolet light from the hot core makes the gas glow."]],
l:"A planetary nebula is a glowing shell of gas thrown off by a Sun-like star near the end of its life. The name is misleading: in the 1780s, William Herschel thought these round, greenish objects looked like the disks of planets through his telescope. In fact, after a star of up to about eight times the Sun’s mass becomes a red giant, it gently puffs its outer layers into space. The exposed core, extremely hot and about the size of Earth, floods the expanding gas with ultraviolet light and makes it glow in colors from different elements: green and blue from oxygen, red from hydrogen and nitrogen. The core then cools into a white dwarf. Planetary nebulae last only about 10,000 to 20,000 years before the gas spreads out and fades, a blink of an eye in a star’s life. They come in remarkable shapes, from rings and hourglasses to spirals and butterflies, often sculpted by companion stars. About 3,000 are known in the Milky Way, and the Sun is expected to make one in about 5 billion years.",
s:[["Famous examples","The Ring Nebula (M57) in Lyra, the Helix Nebula in Aquarius, the Cat’s Eye Nebula in Draco, the Dumbbell Nebula (M27) and the Southern Ring Nebula are favorite targets. The Helix, about 650 light-years away, is one of the closest."],["Strange shapes","Many planetary nebulae are not round. Astronomers think a companion star or planet orbiting the dying star, magnetic fields and fast jets shape the gas into hourglasses, rings and spirals."],["Recycling elements","The gas returned to space is enriched with carbon, nitrogen and other elements made in the star. It mixes into clouds that later form new stars and planets."],["Webb’s view","In 2022 the James Webb Space Telescope’s first images included the Southern Ring Nebula, revealing that its central star has a companion wrapped in dust, and in 2023 it imaged the Ring Nebula in fine detail."]],
k:[["Made by","Stars up to about 8 times the Sun’s mass"],["Lifetime","About 10,000–20,000 years"],["Leftover core","Becomes a white dwarf"],["Known in Milky Way","About 3,000"],["Named by","William Herschel, 1780s"]],
d:[["1764","Charles Messier catalogs the Dumbbell Nebula"],["1779","Ring Nebula discovered"],["1785","Herschel uses the term planetary nebula"],["2022","Webb images the Southern Ring Nebula"]],
s2:[["Mystery element","In 1864 William Huggins found an unknown green line in a planetary nebula’s light and thought it might be a new element, ‘nebulium’. In 1927 it was shown to be oxygen in conditions impossible on Earth."],["The Sun’s turn","When the Sun makes its planetary nebula, any distant astronomers will see a glowing shell where our Solar System used to be."]],
g:[["Planetary nebula","A glowing shell of gas from a dying Sun-like star"],["Red giant","A large, cool star near the end of its life"],["White dwarf","The dense leftover core of a star"],["Ultraviolet","Light with shorter wavelengths than violet"]],
f:["Planetary nebulae have nothing to do with planets.","They are made by Sun-like stars.","Their cores become white dwarfs."],
fun:"The Cat’s Eye Nebula has at least 11 rings of gas, puffed off by its star about every 1,500 years."});

ADDART({id:"protoplanetarydisks",t:"Protoplanetary Disks",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"💿",w:"Protoplanetary disk",r:["solarform","starformation","exoplanet","alma","jwst","comet"],
p:[["Planet nurseries","Young stars are surrounded by disks of gas and dust."],["Rings and gaps","Gaps in disks may be carved by young planets."],["Short-lived","Disks last only a few million years."]],
l:"A protoplanetary disk is a flat, spinning disk of gas and dust around a young star, the material from which planets form. When a cloud of gas collapses to make a star, its spin flattens the leftovers into a disk, much as spinning pizza dough spreads out. Inside the disk, tiny dust grains stick together into pebbles, then boulders and kilometer-sized planetesimals, which collide and grow into planets. Close to the star it is too hot for ice, so rocky planets form there, while beyond the ‘snow line’ ice adds extra material and giant planets can grow and capture gas. Disks are typically a few hundred times wider than the distance from Earth to the Sun and last only about 1 to 10 million years before the gas is blown away or swallowed. In 2014 the ALMA radio observatory in Chile produced a sharp image of the disk around HL Tauri showing bright rings and dark gaps, probably cleared by forming planets. Our Solar System formed from such a disk about 4.6 billion years ago.",
s:[["HL Tauri","HL Tauri, about 450 light-years away, is less than a million years old. ALMA’s 2014 image showed concentric rings so clearly that it surprised astronomers, who had not expected planets to form so early."],["Caught in the act","PDS 70 is the first star where young planets have been directly photographed inside the gaps of their disk. One of them, PDS 70c, has its own disk where moons may be forming."],["The snow line","The snow line is the distance from a young star beyond which water freezes into ice. In the Solar System it was between Mars and Jupiter, roughly where the asteroid belt is now, which helps explain why the giant planets are farther out."],["Webb chemistry","The James Webb Space Telescope has measured water, carbon dioxide and organic molecules in disks, showing what ingredients are available to new planets."]],
k:[["Made of","Gas and dust around a young star"],["Size","A few hundred times the Earth–Sun distance"],["Lifetime","About 1–10 million years"],["Famous image","HL Tauri (ALMA, 2014)"],["Planets photographed in a gap","PDS 70"]],
d:[["1984","First disk imaged around Beta Pictoris"],["2014","ALMA images HL Tauri’s rings"],["2018","Planet PDS 70b photographed in its disk"],["2023","Webb measures water in a disk’s inner region"]],
s2:[["Debris disks","Older stars can have thin ‘debris disks’ of dust from colliding asteroids and comets, like our Solar System’s Kuiper Belt. Beta Pictoris and Fomalhaut have famous examples."],["Shadows","Some disks cast moving shadows from warps in their inner parts, seen by the Hubble Space Telescope sweeping around like a lighthouse beam."]],
g:[["Protoplanetary","Before planets have formed"],["Planetesimal","A small rocky or icy body that builds planets"],["Snow line","Where water freezes in a young planetary system"],["ALMA","A large radio observatory in Chile"]],
f:["Planets form in disks around young stars.","Gaps in disks may be made by planets.","Disks last a few million years."],
fun:"Dust grains in a disk start smaller than smoke particles and must grow a million billion times in mass to become a planet."});

ADDART({id:"pleiades",t:"The Pleiades",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"✨",w:"Pleiades",r:["starclusters","constellations","stars","nebula","hubblest","distances"],
p:[["The Seven Sisters","A bright star cluster known to cultures worldwide."],["Young stars","The cluster is only about 100 million years old."],["Easy to see","Visible with the naked eye from most of the world."]],
l:"The Pleiades, also called the Seven Sisters or Messier 45, is a bright cluster of young stars in the constellation Taurus. It is one of the nearest star clusters to Earth, about 444 light-years away, and one of the easiest to see, appearing as a tiny dipper-shaped group of six or seven blue-white stars. Through binoculars, dozens more stars appear, and the whole cluster contains more than 1,000 members. The stars formed together about 100 million years ago, very young compared with the 4.6-billion-year-old Sun, and the brightest are hot blue stars. Long-exposure photographs show wisps of blue nebula around them; this is a separate cloud of dust the cluster happens to be passing through, reflecting the starlight. The Pleiades appear in the myths, calendars and navigation of cultures across the world, from ancient Greece to Polynesia, Japan, the Andes and Aboriginal Australia, and their rising has often marked the start of the year or the planting season.",
s:[["In world cultures","In Greek myth the stars were seven sisters, daughters of Atlas. In Japan the cluster is Subaru, which gave its name and logo to the car company. Māori people call it Matariki, and its rising in midwinter marks the Māori New Year, a public holiday in New Zealand since 2022. Many Aboriginal Australian peoples tell stories of the sisters being chased by a man represented by stars in Orion."],["How far?","In 2004 and later, a measurement by the Hipparcos satellite suggested the Pleiades were about 390 light-years away, which disagreed with other methods. The Gaia spacecraft and radio telescopes later confirmed a distance of about 444 light-years, resolving the ‘Pleiades distance controversy’."],["The reflection nebula","The blue haze, called the Merope Nebula and others, is light scattered by dust grains. The dust is not left over from the stars’ birth; the cluster is drifting through an unrelated cloud."],["The future","The Pleiades are slowly drifting apart. In about 250 million years the cluster will have dispersed into separate stars wandering the galaxy."]],
k:[["Other names","Seven Sisters, Messier 45, Subaru, Matariki"],["Constellation","Taurus"],["Distance","About 444 light-years"],["Age","About 100 million years"],["Members","More than 1,000 stars"]],
d:[["c. 1600 BCE","Possibly shown on the Nebra sky disk"],["1610","Galileo sees many more stars by telescope"],["1771","Messier lists it as M45"],["2022","Matariki becomes a public holiday in New Zealand"]],
s2:[["Eye test","Many cultures used the Pleiades as an eyesight test: how many stars can you count? Most people see six; people with sharp eyes under dark skies see nine or more."],["Ancient image","The Nebra sky disk, a bronze disk found in Germany and thought to be about 3,600 years old, shows a group of seven dots that many experts think represents the Pleiades."]],
g:[["Open cluster","A loose group of young stars born together"],["Reflection nebula","A dust cloud that reflects starlight"],["Messier object","An object in Charles Messier’s 18th-century catalog"],["Parallax","The shift used to measure distances to stars"]],
f:["The Pleiades are in Taurus.","The Pleiades are young stars.","The blue haze is a cloud the cluster is passing through."],
fun:"Subaru’s car logo shows six stars of the Pleiades, the number usually visible to the naked eye."});

ADDART({id:"etacarinae",t:"Eta Carinae",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"💥",w:"Eta Carinae",r:["hypergiants","supernova","nebula","stars","hubblest","binarystars"],
p:[["A monster star","Eta Carinae is one of the most massive stars known."],["The Great Eruption","In the 1840s it became the second-brightest star in the sky."],["A future supernova","It may explode within the next million years."]],
l:"Eta Carinae is a star system in the southern constellation Carina, about 7,500 light-years away, containing one of the most massive and luminous stars known. The main star is thought to have about 100 times the Sun’s mass and shine about 5 million times brighter; it orbits a smaller but still huge companion every 5.5 years. In the 1840s Eta Carinae underwent the ‘Great Eruption’: it brightened until, in 1843, it was the second-brightest star in the night sky after Sirius, despite its great distance. During the eruption it threw off perhaps 10 or more times the Sun’s mass, which now forms the Homunculus Nebula, a pair of billowing lobes of gas and dust surrounding the star. The star then faded below naked-eye visibility for about a century before slowly brightening again. Eta Carinae is expected to end its life in a supernova, or possibly a more powerful hypernova, but astronomers cannot predict when; it is far enough away that it poses no danger to Earth.",
s:[["The Great Eruption","Sailors and astronomers recorded Eta Carinae’s changes in the 1830s and 1840s, including the astronomer John Herschel, who observed it from South Africa. The cause of the eruption is still debated; one idea is that a third star merged with the main star."],["The Homunculus","The Hubble Space Telescope’s images of the Homunculus, Latin for ‘little man’, show two lobes expanding at about 650 kilometers per second, with a thin disk of material around the middle."],["Light echoes","Light from the Great Eruption bounced off distant dust clouds and is arriving at Earth only now. Astronomers have studied these light echoes to learn what the eruption looked like in the 1840s."],["The Carina Nebula","Eta Carinae sits within the huge Carina Nebula, a star-forming region four times larger than the Orion Nebula. The James Webb Space Telescope’s 2022 image of the ‘Cosmic Cliffs’ shows a nearby part of this nebula."]],
k:[["Constellation","Carina"],["Distance","About 7,500 light-years"],["Main star mass","About 100 Suns"],["Brightness","About 5 million Suns"],["Great Eruption","1840s; brightest in 1843"],["Surrounding nebula","The Homunculus"]],
d:[["1677","Edmond Halley records it as a fourth-magnitude star"],["1843","Great Eruption peaks"],["1890","A smaller eruption"],["1990s","Hubble images the Homunculus"]],
s2:[["Not visible from the far north","Eta Carinae is too far south to see from Europe, most of North America or northern Asia, but it is easy to find in southern skies."],["X-ray cycles","Every 5.5 years, when the two stars swing closest, their colliding winds produce a burst of X-rays that astronomers watch closely."]],
g:[["Luminous blue variable","A very massive, unstable star prone to eruptions"],["Light echo","Light reflected by dust that arrives later"],["Hypernova","An especially powerful supernova"],["Nebula","A cloud of gas and dust in space"]],
f:["Eta Carinae had a Great Eruption in the 1840s.","It is surrounded by the Homunculus Nebula.","It will probably end as a supernova."],
fun:"In 1843 Eta Carinae was brighter than Canopus despite being about 24 times farther away."});

ADDART({id:"orionnebula",t:"The Orion Nebula",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🌫️",w:"Orion Nebula",r:["nebula","starformation","protoplanetarydisks","constellations","jwst","rogueplanets"],
p:[["Star factory","It is the nearest large region of star birth."],["Visible to the eye","It is the fuzzy ‘star’ in Orion’s sword."],["The Trapezium","Four bright young stars light it up."]],
l:"The Orion Nebula, also called Messier 42, is a huge glowing cloud of gas and dust in the constellation Orion, about 1,350 light-years away. It is the closest large region where stars are being born and one of the brightest nebulae in the sky; it can be seen with the naked eye as the fuzzy middle ‘star’ of Orion’s sword, below his belt. The nebula is about 24 light-years across and contains about 2,000 times the Sun’s mass. Its glow comes from gas lit up by a small group of very hot, young stars at its center called the Trapezium, whose ultraviolet light makes hydrogen shine red and oxygen shine green. Hidden inside the cloud are thousands of young stars, many surrounded by protoplanetary disks where planets may be forming. Because it is so close and bright, the Orion Nebula is one of the most studied objects in the sky and has taught astronomers much of what they know about how stars and planets form.",
s:[["The Trapezium","The four brightest Trapezium stars are only about 300,000 years old. The brightest, Theta-1 Orionis C, is about 30 times the Sun’s mass and its radiation is carving out a cavity in the cloud."],["Proplyds","Hubble images in the 1990s revealed dozens of ‘proplyds’, protoplanetary disks being evaporated by the Trapezium’s radiation. They were among the first disks around young stars to be seen directly."],["Webb’s surprises","The James Webb Space Telescope found a molecule called the methyl cation in a young disk in Orion in 2023, and reported dozens of Jupiter-mass objects floating freely without stars, nicknamed JuMBOs."],["Part of a bigger cloud","The nebula is the bright corner of the Orion Molecular Cloud Complex, which stretches across much of the constellation and includes the Horsehead Nebula."]],
k:[["Other name","Messier 42"],["Constellation","Orion"],["Distance","About 1,350 light-years"],["Size","About 24 light-years across"],["Lit by","The Trapezium stars"]],
d:[["1610","Nicolas-Claude Fabri de Peiresc records the nebula by telescope"],["1880","Henry Draper takes the first photograph of a nebula, the Orion Nebula"],["1993","Hubble reveals proplyds"],["2023","Webb finds JuMBOs"]],
s2:[["First nebula photograph","In 1880 Henry Draper made the first photograph of a nebula, a 51-minute exposure of the Orion Nebula."],["Easy target","Even small binoculars show the Orion Nebula as a soft glow, and a small telescope reveals the Trapezium stars."]],
g:[["Nebula","A cloud of gas and dust in space"],["Emission nebula","A nebula that glows because its gas is energized by stars"],["Proplyd","A protoplanetary disk being evaporated by nearby stars"],["Molecular cloud","A cold, dense cloud where stars form"]],
f:["The Orion Nebula is a star-forming region.","It is visible to the naked eye.","The Trapezium stars light it up."],
fun:"The light you see from the Orion Nebula tonight left it around the time of the late Roman Empire."});

ADDART({id:"galaxytypes",t:"Types of Galaxies",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🌀",w:"Galaxy morphological classification",r:["galaxies","milky","andromeda","galaxymergers","dwarfgalaxies","hubble"],
p:[["Spirals, ellipticals and irregulars","Galaxies come in three main shapes."],["The tuning fork","Edwin Hubble sorted galaxies on a diagram shaped like a tuning fork."],["Shape tells history","A galaxy’s shape reflects its age and collisions."]],
l:"Galaxies come in a wide range of shapes and sizes, which astronomers sort into types. In 1926 Edwin Hubble introduced a classification still used today, often drawn as a ‘tuning fork’. Elliptical galaxies, on the handle, are smooth, rounded or egg-shaped systems of mostly old, reddish stars with little gas, ranging from small to the largest galaxies known. Spiral galaxies, on the two prongs, have a flat rotating disk with spiral arms of young blue stars, gas and dust wrapped around a central bulge; in barred spirals, such as the Milky Way, the arms start from a straight bar of stars. Lenticular galaxies sit where the handle meets the prongs, with a disk but no arms. Irregular galaxies, often small, have no clear shape. Hubble once thought galaxies evolved along the fork, but that idea was wrong. Instead, astronomers now think that spirals are fed by gas and keep making stars, while big ellipticals form when galaxies merge and have mostly stopped forming stars.",
s:[["Spirals","About two-thirds of bright galaxies near us are spirals or barred spirals. Their arms are not fixed structures but density waves, like traffic jams, where gas is squeezed and new stars form. The Milky Way, Andromeda and the Whirlpool Galaxy are spirals."],["Ellipticals","Giant ellipticals often sit at the centers of galaxy clusters and contain up to trillions of stars. M87 in the Virgo Cluster is a famous example. Their stars orbit in all directions rather than in a flat disk."],["Irregulars and peculiars","The Large and Small Magellanic Clouds are irregular galaxies. Galaxies distorted by collisions, such as the Antennae, are called peculiar."],["Red and dead","Astronomers often divide galaxies into a ‘blue cloud’ of star-forming galaxies and a ‘red sequence’ of galaxies that have stopped making stars. Galaxies seem to move from blue to red, a process called quenching, possibly because of mergers or black hole feedback."]],
k:[["Classified by","Edwin Hubble, 1926"],["Main types","Elliptical, spiral, barred spiral, lenticular, irregular"],["The Milky Way","Barred spiral"],["Largest galaxies","Giant ellipticals"],["Citizen science","Galaxy Zoo, from 2007"]],
d:[["1845","Lord Rosse sees spiral structure in the Whirlpool Galaxy"],["1926","Hubble publishes his classification"],["1959","Gérard de Vaucouleurs extends the system"],["2007","Galaxy Zoo invites the public to classify galaxies"]],
s2:[["Galaxy Zoo","In the Galaxy Zoo project, launched in 2007, hundreds of thousands of volunteers classified millions of galaxy images, discovering new kinds of objects along the way."],["Ring galaxies","Rare ring galaxies, such as the Cartwheel Galaxy, form when a smaller galaxy plunges through the center of a larger one, sending a ripple of star formation outward."]],
g:[["Elliptical galaxy","A rounded galaxy of mostly old stars"],["Spiral galaxy","A disk galaxy with spiral arms"],["Barred spiral","A spiral with a central bar of stars"],["Lenticular galaxy","A disk galaxy without spiral arms"]],
f:["The Milky Way is a barred spiral.","Giant ellipticals are the largest galaxies.","Hubble drew galaxy types as a tuning fork."],
fun:"Galaxy Zoo volunteers found a mysterious green glowing cloud, now called Hanny’s Voorwerp after the Dutch teacher who spotted it."});

ADDART({id:"dwarfgalaxies",t:"Dwarf Galaxies",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"✴️",w:"Dwarf galaxy",r:["galaxies","localgroup","magellanic","darkmatter","milky","galaxytypes"],
p:[["Small but numerous","Dwarf galaxies are the most common galaxies."],["Full of dark matter","Some are almost entirely dark matter."],["Building blocks","Big galaxies grew by swallowing dwarfs."]],
l:"Dwarf galaxies are small galaxies containing from a few thousand to a few billion stars, compared with hundreds of billions in the Milky Way. They are the most common kind of galaxy in the universe, though they are so faint that most are found only nearby. Dozens orbit the Milky Way as satellites, including the Large and Small Magellanic Clouds, the Sagittarius Dwarf Galaxy and many tiny ‘ultra-faint’ dwarfs discovered by sky surveys since 2005. Dwarf galaxies come in several kinds: dwarf irregulars rich in gas and still forming stars, dwarf ellipticals and dwarf spheroidals made of old stars with little gas, and blue compact dwarfs bursting with new stars. Many contain enormous amounts of dark matter compared with their stars, sometimes hundreds or thousands of times more, which makes them natural laboratories for studying dark matter. In the standard picture of cosmology, large galaxies like the Milky Way grew by merging with and swallowing many dwarf galaxies over billions of years.",
s:[["Ultra-faint dwarfs","Some dwarf galaxies are fainter than a single bright star. Segue 1, found in 2007, has only about a thousand stars but seems to contain far more dark matter. Ultra-faint dwarfs are among the oldest, most primitive galaxies known."],["Being eaten","The Sagittarius Dwarf Galaxy, on the far side of the Milky Way, is being torn apart, its stars stretched into a long stream that wraps around our galaxy. Many such streams have been mapped by the Gaia spacecraft."],["Missing satellites","Computer simulations of dark matter predicted many more small satellite galaxies than were first observed. The discovery of many ultra-faint dwarfs has helped close this gap, and the Vera C. Rubin Observatory is expected to find many more."],["Galaxies without dark matter","In 2018 astronomers reported two ultra-diffuse galaxies, NGC 1052-DF2 and DF4, that seem to have almost no dark matter, possibly created when galaxies collided. Their nature is still debated."]],
k:[["Stars","A few thousand to a few billion"],["Most common galaxy type","Yes"],["Largest Milky Way satellite","Large Magellanic Cloud"],["Kinds","Dwarf irregular, dwarf elliptical, dwarf spheroidal, blue compact"],["Dark matter","Often very high compared with stars"]],
d:[["1938","Harlow Shapley finds the first dwarf spheroidals"],["1994","Sagittarius Dwarf Galaxy discovered"],["2005","Sloan Digital Sky Survey begins finding ultra-faint dwarfs"],["2018","Galaxies apparently lacking dark matter reported"]],
s2:[["Leo T","Leo T, about 1.4 million light-years away, is one of the smallest galaxies still forming stars, with fresh gas despite its tiny size."],["Ultra-diffuse galaxies","Some galaxies are as wide as the Milky Way but contain only about 1 percent as many stars, making them extremely faint ‘ghost’ galaxies."]],
g:[["Dwarf galaxy","A small galaxy with relatively few stars"],["Satellite galaxy","A small galaxy orbiting a larger one"],["Stellar stream","A long trail of stars pulled from a galaxy or cluster"],["Ultra-faint","Extremely dim"]],
f:["Dwarf galaxies are the most common galaxies.","Many dwarf galaxies are rich in dark matter.","The Milky Way grew by swallowing dwarfs."],
fun:"Some ultra-faint dwarf galaxies have fewer stars than a typical star cluster, yet weigh as much as millions of Suns because of dark matter."});

ADDART({id:"starburstgalaxies",t:"Starburst Galaxies",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🎆",w:"Starburst galaxy",r:["galaxies","starformation","galaxymergers","supernova","galaxytypes","jwst"],
p:[["Star factories","They make stars hundreds of times faster than the Milky Way."],["Short bursts","Their gas runs out in a few tens of millions of years."],["Often triggered by collisions","Interactions squeeze gas and spark star birth."]],
l:"A starburst galaxy is a galaxy forming stars at an exceptionally high rate, often tens to hundreds of times faster than the Milky Way, which makes only about one to two Suns’ worth of new stars per year. Such bursts usually happen when a collision or close encounter with another galaxy squeezes large clouds of gas, or when gas is funneled into the galaxy’s center by a bar. Because they use up their gas so quickly, starbursts last only about 10 to 100 million years, brief compared with a galaxy’s billions of years of life. Their many young, massive stars soon explode as supernovae, and the combined winds can blow huge plumes of gas out of the galaxy, sometimes slowing further star formation. Much starburst activity is hidden behind dust, which absorbs the starlight and glows in infrared, so infrared telescopes are key to studying them. The nearest famous starburst is the Cigar Galaxy, Messier 82, about 12 million light-years away. Starbursts were far more common in the early universe.",
s:[["The Cigar Galaxy","M82 is seen edge-on, like a cigar. A close encounter with its neighbor M81 about 100 million years ago triggered its starburst. Images show red plumes of hydrogen gas bursting out above and below its disk, driven by supernovae."],["Hidden by dust","Ultraluminous infrared galaxies shine more than a trillion times brighter than the Sun, almost all in infrared light from dust heated by young stars. Many are merging galaxies."],["Super star clusters","Starbursts often form super star clusters, dense groups of hundreds of thousands of young stars that may become globular clusters billions of years later."],["Early universe","Many galaxies in the young universe were in a starburst state. The James Webb Space Telescope has found that some formed stars intensely in short bursts, which may explain why they appear so bright."]],
k:[["Star formation rate","Tens to hundreds of times the Milky Way’s"],["Milky Way’s rate","About 1–2 Suns per year"],["Duration","About 10–100 million years"],["Nearest famous example","M82, the Cigar Galaxy"],["Common trigger","Galaxy collisions or close encounters"]],
d:[["1774","Johann Bode discovers M82"],["1981","The term starburst comes into use"],["1983","IRAS finds ultraluminous infrared galaxies"],["2014","A supernova is seen in M82"]],
s2:[["Supernova in M82","In January 2014 a type Ia supernova, SN 2014J, appeared in M82, one of the closest such explosions in decades. It was first spotted by students during a teaching session at a London observatory."],["Galactic superwinds","Winds from starburst galaxies can carry gas and heavy elements far into intergalactic space, enriching it."]],
g:[["Starburst","A period of very rapid star formation"],["Super star cluster","A very massive young star cluster"],["Infrared","Light just beyond red, invisible to the eye"],["Galactic wind","An outflow of gas from a galaxy"]],
f:["Starburst galaxies form stars very quickly.","Starbursts are often triggered by collisions.","M82 is a starburst galaxy."],
fun:"Supernovae in the Cigar Galaxy’s core go off roughly once every 10 years, compared with about once or twice a century in the Milky Way."});

ADDART({id:"whirlpool",t:"The Whirlpool Galaxy",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🌀",w:"Whirlpool Galaxy",r:["galaxytypes","galaxies","galaxymergers","hubblest","jwst","supernova"],
p:[["A face-on spiral","Its arms are seen perfectly from above."],["First spiral seen","It was the first galaxy whose spiral shape was noticed."],["A small companion","It is tugging on a smaller galaxy, NGC 5195."]],
l:"The Whirlpool Galaxy, also called Messier 51a or M51, is a grand-design spiral galaxy about 31 million light-years away in the constellation Canes Venatici, near the handle of the Big Dipper. Because it faces Earth almost directly, its two curving spiral arms can be seen beautifully, making it one of the most photographed galaxies. In 1845 the Irish astronomer William Parsons, the third Earl of Rosse, used his giant telescope, the Leviathan of Parsonstown, to see and sketch spiral arms in the Whirlpool, the first time a spiral structure was noticed in a ‘nebula’. At the time, nobody knew it was a separate galaxy. The Whirlpool is interacting with a smaller companion galaxy, NGC 5195, at the end of one of its arms. The gravitational pull of this encounter is thought to have strengthened its spiral arms and triggered bursts of star formation, visible as pink clusters of young stars. Several supernovae have been seen in the Whirlpool, including ones in 1994, 2005 and 2011.",
s:[["Lord Rosse’s sketch","Rosse’s telescope, completed in 1845, had a 1.8-meter mirror and was the largest in the world for more than 70 years. His drawing of the Whirlpool’s spiral is one of the most famous astronomical sketches and inspired debate about what nebulae were."],["Spiral arms","The Whirlpool is a ‘grand-design’ spiral, with two well-defined arms. Dust lanes trace the inner edges of the arms, where gas is compressed and stars are born; hot young stars light up the arms in blue and pink."],["The companion","NGC 5195 passed through the Whirlpool’s disk hundreds of millions of years ago. It now lies slightly behind the larger galaxy and has been distorted by the encounter."],["Webb’s view","In 2023 the James Webb Space Telescope imaged the Whirlpool in infrared, revealing glowing dust filaments and bubbles carved by young stars and supernovae."]],
k:[["Other names","M51, NGC 5194"],["Type","Grand-design spiral"],["Distance","About 31 million light-years"],["Constellation","Canes Venatici"],["Companion","NGC 5195"]],
d:[["1773","Charles Messier discovers it"],["1845","Lord Rosse sees its spiral structure"],["2005","Hubble releases a famous portrait"],["2023","Webb images it in infrared"]],
s2:[["Backyard target","Under dark skies, the Whirlpool can be seen as a faint smudge in binoculars, and its spiral is visible in larger amateur telescopes."],["Ultraluminous X-ray sources","The Whirlpool contains several unusually bright X-ray sources, probably black holes or neutron stars feeding from companion stars."]],
g:[["Grand-design spiral","A spiral galaxy with two clear arms"],["Face-on","Seen from directly above"],["Companion galaxy","A smaller galaxy near a larger one"],["Dust lane","A dark band of dust in a galaxy"]],
f:["The Whirlpool was the first galaxy seen to be spiral.","It is interacting with NGC 5195.","It faces Earth almost directly."],
fun:"Lord Rosse’s telescope tube was so large that a person could walk through it with an umbrella."});

ADDART({id:"centaurusa",t:"Centaurus A",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🌠",w:"Centaurus A",r:["agn","galaxies","galaxymergers","supermassivebh","radioastronomy","chandra"],
p:[["A galaxy with jets","It is the nearest large active galaxy."],["A dark dust lane","A band of dust cuts across its glowing center."],["Huge in radio","Its radio lobes span much of the southern sky."]],
l:"Centaurus A, also called NGC 5128, is a large galaxy about 12 million light-years away in the southern constellation Centaurus. It is the nearest big galaxy with an active, feeding supermassive black hole, about 55 million times the Sun’s mass, which makes it a favorite for studying jets and active galaxies. In visible light it looks like a glowing ball of stars cut in half by a dramatic dark lane of dust. This appearance is thought to be the result of a collision between a large elliptical galaxy and a smaller spiral galaxy rich in gas and dust, a few hundred million years ago. The black hole drives two jets of particles that blast out at about half the speed of light, glowing in X-rays and radio waves, and feed enormous radio lobes stretching about a million light-years. Seen from Earth, the radio lobes cover an area of sky more than 100 times larger than the full Moon. Centaurus A is one of the brightest radio sources in the sky and was among the first identified.",
s:[["Seeing it","Centaurus A is bright enough to see with binoculars from the Southern Hemisphere and the tropics. Its dust lane is visible in small telescopes."],["The jets","Chandra X-ray Observatory images show a jet stretching about 13,000 light-years from the core, with shocks where it plows into surrounding gas. Radio telescopes trace the jets much farther."],["A violent past","Infrared images show a twisted disk of gas and young stars inside the dust lane, the remains of the swallowed spiral. Faint shells of stars in the outer galaxy also reveal past mergers."],["Cosmic rays","Some astronomers suspect Centaurus A may be a source of the most energetic cosmic rays detected on Earth, though this has not been proven."]],
k:[["Other name","NGC 5128"],["Distance","About 12 million light-years"],["Constellation","Centaurus"],["Central black hole","About 55 million Suns"],["Radio lobes","About a million light-years across"]],
d:[["1826","James Dunlop discovers it"],["1949","Identified as a strong radio source"],["1999","Chandra images its X-ray jet"],["2021","Event Horizon Telescope images the base of its jet"]],
s2:[["Event Horizon Telescope","In 2021 the Event Horizon Telescope collaboration published the sharpest-ever image of Centaurus A’s jet near the black hole, showing its edges glowing brighter than its center."],["A supernova","In 1986 a supernova, SN 1986G, appeared in Centaurus A’s dust lane, discovered by the Australian amateur astronomer Robert Evans."]],
g:[["Active galaxy","A galaxy with a very bright, black-hole-powered center"],["Radio lobe","A huge cloud of particles glowing in radio waves"],["Jet","A narrow beam of particles"],["Dust lane","A dark band of dust in a galaxy"]],
f:["Centaurus A is the nearest large active galaxy.","It formed from a galaxy collision.","Its radio lobes are huge."],
fun:"If your eyes could see radio waves, Centaurus A would look about 16 times wider than the full Moon in the southern sky."});

ADDART({id:"virgocluster",t:"The Virgo Cluster",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🌌",w:"Virgo Cluster",r:["galaxyclusters","superclusters","m87bh","localgroup","galaxytypes","darkmatter"],
p:[["Our big neighbor","It is the nearest large galaxy cluster."],["Over a thousand galaxies","It holds perhaps 1,300 to 2,000 galaxies."],["Pulling us in","Its gravity tugs on the Local Group."]],
l:"The Virgo Cluster is the nearest large cluster of galaxies, centered about 54 million light-years away in the direction of the constellations Virgo and Coma Berenices. It contains roughly 1,300 to 2,000 galaxies, spread over a region about 15 million light-years across, and lies at the heart of the Virgo Supercluster, the larger structure that includes our own Local Group. At its center sits the giant elliptical galaxy Messier 87, home of the first black hole ever imaged. The cluster includes all kinds of galaxies: giant ellipticals such as M87, M49 and M86, beautiful spirals such as M100, and hundreds of dwarf galaxies. Hot gas at tens of millions of degrees fills the space between them and glows in X-rays. The Virgo Cluster’s gravity pulls on the Milky Way and its neighbors, slightly slowing how fast we move away from it in the expansion of the universe. Many galaxies in the cluster were catalogued by Charles Messier in the 1700s, long before anyone knew they were galaxies.",
s:[["Messier’s nebulae","Of the 110 objects in Messier’s catalog, 16 are galaxies in the Virgo Cluster. In 1781 Messier and Pierre Méchain noticed an unusual concentration of nebulae in this part of the sky."],["Ram-pressure stripping","As spiral galaxies plunge through the cluster’s hot gas, the gas pushes their own gas out like wind blowing away smoke, leaving long tails. This can stop star formation. Several Virgo spirals show such tails."],["Subclusters","The Virgo Cluster is not a single smooth ball but has several clumps centered on M87, M86 and M49, which are still falling together. The cluster is young and still forming."],["Measuring distances","Astronomers used Cepheid variable stars in the Virgo spiral M100 to measure its distance with the Hubble Space Telescope in the 1990s, an important step in measuring the expansion of the universe."]],
k:[["Distance","About 54 million light-years"],["Galaxies","About 1,300–2,000"],["Size","About 15 million light-years across"],["Central galaxy","M87"],["Part of","Virgo Supercluster"]],
d:[["1781","Messier and Méchain note many nebulae in Virgo"],["1950s","Gérard de Vaucouleurs describes the Local Supercluster around it"],["1994","Hubble measures Cepheids in M100"],["2019","M87’s black hole imaged"]],
s2:[["Infalling","The Local Group is moving away from the Virgo Cluster more slowly than expected from cosmic expansion, because Virgo’s mass pulls us back. Astronomers call this the Virgo infall."],["Intracluster stars","Some stars in the Virgo Cluster float between galaxies, torn from their homes by close encounters."]],
g:[["Galaxy cluster","A large group of galaxies bound by gravity"],["Ram-pressure stripping","Removal of a galaxy’s gas by surrounding hot gas"],["Subcluster","A smaller clump within a cluster"],["Elliptical galaxy","A rounded galaxy of mostly old stars"]],
f:["The Virgo Cluster is the nearest large galaxy cluster.","M87 is at its center.","It contains over a thousand galaxies."],
fun:"Light from the Virgo Cluster that reaches us today left when dinosaurs had been extinct for only about 12 million years."});

ADDART({id:"comacluster",t:"The Coma Cluster",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🌐",w:"Coma Cluster",r:["galaxyclusters","darkmatter","superclusters","galaxytypes","chandra","cosmicweb"],
p:[["Where dark matter was first noticed","Fritz Zwicky’s 1933 study pointed to unseen mass."],["Thousands of galaxies","It holds well over 1,000 large galaxies."],["Mostly ellipticals","Its crowded center is full of old galaxies."]],
l:"The Coma Cluster is a large, rich cluster of galaxies about 320 million light-years away in the constellation Coma Berenices. It contains more than 1,000 bright galaxies and many thousands of fainter ones, packed into a region about 20 million light-years across. Unlike the looser Virgo Cluster, Coma is round and densely crowded at its center, where most galaxies are ellipticals and lenticulars with old stars, dominated by two giant ellipticals, NGC 4874 and NGC 4889. Coma holds a special place in history: in 1933 the Swiss-American astronomer Fritz Zwicky measured how fast its galaxies move and found they were far too fast to be held together by the visible matter alone. He concluded that the cluster must contain huge amounts of invisible ‘dark matter’, an idea that took decades to be widely accepted. Coma also contains vast amounts of hot gas glowing in X-rays and, as found in 2015, hundreds of faint ‘ultra-diffuse’ galaxies. It lies within the Coma Supercluster, part of the giant cosmic wall of galaxies called the CfA2 Great Wall.",
s:[["Zwicky’s missing mass","Zwicky estimated that Coma needed hundreds of times more mass than its galaxies showed. His numbers were too large because of errors in distance and in estimates of starlight, but the basic conclusion was right. Modern estimates suggest Coma’s mass is roughly 85 percent dark matter."],["Ultra-diffuse galaxies","In 2015 astronomers using a small array of camera lenses called Dragonfly found 47 extremely faint galaxies in Coma, as wide as the Milky Way but with about 1 percent as many stars. Hundreds more have been found since."],["Hot gas","X-ray telescopes show Coma’s hot gas, at about 100 million degrees, outweighing all its galaxies combined. Radio telescopes also see a faint glow from particles accelerated by shocks as groups of galaxies fall in."],["Still growing","The Coma Cluster is still swallowing groups of galaxies, including one centered on the galaxy NGC 4839 falling in from one side."]],
k:[["Distance","About 320 million light-years"],["Bright galaxies","More than 1,000"],["Main galaxies","NGC 4874 and NGC 4889"],["Dark matter first proposed","Fritz Zwicky, 1933"],["Constellation","Coma Berenices"]],
d:[["1785","William Herschel notes many nebulae in Coma"],["1933","Zwicky proposes dark matter"],["1970","Hot X-ray gas detected"],["2015","Ultra-diffuse galaxies found"]],
s2:[["Berenice’s Hair","The constellation Coma Berenices is named after the hair of Queen Berenice II of Egypt, who, legend says, offered her hair to the gods for her husband’s safe return from war."],["Galaxy cluster laboratory","Because it is relatively nearby and rich, Coma is a standard comparison for studying how galaxies change in crowded environments."]],
g:[["Rich cluster","A cluster with very many galaxies"],["Ultra-diffuse galaxy","A large but extremely faint galaxy"],["Dark matter","Invisible matter detected by its gravity"],["Lenticular galaxy","A disk galaxy without spiral arms"]],
f:["Zwicky proposed dark matter by studying the Coma Cluster.","Coma is dominated by elliptical galaxies.","Coma contains ultra-diffuse galaxies."],
fun:"Zwicky called the unseen material ‘dunkle Materie’, German for dark matter."});

ADDART({id:"perseuscluster",t:"The Perseus Cluster",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🎵",w:"Perseus Cluster",r:["galaxyclusters","supermassivebh","chandra","agn","darkmatter","jwst"],
p:[["The brightest in X-rays","It is the brightest galaxy cluster in X-ray light."],["A black hole’s note","Pressure waves from its black hole make a ‘sound’ 57 octaves below middle C."],["Mostly hot gas","Its glowing gas outweighs its galaxies."]],
l:"The Perseus Cluster, also called Abell 426, is a massive cluster of thousands of galaxies about 240 million light-years away in the constellation Perseus. It is the brightest galaxy cluster in the sky in X-rays, because it is filled with enormous amounts of hot gas, at tens of millions of degrees, that outweighs all its galaxies put together. At the center sits the giant galaxy NGC 1275, also known as Perseus A, an active galaxy whose supermassive black hole blasts out jets. The jets inflate huge bubbles in the surrounding gas and send out ripples, pressure waves like sound waves, which the Chandra X-ray Observatory detected in 2003. The ripples correspond to a note about 57 octaves below middle C, far too deep for humans to hear. In 2022 NASA turned the data into audible sound. The energy from the black hole heats the gas and keeps it from cooling and forming huge numbers of new stars. In 2023 the European Euclid telescope’s first images showed more than 1,000 galaxies of the Perseus Cluster and many more distant ones behind it.",
s:[["The black hole’s song","The pressure waves in Perseus have a period of about 10 million years. Chandra saw them as ripples in the brightness of the hot gas. In 2022 NASA scaled the waves up 57 and 58 octaves to make a haunting sound people can hear."],["Cooling flows","Astronomers once expected gas at the center of clusters to cool and pour into the central galaxy, making vast numbers of stars. Perseus showed that the black hole’s jets reheat the gas, solving this puzzle."],["NGC 1275","The central galaxy is surrounded by long filaments of glowing gas, held in place by magnetic fields. It is still swallowing smaller galaxies, and a separate galaxy is passing in front of it."],["Euclid’s first look","Euclid’s 2023 image of Perseus showed the cluster’s galaxies along with more than 100,000 galaxies far behind it, a preview of its map of billions of galaxies."]],
k:[["Other name","Abell 426"],["Distance","About 240 million light-years"],["Central galaxy","NGC 1275 (Perseus A)"],["Black hole ‘note’","About 57 octaves below middle C"],["Brightest X-ray cluster","Yes"]],
d:[["1971","X-rays from the cluster detected"],["2003","Chandra detects pressure waves"],["2022","NASA releases a sonification"],["2023","Euclid’s first images include Perseus"]],
s2:[["Sonification","Turning data into sound is called sonification. NASA has made sonifications of many space images, including the Perseus Cluster and M87."],["Hitomi","The Japanese Hitomi X-ray satellite measured the motion of the gas in Perseus in 2016, finding it surprisingly calm, before the spacecraft was lost."]],
g:[["Galaxy cluster","A large group of galaxies bound by gravity"],["Pressure wave","A wave of squeezed and stretched gas, like sound"],["Sonification","Turning data into sound"],["Cooling flow","Gas expected to cool and sink into a cluster’s center"]],
f:["The Perseus Cluster is bright in X-rays.","Its black hole makes pressure waves.","Its hot gas outweighs its galaxies."],
fun:"The ‘note’ from the Perseus black hole is the deepest known in the universe, with one wave taking about 10 million years."});

ADDART({id:"cosmicinflation",t:"Cosmic Inflation",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🎈",w:"Cosmic inflation",r:["bigbang","cmb","cosmicweb","fateuniverse","quantum","primordialbh"],
p:[["An incredible stretch","The early universe may have expanded enormously in a tiny fraction of a second."],["Solves puzzles","It explains why the universe is so smooth and flat."],["Seeds of galaxies","Quantum ripples may have grown into galaxies."]],
l:"Cosmic inflation is a theory that, in the first tiny fraction of a second after the Big Bang, the universe expanded faster than light, doubling in size many times over, by a factor of at least 10^26 in less than 10^-32 seconds. The idea was proposed by the American physicist Alan Guth in 1980 and developed by Andrei Linde, Andreas Albrecht, Paul Steinhardt and others. Inflation was invented to solve puzzles about the Big Bang. The universe looks almost the same in every direction, even in regions too far apart to have ever been in contact, and space appears almost perfectly flat; inflation explains both by stretching a tiny, smooth patch to enormous size. It also predicts that tiny quantum fluctuations, magnified by the expansion, became small differences in density that later grew into galaxies and galaxy clusters. Measurements of the cosmic microwave background by satellites such as COBE, WMAP and Planck match these predictions well. However, inflation has not been directly proven, and scientists still do not know what caused it.",
s:[["The puzzles","The horizon problem asks why distant parts of the sky have the same temperature to 1 part in 100,000. The flatness problem asks why space is so nearly flat. Inflation answers both: everything we see was once close together, and stretching flattened any curvature."],["Ripples into galaxies","In inflation, quantum jitters, normally far too small to matter, were stretched to cosmic size. The pattern of hot and cold spots in the cosmic microwave background matches the pattern predicted by inflation."],["Gravitational waves","Inflation should have produced faint gravitational waves that would leave a twisting pattern in the cosmic microwave background’s polarization. In 2014 the BICEP2 team claimed to see it, but the signal turned out to be caused by dust in our galaxy. The search continues."],["Many universes?","Some versions of inflation suggest it never stops everywhere at once, producing countless separate ‘bubble universes’. This idea, called eternal inflation or the multiverse, is controversial and may be impossible to test."]],
k:[["Proposed","Alan Guth, 1980"],["When","First tiny fraction of a second"],["Expansion","By a factor of at least 10^26"],["Evidence","Patterns in the cosmic microwave background"],["Status","Widely accepted but not proven"]],
d:[["1980","Alan Guth proposes inflation"],["1982","Linde, Albrecht and Steinhardt develop new inflation"],["1992","COBE measures tiny ripples in the microwave background"],["2014","BICEP2 claim later withdrawn"]],
s2:[["Faster than light?","Nothing can travel through space faster than light, but space itself can expand faster, so inflation does not break Einstein’s rules."],["Guth’s notebook","Alan Guth wrote ‘spectacular realization’ in his notebook on 7 December 1979 when he worked out the idea."]],
g:[["Inflation","A very rapid expansion of the early universe"],["Horizon problem","Why distant regions look the same"],["Flatness problem","Why space is so nearly flat"],["Quantum fluctuation","A tiny random change in energy"]],
f:["Inflation was proposed by Alan Guth.","Inflation happened in the first fraction of a second.","Inflation explains why the universe is so smooth."],
fun:"During inflation, a region smaller than a proton may have grown to the size of a grapefruit or larger in far less than a trillionth of a trillionth of a second."});

ADDART({id:"expandinguniverse",t:"The Expanding Universe",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"📏",w:"Expansion of the universe",r:["bigbang","hubble","darkmatter","distances","cmb","fateuniverse","dopplereffect"],
p:[["Galaxies are rushing apart","Space itself is stretching."],["Speeding up","The expansion has been accelerating for billions of years."],["The Hubble tension","Different methods disagree on how fast it expands."]],
l:"The universe is expanding: on large scales, galaxies are moving away from each other as space itself stretches, and the farther away a galaxy is, the faster it recedes. This relationship is known as Hubble’s law, or the Hubble–Lemaître law. The Belgian priest and physicist Georges Lemaître predicted it in 1927, and Edwin Hubble showed it with observations in 1929. The expansion rate is measured by the Hubble constant, the speed at which galaxies recede for each unit of distance, roughly 70 kilometers per second for every megaparsec (about 3.26 million light-years). In 1998 two teams measuring distant exploding stars discovered that the expansion is speeding up, driven by a mysterious ‘dark energy’; they won the 2011 Nobel Prize in Physics. Today there is a puzzle called the Hubble tension: measurements based on the early universe give about 67 to 68 km/s per megaparsec, while some measurements of nearby galaxies give about 73. Recent studies, including results from the DESI survey in 2025, also hint that dark energy may be weakening over time.",
s:[["How we know","Light from distant galaxies is stretched to longer, redder wavelengths by the expansion, a shift called redshift. Measuring both a galaxy’s redshift and its distance shows that more distant galaxies recede faster."],["Accelerating expansion","Astronomers expected gravity to slow the expansion. Instead, observations of type Ia supernovae in 1998 by teams led by Saul Perlmutter, Brian Schmidt and Adam Riess showed it has been speeding up for about the last 5 billion years."],["The Hubble tension","The Planck satellite’s map of the cosmic microwave background, combined with standard cosmology, predicts about 67.4. The SH0ES team, using Cepheid stars and supernovae, measures about 73. Another team using different stars found about 70 in 2025. Scientists debate whether the gap comes from errors or new physics."],["Evolving dark energy?","In March 2025 the Dark Energy Spectroscopic Instrument (DESI) reported that, combined with other data, its 3D map of galaxies fits better if dark energy has weakened over the last few billion years, at a confidence of about 2.8 to 4.2 sigma, below the 5-sigma level needed for a discovery."]],
k:[["Discovered","Lemaître (1927) and Hubble (1929)"],["Hubble constant","Roughly 67–73 km/s per megaparsec"],["Accelerating since","About 5 billion years ago"],["Acceleration discovered","1998"],["Driven by","Dark energy"]],
d:[["1927","Lemaître predicts an expanding universe"],["1929","Hubble publishes his law"],["1998","Accelerating expansion discovered"],["2011","Nobel Prize for the accelerating universe"],["2025","DESI hints that dark energy may evolve"]],
s2:[["Raisin bread","A common analogy: raisins in rising bread dough all move apart from each other as the dough expands, with no center."],["Not expanding locally","Gravity holds galaxies, the Solar System and people together, so they do not expand. Only the space between distant galaxy groups grows."]],
g:[["Hubble constant","The current rate of cosmic expansion"],["Redshift","The stretching of light to longer wavelengths"],["Dark energy","An unknown force speeding up the expansion"],["Megaparsec","About 3.26 million light-years"]],
f:["The universe is expanding.","The expansion is accelerating.","Different methods disagree on the expansion rate."],
fun:"Every second, the distance to a galaxy 3.26 million light-years away grows by about 70 kilometers."});

ADDART({id:"observableuniverse",t:"The Observable Universe",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🔭",w:"Observable universe",r:["expandinguniverse","bigbang","cmb","cosmicweb","earlygalaxies","distances"],
p:[["A cosmic bubble","It is the part of the universe we can, in principle, see."],["93 billion light-years across","It is far larger than 13.8 billion light-years because space has expanded."],["Maybe much bigger","The whole universe may be infinite."]],
l:"The observable universe is the region of space from which light has had time to reach Earth since the universe began, about 13.8 billion years ago. It forms a sphere centered on us, and because space has been expanding while the light traveled, its edge is now about 46 billion light-years away in every direction, making it about 93 billion light-years across. The most distant light we can see is the cosmic microwave background, released about 380,000 years after the Big Bang. The observable universe contains perhaps 2 trillion galaxies, according to one 2016 estimate, though later work suggests the number of detectable galaxies may be closer to hundreds of billions, along with roughly 10^22 to 10^24 stars. Every observer anywhere has their own observable universe centered on themselves, so we are not at the center of the universe, only of our own view. The entire universe is at least many times larger than the observable part and may be infinite.",
s:[["Why bigger than 13.8 billion light-years?","The oldest light has traveled for 13.8 billion years, but during that time the space it crossed kept stretching. The place where that light started is now much farther away, about 46 billion light-years."],["What lies beyond?","Beyond our horizon there is almost certainly more universe, probably looking much the same. Because the expansion is accelerating, light from very distant galaxies will never reach us."],["Counting stars and galaxies","Estimates of the number of galaxies come from deep images such as the Hubble Ultra Deep Field, which shows about 10,000 galaxies in a patch of sky smaller than a grain of sand held at arm’s length."],["A shrinking view","In the far future, galaxies beyond our Local Group will move out of sight as expansion carries them away faster than light can catch up. Astronomers in a trillion years might see only one big galaxy."]],
k:[["Age","About 13.8 billion years"],["Radius","About 46 billion light-years"],["Diameter","About 93 billion light-years"],["Galaxies","Hundreds of billions to about 2 trillion"],["Most distant light","The cosmic microwave background"]],
d:[["1965","Cosmic microwave background discovered"],["2004","Hubble Ultra Deep Field released"],["2016","Estimate of 2 trillion galaxies published"],["2022","Webb’s first deep field"]],
s2:[["More stars than sand?","There are probably more stars in the observable universe than grains of sand on all of Earth’s beaches, though both numbers are rough estimates."],["Your own bubble","Someone on a planet in a galaxy 10 billion light-years away would see a different observable universe, overlapping only partly with ours."]],
g:[["Observable universe","The part of the universe whose light could have reached us"],["Cosmic horizon","The edge of the observable universe"],["Cosmic microwave background","The oldest light, from 380,000 years after the Big Bang"],["Light-year","The distance light travels in a year"]],
f:["The observable universe is about 93 billion light-years across.","Every observer has their own observable universe.","The whole universe may be infinite."],
fun:"The observable universe contains so many stars that if each were a grain of rice, they would fill thousands of cubic kilometers."});

;
