ADDART({id:"dilophosaurus",t:"Dilophosaurus",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦖",w:"Dilophosaurus",r:["allosaurus","trex","dino","fossils","paleontology","carnotaurus"],
p:[["Two crests","It had a pair of thin bony crests on its head."],["Early Jurassic","It lived about 186 million years ago."],["Not like the movie","It did not spit venom or have a neck frill."]],
l:"Dilophosaurus was a meat-eating dinosaur that lived in what is now Arizona, in the United States, about 186 million years ago, early in the Jurassic Period. About 7 meters long and weighing around 400 kilograms, it was one of the largest land predators of its time. Its name means ‘two-crested lizard’, after the pair of thin, rounded bony crests that ran along the top of its skull, probably used for display rather than fighting. Dilophosaurus had a long neck and tail, strong arms with grasping claws, and a notch in its upper jaw between the front and side teeth. It is famous from the 1993 film Jurassic Park, where it was shown as a small animal with a frill around its neck that spat venom, but there is no fossil evidence for either feature, and the real animal was much bigger. A detailed 2020 study of all known skeletons showed that its skull was sturdy and that it was a powerful hunter, not the weak-jawed scavenger some scientists once suggested.",
s:[["Discovery","The first skeletons were found in 1940 on Navajo Nation land in northern Arizona, guided by Jesse Williams, a Navajo man who had come across the bones. At first the animal was thought to be a species of Megalosaurus. When a better skeleton showed its crests, it was renamed Dilophosaurus in 1970."],["The crests","The crests were made of thin bone and were too fragile to use as weapons. Like the crests and frills of many living animals, they may have helped Dilophosaurus recognize its own kind or attract mates."],["A powerful hunter","Earlier reconstructions were based on skeletons that had been damaged and pieced together with plaster. A 2020 study by Adam Marsh and Timothy Rowe found that the skull had air-filled spaces that made it light but strong, and that the jaw muscles were powerful."],["Healed injuries","One Dilophosaurus skeleton shows eight injuries, including broken bones that healed over time, showing that it survived for months or years after being hurt."]],
k:[["Lived","About 186 million years ago (Early Jurassic)"],["Where","Arizona, United States"],["Length","About 7 meters"],["Weight","About 400 kilograms"],["Diet","Meat"],["Name means","Two-crested lizard"]],
d:[["1940","First skeletons found in Arizona"],["1954","Described as a species of Megalosaurus"],["1970","Named Dilophosaurus"],["1993","Appears in Jurassic Park"],["2020","Major study of all known skeletons"]],
s2:[["State dinosaur","Dilophosaurus was named the official state dinosaur of Connecticut, where footprints of a similar dinosaur were found."],["Footprints","Fossil tracks in Arizona and elsewhere show where animals like Dilophosaurus walked, sometimes in groups."]],
g:[["Jurassic","The period from about 201 to 145 million years ago"],["Crest","A ridge on the head"],["Theropod","A two-legged, mostly meat-eating dinosaur"],["Reconstruction","A model or drawing of how an extinct animal looked"]],
f:["Dilophosaurus had two crests on its head.","Dilophosaurus lived in the Early Jurassic.","There is no evidence it spat venom."],
fun:"The real Dilophosaurus was about the length of a car and a half, far bigger than the one in Jurassic Park."});

ADDART({id:"deinonychus",t:"Deinonychus",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦅",w:"Deinonychus",r:["velociraptor","microraptor","dinobirds","archaeopteryx","fossils","paleontology"],
p:[["Terrible claw","Its name refers to a large curved claw on each foot."],["Changed our view","It showed that some dinosaurs were active and bird-like."],["Model for movie raptors","Jurassic Park’s ‘Velociraptors’ were really based on it."]],
l:"Deinonychus was a feathered meat-eating dinosaur that lived in what is now the western United States about 115 to 108 million years ago, during the Early Cretaceous Period. It was about 3.4 meters long and weighed around 70 kilograms, roughly the size of a large wolf. Its name means ‘terrible claw’, after the enlarged, sickle-shaped claw on the second toe of each foot, which it held off the ground while walking. Deinonychus was a dromaeosaur, a close relative of Velociraptor, and an important animal in the history of science. When the American paleontologist John Ostrom described it in 1969, he argued that such an agile, lightly built hunter must have been active and warm-blooded, more like a bird than a sluggish reptile. His work started the ‘Dinosaur Renaissance’, which changed how scientists and the public think about dinosaurs and revived the idea that birds are living dinosaurs. The fast, clever ‘raptors’ in the film Jurassic Park were based largely on Deinonychus.",
s:[["The sickle claw","The claw on each foot was about 12 centimeters long along its curve. Scientists once thought it was used to slash prey open, but studies suggest it was better at gripping and pinning prey down, much as hawks and eagles use their talons today."],["Pack hunter?","Several Deinonychus skeletons were found with the remains of Tenontosaurus, a large plant-eater, leading to the idea that they hunted in packs. Others think they may have gathered at a carcass and fought over it, as some birds and reptiles do."],["Feathers","No feather impressions have been found with Deinonychus, but its close relatives, including Velociraptor and Microraptor, had feathers, so scientists are confident Deinonychus did too."],["Eggs","A 2015 study of a Deinonychus egg found alongside a skeleton suggested that it may have sat on its eggs to keep them warm, like a bird."]],
k:[["Lived","About 115–108 million years ago"],["Where","Western United States"],["Length","About 3.4 meters"],["Weight","About 70 kilograms"],["Group","Dromaeosaur"],["Name means","Terrible claw"]],
d:[["1964","John Ostrom’s team finds fossils in Montana"],["1969","Deinonychus described"],["1993","Inspires the raptors of Jurassic Park"],["2015","Study of a Deinonychus egg"]],
s2:[["Velociraptor’s cousin","The real Velociraptor was much smaller, about the size of a turkey. The film used the name Velociraptor but gave the animals Deinonychus’s size."],["Big brain","Like other dromaeosaurs, Deinonychus had a relatively large brain for a dinosaur of its size."]],
g:[["Dromaeosaur","A family of feathered, sickle-clawed dinosaurs"],["Dinosaur Renaissance","A change in thinking that dinosaurs were active and bird-like"],["Warm-blooded","Making its own body heat"],["Talon","A sharp hooked claw"]],
f:["Deinonychus had a large sickle claw on each foot.","John Ostrom described Deinonychus.","Deinonychus was related to Velociraptor."],
fun:"The fearsome ‘Velociraptors’ in Jurassic Park are really closer in size to Deinonychus."});

ADDART({id:"plateosaurus",t:"Plateosaurus",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦕",w:"Plateosaurus",r:["brachiosaurus","diplodocus","argentinosaurus","dino","fossils","pangaea"],
p:[["An early giant","One of the first big plant-eating dinosaurs."],["Triassic Europe","It lived about 214 to 204 million years ago."],["Hundreds of skeletons","It is one of the best-known dinosaurs."]],
l:"Plateosaurus was one of the earliest large dinosaurs, a plant-eater that lived across central and northern Europe about 214 to 204 million years ago, in the Late Triassic Period, when the continents were joined in the supercontinent Pangaea. It grew to about 5 to 10 meters long and weighed from about 600 kilograms to several tonnes. Plateosaurus had a small head, a long neck, a long tail and strong hands with large thumb claws. Unlike its gigantic relatives such as Brachiosaurus and Argentinosaurus, which walked on four legs, Plateosaurus walked on its two hind legs, using its hands for grasping plants and perhaps for defense. It belongs to a group often called prosauropods, early relatives of the giant long-necked sauropods. More than 100 skeletons have been found, many in Germany, Switzerland and France, making it one of the best-understood dinosaurs. Many died stuck in mud, which helped preserve them.",
s:[["Discovery","Plateosaurus was named in 1837 by the German naturalist Hermann von Meyer, making it one of the first dinosaurs ever named, even before the word ‘dinosaur’ was coined in 1842. Large bone beds were later found at Trossingen in Germany and Frick in Switzerland."],["Two legs, not four","Earlier museum displays showed Plateosaurus on all fours. Studies of its hands, which could not turn palm-down to walk, showed it moved on two legs."],["Growing up","Studies of growth rings in its bones show that Plateosaurus individuals of the same age could differ greatly in size, perhaps depending on how much food they found, more like reptiles than birds."],["Stuck in mud","At Trossingen and Frick, many Plateosaurus seem to have become trapped in soft mud while crossing wet ground. Their heavy bodies sank, and the mud preserved their skeletons."]],
k:[["Lived","About 214–204 million years ago (Late Triassic)"],["Where","Central and northern Europe"],["Length","About 5–10 meters"],["Diet","Plants"],["Walked on","Two legs"],["Named","1837"]],
d:[["1834","First bones found near Nuremberg, Germany"],["1837","Plateosaurus named"],["1911–1932","Many skeletons dug up at Trossingen"],["1961","Major finds at Frick, Switzerland"]],
s2:[["Greenland","Fossils of Plateosaurus have also been found in Greenland, which was much closer to Europe in the Triassic."],["Museum favorite","Because so many skeletons exist, Plateosaurus is displayed in museums across Europe."]],
g:[["Triassic","The period from about 252 to 201 million years ago"],["Prosauropod","An early relative of the long-necked sauropods"],["Pangaea","The supercontinent that joined all the land"],["Bone bed","A layer of rock with many fossil bones"]],
f:["Plateosaurus walked on two legs.","Plateosaurus lived in the Triassic.","Plateosaurus fossils are common in Europe."],
fun:"Plateosaurus was named five years before the word ‘dinosaur’ even existed."});

ADDART({id:"helicoprion",t:"Helicoprion",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🌀",w:"Helicoprion",r:["megalodon","shark","dunkleosteus","massext","fossils","fishes"],
p:[["The buzz-saw fish","Its teeth grew in a spiral called a tooth whorl."],["A century-old puzzle","Scientists argued for 100 years about where the whorl went."],["Related to ratfish","It was not a true shark."]],
l:"Helicoprion was a large fish that lived in the oceans about 290 to 270 million years ago, during the Permian Period, before the dinosaurs. It is famous for one of the strangest features of any animal: its teeth grew in a spiral, called a tooth whorl, shaped like a circular saw blade or a curled-up snail shell. New teeth formed at one end and older, smaller teeth were pushed toward the center of the spiral instead of falling out. For more than a century, fossils of the tooth whorl were found without any other part of the body, and scientists guessed it belonged on the snout, the tail, the back or even curled outside the mouth. In 2013, CT scans of a fossil from Idaho, in the United States, showed that the whorl sat inside the lower jaw, filling the space at the back of the mouth. Helicoprion was a relative of today’s ratfish, or chimaeras, rather than a true shark. It may have reached 5 to 8 meters long and probably ate soft prey such as squid-like animals.",
s:[["The tooth whorl","The largest whorls had more than 100 teeth and measured up to about 60 centimeters across. As the jaw closed, the teeth may have rolled backward, slicing prey and pulling it into the throat."],["Solving the mystery","The first Helicoprion fossils were described in 1899 by the Russian geologist Alexander Karpinsky, who placed the whorl on the snout. The 2013 study led by Leif Tapanila used CT scans of a specimen from Idaho that preserved parts of the skull and jaw cartilage."],["Where it lived","Helicoprion fossils have been found in Russia, the United States, Canada, Australia, Japan and other places, showing it swam in seas around the world."],["Chimaeras","Chimaeras, also called ratfish or ghost sharks, are living relatives of sharks and rays that mostly live in deep water. Helicoprion belonged to an extinct group called eugeneodonts, related to them."]],
k:[["Lived","About 290–270 million years ago (Permian)"],["Length","About 5–8 meters (estimates vary)"],["Famous for","Spiral tooth whorl"],["Whorl size","Up to about 60 cm across"],["Relatives","Chimaeras (ratfish)"],["Mystery solved","2013, CT scans"]],
d:[["1899","Helicoprion described by Alexander Karpinsky"],["1950","Idaho specimen collected"],["2013","CT scans show the whorl was in the lower jaw"]],
s2:[["State fossil","Helicoprion is the official state fossil of Idaho, where many whorls have been found in phosphate mines."],["Many guesses","Old reconstructions showed the whorl as a tail spine, a fin or a tooth curled out of the snout like an elephant’s trunk."]],
g:[["Tooth whorl","A spiral of teeth"],["Permian","The period from about 299 to 252 million years ago"],["Chimaera","A deep-sea relative of sharks"],["CT scan","A detailed X-ray image in slices"]],
f:["Helicoprion had a spiral of teeth.","The tooth whorl was in the lower jaw.","Helicoprion lived before the dinosaurs."],
fun:"Scientists spent more than 100 years arguing about where Helicoprion’s buzz-saw teeth belonged."});

ADDART({id:"arthropleura",t:"Arthropleura",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐛",w:"Arthropleura",r:["meganeura","trilobites","eurypterids","fossils","insect","fossilfuels"],
p:[["Giant millipede","The largest known land invertebrate ever."],["As long as a car","Some reached about 2.6 meters."],["Coal forests","It lived in swampy forests over 300 million years ago."]],
l:"Arthropleura was a giant millipede-like arthropod that lived about 346 to 290 million years ago, during the Carboniferous and early Permian periods. The largest individuals reached about 2.6 meters long and perhaps 50 kilograms, making Arthropleura the biggest invertebrate, an animal without a backbone, ever known to have lived on land. It had a long, flat body made of many segments covered in tough plates, with dozens of legs. Arthropleura lived in the swampy forests of giant club mosses and tree ferns whose remains later formed much of the world’s coal. Its fossils, and its tracks, which can be up to 50 centimeters wide, have been found in Europe and North America, which were then near the equator. For a long time no complete head had been found, but in 2024 scientists described young Arthropleura fossils from France with heads preserved, showing features of both millipedes and centipedes. Arthropleura was probably a plant-eater or ate rotting plants, though it may have eaten small animals too.",
s:[["How it grew so big","The Carboniferous atmosphere may have had more oxygen than today, which some scientists think allowed arthropods such as Arthropleura and the giant dragonfly Meganeura to grow larger. Another reason may be that there were few large land predators."],["Fossil finds","In 2018, a large section of an Arthropleura body was found in a fallen boulder on a beach in Northumberland, England, and described in 2021. It was about 76 centimeters long and probably came from an animal about 2.6 meters long."],["The head revealed","In 2024, researchers used CT scans to study young Arthropleura fossils from Montceau-les-Mines in France, revealing eyes on stalks and mouthparts, and confirming that Arthropleura was a giant millipede."],["Tracks","Wide trackways called Diplichnites show where Arthropleura walked across damp ground. Some trackways have been found in Scotland, Nova Scotia and the United States."]],
k:[["Lived","About 346–290 million years ago"],["Length","Up to about 2.6 meters"],["Group","Millipedes (myriapods)"],["Habitat","Swampy coal forests"],["Diet","Probably plants"],["Head described","2024"]],
d:[["1854","Arthropleura named"],["2021","Giant body section from England described"],["2024","Fossils with heads described"]],
s2:[["Coal","The forests where Arthropleura lived became coal, which people began burning in large amounts during the Industrial Revolution."],["Many legs","Despite its name, no millipede has a thousand legs, though one living species has more than 1,300."]],
g:[["Invertebrate","An animal without a backbone"],["Carboniferous","The period from about 359 to 299 million years ago"],["Myriapod","Millipedes, centipedes and their relatives"],["Trackway","A line of fossil footprints"]],
f:["Arthropleura was a giant millipede.","It lived in Carboniferous coal forests.","It was the largest land invertebrate known."],
fun:"Arthropleura was longer than most people are tall and as wide as a car tire."});

ADDART({id:"paraceratherium",t:"Paraceratherium",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦏",w:"Paraceratherium",r:["rhino","woollyrhino","mammoth","iceagemammals","elephant","fossils","andrewsarchus"],
p:[["Biggest land mammal","Probably the largest land mammal that ever lived."],["A hornless rhino","It was a relative of rhinoceroses."],["Treetop browser","Its head reached about 5 meters up."]],
l:"Paraceratherium was a gigantic hornless relative of the rhinoceros that lived in Asia about 34 to 23 million years ago, during the Oligocene Epoch. It is generally thought to be the largest land mammal ever known, standing about 4.8 meters tall at the shoulder, with its head held up to about 7 meters high, measuring around 7 to 8 meters long and weighing about 15 to 20 tonnes, heavier than three or four African elephants. It had long, pillar-like legs, a long neck and a large head with a flexible upper lip, perhaps like a short trunk, which it used to pull leaves and twigs from tall trees, much as giraffes do today. Fossils have been found across a wide area of Asia, from Türkiye and the Balkans to Mongolia, China and Pakistan. A 2021 study describing a new species from Gansu, China, suggested that these giants spread between Central and South Asia as the climate changed. Paraceratherium died out about 23 million years ago, perhaps as forests gave way to drier grasslands.",
s:[["Names","Fossils of these giant rhinos were given several names, including Indricotherium and Baluchitherium, by scientists working in different countries. Many experts now group them under Paraceratherium, though the classification is still debated."],["Size estimates","Because no complete skeleton has been found, estimates of its size are based on parts of different animals. Older estimates of over 30 tonnes are now considered too high. Some mammoths and elephants may have rivaled it in weight."],["Life of a giant","Its huge size would have protected adults from predators. Females were probably pregnant for about two years, like elephants, and gave birth to one calf at a time."],["Discovery","The first fossils were found in Pakistan in the early 1900s. The American Museum of Natural History’s expeditions to Mongolia in the 1920s, led by Roy Chapman Andrews, found well-preserved skulls."]],
k:[["Lived","About 34–23 million years ago (Oligocene)"],["Where","Asia"],["Shoulder height","About 4.8 meters"],["Weight","About 15–20 tonnes"],["Relatives","Rhinoceroses"],["Diet","Leaves and twigs from trees"]],
d:[["1911","Paraceratherium named from fossils in Pakistan"],["1922","Skull found in Mongolia by Andrews’s expedition"],["2021","New species from Gansu, China, described"]],
s2:[["No horns","Unlike modern rhinos, Paraceratherium had no horns on its nose."],["Neighbors","It shared its world with early cats, hyenas and the giant pig-like entelodonts."]],
g:[["Oligocene","The epoch from about 34 to 23 million years ago"],["Browser","An animal that eats leaves from trees and bushes"],["Megafauna","Very large animals"],["Classification","Grouping living things by their relationships"]],
f:["Paraceratherium was a relative of rhinos.","It was probably the largest land mammal.","It lived in Asia."],
fun:"A person could have walked under Paraceratherium’s belly without ducking."});

ADDART({id:"autumncolors",t:"Why Leaves Change Color",c:"Plants",i:"🍂",w:"Autumn leaf color",r:["leaves","photo","trees","maples","seasons","oaks"],
p:[["Green fades","As days shorten, trees stop making green chlorophyll."],["Hidden yellows","Yellow and orange pigments were there all along."],["New reds","Red and purple pigments are made fresh in autumn."]],
l:"Every autumn, the leaves of many trees in temperate parts of the world turn yellow, orange, red and purple before falling. During spring and summer, leaves are green because they are packed with chlorophyll, the pigment plants use to capture sunlight for photosynthesis. As days get shorter and cooler in autumn, deciduous trees prepare for winter by breaking down their chlorophyll and pulling valuable nutrients such as nitrogen back into their branches and roots. As the green fades, yellow and orange pigments called carotenoids, which were in the leaves all along but hidden by the chlorophyll, become visible. Some trees, such as many maples, also make new red and purple pigments called anthocyanins in autumn, which may protect the leaves from sunlight and cold while nutrients are being reclaimed. Finally, a layer of cells forms where each leaf stalk joins the twig, cutting the leaf off so it drops. The brightest displays occur where sunny days and cool nights are common, such as in eastern North America, Japan, Korea and northern Europe.",
s:[["The pigments","Chlorophyll is green; carotenoids, also found in carrots, are yellow and orange; anthocyanins, also found in red grapes and blueberries, are red and purple. Brown comes from tannins left behind after other pigments break down."],["Why trees drop leaves","Broad, thin leaves lose water and can be damaged by freezing. By dropping them, deciduous trees save water and energy through the winter, then grow new leaves in spring. Evergreen trees, such as pines, have tough, waxy needles that survive the cold."],["Weather and color","Warm, sunny days and cool, but not freezing, nights produce the most red, because more sugars are trapped in the leaves to make anthocyanins. A dry summer, early frost or warm, cloudy autumn can make colors duller."],["Leaf peeping","Autumn color draws millions of visitors to places such as New England in the United States, Kyoto in Japan and the Canadian province of Quebec. In Japan, viewing autumn leaves is called momijigari."]],
k:[["Green pigment","Chlorophyll"],["Yellow and orange","Carotenoids"],["Red and purple","Anthocyanins"],["Brown","Tannins"],["Trees that change","Deciduous trees"],["Trigger","Shorter days and cooler nights"]],
s2:[["Tropics","In the tropics, where days stay about the same length all year, most trees stay green, though some drop their leaves in the dry season."],["Ginkgo gold","Ginkgo trees turn a brilliant butter-yellow and often drop all their leaves within a day or two."]],
g:[["Chlorophyll","The green pigment that captures sunlight"],["Deciduous","Losing its leaves every year"],["Carotenoid","A yellow or orange pigment"],["Anthocyanin","A red or purple pigment"]],
f:["Chlorophyll makes leaves green.","Yellow pigments are hidden in leaves all summer.","Red pigments are made in autumn."],
fun:"The yellow in an autumn leaf is the same kind of pigment that makes carrots orange."});

ADDART({id:"parasiticplants",t:"Parasitic Plants",c:"Plants",i:"🪢",w:"Parasitic plant",r:["rafflesia","symbiosis","fungi","photo","vines","roots"],
p:[["Stealing food","They take water and nutrients from other plants."],["Mistletoe and dodder","Two of the best-known examples."],["The biggest flower","Rafflesia is a parasite with no leaves at all."]],
l:"Parasitic plants get some or all of their water and food by tapping into other plants, called hosts. They do this with a special organ called a haustorium, which grows into the host’s stem or roots and connects to its plumbing. There are about 4,500 species of parasitic plants, found on every continent except Antarctica. Some, such as mistletoe, are partial parasites: they have green leaves and make some of their own food by photosynthesis but take water and minerals from their host. Others are complete parasites with no chlorophyll at all, getting everything from their host. Dodder, for example, is a leafless, orange or yellow vine that wraps around other plants, and can even ‘smell’ chemicals given off by its favorite hosts. Rafflesia, which produces the world’s largest single flower, lives entirely hidden inside a jungle vine, appearing only when it blooms. Some parasitic plants, such as witchweed in Africa and Asia, are serious crop pests, while mistletoes provide food for many birds and insects.",
s:[["Mistletoe","Mistletoes grow on tree branches. Birds eat their sticky berries and wipe or pass the seeds onto other branches, where they sprout. In Europe and North America, mistletoe is a traditional Christmas decoration. The world’s largest mistletoe, the Western Australian Christmas tree, grows as a tree and taps into the roots of nearby plants."],["Dodder","Dodder seedlings must find a host within days or die. Experiments in 2006 showed that dodder seedlings grow toward the smell of tomato plants, choosing them over wheat."],["Rafflesia","Rafflesia arnoldii, found in the rainforests of Sumatra and Borneo, produces a flower up to about 1 meter across that smells of rotting meat, attracting flies to pollinate it."],["Crop pests","Witchweed (Striga) attacks maize, sorghum, millet and rice, causing large crop losses for millions of farmers in Africa. Broomrapes attack crops such as sunflowers, tomatoes and legumes."]],
k:[["Number of species","About 4,500"],["Connecting organ","Haustorium"],["Partial parasites","Mistletoe"],["Complete parasites","Dodder, Rafflesia"],["Crop pest","Witchweed (Striga)"],["Largest flower","Rafflesia arnoldii"]],
s2:[["Ghost plants","Some pale, leafless plants, such as the ‘ghost pipe’, get their food from fungi connected to tree roots, an indirect way of feeding on trees."],["Sharing genes","Parasitic plants sometimes pick up genes from their hosts through their close connections."]],
g:[["Parasite","A living thing that feeds on another"],["Host","The living thing a parasite feeds on"],["Haustorium","The organ a parasitic plant uses to tap its host"],["Hemiparasite","A partial parasite that still makes some food"]],
f:["Parasitic plants take food or water from other plants.","Mistletoe is a partial parasite.","Dodder has no leaves."],
fun:"A dodder seedling can sniff out a tomato plant and grow toward it."});

ADDART({id:"seedvaults",t:"Seed Banks and the Svalbard Vault",c:"Plants",i:"🫙",w:"Svalbard Global Seed Vault",r:["seeds","urbanfarm","biodivloss","conservation","climchange","rice"],
p:[["Saving crops","Seed banks store seeds to protect the world’s crops."],["Doomsday vault","The Svalbard vault is buried in an Arctic mountain."],["Over 1.4 million samples","Its collection passed this milestone in 2026."]],
l:"A seed bank, or genebank, is a place where seeds are dried, sealed and stored, often frozen, so that plant varieties can be saved for the future. There are about 1,700 genebanks around the world. They protect the genetic diversity of crops, the thousands of local varieties of rice, wheat, maize, beans and other plants that farmers have developed over thousands of years, which may hold traits such as resistance to drought, heat or disease. The most famous is the Svalbard Global Seed Vault, opened in 2008 deep inside a mountain on the Norwegian island of Spitsbergen, about 1,300 kilometers from the North Pole. It acts as a backup for genebanks around the world, which send duplicate copies of their seeds to be stored at about −18 °C. Each country or institution keeps ownership of its seeds. In June 2026 the vault’s collection passed 1.4 million seed samples. The vault is run by Norway together with the Crop Trust and the Nordic Genetic Resource Center.",
s:[["Why seed banks matter","Many traditional crop varieties have been lost as farmers switched to a few high-yielding types. Genebanks keep the variety that plant breeders need to develop crops for changing climates and new diseases."],["Inside the vault","A tunnel leads about 130 meters into the mountain to three chambers. Even if the power failed, the surrounding permafrost would keep the seeds frozen. The vault was designed to hold up to 4.5 million samples."],["The first withdrawal","In 2015, the International Center for Agricultural Research in the Dry Areas (ICARDA), which had moved from Aleppo during the war in Syria, withdrew seeds from Svalbard to rebuild its collection. It later returned new copies."],["Other seed banks","The Millennium Seed Bank at Kew’s Wakehurst gardens in England focuses on wild plants and has stored seeds of tens of thousands of species. National genebanks exist in India, China, the United States, Brazil, Ethiopia and many other countries."]],
k:[["Genebanks worldwide","About 1,700"],["Svalbard vault opened","26 February 2008"],["Location","Spitsbergen, Svalbard, Norway"],["Storage temperature","About −18 °C"],["Samples stored","Over 1.4 million (June 2026)"],["Capacity","About 4.5 million samples"]],
d:[["2008","Svalbard Global Seed Vault opens"],["2015","First withdrawal, by ICARDA"],["2017","Meltwater enters the entrance tunnel; repairs follow"],["2026","Collection passes 1.4 million samples"]],
s2:[["Not just crops","Seed banks also save wild plants, such as relatives of crops and endangered species, for conservation and research."],["Ancient seeds","In 2012, Russian scientists grew a plant from fruit tissue about 32,000 years old that had been buried in Siberian permafrost by a ground squirrel."]],
g:[["Seed bank","A place where seeds are stored for the future"],["Genebank","A collection of seeds or other material to preserve plant diversity"],["Genetic diversity","Variety in the genes of a species"],["Permafrost","Ground that stays frozen all year"]],
f:["The Svalbard vault opened in 2008.","Seeds are stored at about −18 °C.","Countries keep ownership of their seeds."],
fun:"The Svalbard vault’s entrance is decorated with an artwork that glows in the polar night."});

ADDART({id:"cloudforests",t:"Cloud Forests",c:"Plants",i:"🌫️",w:"Cloud forest",r:["rainforests","trees","orchids","ferns","wcycle","mountainform","fog"],
p:[["Forests in the clouds","Tropical mountain forests often wrapped in mist."],["Catching water","Trees comb water from the clouds."],["Full of life","Home to orchids, mosses and rare animals."]],
l:"A cloud forest is a tropical or subtropical mountain forest that is frequently covered in low clouds or mist. Cloud forests usually grow between about 1,000 and 3,500 meters above sea level, where moist air rising from the lowlands cools and condenses into clouds that drift through the trees. The trees are often shorter and more twisted than in lowland rainforests, and their branches are thickly covered in mosses, ferns, lichens, orchids and bromeliads, plants called epiphytes that grow on other plants without harming them. Cloud forests cover only a small fraction of the world’s land, found in places such as the Andes, Central America, East Africa, Southeast Asia and Hawaii, but they are extraordinarily rich in species, many found nowhere else. They are also important water sources: the trees and epiphytes catch tiny cloud droplets, which drip to the ground and feed streams and rivers that supply cities and farms below. Cloud forests are threatened by clearing for farming and by climate change, which can raise the height of the clouds.",
s:[["Catching clouds","When fog blows through a cloud forest, droplets collect on leaves, mosses and needles and drip down, a process called fog drip or cloud stripping. This can add a large share of extra water beyond normal rainfall."],["Monteverde","The Monteverde Cloud Forest in Costa Rica is one of the most famous, home to the resplendent quetzal and hundreds of orchid species. It was also home to the golden toad, last seen in 1989 and now considered extinct."],["Famous cloud forests","Others include the Yungas of Bolivia and Peru, Mount Kinabalu in Borneo, the Rwenzori Mountains of Uganda and Congo, and the Cerro Punta forests of Panama. Mountain gorillas live in cloud forests in Rwanda, Uganda and Congo."],["Rising clouds","As the climate warms, the level where clouds form can move higher up mountains, leaving lower cloud forests drier. Species that need constant mist may have nowhere to go."]],
k:[["Typical height","About 1,000–3,500 m above sea level"],["Climate","Cool, very humid, often misty"],["Key plants","Mosses, ferns, orchids, bromeliads"],["Water role","Catch fog and feed rivers"],["Famous example","Monteverde, Costa Rica"],["Threats","Clearing and climate change"]],
s2:[["Elfin forest","At the highest levels, cloud forest trees can be stunted and gnarled, forming what is called elfin forest."],["Epiphyte gardens","A single large cloud forest tree can carry hundreds of epiphyte plants on its branches."]],
g:[["Cloud forest","A misty tropical mountain forest"],["Epiphyte","A plant that grows on another plant"],["Bromeliad","A plant family including pineapples, many growing on trees"],["Fog drip","Water collected from fog by plants"]],
f:["Cloud forests grow on tropical mountains.","Cloud forests catch water from fog.","Epiphytes grow on trees in cloud forests."],
fun:"In some cloud forests, trees can collect more water from fog than falls as rain."});

ADDART({id:"alpineplants",t:"Alpine Plants",c:"Plants",i:"🏔️",w:"Alpine plant",r:["alps","himal","tundra","flower","adaptation","glaciers"],
p:[["Living above the trees","Alpine plants grow high on mountains above the tree line."],["Small and tough","They hug the ground to escape wind and cold."],["Short summers","They must grow and flower quickly."]],
l:"Alpine plants grow high on mountains, above the tree line, the height beyond which it is too cold and windy for trees to survive. They live in harsh conditions: freezing nights, strong winds, intense sunlight, thin soil and a growing season that may last only a few weeks. To cope, many alpine plants are small and grow in low cushions or mats close to the ground, where the air is warmer and calmer. Many have hairy or waxy leaves that protect against cold, drying winds and ultraviolet light, and deep roots that anchor them among rocks and reach water. Because the summer is so short, many are perennials that live for years, storing food in their roots and flowering quickly when the snow melts. Famous alpine plants include the edelweiss of the European Alps, gentians with deep blue flowers, saxifrages that grow in rock cracks, and the Himalayan blue poppy. Alpine zones exist on mountains around the world, from the Andes and Rockies to the mountains of Africa and New Zealand.",
s:[["The tree line","The tree line is lower near the poles and higher near the equator. In the Alps it is around 2,000 meters, while in the tropical Andes and Himalayas it can be about 4,000 meters or more."],["Clever shapes","Cushion plants such as moss campion can be several degrees warmer inside than the surrounding air. In the mountains of East Africa, giant groundsels and lobelias grow up to several meters tall, folding their leaves at night like a blanket."],["The highest plants","In 2024, scientists reported flowering plants growing at more than 6,000 meters on mountains in the Himalayas, among the highest known."],["Climbing higher","As the climate warms, many alpine plants are moving up mountains. Studies of European summits show more plant species growing near the top than a century ago, but species already at the highest levels have nowhere left to go."]],
k:[["Where","Above the tree line on mountains"],["Challenges","Cold, wind, strong sunlight, short summer"],["Typical shapes","Cushions, mats, rosettes"],["Famous examples","Edelweiss, gentian, saxifrage, blue poppy"],["Tree line in the Alps","About 2,000 meters"],["Tropical giants","Giant groundsels and lobelias"]],
s2:[["Rock gardens","Gardeners grow alpine plants in rock gardens, imitating their stony mountain homes."],["Edelweiss","Edelweiss, with its white woolly star-shaped flowers, is a national symbol of Switzerland and Austria."]],
g:[["Tree line","The height above which trees cannot grow"],["Perennial","A plant that lives for more than two years"],["Cushion plant","A plant that grows in a low, dense mound"],["Rosette","A circle of leaves close to the ground"]],
f:["Alpine plants grow above the tree line.","Many alpine plants grow close to the ground.","Edelweiss is an alpine plant."],
fun:"The inside of a cushion plant can be warmer than the air around it, like a tiny greenhouse."});

ADDART({id:"plantgrowth",t:"How Plants Grow",c:"Plants",i:"🌱",w:"Plant development",r:["seeds","roots","leaves","photo","plantsenses","cell"],
p:[["From seed to plant","Germination wakes a seed into growth."],["Growing toward light","Plants bend toward light and away from gravity."],["Growth tips","New growth happens at tips called meristems."]],
l:"Plants grow throughout their lives, unlike most animals, which stop growing when they become adults. A plant begins as a seed, which contains a tiny embryo and a store of food. When the seed absorbs water and has the right temperature and oxygen, it germinates: a root grows down first, then a shoot pushes up toward the light. Most growth happens in areas called meristems at the tips of roots and shoots, where cells divide rapidly. Behind the tips, cells stretch and take on special jobs, becoming parts of leaves, stems, roots or flowers. In trees and other woody plants, a thin layer of meristem under the bark adds new wood each year, making the trunk thicker. Plant growth is controlled by hormones such as auxin, which makes shoots bend toward light, gibberellins, which make stems grow taller, and others that control flowering, ripening and leaf fall. Plants also respond to gravity, touch and the length of day and night, turning their growth in the directions that help them survive.",
s:[["Tropisms","A tropism is growth toward or away from something. Phototropism is bending toward light; gravitropism makes roots grow down and shoots up; thigmotropism makes climbing plants curl around supports they touch."],["Auxin","Charles Darwin and his son Francis showed in 1880 that the tip of a grass seedling senses light. Later scientists found that a hormone, auxin, moves to the shaded side of a shoot, making the cells there grow longer so the shoot bends toward the light."],["Day length","Many plants flower according to the length of night. Short-day plants, such as chrysanthemums, flower as nights lengthen in autumn; long-day plants, such as spinach, flower in summer."],["Fastest and slowest","Some bamboos can grow nearly a meter in a day. Some bristlecone pines grow only a fraction of a millimeter thicker each year but live for thousands of years."]],
k:[["Starts as","A seed with an embryo"],["Growth regions","Meristems at the tips"],["Key hormones","Auxin, gibberellins, cytokinins, ethylene"],["Bending to light","Phototropism"],["Responding to gravity","Gravitropism"],["Fast grower","Bamboo, up to about 90 cm a day"]],
d:[["1880","Charles and Francis Darwin study bending seedlings"],["1926","Frits Went isolates auxin"],["1930s","Gibberellins discovered in Japan"]],
s2:[["Ripening","Ethylene gas makes fruit ripen. One ripe banana in a bag can speed the ripening of others nearby."],["Rice and gibberellins","Gibberellins were discovered by Japanese scientists studying ‘foolish seedling’ disease, which made rice plants grow very tall and weak."]],
g:[["Germination","When a seed begins to grow"],["Meristem","A plant growth region where cells divide"],["Tropism","Growth toward or away from something"],["Auxin","A plant hormone that controls bending and growth"]],
f:["Plants grow at meristems.","Shoots bend toward light.","Auxin is a plant hormone."],
fun:"A ripe banana gives off a gas that makes nearby fruit ripen faster."});

ADDART({id:"fairtrade",t:"Fair Trade",c:"Economics and Society",i:"🤝",w:"Fair trade",r:["trade","coffee","choco","inequality","fastfashion","consumerrights"],
p:[["Fairer prices","Fair trade aims to pay farmers a fair, stable price."],["A label on products","Look for it on coffee, cocoa, bananas and more."],["Extra money for communities","A premium funds schools, wells and training."]],
l:"Fair trade is a movement and a set of trading rules that aim to give farmers and workers in developing countries better prices, decent working conditions and a stronger position in world markets. Products such as coffee, cocoa, tea, bananas, sugar, cotton and flowers that meet fair trade standards can carry a certification label. The best-known system, Fairtrade, run by Fairtrade International, sets a minimum price that protects farmers when world market prices fall, and adds an extra sum called the Fairtrade Premium, which farmer cooperatives and workers decide together how to spend, for example on schools, clean water, health care or better farming methods. Fair trade standards also ban forced labor and the worst forms of child labor and encourage environmental protection. The modern movement began after the Second World War, with charities selling handicrafts from poor communities, and the first fair trade coffee label, Max Havelaar, launched in the Netherlands in 1988. Supporters say fair trade helps millions of producers; critics question how much of the extra price reaches them.",
s:[["How it works","Farmers usually join cooperatives that are inspected to check they meet the standards. Companies that buy from them pay at least the Fairtrade Minimum Price and the Premium. Fairtrade International reported that producer organizations earned more than €1 billion in Premium over 2021–2025."],["History","Ten Thousand Villages in the United States and Oxfam in Britain began selling crafts from developing countries in the 1940s and 1950s. Max Havelaar, named after a Dutch novel about colonial exploitation, was the first fair trade label in 1988. Fairtrade International was formed in 1997."],["Living incomes","Fair trade organizations now campaign for living incomes, enough for a decent standard of living, and have set reference prices for products such as cocoa and coffee in some countries."],["Debates","Some economists argue that fair trade prices can encourage overproduction or help only some farmers, and that inspection costs are high. Others find benefits such as more stable incomes and stronger cooperatives. Many companies also run their own ethical sourcing programs."]],
k:[["Aims","Fair prices and decent conditions"],["Common products","Coffee, cocoa, tea, bananas, sugar, cotton, flowers"],["First label","Max Havelaar, Netherlands, 1988"],["Main certifier","Fairtrade International (1997)"],["Extra payment","Fairtrade Premium"],["Premium earned 2021–2025","Over €1 billion"]],
d:[["1946","Ten Thousand Villages begins selling crafts"],["1988","Max Havelaar label launched"],["1997","Fairtrade International founded"],["2000s","Fair trade products reach major supermarkets"]],
s2:[["Fair trade towns","Thousands of towns, schools and universities around the world have pledged to support fair trade products."],["World Fair Trade Day","World Fair Trade Day is celebrated on the second Saturday of May."]],
g:[["Fair trade","Trading that aims to give producers fair prices and conditions"],["Cooperative","A group owned and run by its members"],["Premium","Extra money paid on top of the price"],["Certification","Official checking that standards are met"]],
f:["Fair trade sets a minimum price.","The first fair trade label was in the Netherlands.","The Premium is spent on community projects."],
fun:"The first fair trade label was named after a character in a 19th-century Dutch novel."});

ADDART({id:"sharingeconomy",t:"The Sharing Economy",c:"Economics and Society",i:"🔑",w:"Sharing economy",r:["gigeconomy","smartphone","ecommerce","micromobility","overtourism","remotework"],
p:[["Renting instead of owning","Apps let people share homes, cars, bikes and tools."],["Ride-hailing and home sharing","Uber and Airbnb are famous examples."],["Debates","Questions about workers’ rights, housing and safety."]],
l:"The sharing economy, also called the collaborative or platform economy, describes businesses and services in which people rent, share or lend things they own, often through smartphone apps that connect strangers. Instead of buying a car, someone might hail a ride with an app; instead of a hotel, a traveler might stay in a stranger’s spare room; instead of buying a drill, a neighbor might borrow one. Well-known examples include ride-hailing apps such as Uber, Lyft, Didi, Grab and Bolt; home-sharing platforms such as Airbnb; bike and scooter sharing schemes; food delivery apps; and car-sharing services. These services grew rapidly from the late 2000s, helped by smartphones, GPS, online payments and rating systems that help strangers trust each other. Supporters say the sharing economy makes better use of idle resources, offers cheaper options and lets people earn extra money. Critics say many companies treat workers as independent contractors without employee protections, and that short-term rentals can push up rents in popular cities.",
s:[["How it grew","Airbnb was founded in 2008 in San Francisco when its founders rented out air mattresses in their apartment. Uber launched its app in 2010. By the 2020s, ride-hailing and home-sharing companies operated in hundreds of cities across the world."],["Trust between strangers","Ratings and reviews let users judge drivers, hosts and guests. Payments through the app, identity checks and insurance also help make it feel safer to share with people you do not know."],["Workers’ rights","Courts and governments in many countries have debated whether ride-hailing drivers and delivery riders should be treated as employees, with minimum wages and sick pay. In 2021 the UK Supreme Court ruled that Uber drivers were workers entitled to some protections, and the European Union agreed new rules for platform workers in 2024."],["Housing and cities","Cities such as Barcelona, Amsterdam, New York and Kyoto have limited short-term rentals, saying they reduce homes available for residents and change neighborhoods. Others welcome the extra tourism income."]],
k:[["Also called","Collaborative or platform economy"],["Examples","Ride-hailing, home sharing, bike sharing, food delivery"],["Airbnb founded","2008"],["Uber app launched","2010"],["Key technology","Smartphones, GPS, online payments, ratings"],["Main debates","Workers’ rights, housing, safety"]],
d:[["1999","Zipcar car-sharing founded"],["2008","Airbnb founded"],["2010","Uber launches its app"],["2021","UK Supreme Court rules on Uber drivers’ status"],["2024","EU agrees platform work rules"]],
s2:[["Libraries","Public libraries are an old form of sharing, and some now lend tools, sewing machines and musical instruments as well as books."],["Bike sharing","Large public bike-sharing schemes operate in cities such as Paris, Hangzhou, London and Mexico City."]],
g:[["Sharing economy","Businesses where people share or rent what they own"],["Platform","An app or website that connects buyers and sellers"],["Independent contractor","A self-employed worker"],["Short-term rental","Renting a home for a few nights"]],
f:["Airbnb is a home-sharing platform.","Ratings help strangers trust each other.","Workers’ rights are debated in the sharing economy."],
fun:"Airbnb began when its founders rented out three air mattresses on their living room floor."});

ADDART({id:"philanthropy",t:"Philanthropy and Charity",c:"Economics and Society",i:"💝",w:"Philanthropy",r:["volunteering","redcross","inequality","un","who","nobel"],
p:[["Giving for the common good","Philanthropy means giving money, time or skills to help others."],["From coins to billions","Everyday donors and huge foundations both give."],["Debates","How giving is spent, and who decides, is often discussed."]],
l:"Philanthropy is the giving of money, time, skills or resources to promote the well-being of others and improve society. Charity often refers to giving that helps people in immediate need, such as food, shelter or disaster relief, while philanthropy is often used for larger efforts to tackle the causes of problems, such as funding research, education or public health. Giving is part of nearly every culture and religion: Islam includes zakat, a required form of charitable giving; Judaism teaches tzedakah; Christianity, Hinduism, Buddhism and Sikhism all encourage charity and generosity. Today, ordinary people give to charities through donations, collections and online fundraising, while wealthy individuals and families set up foundations that can give away billions of dollars. The Giving Pledge, launched in 2010 by Warren Buffett, Bill Gates and Melinda French Gates, invites billionaires to promise to give most of their wealth away and had more than 250 signatories from 30 countries by 2026. Supporters say philanthropy funds vital work governments cannot; critics question the influence it gives the very rich.",
s:[["Big foundations","Large foundations include the Gates Foundation, which funds global health and education, the Wellcome Trust in Britain, which funds medical research, and the Novo Nordisk Foundation in Denmark. In 2025, Bill Gates announced that the Gates Foundation would give away nearly all its money and close in 2045."],["History","Andrew Carnegie, a Scottish-American steel magnate, wrote in 1889 that the rich should give away their wealth during their lives. He paid for more than 2,500 public libraries around the world. Earlier, religious institutions, guilds and wealthy patrons funded hospitals, schools and almshouses."],["Effective giving","Some people try to give in the most effective way possible, using evidence to choose charities that save or improve the most lives per dollar, for example by preventing malaria or deworming children."],["Everyday giving","Most charitable giving in many countries comes from ordinary people. Crowdfunding websites let people raise money quickly for medical bills, disaster relief or community projects. Giving Tuesday, started in 2012, encourages donations after the holiday shopping season."]],
k:[["Means","Giving to help others and improve society"],["Religious giving","Zakat, tzedakah, dana and others"],["Giving Pledge launched","2010"],["Giving Pledge signatories (2026)","Over 250 from 30 countries"],["Famous early philanthropist","Andrew Carnegie"],["Giving Tuesday","Started 2012"]],
d:[["1889","Carnegie’s essay The Gospel of Wealth"],["1913","Rockefeller Foundation founded"],["2000","Gates Foundation formed"],["2010","Giving Pledge launched"],["2012","Giving Tuesday begins"]],
s2:[["Not only money","Volunteering time, donating blood and sharing professional skills for free are also forms of philanthropy."],["The word","Philanthropy comes from Greek words meaning ‘love of humankind’."]],
g:[["Philanthropy","Giving to improve the well-being of others"],["Foundation","An organization that gives money to good causes"],["Zakat","Charitable giving required in Islam"],["Crowdfunding","Raising money from many people online"]],
f:["The Giving Pledge began in 2010.","Andrew Carnegie funded thousands of libraries.","Zakat is a form of charitable giving in Islam."],
fun:"Andrew Carnegie paid for more than 2,500 public libraries, many still in use today."});

ADDART({id:"climateactivism",t:"Climate Activism",c:"Economics and Society",i:"📣",w:"Climate movement",r:["climchange","energytransition","carbonfootprint","un","sdgs","mentalyouth"],
p:[["People pressing for action","Campaigns urging governments and companies to tackle climate change."],["School strikes","Young people led mass protests from 2018."],["Many methods","From marches and lawsuits to lobbying and protests."]],
l:"Climate activism is the work of individuals and groups who campaign for faster action against climate change, pressing governments, businesses and the public to cut greenhouse gas emissions, protect forests and help communities adapt. The movement includes large environmental organizations, scientists, Indigenous groups, faith communities, students and local campaigners. It uses many methods: marches and protests, petitions, lobbying, voting campaigns, lawsuits against governments and companies, campaigns to persuade investors to move money away from fossil fuels, and sometimes civil disobedience, deliberately breaking laws peacefully, such as blocking roads. In August 2018 the Swedish student Greta Thunberg began striking from school outside the Swedish parliament, inspiring the Fridays for Future movement; in September 2019, millions of people joined climate strikes in cities around the world. Climate activists have helped bring climate change into the political mainstream, but some tactics, such as disrupting traffic or throwing soup at protected paintings, have drawn criticism, and in some countries governments have passed tougher laws against such protests.",
s:[["Youth movement","Fridays for Future grew from one student to strikes in thousands of cities. Young activists from many countries, such as Vanessa Nakate of Uganda and Licypriya Kangujam of India, have spoken at international meetings."],["Climate lawsuits","Courts have become an important arena. In 2019 the Supreme Court of the Netherlands ordered the government to cut emissions faster in a case brought by the group Urgenda. In 2024 the European Court of Human Rights ruled that Switzerland had violated human rights by not doing enough. In 2025 the International Court of Justice gave an advisory opinion that countries have legal duties to address climate change."],["Divestment","Campaigns have persuaded many universities, churches, cities and pension funds to sell investments in fossil fuel companies."],["Different views","Supporters say urgent pressure is needed because the dangers of climate change are serious. Critics argue that some protests disrupt ordinary people’s lives or that some proposed policies cost too much. Debate continues about which tactics and policies work best."]],
k:[["Aim","Faster action on climate change"],["Methods","Protests, lobbying, lawsuits, divestment, civil disobedience"],["School strike began","August 2018, Stockholm"],["Global climate strike","September 2019"],["Landmark court cases","Urgenda (2019), Swiss case (2024), ICJ opinion (2025)"]],
d:[["1988","Climate scientist James Hansen testifies to the US Congress"],["2015","Paris Agreement adopted"],["2018","Greta Thunberg’s school strike begins"],["2019","Millions join global climate strikes"],["2025","International Court of Justice climate opinion"]],
s2:[["Indigenous leadership","Indigenous groups in the Amazon, the Arctic and elsewhere campaign to protect forests and lands that store huge amounts of carbon."],["COP meetings","Activists gather each year at the United Nations climate conferences, called COPs, to press governments."]],
g:[["Activism","Campaigning for social or political change"],["Civil disobedience","Peacefully breaking a law in protest"],["Divestment","Selling investments for ethical reasons"],["Emissions","Gases released into the air"]],
f:["Greta Thunberg began a school strike in 2018.","Climate activists use lawsuits as well as protests.","Some protest tactics are controversial."],
fun:"Greta Thunberg’s first protest sign read, in Swedish, ‘School strike for climate’."});

ADDART({id:"fjords",t:"Fjords",c:"Earth Science",i:"🏞️",w:"Fjord",r:["glaciers","iceage","coasts","norwaycountry","mountainform","nzcountry"],
p:[["Valleys carved by ice","Glaciers dug deep valleys that the sea later filled."],["Steep and deep","Fjords have tall cliffs and very deep water."],["Norway, Chile, New Zealand","Famous fjord coasts are in cold, mountainous places."]],
l:"A fjord is a long, narrow, deep inlet of the sea between steep cliffs, formed when a glacier carved a valley that was later flooded by the ocean. During the ice ages, huge glaciers flowed down from mountains to the coast, scraping and deepening river valleys into U-shaped troughs, often far below sea level. When the ice melted and sea levels rose, seawater flooded these valleys, creating fjords. Many fjords are deeper in the middle than at their mouths, where a ridge of rock or glacial debris called a sill marks the farthest point the glacier reached. Fjords are found on coasts that were heavily glaciated, especially Norway, Greenland, Iceland, Alaska, British Columbia in Canada, southern Chile, Scotland, where they are often called sea lochs, and the Fiordland region of New Zealand. Their dramatic scenery, with waterfalls pouring off cliffs, draws many visitors. The word fjord comes from Norwegian. Norway’s Sognefjord is more than 200 kilometers long and over 1,300 meters deep.",
s:[["How fjords form","A glacier is like a giant file, grinding rock with the stones frozen into its base. It turns V-shaped river valleys into wide, steep-sided U-shapes. Side valleys left high above the main valley are called hanging valleys, and their streams often form tall waterfalls."],["Famous fjords","Norway’s Geirangerfjord and Nærøyfjord are UNESCO World Heritage Sites. Milford Sound in New Zealand is famous for its rain and waterfalls. Greenland’s Scoresby Sund is the world’s largest fjord system, about 350 kilometers long."],["Life in fjords","Because fjords are sheltered, they can host unusual wildlife. Some Norwegian fjords contain cold-water coral reefs, and Fiordland’s dark surface water allows deep-sea creatures to live closer to the surface than usual."],["Hazards","Rockfalls into fjords can cause dangerous waves. In 1934 a rockslide into Tafjord in Norway caused a wave that killed 40 people, and scientists monitor unstable mountainsides today."]],
k:[["Formed by","Glaciers, then flooded by the sea"],["Shape","Long, narrow, deep, steep-sided"],["Main regions","Norway, Greenland, Alaska, Canada, Chile, New Zealand"],["Longest in Norway","Sognefjord, over 200 km"],["Largest system","Scoresby Sund, Greenland"],["Word origin","Norwegian"]],
s2:[["Sea lochs","In Scotland, fjords are called sea lochs, such as Loch Linnhe and Loch Fyne."],["Sea or lake","Some fjords are now lakes, cut off from the sea as the land rose after the ice melted."]],
g:[["Fjord","A long, deep sea inlet carved by a glacier"],["U-shaped valley","A valley carved by ice"],["Hanging valley","A side valley left high above a main valley"],["Sill","A ridge at the mouth of a fjord"]],
f:["Fjords were carved by glaciers.","Norway is famous for fjords.","Fjords are often deeper in the middle than at their mouths."],
fun:"Norway’s Sognefjord is deeper than many of the mountains around it are high above the water."});

ADDART({id:"permafrost",t:"Permafrost",c:"Earth Science",i:"🧊",w:"Permafrost",r:["tundra","arctic","climchange","glaciers","seedvaults","mammoth"],
p:[["Ground that stays frozen","Frozen for at least two years in a row."],["A quarter of northern lands","Found across the Arctic and high mountains."],["Thawing","Warming is melting it, releasing greenhouse gases."]],
l:"Permafrost is ground, soil, rock or sediment that stays at or below 0 °C for at least two years in a row. It covers about a quarter of the land in the Northern Hemisphere, mainly in Siberia, Alaska, northern Canada, Greenland and the Tibetan Plateau, as well as high mountains and parts of the seafloor in the Arctic. Above it lies an active layer that thaws each summer and freezes again in winter. Permafrost can be hundreds of meters thick, and some has been frozen for hundreds of thousands of years. It holds enormous amounts of carbon in the frozen remains of plants and animals, roughly twice as much as is currently in the atmosphere. As the Arctic warms faster than the rest of the world, permafrost is thawing. When it thaws, microbes break down the old plant material and release carbon dioxide and methane, greenhouse gases that can add to warming. Thawing ground also sinks and buckles, damaging roads, buildings and pipelines, and can uncover frozen remains such as woolly mammoths.",
s:[["The active layer","In summer, the top layer of ground above permafrost thaws, from a few centimeters to a few meters deep. Plants of the tundra grow in this layer. Because water cannot drain down through the frozen ground, summer tundra is often boggy, with many lakes."],["Ground ice and strange landscapes","Permafrost often contains wedges and lenses of ice. When it thaws, the ground can collapse into hollows and lakes, a landscape called thermokarst. Pingos are hills pushed up by growing ice cores."],["A carbon store","Scientists estimate permafrost soils hold about 1,500 billion tonnes of carbon. How quickly it will be released as the climate warms is a major question in climate science."],["Frozen time capsules","Thawing permafrost in Siberia and Canada has revealed well-preserved woolly mammoths, cave lion cubs and ancient wolves, as well as long-frozen seeds and microbes."]],
k:[["Definition","Ground frozen for at least two years"],["Coverage","About a quarter of Northern Hemisphere land"],["Main regions","Siberia, Alaska, Canada, Greenland, Tibet"],["Carbon stored","About 1,500 billion tonnes"],["Greenhouse gases released","Carbon dioxide and methane"],["Thaw landscape","Thermokarst"]],
s2:[["Building on ice","In permafrost regions, buildings are often raised on stilts and pipelines on supports so their heat does not melt the ground."],["Batagaika crater","The Batagaika crater in Siberia, a huge thaw slump more than a kilometer long, keeps growing as permafrost melts."]],
g:[["Permafrost","Ground that stays frozen for at least two years"],["Active layer","The top layer that thaws each summer"],["Thermokarst","Uneven ground caused by thawing permafrost"],["Methane","A powerful greenhouse gas"]],
f:["Permafrost stays frozen for at least two years.","Permafrost stores a lot of carbon.","Thawing permafrost releases greenhouse gases."],
fun:"Thawing permafrost in Siberia has revealed woolly mammoths with hair and flesh still preserved."});

ADDART({id:"atolls",t:"Atolls",c:"Earth Science",i:"🏝️",w:"Atoll",r:["coral","pacific","indocean","volc","sealevel"],
p:[["Rings of coral","An atoll is a ring of coral reef around a lagoon."],["Built on volcanoes","They form on top of sinking volcanic islands."],["Low and vulnerable","Most are only a few meters above the sea."]],
l:"An atoll is a ring-shaped coral reef or chain of low coral islands surrounding a lagoon, with no central island. Atolls are found mainly in warm tropical waters of the Pacific and Indian Oceans. They form over millions of years: first a volcano rises from the seafloor to form an island, and coral reefs grow around its shores. As the volcano becomes inactive, it slowly sinks and is worn away, while the corals keep growing upward toward the light. Eventually the volcano disappears beneath the sea, leaving a ring of reef around a lagoon. Waves pile broken coral and sand on the reef to form small islands called motu or islets. This explanation was first proposed by Charles Darwin after his voyage on HMS Beagle and later confirmed when drilling on Enewetak Atoll in 1951 reached volcanic rock beneath more than 1,200 meters of coral. Countries made mostly or entirely of atolls include the Maldives, Kiribati, the Marshall Islands and Tuvalu. Because most atoll islands rise only a few meters above sea level, they are especially threatened by rising seas and storm surges.",
s:[["Darwin’s theory","In 1842 Darwin published his theory that fringing reefs around volcanic islands become barrier reefs and then atolls as the islands sink. It was controversial for decades until drilling proved it."],["Famous atolls","Kiritimati (Christmas Island) in Kiribati is one of the largest atolls by land area. Aldabra in the Seychelles is home to about 100,000 giant tortoises. The Great Chagos Bank in the Indian Ocean is the largest atoll structure in the world."],["Life on an atoll","Atoll soils are thin and salty, so few crops grow. Coconut palms, breadfruit and pandanus are important, and fishing is central to life. Fresh water comes from rain and from a thin lens of fresh groundwater floating above seawater."],["Rising seas","Atoll nations face flooding, salty groundwater and coastal erosion as seas rise. Tuvalu signed a treaty with Australia in 2023 allowing some of its citizens to move there. Studies show some reef islands can grow by collecting sand, but many communities are at serious risk."]],
k:[["Shape","A ring of reef around a lagoon"],["Formed on","Sinking volcanic islands"],["Where","Mainly Pacific and Indian Oceans"],["Atoll nations","Maldives, Kiribati, Marshall Islands, Tuvalu"],["Theory by","Charles Darwin (1842)"],["Proved by drilling","Enewetak Atoll, 1951–52"]],
d:[["1836","Darwin studies the Cocos (Keeling) Islands"],["1842","Darwin publishes his coral reef theory"],["1951","Drilling at Enewetak reaches volcanic rock"],["2023","Tuvalu–Australia treaty signed"]],
s2:[["Nuclear tests","Bikini and Enewetak atolls in the Marshall Islands were used by the United States for nuclear weapons tests from 1946 to 1958, forcing residents to leave."],["The word","Atoll comes from the Dhivehi language of the Maldives."]],
g:[["Atoll","A ring-shaped coral reef around a lagoon"],["Lagoon","A shallow body of water separated from the sea"],["Motu","A small island on an atoll reef"],["Freshwater lens","A layer of fresh groundwater above seawater"]],
f:["Atolls are rings of coral.","Atolls form on sinking volcanoes.","Charles Darwin explained how atolls form."],
fun:"The word atoll comes from Dhivehi, the language of the Maldives, a country made almost entirely of atolls."});

ADDART({id:"sanddunes",t:"Sand Dunes",c:"Earth Science",i:"🏜️",w:"Dune",r:["desert","sahara","coasts","climate","mars"],
p:[["Hills of sand","Dunes are piles of sand shaped by wind."],["Always on the move","Some dunes travel many meters each year."],["Many shapes","Crescents, stars, long ridges and more."]],
l:"A sand dune is a hill or ridge of sand piled up by the wind. Dunes form wherever there is plenty of loose sand, wind strong enough to move it, and something to slow the wind and make the sand drop, such as a rock, a bush or another dune. They are found in deserts, along coasts and beside lakes and rivers. Wind pushes sand grains up the gentle side of a dune, called the windward slope, in small hops, a process called saltation. At the top, the sand spills over and slides down the steep side, called the slip face, so the whole dune slowly creeps forward. Some dunes move more than 10 meters a year and can bury roads, farms and villages. The shape of a dune depends on how much sand there is and how the wind blows: crescent-shaped barchans form where wind blows from one direction, long linear dunes where it blows from two, and tall star dunes where it comes from many directions. The tallest dunes on Earth, in deserts such as the Badain Jaran in China and the Namib, rise to around 300 to 500 meters.",
s:[["Types of dune","Barchans are crescents with horns pointing downwind. Transverse dunes form long ridges at right angles to the wind. Linear or seif dunes run parallel to the wind for many kilometers. Star dunes have several arms and grow tall rather than moving far."],["Sand seas","Huge areas of dunes are called ergs or sand seas. The Rub’ al Khali, or Empty Quarter, on the Arabian Peninsula is the largest continuous sand desert in the world. Only about a fifth of the Sahara is covered by sand dunes."],["Coastal dunes","Along coasts, dunes form behind beaches and protect the land from storms and high tides. Plants such as marram grass trap sand with their roots and help dunes grow. People plant grasses and build fences to stabilize them."],["Singing sands","Some dunes make a loud booming or humming sound when sand slides down them, heard in places such as the Mingsha Shan in China and the Sahara. The sound comes from grains moving together in sync."]],
k:[["Formed by","Wind"],["Gentle side","Windward slope"],["Steep side","Slip face"],["Common shapes","Barchan, linear, transverse, star"],["Largest sand desert","Rub’ al Khali (Empty Quarter)"],["Tallest dunes","About 300–500 meters"]],
s2:[["Dunes on other worlds","Dunes have been seen on Mars, Venus, Saturn’s moon Titan and even Pluto."],["Sandboarding","People slide down large dunes on boards in places such as Namibia, Peru and Australia."]],
g:[["Dune","A hill of sand piled up by wind"],["Saltation","Sand moving in small hops"],["Slip face","The steep downwind side of a dune"],["Erg","A large area of sand dunes"]],
f:["Dunes are shaped by wind.","Dunes move as sand slides down the slip face.","Star dunes form where winds come from many directions."],
fun:"Some sand dunes sing, making a deep humming sound when the sand slides."});

ADDART({id:"bodyclock",t:"The Body Clock",c:"Health and the Body",i:"⏰",w:"Circadian rhythm",r:["sleep","brain","hormone","screentime","timezones","daylightsaving"],
p:[["A 24-hour rhythm","Your body follows an internal daily cycle."],["Set by light","Daylight keeps the clock in time."],["Jet lag","Crossing time zones throws it off."]],
l:"The body clock is the internal system that keeps many of the body’s activities on a roughly 24-hour cycle, called a circadian rhythm, from the Latin for ‘about a day’. It controls when we feel sleepy or alert, and changes in body temperature, hunger, digestion, blood pressure and hormone levels through the day and night. The master clock is a tiny cluster of about 20,000 nerve cells in the brain called the suprachiasmatic nucleus, which sits just above where the optic nerves cross. It receives signals from special light-sensing cells in the eyes and uses daylight to stay in time with the outside world. In the evening, as light fades, the brain’s pineal gland releases the hormone melatonin, which helps make us sleepy. Almost every cell in the body also has its own clock, controlled by ‘clock genes’. When the body clock is out of step with our surroundings, as in jet lag or night-shift work, people can feel tired, unwell and unable to concentrate. Teenagers’ body clocks naturally shift later, which makes early mornings harder for them.",
s:[["Clock genes","In 2017, Jeffrey Hall, Michael Rosbash and Michael Young won the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine for discovering the genes and proteins that keep circadian time inside cells, first studied in fruit flies."],["Jet lag","When you fly across several time zones, your body clock stays on home time for a while. It usually adjusts by about one time zone a day. Getting daylight at the right times helps reset it faster. Flying east is often harder than flying west."],["Light at night","Bright light in the evening, including from screens, can delay the release of melatonin and make it harder to fall asleep. Keeping bedrooms dark and limiting screens before bed can help."],["Larks and owls","People naturally differ in timing: ‘morning larks’ wake early and ‘night owls’ prefer to stay up late. This is called chronotype and is partly inherited. It tends to shift later in the teens and earlier in older age."]],
k:[["Rhythm length","About 24 hours"],["Master clock","Suprachiasmatic nucleus in the brain"],["Main time signal","Daylight"],["Sleep hormone","Melatonin"],["Nobel Prize","2017, for clock genes"],["Jet lag recovery","About one time zone a day"]],
d:[["1729","Jean-Jacques d’Ortous de Mairan shows plants keep daily rhythms in the dark"],["1971","First clock gene found in fruit flies"],["2017","Nobel Prize for circadian rhythm research"]],
s2:[["Plants too","Many plants open and close their leaves or flowers on a daily schedule even in constant darkness, showing they too have internal clocks."],["Living in caves","In 1962 the French explorer Michel Siffre spent two months in a cave without clocks or daylight; his days drifted longer than 24 hours."]],
g:[["Circadian rhythm","A cycle of about 24 hours in the body"],["Suprachiasmatic nucleus","The brain’s master clock"],["Melatonin","A hormone that helps bring on sleep"],["Chronotype","Whether a person is a morning or evening type"]],
f:["The body clock runs on about 24 hours.","Daylight resets the body clock.","Melatonin helps bring on sleep."],
fun:"Even without clocks or sunlight, your body keeps a rhythm of a little over 24 hours."});

ADDART({id:"twins",t:"Twins",c:"Health and the Body",i:"👯",w:"Twin",r:["mendel","dna","pregnancy","genome","psychology","cell","handedness"],
p:[["Identical or fraternal","Twins come from one egg or two."],["Same genes","Identical twins share almost all their DNA."],["Science helpers","Twin studies show how genes and environment shape us."]],
l:"Twins are two children born from the same pregnancy. There are two main kinds. Identical, or monozygotic, twins form when a single fertilized egg splits into two embryos early in development; they share almost all of the same genes and are always the same sex. Fraternal, or dizygotic, twins form when two separate eggs are fertilized by two separate sperm; they are as genetically similar as any brothers and sisters and can be the same or different sexes. About 1 in 250 births produces identical twins, a rate that is similar around the world. Fraternal twins are more common in some populations, such as parts of West Africa, and less common in East Asia, and are more likely with older mothers, in families with a history of twins and with fertility treatments. Twin rates have risen in many countries since the 1980s. Because identical twins share their genes, scientists compare them with fraternal twins to learn how much traits such as height, personality and the risk of certain diseases are shaped by genes and how much by environment.",
s:[["How identical twins form","If the egg splits within the first few days, each twin has its own placenta. If it splits later, the twins may share a placenta, and very rarely, if it splits very late, they may be conjoined twins, joined at part of their bodies."],["Twin studies","By comparing how often identical and fraternal twins share a trait, scientists estimate its heritability. Studies of identical twins raised apart, such as the Minnesota Study begun in 1979, found striking similarities, but also many differences."],["Not quite identical","Identical twins have different fingerprints, and small genetic changes and differences in which genes are switched on, called epigenetics, build up over their lives. NASA’s Twins Study compared astronaut Scott Kelly, who spent nearly a year in space, with his identical twin Mark."],["Twin towns","Igbo-Ora in Nigeria is known as the ‘twin capital of the world’ because of its very high rate of twin births, celebrated with an annual festival."]],
k:[["Identical twins","From one egg; share almost all genes"],["Fraternal twins","From two eggs; like ordinary siblings"],["Identical twin rate","About 1 in 250 births"],["Highest twin rates","Parts of West Africa"],["Used in science","Twin studies of genes and environment"]],
d:[["1875","Francis Galton proposes using twins to study heredity"],["1979","Minnesota Study of Twins Reared Apart begins"],["2019","NASA publishes results of its Twins Study"]],
s2:[["Twin talk","Some young twins develop their own private words to communicate, known as cryptophasia."],["Triplets and more","Triplets, quadruplets and larger multiple births can be identical, fraternal or a mix."]],
g:[["Monozygotic","From one fertilized egg"],["Dizygotic","From two fertilized eggs"],["Heritability","How much variation in a trait is due to genes"],["Epigenetics","Changes in how genes are switched on and off"]],
f:["Identical twins come from one egg.","Fraternal twins come from two eggs.","Identical twins have different fingerprints."],
fun:"Identical twins share almost all their DNA, but they never have the same fingerprints."});

ADDART({id:"circulation",t:"Blood Vessels and Circulation",c:"Health and the Body",i:"🫀",w:"Circulatory system",r:["heart","blood","lungs","hypertension","heartdisease","exercise","cholesterol","bloodclotting"],
p:[["A body-wide network","Arteries, veins and capillaries carry blood everywhere."],["Two loops","One loop goes to the lungs, the other to the body."],["Very long","An adult’s blood vessels could stretch about 100,000 km."]],
l:"The circulatory system moves blood around the body, carrying oxygen and nutrients to every cell and taking away carbon dioxide and wastes. It is made up of the heart, which pumps the blood, and a vast network of blood vessels. Arteries carry blood away from the heart; they have thick, stretchy, muscular walls to cope with the high pressure of each heartbeat. Arteries branch into smaller arterioles and then into capillaries, tiny vessels with walls just one cell thick, where oxygen and nutrients pass into the tissues. Capillaries join into venules and then veins, which carry blood back to the heart; many veins have valves that stop blood flowing backward. Human blood travels in two loops: the pulmonary circulation takes blood from the right side of the heart to the lungs to pick up oxygen, and the systemic circulation carries oxygen-rich blood from the left side of the heart to the rest of the body. Laid end to end, an adult’s blood vessels would stretch about 100,000 kilometers. Healthy blood vessels are vital; blockages can cause heart attacks and strokes.",
s:[["Discovering circulation","In 1628 the English doctor William Harvey showed that the heart pumps blood around the body in a circuit. Earlier, the 13th-century Arab physician Ibn al-Nafis had described blood passing through the lungs. Capillaries were first seen with a microscope by Marcello Malpighi in 1661."],["Blood pressure","Blood pressure is the force of blood against artery walls, measured as two numbers: the pressure when the heart beats and when it rests. High blood pressure strains the heart and vessels and raises the risk of heart attacks and strokes."],["Keeping vessels healthy","Fatty deposits called plaques can build up in arteries, a condition called atherosclerosis. Not smoking, regular exercise, a balanced diet and controlling blood pressure and cholesterol help protect blood vessels."],["Pulse","With each heartbeat, a wave of pressure travels along the arteries. You can feel it as your pulse at the wrist or neck. A resting adult pulse is usually 60 to 100 beats per minute."]],
k:[["Main parts","Heart, arteries, veins, capillaries"],["Arteries","Carry blood away from the heart"],["Veins","Carry blood back to the heart"],["Capillaries","Tiny vessels where exchange happens"],["Total length","About 100,000 km"],["Discovered by","William Harvey, 1628"]],
d:[["c. 1242","Ibn al-Nafis describes blood flow through the lungs"],["1628","William Harvey describes circulation"],["1661","Malpighi sees capillaries"],["1896","Scipione Riva-Rocci invents the modern blood pressure cuff"]],
s2:[["Blue veins?","Veins look blue through the skin, but the blood inside them is dark red. The blue color comes from how skin scatters light."],["One minute","It takes roughly one minute for blood to travel all the way around the body when you are resting."]],
g:[["Artery","A vessel carrying blood away from the heart"],["Vein","A vessel carrying blood back to the heart"],["Capillary","A tiny blood vessel"],["Atherosclerosis","Narrowing of arteries by fatty deposits"]],
f:["Arteries carry blood away from the heart.","Blood travels in two loops.","William Harvey described circulation."],
fun:"Your blood makes a full trip around your body in about a minute."});

ADDART({id:"metabolism",t:"Metabolism",c:"Health and the Body",i:"🔥",w:"Metabolism",r:["nutri","digest","hormone","exercise","energyphys","obesity"],
p:[["The body’s chemistry","All the chemical reactions that keep you alive."],["Making energy","Food is broken down to power cells."],["Building up","Metabolism also builds new tissues."]],
l:"Metabolism is the name for all the chemical reactions that happen inside the body to keep it alive. These reactions turn the food we eat into energy, build and repair tissues, and get rid of wastes. Metabolism has two sides: catabolism breaks large molecules, such as carbohydrates, fats and proteins from food, into smaller ones, releasing energy; anabolism uses energy to build larger molecules, such as proteins for muscles or new cells. Much of the energy from food is captured in a molecule called ATP, which cells use like rechargeable batteries to power everything from muscle contractions to nerve signals. Most of this energy is made in tiny structures inside cells called mitochondria, which use oxygen in a process called cellular respiration. The energy the body uses at complete rest, to keep the heart beating, lungs breathing and body warm, is called the basal metabolic rate, and it accounts for most of the energy a typical person uses each day. Metabolism is controlled by enzymes and hormones, such as insulin and thyroid hormones, and is affected by age, body size, muscle mass, activity and genes.",
s:[["Energy in food","Food energy is measured in kilocalories (often called calories) or kilojoules. Fats provide about 9 kilocalories per gram, and carbohydrates and proteins about 4. The body stores extra energy as glycogen in the liver and muscles and as fat."],["Fast and slow","People’s basal metabolic rates differ, but less than many think. A large 2021 study found that metabolism, adjusted for body size, stays fairly steady from about age 20 to 60 and declines slowly afterward."],["Enzymes","Enzymes are proteins that speed up chemical reactions. Without them, the reactions of metabolism would happen far too slowly to keep us alive. Some inherited diseases are caused by a missing enzyme."],["Hormones","Insulin, made by the pancreas, helps cells take in sugar from the blood. Thyroid hormones set the pace of metabolism in many tissues. Problems with these hormones can cause diabetes or thyroid disorders."]],
k:[["Means","All chemical reactions in the body"],["Breaking down","Catabolism"],["Building up","Anabolism"],["Energy molecule","ATP"],["Energy factories","Mitochondria"],["Key hormones","Insulin, thyroid hormones"]],
d:[["1614","Santorio Santorio weighs himself to study metabolism"],["1937","Hans Krebs describes the citric acid cycle"],["2021","Study tracks metabolism across the human lifespan"]],
s2:[["Body heat","Much of the energy released by metabolism becomes heat, which keeps body temperature at about 37 °C."],["Brain power","The brain makes up about 2 percent of body weight but uses about 20 percent of the body’s energy at rest."]],
g:[["Metabolism","All the chemical reactions in the body"],["ATP","The molecule cells use to store and use energy"],["Mitochondria","Cell structures that make energy"],["Basal metabolic rate","Energy used at complete rest"]],
f:["Metabolism includes breaking down and building up molecules.","Mitochondria make most of the cell’s energy.","Insulin helps control metabolism."],
fun:"Your brain uses about a fifth of your body’s energy even while you rest."});

