ADDART({id:"oases",t:"Oases",c:"Earth Science",i:"🌴",w:"Oasis",r:["desert","sahara","palms","silk","wcycle","saltflats"],
p:[["Water in the desert","An oasis is a fertile spot fed by underground water."],["Date palms","Oases often grow dates, fruits and vegetables."],["Trade routes","Caravans traveled from oasis to oasis."]],
l:"An oasis is a fertile area in a desert where water reaches the surface or lies close enough to dig for, allowing plants to grow and people and animals to live. Most oases are fed by groundwater stored in underground layers of rock called aquifers, which may have been filled by rain thousands of years ago or by water flowing underground from distant mountains. The water may rise in natural springs or be reached through wells. Oases range from a small pool with a few palm trees to large areas covering hundreds of square kilometers with towns and farms. Date palms are the classic oasis crop: their tall canopy shades smaller fruit trees such as figs, pomegranates and citrus, which in turn shade vegetables and grains below, a system of layered farming. For thousands of years, oases were vital stopping points on trade routes across the Sahara, Arabia and Central Asia, such as the Silk Road. Famous oases include Siwa in Egypt, Al-Ahsa in Saudi Arabia, the largest oasis in the world, and Huacachina in Peru. Many oases are threatened by overuse of groundwater and spreading sand.",
s:[["Al-Ahsa","Al-Ahsa in eastern Saudi Arabia has more than 2.5 million date palms fed by springs and wells, making it one of the largest oases in the world. It is a UNESCO World Heritage Site."],["Underground channels","In Iran, North Africa and Central Asia, people built underground tunnels called qanats or foggaras that carry groundwater by gravity from hills to oases, some running many kilometers. The qanats of Iran are a World Heritage Site."],["Siwa Oasis","Siwa, in Egypt’s Western Desert, has springs and salt lakes and was home to a famous oracle of the god Amun that Alexander the Great visited in 331 BCE."],["Threats","Pumping groundwater faster than it refills can dry up springs. Sand dunes can bury oasis farms, and young people often move to cities. Some communities are reviving traditional water systems."]],
k:[["Definition","A fertile area in a desert with water"],["Water source","Groundwater from aquifers"],["Classic crop","Date palms"],["Largest","Al-Ahsa, Saudi Arabia"],["Ancient water tunnels","Qanats"],["Threats","Overpumping, sand, depopulation"]],
s2:[["Mirages","Desert travelers sometimes mistake a mirage, light bent by hot air that looks like shimmering water, for an oasis."],["Oases in cities","Several desert cities, such as Damascus and Marrakesh, grew up around oases."]],
g:[["Oasis","A fertile spot in a desert"],["Aquifer","Underground rock that holds water"],["Qanat","An underground channel carrying water"],["Caravan","A group traveling together, often with camels"]],
f:["Oases are fed by groundwater.","Date palms grow in oases.","Al-Ahsa is one of the largest oases."],
fun:"Al-Ahsa has more than 2.5 million date palms."});

ADDART({id:"icebergs",t:"Icebergs",c:"Earth Science",i:"🧊",w:"Iceberg",r:["glaciers","antarc","arctic","titanic","sealevel","oceancurrents","buoyancy","seaice"],
p:[["Floating ice","Huge pieces of ice that break off glaciers."],["Mostly hidden","About 90 percent of an iceberg is underwater."],["Giant bergs","Some are larger than small countries."]],
l:"An iceberg is a large piece of freshwater ice that has broken off a glacier or ice shelf and floats in the sea. The breaking off is called calving. Icebergs are made of compressed snow that fell many years earlier, so they are fresh, not salty, and can be thousands of years old. Because ice is slightly less dense than seawater, an iceberg floats with only about a tenth of its volume above the surface; the rest is hidden below, which is where the saying ‘the tip of the iceberg’ comes from. Most icebergs come from Antarctica and Greenland. Those from Antarctica’s ice shelves are often flat-topped and enormous: in 2000 an iceberg called B-15, about 11,000 square kilometers, broke from the Ross Ice Shelf, the largest ever recorded. Greenland’s glaciers produce many smaller, jagged icebergs, some of which drift south into the North Atlantic shipping lanes; one sank the Titanic in 1912, leading to the creation of the International Ice Patrol. As icebergs melt, they release nutrients that feed plankton, and their freshwater adds to the ocean.",
s:[["Shapes and colors","Icebergs can be tabular (flat-topped), domed, pinnacled or wedge-shaped. Old, dense ice can look deep blue because it absorbs red light. Some icebergs are striped with mud or green from frozen algae."],["Giant icebergs","Iceberg A23a, which broke from Antarctica in 1986, stayed grounded for decades before drifting north in the 2020s and slowly breaking apart. Scientists track large icebergs by satellite and give them letters and numbers."],["Ice Patrol","After the Titanic disaster, countries set up the International Ice Patrol in 1914 to watch for icebergs in the North Atlantic and warn ships. It now uses aircraft and satellites."],["Melting and sea level","Icebergs already floating do not raise sea level when they melt, but ice flowing from land into the sea does. Faster calving from Greenland and Antarctica is adding to sea level rise."]],
k:[["Made of","Fresh water (compressed snow)"],["Below the surface","About 90%"],["Formed by","Calving from glaciers and ice shelves"],["Main sources","Antarctica and Greenland"],["Largest recorded","B-15, about 11,000 km² (2000)"],["Ice Patrol founded","1914"]],
d:[["1912","Titanic sinks after hitting an iceberg"],["1914","International Ice Patrol founded"],["1986","Iceberg A23a breaks off Antarctica"],["2000","Iceberg B-15 calves"]],
s2:[["Growlers and bergy bits","Small pieces of floating ice have names too: bergy bits are about the size of a house, and growlers about the size of a car."],["Iceberg Alley","Off the coast of Newfoundland and Labrador in Canada, ‘Iceberg Alley’ is a popular place to watch icebergs drift past in spring."]],
g:[["Iceberg","A large floating piece of glacier ice"],["Calving","Ice breaking off a glacier"],["Ice shelf","A floating sheet of ice attached to land"],["Tabular iceberg","A flat-topped iceberg"]],
f:["Icebergs are made of fresh water.","Most of an iceberg is underwater.","An iceberg sank the Titanic."],
fun:"When icebergs melt, they fizz, as tiny bubbles of ancient air trapped in the ice escape."});

ADDART({id:"pancreas",t:"The Pancreas",c:"Health and the Body",i:"🫁",w:"Pancreas",r:["insulin","digest","diabetes","hormone","liver","metabolism"],
p:[["Two jobs in one","Makes digestive juices and hormones."],["Insulin","It controls blood sugar."],["Hidden organ","It lies behind the stomach."]],
l:"The pancreas is an organ about 15 centimeters long that lies deep in the abdomen, behind the stomach. It has two very different jobs. First, it is a digestive gland: it makes about 1 to 1.5 liters of pancreatic juice a day, full of enzymes that break down proteins, fats and carbohydrates, and sends it through a duct into the small intestine. The juice also contains bicarbonate, which neutralizes the acid coming from the stomach. Second, it is a hormone gland. Scattered through the pancreas are about a million tiny clusters of cells called islets of Langerhans, which release hormones directly into the blood. The most important are insulin, which lowers blood sugar by helping cells take in glucose, and glucagon, which raises blood sugar when it is low. When the islets do not make enough insulin, or the body does not respond to it properly, a person has diabetes. Other conditions affecting the pancreas include pancreatitis, an inflammation often caused by gallstones or heavy drinking, and pancreatic cancer.",
s:[["Islets of Langerhans","The islets were discovered in 1869 by the German medical student Paul Langerhans, who did not know their purpose. They make up only about 1 to 2 percent of the pancreas but are vital for controlling blood sugar."],["Discovering insulin","In 1921–22, Frederick Banting, Charles Best, James Collip and John Macleod in Toronto, Canada, extracted insulin and used it to treat a 14-year-old boy, Leonard Thompson, who had type 1 diabetes. Banting and Macleod won the 1923 Nobel Prize."],["Type 1 and type 2 diabetes","In type 1 diabetes, the immune system destroys the insulin-making cells. In type 2, the body does not respond well to insulin and may not make enough. Both lead to high blood sugar if not treated."],["Transplants","Pancreas and islet transplants can restore insulin production for some people with severe type 1 diabetes. Researchers are also testing islets grown from stem cells."]],
k:[["Location","Behind the stomach"],["Length","About 15 cm"],["Digestive juice","About 1–1.5 liters a day"],["Key hormones","Insulin and glucagon"],["Hormone cells","Islets of Langerhans"],["Related disease","Diabetes"]],
d:[["1869","Paul Langerhans describes the islets"],["1889","Removing the pancreas shown to cause diabetes in dogs"],["1922","Insulin first used to treat diabetes"],["1923","Nobel Prize for insulin"]],
s2:[["Sweetbreads","In cooking, the pancreas of calves or lambs is sometimes eaten as ‘sweetbread’."],["Blood sugar balance","Insulin and glucagon work like a seesaw, keeping blood sugar within a narrow healthy range."]],
g:[["Pancreas","An organ making digestive juices and hormones"],["Insulin","A hormone that lowers blood sugar"],["Glucagon","A hormone that raises blood sugar"],["Pancreatitis","Inflammation of the pancreas"]],
f:["The pancreas makes insulin.","The pancreas makes digestive enzymes.","Insulin was first used in 1922."],
fun:"The first person treated with insulin, in 1922, was a 14-year-old boy in Toronto."});

ADDART({id:"cholesterol",t:"Cholesterol",c:"Health and the Body",i:"🩸",w:"Cholesterol",r:["heartdisease","circulation","nutri","liver","hypertension","exercise"],
p:[["Needed by the body","Cholesterol builds cells and hormones."],["Good and bad","HDL and LDL carry it in the blood."],["Too much","High LDL can clog arteries."]],
l:"Cholesterol is a waxy, fat-like substance found in every cell of the body. It is essential: the body uses it to build cell membranes, to make vitamin D and hormones such as estrogen, testosterone and cortisol, and to make bile, which helps digest fats. The liver makes most of the cholesterol the body needs, and some comes from foods of animal origin, such as meat, eggs and dairy. Because cholesterol does not dissolve in blood, it is carried by particles called lipoproteins. Low-density lipoprotein (LDL), often called ‘bad’ cholesterol, carries cholesterol to the tissues; too much LDL can build up in the walls of arteries, forming fatty plaques that narrow them, a process called atherosclerosis, which raises the risk of heart attacks and strokes. High-density lipoprotein (HDL), sometimes called ‘good’ cholesterol, carries extra cholesterol back to the liver. Cholesterol levels are affected by genes, diet, weight, exercise and smoking. High cholesterol usually causes no symptoms, so it is found with a blood test, and it can be lowered with lifestyle changes and medicines such as statins.",
s:[["LDL and HDL","Think of LDL as delivery trucks dropping cholesterol off and HDL as trucks collecting it. High LDL is linked to heart disease. Higher HDL has usually been linked to lower risk, though raising HDL with drugs has not proved helpful."],["Diet","Eating less saturated fat, found in fatty meats, butter and some oils, and trans fats can lower LDL. Fiber from oats, beans and fruit also helps. For most people, cholesterol in food has a smaller effect than saturated fat."],["Statins","Statins, first approved in 1987, block an enzyme the liver uses to make cholesterol. Large studies show they lower the risk of heart attacks and strokes in people at high risk. Other drugs can lower LDL further when needed."],["Inherited high cholesterol","Familial hypercholesterolemia is an inherited condition causing very high LDL from birth. It affects roughly 1 in 250 people, and early treatment can prevent heart disease."]],
k:[["Made by","Mainly the liver"],["Used for","Cell membranes, hormones, vitamin D, bile"],["‘Bad’ cholesterol","LDL"],["‘Good’ cholesterol","HDL"],["Risk","Atherosclerosis, heart attack, stroke"],["Common medicine","Statins"]],
d:[["1769","Cholesterol first found in gallstones"],["1913","Nikolai Anichkov links cholesterol to artery disease"],["1985","Nobel Prize for research on how cells take in LDL"],["1987","First statin approved"]],
s2:[["Plants","Plants do not make cholesterol, but they contain similar substances called plant sterols, which can help block cholesterol absorption."],["Brain","About a quarter of the body’s cholesterol is in the brain, where it helps insulate nerve fibers."]],
g:[["Cholesterol","A waxy substance in cells"],["Lipoprotein","A particle carrying fats in the blood"],["Atherosclerosis","Hardening and narrowing of arteries"],["Statin","A medicine that lowers cholesterol"]],
f:["The liver makes most cholesterol.","LDL can build up in arteries.","High cholesterol usually has no symptoms."],
fun:"About a quarter of all the cholesterol in your body is in your brain."});

ADDART({id:"handedness",t:"Left- and Right-Handedness",c:"Health and the Body",i:"✋",w:"Handedness",r:["brain","nervous","genome","twins","writing"],
p:[["Most people are right-handed","About 9 in 10 people prefer their right hand."],["Lefties","About 10 percent are left-handed."],["Genes and chance","Handedness comes from many genes and early development."]],
l:"Handedness is a person’s natural preference for using one hand over the other for tasks such as writing, throwing and using tools. Across the world, about 90 percent of people are right-handed and about 10 percent are left-handed, with a small number ambidextrous, able to use both hands about equally well. This balance appears to have been similar for thousands of years, judging from ancient tools, cave art handprints and teeth marks. Handedness is linked to how the brain is organized: each side of the brain controls the opposite side of the body, and in most people, language is handled mainly by the left half. Scientists believe handedness is influenced by many genes, each with a tiny effect, together with chance events during development before birth; even identical twins can have different handedness. Babies may show a preference in the womb, and most children settle on a dominant hand by about age 4 to 6. In the past, many left-handed children were forced to write with their right hands, a practice now discouraged in most countries.",
s:[["The brain","About 95 percent of right-handers process language mainly in the left side of the brain. For left-handers the figure is around 70 percent; the rest use the right side or both."],["Left-handers in sport","Left-handers are more common at the top of interactive sports such as tennis, fencing, cricket, baseball and boxing, perhaps because opponents are less used to facing them."],["Tools and design","Many everyday objects, such as scissors, can openers and some desks, are designed for right-handers. Left-handed versions are now widely available."],["Words and beliefs","In many languages, words for ‘right’ also mean correct or skillful, while ‘left’ words had negative meanings; the English word ‘sinister’ comes from the Latin for ‘left’. Such beliefs led to left-handers being stigmatized in the past."]],
k:[["Right-handed","About 90% of people"],["Left-handed","About 10%"],["Language side (right-handers)","Usually the left brain"],["Decided by","Many genes plus chance"],["Hand preference settles","By about age 4–6"],["International Left-Handers Day","13 August"]],
s2:[["Other sides","People also have a preferred foot, eye and ear, which do not always match their dominant hand."],["Animals","Some animals also show handedness, such as parrots favoring one foot for holding food and kangaroos favoring their left paw."]],
g:[["Handedness","Preference for one hand"],["Ambidextrous","Able to use both hands equally well"],["Dominant hand","The hand a person prefers"],["Hemisphere","One half of the brain"]],
f:["About 10 percent of people are left-handed.","Each side of the brain controls the opposite side of the body.","Handedness is influenced by many genes."],
fun:"The word ‘sinister’ comes from the Latin word for ‘left’."});

