ADDART({id:"ceratosaurus",t:"Ceratosaurus",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦖",w:"Ceratosaurus",r:["allosaurus","stegosaurus","diplodocus","dino","fossils","carnotaurus"],
p:[["A horned hunter","It had a horn on its nose and two above its eyes."],["Jurassic predator","It lived alongside Allosaurus and Stegosaurus."],["Long, blade-like teeth","Its upper teeth were unusually long."]],
l:"Ceratosaurus was a meat-eating dinosaur that lived about 153 to 148 million years ago, in the Late Jurassic, in what are now the western United States and Portugal. It was about 5 to 7 meters long and weighed perhaps 400 to 1,000 kilograms, smaller than its neighbor Allosaurus. Its name means ‘horned lizard’, after the narrow, blade-like horn on its snout and two smaller hornlets above its eyes, which were probably used for display rather than fighting. Ceratosaurus had a large head with very long, curved upper teeth, a deep tail and, unusually for a theropod, a row of small bony plates called osteoderms running down its back. Its four-fingered hands were short. Fossils show it lived in the same floodplains as Allosaurus, Torvosaurus, Stegosaurus and giant sauropods such as Diplodocus. Some scientists think Ceratosaurus may have hunted in and around water, catching fish and crocodiles, which would have helped it avoid competing with Allosaurus. It was named in 1884 by the American paleontologist Othniel Charles Marsh from a skeleton found in Colorado.",
s:[["Discovery","The first skeleton was found at Garden Park near Cañon City, Colorado, in 1883 and named by O. C. Marsh the next year, during the so-called ‘Bone Wars’ between Marsh and his rival Edward Drinker Cope. It is displayed at the Smithsonian’s National Museum of Natural History."],["Horns and teeth","Ceratosaurus’s nose horn was thin and fragile, so it was likely for show. Its upper teeth were so long that they reached below the lower jaw when its mouth was closed, a feature shared with few other theropods."],["Living with giants","The Morrison Formation, where many Ceratosaurus fossils are found, contains a remarkable range of dinosaurs. Ceratosaurus fossils are rarer than Allosaurus, suggesting it was less common."],["Relatives","Ceratosaurus gives its name to the ceratosaurs, a group that later included the abelisaurids such as Carnotaurus and Majungasaurus."]],
k:[["Lived","About 153–148 million years ago"],["Where","Western United States and Portugal"],["Length","About 5–7 meters"],["Diet","Meat, possibly fish"],["Name means","Horned lizard"],["Named","1884, by O. C. Marsh"]],
d:[["1883","First skeleton found in Colorado"],["1884","Ceratosaurus named"],["2000","Ceratosaurus fossils described from Portugal"]],
s2:[["In films","Ceratosaurus appears in Jurassic Park III and Jurassic World: Fallen Kingdom, as well as older dinosaur films."],["Back plates","The small bony plates along its back may have offered a little protection or been for display."]],
g:[["Theropod","A two-legged, mostly meat-eating dinosaur"],["Hornlet","A small horn"],["Osteoderm","A bony plate in the skin"],["Morrison Formation","Rock layers in the western United States rich in Jurassic fossils"]],
f:["Ceratosaurus had a horn on its nose.","Ceratosaurus lived in the Jurassic.","It lived alongside Allosaurus."],
fun:"Ceratosaurus’s upper teeth were so long they stuck out below its lower jaw."});

ADDART({id:"kentrosaurus",t:"Kentrosaurus",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦔",w:"Kentrosaurus",r:["stegosaurus","brachiosaurus","ankylosaurus","dino","fossils","tanzaniacountry"],
p:[["A spiky stegosaur","Rows of plates turned into long spikes down its back."],["African dinosaur","Found in Tanzania."],["Shoulder spikes","Long spikes stuck out from its shoulders."]],
l:"Kentrosaurus was a plant-eating stegosaur, a relative of Stegosaurus, that lived about 152 million years ago, in the Late Jurassic, in what is now Tanzania in East Africa. It was smaller than Stegosaurus, about 4 to 4.5 meters long and weighing around 1 tonne. Its name means ‘sharp-point lizard’. Like Stegosaurus, it had a small head, a heavy body and two rows of bony plates along its neck and back, but toward the hips the plates turned into long, sharp spikes, and a pair of long spikes jutted out from its shoulders or hips. It probably defended itself by swinging its spiky tail, which studies suggest could whip around fast enough to cause serious wounds to attacking predators. Kentrosaurus was discovered during German expeditions to Tendaguru Hill between 1909 and 1912, which also unearthed the giant sauropod Giraffatitan. Hundreds of Kentrosaurus bones were found, but many were destroyed when a museum in Germany was bombed during the Second World War.",
s:[["Tendaguru","The Tendaguru expeditions, led by German paleontologists using hundreds of local workers, dug up about 250 tonnes of fossils, which were carried by hand to the coast and shipped to Berlin. The site is one of the richest Jurassic fossil beds in Africa."],["Tail weapon","Computer models published in 2013 suggested Kentrosaurus could swing its tail through a wide arc, striking with its spikes hard enough to break bones of predators."],["Losses in the war","Much of the Kentrosaurus material kept in Göttingen and Berlin was lost or damaged by bombing in the Second World War. A mounted skeleton survives in Berlin’s Museum für Naturkunde."],["Shoulder or hip?","Scientists have debated whether the long side spikes grew from the shoulders or the hips. Most reconstructions now place them on the shoulders."]],
k:[["Lived","About 152 million years ago"],["Where","Tanzania"],["Length","About 4–4.5 meters"],["Weight","About 1 tonne"],["Group","Stegosaur"],["Name means","Sharp-point lizard"]],
d:[["1909–1912","German expeditions dig at Tendaguru"],["1915","Kentrosaurus named"],["2013","Study models its tail swing"]],
s2:[["Small brain","Like other stegosaurs, Kentrosaurus had a very small brain for its body size."],["Related across continents","Stegosaurs lived in North America, Europe, Africa and Asia, showing how land was connected in the Jurassic."]],
g:[["Stegosaur","A plated, spiky plant-eating dinosaur"],["Plate","A flat bony structure on the back"],["Spike","A long, sharp point"],["Excavation","Digging up fossils or remains"]],
f:["Kentrosaurus was a stegosaur.","Kentrosaurus was found in Tanzania.","It had long spikes on its tail and shoulders."],
fun:"Kentrosaurus’s tail spikes could swing fast enough to break a predator’s bones."});

ADDART({id:"amargasaurus",t:"Amargasaurus",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦕",w:"Amargasaurus",r:["diplodocus","brachiosaurus","argentinosaurus","dino","fossils","patagonia"],
p:[["Spiny neck","Two rows of tall spines ran along its neck."],["A smaller sauropod","About 10 meters long."],["From Argentina","It lived about 129 to 122 million years ago."]],
l:"Amargasaurus was a long-necked, plant-eating sauropod dinosaur that lived in what is now Neuquén Province, Argentina, about 129 to 122 million years ago, in the Early Cretaceous. At around 9 to 10 meters long, it was small for a sauropod. Its most remarkable feature was a double row of tall, thin spines growing from the bones of its neck and back, the longest about 60 centimeters long. Scientists are not sure what the spines looked like in life: they may have supported a skin sail or frill, been covered in horny sheaths like a bull’s horns, or stood as separate spikes used for display, defense or both. Amargasaurus belonged to the dicraeosaurids, relatives of Diplodocus with short necks for sauropods, which probably fed on low-growing plants rather than treetops. It is known from a single, fairly complete skeleton discovered in 1984 by an expedition led by the Argentine paleontologist José Bonaparte, the same scientist who found Carnotaurus, and named in 1991 after the La Amarga Canyon where it was found.",
s:[["The spines","The spines were extensions of the vertebrae, pointing up and slightly back. A 2022 study suggested they were covered in keratin, the material of horns and claws, making them look like a double row of horns, possibly used for display or to deter predators."],["Feeding","Studies of its inner ear and neck suggest Amargasaurus usually held its head low, about 80 centimeters above the ground, browsing on low plants."],["Relatives","Close relatives include Dicraeosaurus from Tanzania and Bajadasaurus, another Argentine sauropod with very long neck spines, described in 2019."],["Discovery","The skeleton was found in the La Amarga Formation in 1984 and named Amargasaurus cazaui in 1991 by Leonardo Salgado and José Bonaparte. It is displayed in Buenos Aires."]],
k:[["Lived","About 129–122 million years ago"],["Where","Neuquén, Argentina"],["Length","About 9–10 meters"],["Diet","Low-growing plants"],["Famous feature","Double row of neck spines"],["Longest spines","About 60 cm"]],
d:[["1984","Skeleton found by Bonaparte’s team"],["1991","Amargasaurus named"],["2019","Relative Bajadasaurus described"],["2022","Study suggests horn-covered spines"]],
s2:[["Sail or spikes?","Museum models show Amargasaurus in many ways, with sails, frills or bare spikes, because the soft parts were not preserved."],["Name","It is named after La Amarga, meaning ‘the bitter one’, the canyon where it was found."]],
g:[["Sauropod","A huge, long-necked plant-eating dinosaur"],["Vertebra","A bone of the backbone"],["Keratin","The material of horns, claws and hair"],["Browse","To feed on leaves and shoots"]],
f:["Amargasaurus had spines along its neck.","Amargasaurus was a sauropod.","It was found in Argentina."],
fun:"Amargasaurus’s neck spines were taller than a ruler is long."});

ADDART({id:"pliosaurus",t:"Pliosaurs",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦈",w:"Pliosauridae",r:["marinereptiles","elasmosaurus","ichthyosaurs","mosasaurus","nothosaurus","megalodon"],
p:[["Short-necked sea monsters","Big heads, powerful jaws and four flippers."],["Top ocean predators","They ruled Jurassic and Cretaceous seas."],["A giant skull","A huge skull was dug from English cliffs in 2023."]],
l:"Pliosaurs were large marine reptiles that lived in the oceans from about 200 to 90 million years ago, during the Jurassic and Cretaceous periods. They were close relatives of the long-necked plesiosaurs but had short necks, enormous heads and massive jaws lined with large, conical teeth, making them among the top predators of the ancient seas. They swam with four large flippers, flying through the water rather like sea turtles or penguins. Famous pliosaurs include Pliosaurus and Liopleurodon from Europe, Kronosaurus from Australia and Colombia, and Megacephalosaurus from North America. The biggest reached around 10 to 12 meters long, with skulls up to about 2 meters, and could bite with immense force, feeding on fish, squid-like animals, sharks and other marine reptiles. In 2023, a nearly complete pliosaur skull about 2 meters long was excavated from the cliffs of the Jurassic Coast in Dorset, England, after a fossil collector found its snout on the beach; it became famous through a television documentary by David Attenborough.",
s:[["The Dorset skull","Local fossil collector Phil Jacobs found the tip of the snout on Kimmeridge Bay beach in 2022. Steve Etches and a team abseiled down the cliff to remove the rest. The skull, about 150 million years old, is displayed at the Etches Collection museum in Kimmeridge."],["Exaggerated sizes","Liopleurodon was once described as 25 meters long, a figure shown in the 1999 television series Walking with Dinosaurs. Scientists now think it reached about 6 to 7 meters, and the largest pliosaurs about 10 to 12 meters."],["Swimming","Pliosaurs used all four flippers. Studies suggest they could make fast bursts to ambush prey, with the front and back flippers working together."],["Stomach contents","Fossil stomach contents show that Kronosaurus ate turtles and plesiosaurs, and other pliosaurs ate fish and ammonites."]],
k:[["Lived","About 200–90 million years ago"],["Group","Short-necked plesiosaurs"],["Largest","About 10–12 meters long"],["Skull","Up to about 2 meters"],["Diet","Fish, squid, sharks, other reptiles"],["Famous kinds","Pliosaurus, Liopleurodon, Kronosaurus"]],
d:[["1841","Pliosaurus named by Richard Owen"],["1924","Kronosaurus named from Australian fossils"],["1999","Liopleurodon stars in Walking with Dinosaurs"],["2023","Giant pliosaur skull excavated in Dorset"]],
s2:[["Not dinosaurs","Pliosaurs were marine reptiles, not dinosaurs, though they lived at the same time."],["Living today?","Some people have claimed the Loch Ness Monster is a surviving plesiosaur or pliosaur, but there is no evidence; these reptiles died out millions of years ago."]],
g:[["Pliosaur","A short-necked, big-headed marine reptile"],["Plesiosaur","A long-necked marine reptile"],["Flipper","A flat limb for swimming"],["Ammonite","An extinct shelled sea creature"]],
f:["Pliosaurs were marine reptiles.","Pliosaurs had short necks and big heads.","A giant pliosaur skull was found in England in 2023."],
fun:"Some pliosaur skulls were longer than an adult person is tall."});

ADDART({id:"andrewsarchus",t:"Andrewsarchus",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐗",w:"Andrewsarchus",r:["iceagemammals","whale","fossils","paraceratherium","basilosaurus","mammoth"],
p:[["A giant mystery mammal","Known mainly from one huge skull."],["Mongolia","Found in 1923 in Inner Mongolia."],["Related to hoofed animals","Probably a relative of pigs and hippos."]],
l:"Andrewsarchus was a large mammal that lived about 45 to 42 million years ago, in the Eocene Epoch, in what is now Inner Mongolia, China. It is known mainly from a single enormous skull, about 83 centimeters long, discovered in 1923 by Kan Chuen-pao, a member of the Central Asiatic Expeditions of the American Museum of Natural History, and named after the expedition’s leader, Roy Chapman Andrews. The skull, with large teeth and a long snout, is one of the biggest of any land mammal predator or scavenger, and Andrewsarchus has often been called the largest meat-eating land mammal ever. But because the rest of the body has not been found, its size, appearance and diet are uncertain. Scientists once thought it was related to a group of wolf-like hoofed mammals called mesonychids; more recent studies place it among the even-toed hoofed mammals, the group that includes pigs, hippos, camels and, more distantly, whales. It may have been an omnivore, eating plants as well as meat, perhaps like a giant hog, cracking bones and shells with its powerful jaws.",
s:[["The skull","The skull is displayed at the American Museum of Natural History in New York. Its huge size led to estimates that Andrewsarchus might have been 3 to 4 meters long, but these are guesses."],["Classification","Andrewsarchus has been placed in several groups over the years. Some studies link it with entelodonts, sometimes called ‘hell pigs’, and others with the ancestors of hippos and whales."],["New fossils","In 2024, scientists described new jaw and tooth fossils of Andrewsarchus from Inner Mongolia, giving more clues about its age and relationships."],["Eocene world","During the Eocene, the world was warmer than today, and many mammal groups, such as early horses, rhinos and whales, were evolving rapidly."]],
k:[["Lived","About 45–42 million years ago (Eocene)"],["Where","Inner Mongolia, China"],["Known from","Mainly one skull (83 cm)"],["Discovered","1923, by Kan Chuen-pao"],["Named after","Roy Chapman Andrews"],["Relatives","Even-toed hoofed mammals"]],
d:[["1923","Skull found in Inner Mongolia"],["1924","Andrewsarchus named"],["2024","New jaw fossils described"]],
s2:[["Documentary star","Andrewsarchus appeared in the 2001 television series Walking with Beasts as a giant scavenger on an Eocene beach."],["Whale cousins","Even-toed hoofed mammals are the closest living land relatives of whales; hippos are the closest of all."]],
g:[["Eocene","The epoch from about 56 to 34 million years ago"],["Omnivore","An animal that eats plants and meat"],["Even-toed hoofed mammal","Pigs, hippos, camels, deer and their relatives"],["Skull","The bones of the head"]],
f:["Andrewsarchus is known mainly from one skull.","It lived in Inner Mongolia.","Its body size is uncertain."],
fun:"Andrewsarchus’s skull, about 83 centimeters long, is longer than most adults’ arms."});

ADDART({id:"cavebear",t:"The Cave Bear",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐻",w:"Cave bear",r:["iceagemammals","mammoth","neanderthals","caveart","caves","woollyrhino"],
p:[["An Ice Age giant","A large bear that lived in Europe and Asia."],["Cave sleepers","Many died while hibernating in caves."],["Mostly plant-eating","Its teeth show a largely vegetarian diet."]],
l:"The cave bear was a large bear that lived across Europe and western Asia during the last ice age, from about 300,000 years ago until it became extinct around 24,000 years ago. Males could weigh 500 kilograms or more, larger than most brown bears today, and had a high, domed forehead and heavy limbs. It got its name because its bones are found in enormous numbers in caves, where many bears died during their winter hibernation over thousands of years; some caves contain the remains of tens of thousands of bears. Studies of its teeth and chemical signatures in its bones show that the cave bear ate mostly plants, such as grasses, leaves, roots and berries, unlike the more mixed diet of brown bears. Early humans and Neanderthals shared the landscape with cave bears and competed with them for caves, and cave bears appear in Ice Age cave art, including at Chauvet Cave in France. Their extinction may have been caused by colder climate reducing plant food, combined with competition from and hunting by humans. Some cave bear DNA survives in living brown bears.",
s:[["Bear caves","Caves such as Drachenloch in Switzerland, the Bear Cave in Romania and Chauvet in France contain huge numbers of cave bear bones, scratches on the walls and hollows where bears slept. In medieval times, people sometimes thought the skulls belonged to dragons."],["Diet","Cave bears had broad molars suited to grinding tough plants. Chemical studies of nitrogen in their bones suggest most were vegetarian, which may have made them vulnerable when the climate cooled and plant food became scarce."],["Extinction","DNA studies show cave bear numbers began falling about 50,000 years ago, around when modern humans spread across Europe. Climate cooling during the coldest part of the ice age finished them off."],["Genes today","A 2018 study found that some living brown bears carry small amounts of cave bear DNA, inherited from ancient interbreeding."]],
k:[["Lived","About 300,000–24,000 years ago"],["Where","Europe and western Asia"],["Weight","Males up to about 500 kg or more"],["Diet","Mostly plants"],["Name from","Bones found in caves"],["Extinction causes","Cold climate and human competition"]],
d:[["1774","Cave bear bones described from caves in Germany"],["1794","Ursus spelaeus named"],["1994","Chauvet Cave, with cave bear images, discovered"],["2018","Cave bear DNA found in brown bears"]],
s2:[["Dragon bones","Before cave bears were understood, their large skulls found in caves were sometimes displayed as dragon skulls."],["Not a cave dweller all year","Cave bears used caves mainly for hibernation and giving birth, spending the rest of the year outdoors."]],
g:[["Hibernation","A long, deep winter sleep"],["Ice age","A long period of cold climate with large ice sheets"],["Interbreeding","Mating between different species or populations"],["Molar","A broad back tooth for grinding"]],
f:["Cave bears ate mostly plants.","Many cave bears died while hibernating.","Cave bears appear in Ice Age cave art."],
fun:"Some caves contain the bones of tens of thousands of cave bears, built up over thousands of winters."});

ADDART({id:"fog",t:"Fog",c:"Earth Science",i:"🌫️",w:"Fog",r:["clouds","wcycle","weatherforecast","cloudforests","airpollution","climate"],
p:[["A cloud at ground level","Fog is made of tiny water droplets in the air."],["Many kinds","Radiation fog, sea fog, valley fog and more."],["Dangerous and useful","It blocks visibility but can water deserts."]],
l:"Fog is a cloud that touches the ground. It is made of billions of tiny water droplets, or sometimes ice crystals, suspended in the air, and by definition it reduces visibility to less than 1 kilometer. Fog forms when air near the ground cools to its dew point, the temperature at which water vapor condenses into droplets, or when extra moisture is added to the air. Tiny particles of dust, salt or pollution give the droplets something to form on. There are several kinds. Radiation fog forms on clear, calm nights when the ground loses heat and cools the air above it, often filling valleys by morning. Advection fog forms when warm, moist air moves over a cold surface, such as the cold ocean currents off San Francisco, Peru, Chile and Namibia. Sea smoke, or steam fog, forms when cold air passes over warmer water. Fog can be very dangerous for road, air and sea travel, and in polluted cities it can mix with smoke to form smog. But in some dry coastal deserts, fog is a vital source of water for plants, animals and even people, who collect it with mesh nets.",
s:[["Famous foggy places","The Grand Banks off Newfoundland, Canada, where cold and warm ocean currents meet, are among the foggiest places on Earth. San Francisco’s summer fog, nicknamed ‘Karl’ by some residents, rolls in through the Golden Gate."],["Fog in deserts","In the Atacama Desert of Chile and the Namib Desert, fog drifting in from the ocean supplies moisture to plants and animals. The Namib fog-basking beetle stands on dunes with its back to the wind, collecting droplets that run down to its mouth."],["Fog catchers","Villages in Chile, Peru, Morocco and elsewhere use large mesh nets called fog collectors to capture drinking and irrigation water from fog."],["Smog","In December 1952, a thick, smoky fog called the Great Smog of London lasted five days and caused thousands of deaths, leading to clean air laws in the United Kingdom."]],
k:[["Definition","A cloud at ground level"],["Visibility","Less than 1 kilometer"],["Forms when","Air cools to its dew point"],["Main types","Radiation, advection, steam, valley fog"],["Foggy place","Grand Banks, Newfoundland"],["Useful for","Watering coastal deserts"]],
d:[["1952","Great Smog of London"],["1987","Fog collection project begins in Chungungo, Chile"]],
s2:[["Mist or fog?","Mist is thinner than fog: if you can see more than 1 kilometer, it is called mist."],["Fog horns","Lighthouses and ships use loud fog horns to warn others of their position in thick fog."]],
g:[["Fog","A cloud touching the ground"],["Dew point","The temperature at which water vapor condenses"],["Condensation","Gas turning into liquid"],["Smog","A mixture of smoke and fog, or polluted haze"]],
f:["Fog is a cloud at ground level.","Fog forms when air cools to its dew point.","Some deserts get water from fog."],
fun:"The Namib fog-basking beetle drinks by catching fog droplets on its back."});

ADDART({id:"estuaries",t:"Estuaries",c:"Earth Science",i:"🏞️",w:"Estuary",r:["rivers","deltas","wetlands","coasts","tides","fish"],
p:[["Where rivers meet the sea","Fresh water mixes with salt water."],["Nurseries of the sea","Many fish start life in estuaries."],["Busy places","Many great ports and cities grew on estuaries."]],
l:"An estuary is a partly enclosed body of water where a river meets the sea, and fresh water from the river mixes with salt water from the ocean, producing brackish water, which is slightly salty. The amount of salt changes with the tides, the seasons and the river’s flow. Estuaries form in different ways: some are drowned river valleys, flooded when sea levels rose after the last ice age, such as the Chesapeake Bay in the United States; others are created by sandbars or barrier islands that shelter the coast, or by glaciers, like fjords. Estuaries are among the most productive ecosystems on Earth. Rivers bring nutrients, and the shallow, sheltered waters support salt marshes, mangroves, seagrass beds and mudflats that feed huge numbers of birds and act as nurseries for fish, shrimp and shellfish. They also filter pollution and protect coasts from storms. Because they offer sheltered harbors and access inland, many of the world’s great cities grew up beside estuaries, including London on the Thames, New York on the Hudson, Shanghai near the Yangtze and Buenos Aires on the Río de la Plata.",
s:[["Mixing waters","Salt water is denser than fresh water, so in many estuaries a wedge of seawater slides under the river water along the bottom. Tides push salt water in and out twice a day."],["Life in estuaries","Salt marshes and mangroves trap sediment and provide shelter. Estuaries provide nursery habitat for many commercially important fish, and mudflats feed millions of migrating shorebirds."],["The Río de la Plata","The Río de la Plata between Argentina and Uruguay, formed by the Paraná and Uruguay rivers, is one of the widest estuaries in the world, about 220 kilometers across at its mouth."],["Threats","Estuaries face pollution from farms, cities and industry, land reclamation, dredging for shipping and rising sea levels. Too many nutrients can cause algal blooms and low-oxygen ‘dead zones’."]],
k:[["Definition","Where a river meets the sea"],["Water","Brackish (slightly salty)"],["Habitats","Salt marshes, mangroves, mudflats, seagrass"],["Role","Nurseries for fish and shellfish"],["Largest in the US","Chesapeake Bay"],["Very wide estuary","Río de la Plata"]],
s2:[["Bore waves","In some estuaries, incoming tides form a wave called a tidal bore that travels upriver. The Qiantang River bore in China can be several meters high."],["Thames recovery","The Thames estuary in London was once so polluted it was declared ‘biologically dead’ in the 1950s; today seals, seahorses and over 100 kinds of fish live there."]],
g:[["Estuary","Where a river meets the sea"],["Brackish","Slightly salty water"],["Salt marsh","A coastal grassland flooded by tides"],["Tidal bore","A wave that travels up a river with the tide"]],
f:["Estuaries mix fresh and salt water.","Estuaries are nurseries for fish.","Many great cities grew on estuaries."],
fun:"The Qiantang River’s tidal bore in China draws crowds who watch a wall of water rush upriver."});

ADDART({id:"badlands",t:"Badlands and Hoodoos",c:"Earth Science",i:"🪨",w:"Badlands",r:["climate","canyon","desert","fossils","rocks","plateaus"],
p:[["Land carved by water","Soft rock eroded into steep ridges and gullies."],["Striped hills","Layers of colorful rock are exposed."],["Hoodoos","Tall rock pillars topped with harder caps."]],
l:"Badlands are dry areas where soft sedimentary rocks, such as clay, mudstone and sandstone, have been heavily eroded by wind and water into a maze of steep slopes, sharp ridges, narrow gullies and pinnacles, with little vegetation. They form where rare but heavy rains wash quickly over bare, soft ground, carving it away faster than plants can grow. The name comes from early travelers who found such land ‘bad’ to cross; in French it was ‘mauvaises terres à traverser’ and in the Lakota language ‘makȟóšiča’. The exposed layers of rock often form colorful stripes, red, yellow, gray and purple, revealing millions of years of Earth’s history, and badlands are among the best places to find fossils, because erosion constantly uncovers new bones. Hoodoos, also called fairy chimneys or earth pyramids, are tall, thin spires of rock found in badlands and similar landscapes; they form where a hard cap rock protects the softer rock beneath it from erosion. Famous badlands and hoodoo landscapes include Badlands National Park and Bryce Canyon in the United States, Dinosaur Provincial Park in Canada, Cappadocia in Türkiye and the Valley of the Moon in Argentina.",
s:[["How hoodoos form","Frost wedging, where water freezes and expands in cracks, and rain gradually wear away rock. Where a layer of harder rock sits on top, it acts like an umbrella, protecting the column beneath. Bryce Canyon in Utah has the largest collection of hoodoos in the world."],["Fossil treasure","Dinosaur Provincial Park in Alberta, Canada, has yielded fossils of more than 50 dinosaur species. Badlands National Park in South Dakota is rich in fossils of ancient mammals such as early horses and rhinos."],["Cappadocia","In Cappadocia, Türkiye, volcanic ash hardened into soft rock that eroded into ‘fairy chimneys’. For centuries, people carved homes, churches and even underground cities into them."],["Fast-changing landscapes","Badlands erode quickly by geological standards. In some places, slopes wear away by over a centimeter a year, and hoodoos eventually topple when their caps fall."]],
k:[["Rock type","Soft sedimentary rocks"],["Formed by","Erosion from rare, heavy rains"],["Hoodoo","A rock spire with a hard cap"],["Most hoodoos","Bryce Canyon, Utah"],["Fossil site","Dinosaur Provincial Park, Canada"],["Fairy chimneys","Cappadocia, Türkiye"]],
s2:[["Ischigualasto","Argentina’s Ischigualasto, or Valley of the Moon, preserves some of the earliest dinosaurs, about 230 million years old."],["Mars-like","Some badlands look so otherworldly that they are used to test Mars rovers and as sets for science fiction films."]],
g:[["Badlands","Heavily eroded, dry land"],["Hoodoo","A tall spire of rock"],["Sedimentary rock","Rock formed from layers of sediment"],["Gully","A narrow channel cut by water"]],
f:["Badlands are carved by erosion.","Hoodoos have hard caps.","Badlands are good places to find fossils."],
fun:"In Cappadocia, people carved entire underground cities into soft volcanic rock."});

ADDART({id:"rockarches",t:"Natural Arches",c:"Earth Science",i:"🌉",w:"Natural arch",r:["badlands","canyon","coasts","rocks","waterfalls","plateaus"],
p:[["Bridges of rock","Openings carved through rock by nature."],["Wind, water and ice","Erosion wears holes through fins of rock."],["Not forever","Arches eventually collapse."]],
l:"A natural arch is a rock formation in which an opening has been worn through a wall or ridge of rock, leaving a bridge of stone overhead. Arches form by erosion: water, ice, wind and gravity slowly wear away weaker parts of the rock while stronger parts remain. In deserts, arches usually begin as narrow walls of sandstone called fins; rainwater seeping in, freezing and thawing loosens grains and flakes from the sides until a hole breaks through and widens. On coasts, waves pounding a headland can carve caves from both sides that meet to form a sea arch. A natural bridge is a type of arch formed by a stream or river cutting through rock. Arches National Park in Utah, United States, has more than 2,000 arches, including the famous Delicate Arch and Landscape Arch, one of the longest in the world at about 88 meters. Other famous arches include Durdle Door in England, the arches of Zhangjiajie in China and the Rock of Gibraltar’s nearby coasts. Arches are temporary: eventually they become too thin and collapse, as Wall Arch in Utah did in 2008 and Malta’s Azure Window did in 2017.",
s:[["Arches National Park","The park in Utah contains the greatest concentration of natural arches in the world. They formed in salmon-colored Entrada sandstone over millions of years, helped by salt layers deep underground that pushed the rock up and cracked it."],["Sea arches","Durdle Door on England’s Jurassic Coast is a limestone sea arch about 10,000 years old. When a sea arch collapses, the outer pillar left standing is called a sea stack."],["The longest arches","The longest known natural arches are in China, including Fairy Bridge (Xianren Bridge) in Guangxi, with a span of about 120 meters."],["Collapses","Malta’s Azure Window, a famous sea arch, fell into the sea during a storm in March 2017. In Utah, part of Landscape Arch fell in 1991, and Wall Arch collapsed in 2008."]],
k:[["Formed by","Erosion from water, ice and wind"],["Desert arches start as","Sandstone fins"],["Coastal type","Sea arch"],["Most arches","Arches National Park, Utah (over 2,000)"],["Long arch","Landscape Arch, about 88 m"],["Longest known","Fairy Bridge, China, about 120 m"]],
d:[["1929","Arches National Monument created in Utah"],["1991","Slab falls from Landscape Arch"],["2008","Wall Arch collapses"],["2017","Malta’s Azure Window collapses"]],
s2:[["Licence plate star","Delicate Arch appears on Utah’s license plates."],["Arch or bridge?","Geologists usually call it a natural bridge when running water carved the opening, and an arch when other forms of erosion did."]],
g:[["Natural arch","An opening worn through rock"],["Fin","A narrow wall of rock"],["Sea stack","A pillar of rock left in the sea"],["Erosion","The wearing away of rock and soil"]],
f:["Natural arches are made by erosion.","Arches National Park has over 2,000 arches.","Arches eventually collapse."],
fun:"Malta’s famous Azure Window arch collapsed into the sea in a single stormy morning in 2017."});

ADDART({id:"seaice",t:"Sea Ice",c:"Earth Science",i:"🧊",w:"Sea ice",r:["arctic","antarc","icebergs","climchange","polarbear","sealevel"],
p:[["Frozen ocean","Sea ice forms when seawater freezes."],["Growing and shrinking","It expands each winter and melts each summer."],["Shrinking Arctic","Arctic summer ice has declined sharply since 1979."]],
l:"Sea ice is frozen seawater that floats on the ocean surface in the polar regions. Unlike icebergs, which break off glaciers and are made of fresh water from snow, sea ice forms directly from the ocean. Because salt lowers the freezing point, seawater freezes at about −1.8 °C. As it freezes, most of the salt is pushed out, so sea ice is much less salty than seawater. Sea ice grows each autumn and winter and shrinks each spring and summer: in the Arctic it typically covers about 15 million square kilometers in March and falls to around 4 to 5 million square kilometers in September. Around Antarctica it expands from about 3 million to about 18 million square kilometers. Sea ice is vital to life and climate. Its bright white surface reflects much of the Sun’s energy back into space, helping keep the planet cool. Polar bears hunt seals from it, walruses rest on it, and algae growing beneath it feed tiny animals at the base of the food web. Satellite records since 1979 show Arctic summer sea ice has shrunk dramatically as the climate warms, and Antarctic sea ice reached record lows in 2023.",
s:[["From slush to pack ice","Sea ice starts as needle-like crystals called frazil, forming a slushy layer called grease ice. Waves break it into round ‘pancake ice’, which freezes together into solid sheets. Ice that survives a summer is called multiyear ice and is thicker and stronger."],["The albedo effect","Ice reflects most sunlight, while dark open ocean absorbs most of it. As ice melts, more sunlight is absorbed, warming the water and melting more ice, a feedback that helps explain why the Arctic is warming much faster than the rest of the world."],["Arctic decline","The lowest Arctic summer extent on record, about 3.4 million square kilometers, was in September 2012. The ice has also become much thinner, with far less old, multiyear ice than in the 1980s."],["Life on the ice","Polar bears, ringed seals, walruses and Arctic peoples such as the Inuit depend on sea ice for hunting and travel. In Antarctica, emperor penguins breed on sea ice attached to the coast."]],
k:[["Forms from","Seawater"],["Freezing point","About −1.8 °C"],["Arctic range","About 15 million km² (March) to 4–5 million km² (September)"],["Antarctic range","About 3 to 18 million km²"],["Satellite records since","1979"],["Arctic record low","September 2012"]],
d:[["1979","Continuous satellite records begin"],["2007","Arctic summer ice reaches a new record low"],["2012","Lowest Arctic summer extent on record"],["2023","Record-low Antarctic sea ice"]],
s2:[["Melting ice and sea level","Melting sea ice does not raise sea level, because it is already floating, but its loss speeds up warming."],["Shipping routes","As Arctic ice shrinks, ships increasingly use the Northern Sea Route along Russia’s coast in summer."]],
g:[["Sea ice","Frozen seawater"],["Albedo","How much light a surface reflects"],["Pack ice","Sea ice floating freely"],["Multiyear ice","Sea ice that has survived at least one summer"]],
f:["Sea ice forms from seawater.","Sea ice reflects sunlight.","Arctic sea ice has shrunk since 1979."],
fun:"Young sea ice can look like floating pancakes, called pancake ice."});

ADDART({id:"duststorms",t:"Dust and Sand Storms",c:"Earth Science",i:"🌪️",w:"Dust storm",r:["desert","sahara","droughts","airpollution","sanddunes","mars"],
p:[["Walls of dust","Strong winds lift dust and sand into the air."],["Crossing oceans","Saharan dust reaches the Americas."],["A health hazard","Dust storms harm breathing and travel."]],
l:"A dust storm is a weather event in which strong winds lift large amounts of dust from dry, bare ground into the air, sometimes reducing visibility to near zero. In sandy deserts, similar storms that carry heavier sand grains closer to the ground are called sandstorms. Dust storms happen mostly in dry regions such as the Sahara, the Arabian Peninsula, the Gobi Desert, Central Asia, Australia and the American Southwest. A dramatic kind, called a haboob, forms when cold air rushing out of a thunderstorm pushes up a towering wall of dust that can be over a kilometer high. Dust storms can travel enormous distances: each year, hundreds of millions of tonnes of Saharan dust blow across the Atlantic Ocean to the Caribbean and the Americas, where its minerals fertilize the Amazon rainforest and ocean plankton. Dust storms can also damage crops, strip away fertile soil, ground flights, cause road accidents and worsen asthma and other breathing problems. Drought, overgrazing and poor farming can make them worse, as in the Dust Bowl that struck the American Great Plains in the 1930s.",
s:[["Haboobs","The word haboob comes from Arabic, meaning ‘strong wind’. Haboobs are common in Sudan and also strike places such as Phoenix, Arizona, where walls of dust roll across the city in summer."],["Saharan dust","Satellites track plumes of Saharan dust crossing the Atlantic. The Bodélé Depression in Chad, an ancient dried-up lakebed, is the single biggest source of dust on Earth."],["The Dust Bowl","In the 1930s, drought and the plowing up of prairie grasslands in the United States left soil exposed. Huge dust storms, called ‘black blizzards’, buried farms and forced many families to leave. New soil conservation methods followed."],["Dust on Mars","Mars has the biggest dust storms in the solar system, sometimes covering the whole planet. A global dust storm in 2018 ended the mission of NASA’s Opportunity rover by blocking sunlight from its solar panels."]],
k:[["Caused by","Strong winds over dry, bare ground"],["Dust wall from thunderstorms","Haboob"],["Biggest dust source","Bodélé Depression, Chad"],["Saharan dust fertilizes","The Amazon and oceans"],["Historic disaster","The Dust Bowl (1930s)"],["Hazards","Breathing problems, accidents, crop damage"]],
d:[["1935","‘Black Sunday’ dust storm during the Dust Bowl"],["2018","Global dust storm on Mars ends Opportunity rover"]],
s2:[["Red snow","Saharan dust sometimes falls with rain or snow in Europe, turning snow orange and leaving cars coated in reddish dust."],["Great Green Wall","Countries across Africa’s Sahel are planting trees and restoring land in the Great Green Wall project, partly to reduce dust and desertification."]],
g:[["Dust storm","Strong winds carrying dust"],["Haboob","A wall of dust pushed by thunderstorm winds"],["Desertification","Land becoming desert"],["Plume","A long cloud of dust or smoke"]],
f:["Dust storms happen in dry regions.","Saharan dust crosses the Atlantic.","The Dust Bowl struck the United States in the 1930s."],
fun:"Dust from the Sahara helps fertilize the Amazon rainforest on the other side of the Atlantic."});

ADDART({id:"bloodclotting",t:"Blood Clotting",c:"Health and the Body",i:"🩹",w:"Coagulation",r:["blood","circulation","skin","vitamins","heartdisease","stroke"],
p:[["Stopping bleeding","Clotting seals cuts and wounds."],["Platelets and fibrin","Cell fragments and protein threads form a plug."],["Too much or too little","Clotting problems can be dangerous."]],
l:"Blood clotting, or coagulation, is the process that stops bleeding when a blood vessel is damaged. It happens in a few quick steps. First, the injured blood vessel narrows to slow blood flow. Second, tiny cell fragments in the blood called platelets rush to the site, stick to the damaged wall and to each other, forming a soft plug. Third, a chain of reactions involving more than a dozen proteins in the blood plasma, called clotting factors, turns a protein called fibrinogen into long, sticky threads of fibrin, which weave through the platelet plug and trap red blood cells, forming a firm clot. On the skin, the clot dries to become a scab, and underneath, the vessel repairs itself; later the clot is broken down. Vitamin K is needed to make several clotting factors. Clotting must be carefully balanced. If blood cannot clot properly, as in the inherited disorder hemophilia, even small injuries can cause serious bleeding. If clots form when they should not, inside blood vessels, they can block blood flow and cause heart attacks, strokes or deep vein thrombosis.",
s:[["The clotting cascade","Clotting factors activate each other in a chain, like dominoes, each step amplifying the next, so a small trigger quickly produces lots of fibrin. The factors are numbered with Roman numerals, such as factor VIII."],["Hemophilia","People with hemophilia lack enough of a clotting factor, usually factor VIII or IX. It mostly affects males, because the genes are on the X chromosome. It was known as the ‘royal disease’ because Queen Victoria passed it to several European royal families. Today it is treated with factor replacement and gene therapy."],["Unwanted clots","Long periods of sitting, such as on long flights, can raise the risk of clots in leg veins. Doctors use anticoagulant medicines, sometimes called blood thinners, such as heparin and warfarin, to prevent dangerous clots."],["Platelets","Platelets are made in the bone marrow from large cells called megakaryocytes and live for about 7 to 10 days. A microliter of blood contains about 150,000 to 450,000 platelets."]],
k:[["Also called","Coagulation"],["Key cells","Platelets"],["Key protein","Fibrin (from fibrinogen)"],["Clotting factors","More than a dozen proteins"],["Needed vitamin","Vitamin K"],["Bleeding disorder","Hemophilia"]],
d:[["1905","Paul Morawitz describes the basic steps of clotting"],["1916","Heparin discovered"],["1954","Warfarin approved for use in people"],["2022","First gene therapy for hemophilia approved"]],
s2:[["Scabs","Picking a scab can reopen the wound and slow healing, because the scab protects new skin growing underneath."],["Leeches","Leech saliva contains a substance called hirudin that stops blood clotting, which is why leeches are sometimes used in medicine."]],
g:[["Coagulation","The process of blood clotting"],["Platelet","A tiny cell fragment that helps blood clot"],["Fibrin","Protein threads that form a clot"],["Anticoagulant","A medicine that prevents clots"]],
f:["Platelets help blood clot.","Fibrin forms threads in a clot.","Hemophilia is a bleeding disorder."],
fun:"Leeches produce a chemical that stops blood from clotting while they feed."});

ADDART({id:"bodytemperature",t:"Body Temperature, Sweat and Shivering",c:"Health and the Body",i:"🌡️",w:"Thermoregulation",r:["temperature","fever","skin","extremeheat","hydration","metabolism"],
p:[["Staying at 37 °C","The body keeps its core temperature steady."],["Cooling down","Sweating and widening blood vessels release heat."],["Warming up","Shivering and goosebumps help keep warm."]],
l:"The human body works best at a core temperature of about 37 °C, and it keeps that temperature remarkably steady, within about a degree, whether the weather is freezing or scorching. This control, called thermoregulation, is run by a part of the brain called the hypothalamus, which acts like a thermostat. It receives signals from temperature sensors in the skin and inside the body and triggers responses to warm up or cool down. When the body is too hot, blood vessels in the skin widen, sending more blood to the surface to release heat, which is why people look flushed; and sweat glands release sweat, which cools the skin as it evaporates. When the body is too cold, skin blood vessels narrow to keep warm blood in the core; muscles shiver, producing heat through rapid small contractions; and tiny muscles at the base of hairs contract, causing goosebumps, a leftover from furry ancestors whose raised fur trapped warm air. If these systems are overwhelmed, body temperature can rise dangerously, causing heatstroke, or fall too low, causing hypothermia.",
s:[["Sweat","Humans have about 2 to 4 million sweat glands, more than almost any other mammal. In hot conditions, a person can sweat over a liter an hour. Evaporating sweat carries away heat, but in very humid air sweat evaporates poorly, making heat more dangerous."],["Goosebumps","Goosebumps are caused by tiny muscles called arrector pili. In furry animals they make fur stand up for warmth or to look bigger when frightened. People also get goosebumps from strong emotions, such as moving music."],["Fever","During an infection, the hypothalamus raises the body’s set point, causing a fever. Feeling cold and shivering at the start of a fever helps the body reach the new, higher temperature."],["Too hot or too cold","Heatstroke, when the core rises above about 40 °C, is a medical emergency. Hypothermia begins when the core falls below 35 °C, causing shivering, confusion and slowing of the heart."]],
k:[["Normal core temperature","About 37 °C"],["Control center","Hypothalamus"],["Cooling methods","Sweating, widening skin blood vessels"],["Warming methods","Shivering, narrowing skin blood vessels"],["Sweat glands","About 2–4 million"],["Dangers","Heatstroke and hypothermia"]],
s2:[["Babies","Newborn babies have a special ‘brown fat’ that burns energy to produce heat without shivering."],["Breathing out heat","Dogs cool down mainly by panting, because they have few sweat glands, while humans rely mostly on sweating."]],
g:[["Thermoregulation","Control of body temperature"],["Hypothalamus","The brain area that controls temperature"],["Hypothermia","Dangerously low body temperature"],["Heatstroke","Dangerously high body temperature"]],
f:["Normal body temperature is about 37 °C.","Sweating cools the body.","Shivering produces heat."],
fun:"Goosebumps are left over from ancestors who could fluff up their fur to stay warm."});

ADDART({id:"appendix",t:"The Appendix",c:"Health and the Body",i:"🪱",w:"Appendix (anatomy)",r:["digest","microbiome","immune","surgery","lymphatic","evolution"],
p:[["A small pouch","A finger-shaped tube attached to the large intestine."],["Not useless after all","It may store helpful gut bacteria."],["Appendicitis","Inflammation of the appendix needs urgent treatment."]],
l:"The appendix is a narrow, finger-shaped pouch about 5 to 10 centimeters long, attached to the beginning of the large intestine in the lower right part of the abdomen. For a long time, it was thought to be a useless leftover from evolution. Charles Darwin suggested it was the shrunken remnant of a larger organ used by plant-eating ancestors to digest tough leaves. Today, scientists think the appendix does have uses. It contains a lot of lymphoid tissue, part of the immune system, and it may act as a ‘safe house’ for helpful gut bacteria, which can repopulate the intestines after an illness such as severe diarrhea flushes them out. Studies have found that appendixes have evolved independently many times in different mammals, suggesting they are useful. The best-known problem with the appendix is appendicitis, when it becomes blocked and inflamed, causing pain that usually starts around the belly button and moves to the lower right abdomen, along with fever and nausea. If untreated, the appendix can burst, which is dangerous, so appendicitis is usually treated quickly with surgery or sometimes antibiotics.",
s:[["Appendicitis","Appendicitis is one of the most common causes of emergency abdominal surgery, most often affecting people between 10 and 30 years old. About 1 in 20 people get it at some point."],["Surgery and antibiotics","An appendectomy, removal of the appendix, is often done through small cuts using a camera, called keyhole surgery. Studies since the 2010s show that some uncomplicated cases can be treated with antibiotics alone."],["A safe house for bacteria","In 2007, researchers at Duke University proposed that the appendix stores beneficial bacteria. Some studies have found that people without an appendix may be more likely to have repeat gut infections, though evidence is mixed."],["Living without it","People can live completely normal lives without an appendix. In 1961, a Soviet doctor in Antarctica, Leonid Rogozov, famously removed his own appendix when no one else could operate."]],
k:[["Location","Lower right abdomen"],["Length","About 5–10 cm"],["Attached to","The start of the large intestine"],["Possible roles","Immune tissue, storing gut bacteria"],["Main problem","Appendicitis"],["Treatment","Surgery (appendectomy) or antibiotics"]],
d:[["1735","First recorded appendectomy, by Claudius Amyand"],["1886","Reginald Fitz describes appendicitis"],["1961","Leonid Rogozov removes his own appendix in Antarctica"],["2007","Safe-house theory proposed"]],
s2:[["Other animals","Rabbits, koalas and some primates have an appendix, while dogs and cats do not."],["Pain moves","Appendicitis pain often starts near the belly button and moves to the lower right side, a clue doctors use."]],
g:[["Appendix","A small pouch off the large intestine"],["Appendicitis","Inflammation of the appendix"],["Appendectomy","Surgery to remove the appendix"],["Lymphoid tissue","Tissue that helps fight infection"]],
f:["The appendix is attached to the large intestine.","Appendicitis is inflammation of the appendix.","The appendix may store helpful bacteria."],
fun:"In 1961, a doctor stranded in Antarctica removed his own appendix using a mirror."});

ADDART({id:"hungerappetite",t:"Hunger and Appetite",c:"Health and the Body",i:"🍽️",w:"Hunger",r:["digest","nutri","hormone","brain","metabolism","obesity"],
p:[["Why we feel hungry","Signals from the stomach, gut and fat reach the brain."],["Hunger hormones","Ghrelin says eat; leptin says stop."],["More than need","Smell, mood and habit affect appetite."]],
l:"Hunger is the body’s physical need for food, while appetite is the desire to eat, which can be influenced by much more than need, such as the sight or smell of food, emotions, habits and the time of day. Both are controlled mainly by the brain, especially an area called the hypothalamus, which receives a stream of signals from the digestive system and the rest of the body. When the stomach is empty, it releases a hormone called ghrelin, often called the hunger hormone, which tells the brain to seek food; levels rise before meals and fall after eating. As food fills the stomach, stretch sensors send ‘full’ signals along nerves, and the intestines release hormones such as GLP-1 and peptide YY that promote fullness, called satiety. Fat tissue releases another hormone, leptin, which tells the brain how much energy is stored long term. Blood sugar levels also play a part. Stomach growling, called borborygmi, is the sound of muscles moving gas and fluid through the gut. Medicines that mimic GLP-1, developed for diabetes, are now widely used to reduce appetite and help weight loss.",
s:[["Ghrelin and leptin","Ghrelin, discovered in 1999, rises when the stomach is empty and stimulates hunger. Leptin, discovered in 1994, is made by fat cells and helps regulate long-term energy balance. Some people with obesity have high leptin levels but respond poorly to it."],["Sleep and stress","Lack of sleep tends to raise ghrelin and lower leptin, increasing hunger. Stress can either increase or decrease appetite, depending on the person."],["GLP-1 medicines","Drugs such as semaglutide mimic the gut hormone GLP-1. They slow stomach emptying and act on the brain to reduce appetite. Originally made for type 2 diabetes, they are now also used to treat obesity."],["Thirst or hunger?","Thirst signals are sometimes mistaken for hunger. Drinking water and eating foods rich in fiber and protein help people feel full for longer."]],
k:[["Control center","Hypothalamus in the brain"],["Hunger hormone","Ghrelin"],["Long-term fullness signal","Leptin (from fat cells)"],["Gut fullness hormones","GLP-1, peptide YY"],["Stomach growling","Borborygmi"],["Appetite influenced by","Smell, sight, mood, habit, sleep"]],
d:[["1994","Leptin discovered"],["1999","Ghrelin discovered"],["2017","Semaglutide approved for type 2 diabetes"],["2021","Semaglutide approved for weight management in the US"]],
s2:[["Dessert stomach","After eating lots of one food, people often still want something different, a phenomenon called sensory-specific satiety."],["The 20-minute rule","Fullness signals take time to reach the brain, so eating slowly can help people notice when they have had enough."]],
g:[["Hunger","The body’s need for food"],["Appetite","The desire to eat"],["Satiety","The feeling of fullness"],["Ghrelin","A hormone that stimulates hunger"]],
f:["Ghrelin makes us feel hungry.","Leptin is made by fat cells.","The hypothalamus controls hunger."],
fun:"A growling stomach has a scientific name: borborygmi."});

ADDART({id:"tears",t:"Tears and Crying",c:"Health and the Body",i:"😢",w:"Tears",r:["eye","brain","emotions","stress","laughter","nervous"],
p:[["Three kinds of tears","Basal, reflex and emotional tears."],["Protecting the eyes","Tears keep eyes moist and clean."],["Only humans cry for feelings","Emotional tears seem to be uniquely human."]],
l:"Tears are a salty fluid made by the lacrimal glands, which sit above the outer corner of each eye. Every time we blink, the eyelids spread a thin layer of tears across the surface of the eye, keeping it moist, smooth and clear for good vision. Tears drain away through tiny holes in the inner corners of the eyelids into the nose, which is why crying can make your nose run. There are three kinds of tears. Basal tears are produced all the time to keep the eyes lubricated and protected; they contain water, oils, mucus, salts and germ-fighting proteins such as lysozyme. Reflex tears flood the eyes to wash out irritants such as dust, smoke or the gas released when cutting onions. Emotional tears are triggered by strong feelings such as sadness, joy, relief or frustration. Humans appear to be the only animals that shed tears for emotional reasons. Scientists think emotional crying may help signal to others that we need help or comfort, strengthening social bonds, and many people report feeling calmer after a good cry.",
s:[["Tear film","The tear film has three layers: an oily outer layer that slows evaporation, a watery middle layer, and a mucus layer that helps tears stick to the eye. When tears are not good enough, people develop dry eye, a common condition."],["Onions","Cutting an onion releases a chemical called syn-propanethial-S-oxide, which irritates the eyes and triggers reflex tears. Chilling onions or using a sharp knife can reduce it."],["Why we cry","Babies cry to get care from adults. Studies suggest adult emotional tears may make others more likely to help, and may signal sadness more clearly than facial expression alone."],["How often","Studies suggest that on average, women report crying more often than men, though this varies widely across cultures and individuals."]],
k:[["Made by","Lacrimal glands"],["Types","Basal, reflex, emotional"],["Contain","Water, salts, oils, mucus, lysozyme"],["Drain into","The nose"],["Emotional tears","Seem unique to humans"],["Common condition","Dry eye"]],
s2:[["Not just sadness","People cry from joy, laughter, relief and when moved by music or films."],["Crocodile tears","Crocodiles produce tears while eating, giving rise to the phrase ‘crocodile tears’ for fake sorrow, though their tears simply keep their eyes moist."]],
g:[["Lacrimal gland","The gland that makes tears"],["Lysozyme","A protein in tears that kills bacteria"],["Reflex tears","Tears that wash out irritants"],["Dry eye","When eyes do not make enough good-quality tears"]],
f:["Tears are made by lacrimal glands.","Onions trigger reflex tears.","Tears drain into the nose."],
fun:"Tears drain into your nose, which is why crying can make your nose run."});

ADDART({id:"spleen",t:"The Spleen and Thymus",c:"Health and the Body",i:"🛡️",w:"Spleen",r:["immune","lymphatic","blood","liver","vacc","transplant"],
p:[["The spleen filters blood","It removes old red blood cells and germs."],["The thymus trains T cells","It teaches immune cells to fight invaders."],["Hidden helpers","Both are part of the immune system."]],
l:"The spleen and the thymus are two organs that are important parts of the immune and lymphatic systems. The spleen is a soft, fist-sized organ in the upper left of the abdomen, behind the ribs. It filters the blood, removing old and damaged red blood cells, recycling their iron, and catching bacteria and other germs in the bloodstream. It also stores white blood cells and platelets and makes antibodies, helping the body fight infection, especially from certain bacteria. People can live without a spleen, but they are more vulnerable to some serious infections and are advised to have extra vaccinations. The thymus is a small organ in the chest, behind the breastbone and in front of the heart. It is where a type of white blood cell called the T cell matures: young T cells are ‘trained’ to recognize invaders such as viruses while not attacking the body’s own tissues. The thymus is largest and most active in childhood and gradually shrinks and is replaced by fat after puberty, though the T cells it trained continue to protect the body for life.",
s:[["Red pulp and white pulp","The spleen has two kinds of tissue. Red pulp filters the blood and removes worn-out red cells. White pulp contains lymphocytes, which make antibodies and respond to infections."],["Injuries","Because it is full of blood, the spleen can bleed heavily if it is injured, for example in car accidents or contact sports. Infections such as glandular fever can make it swell, so doctors may advise avoiding contact sports for a while."],["Training T cells","In the thymus, developing T cells that would attack the body’s own tissues are destroyed, while useful ones are kept. Problems with this process can contribute to autoimmune diseases."],["Discovering the thymus’s role","The thymus’s role in immunity was discovered in 1961 by the French-Australian scientist Jacques Miller, who found that mice without a thymus could not fight infections or reject grafts."]],
k:[["Spleen location","Upper left abdomen"],["Spleen jobs","Filters blood, fights infection"],["Thymus location","Chest, behind the breastbone"],["Thymus job","Trains T cells"],["Thymus largest in","Childhood"],["Living without a spleen","Possible, with extra vaccines"]],
d:[["1961","Jacques Miller discovers the thymus’s role"]],
s2:[["Stitch","The sharp ‘stitch’ some people feel in their side when running is not usually caused by the spleen, though its cause is still debated."],["Diving animals","Seals and some freedivers’ spleens contract during deep dives, releasing extra oxygen-carrying red blood cells into the blood."]],
g:[["Spleen","An organ that filters blood"],["Thymus","An organ where T cells mature"],["T cell","A white blood cell that helps fight infection"],["Antibody","A protein that targets germs"]],
f:["The spleen filters blood.","T cells mature in the thymus.","The thymus shrinks after puberty."],
fun:"Seals squeeze their spleens during deep dives to release extra red blood cells."});

ADDART({id:"daylightsaving",t:"Daylight Saving Time",c:"Economics and Society",i:"⏰",w:"Daylight saving time",r:["timezones","clocks","calendars","bodyclock","sleep","energytransition"],
p:[["Clocks forward in spring","And back again in autumn."],["About 70 countries","Mostly in Europe and North America."],["A long debate","Many want to stop changing the clocks."]],
l:"Daylight saving time (DST), called summer time in many countries, is the practice of setting clocks forward by one hour in spring and back by one hour in autumn, so that evenings have more daylight and mornings less during the warmer months. The idea was promoted by the British builder William Willett in 1907, and Germany and Austria-Hungary became the first countries to adopt it nationally in 1916, during the First World War, to save fuel for lighting. Many other countries followed. Today about 70 countries use DST, mostly in Europe and North America, while most countries near the equator, where day length changes little, and most of Asia and Africa do not. Supporters say lighter evenings encourage outdoor activity, help businesses and reduce traffic accidents. Critics point to studies linking the spring clock change to lost sleep, more heart attacks and accidents in the following days, and say any energy savings are small. Many people would like to end the clock changes, but disagree about whether to keep permanent summer time or permanent standard time. In July 2026 the US House of Representatives passed a bill for permanent daylight saving time, but by late 2026 the Senate had not voted on it.",
s:[["Spring forward, fall back","In the Northern Hemisphere, clocks usually go forward in March and back in October or November. In the Southern Hemisphere, countries such as Australia, New Zealand, Chile and Paraguay change in the opposite months."],["Sleep and health","Losing an hour of sleep in spring disrupts the body clock. Studies have found a small increase in heart attacks and traffic accidents in the days after the change. Sleep scientists often favor permanent standard time."],["Trying to stop","In 2019, the European Parliament voted to end seasonal clock changes, but EU countries did not agree how to do it. In the United States, the Sunshine Protection Act would make daylight saving time permanent; the Senate passed a version in 2022 and the House passed one in July 2026."],["Past experiments","Russia tried permanent summer time in 2011 but switched to permanent standard time in 2014 after complaints about dark winter mornings. The United States briefly tried year-round DST in 1974 during an energy crisis."]],
k:[["Also called","Summer time"],["Proposed by","William Willett (1907)"],["First national use","Germany and Austria-Hungary, 1916"],["Countries using it","About 70"],["Main debate","Sleep and health versus lighter evenings"],["US status (2026)","House passed permanent DST; Senate had not voted"]],
d:[["1907","Willett proposes daylight saving"],["1916","Germany first adopts it nationally"],["1974","US tries year-round DST"],["2019","European Parliament votes to end clock changes"],["2026","US House passes the Sunshine Protection Act"]],
s2:[["Benjamin Franklin","In 1784 Benjamin Franklin jokingly suggested Parisians could save candles by getting up earlier, often cited as an early version of the idea."],["Arizona and Hawaii","Most of Arizona and all of Hawaii do not use daylight saving time."]],
g:[["Daylight saving time","Moving clocks forward in summer"],["Standard time","The normal clock time of a time zone"],["Time zone","A region using the same clock time"],["Circadian rhythm","The body’s daily cycle"]],
f:["Clocks go forward in spring.","Germany first adopted DST in 1916.","Many countries near the equator do not use DST."],
fun:"The spring clock change makes many people lose an hour of sleep overnight."});

ADDART({id:"vinylrevival",t:"The Vinyl Revival",c:"Economics and Society",i:"💿",w:"Vinyl revival",r:["soundrecording","streaming","rockpop","hiphop","fandom","sneakerculture"],
p:[["Records are back","Vinyl sales have grown for nearly two decades."],["Over $1 billion","US vinyl sales passed $1 billion in 2025."],["A physical connection","Fans love the artwork, sound and ritual."]],
l:"The vinyl revival is the return in popularity of vinyl records, the large black discs played on turntables, after decades of decline. Vinyl records were the main format for recorded music from the 1950s to the 1980s, but sales collapsed after compact discs (CDs) arrived in the 1980s and then digital downloads and streaming took over. By the mid-2000s, vinyl had almost disappeared, but sales began rising again from about 2007. In the United States, vinyl sales grew for 19 years in a row, passing 1 billion US dollars in 2025 for the first time since 1983, with about 46.8 million records sold, far more than CDs. Similar growth has happened in the United Kingdom, Japan, Germany and elsewhere. Fans say they enjoy the large album artwork, the warm sound, the ritual of playing a record and owning something physical in a digital world. Many buyers also stream music and buy records to support artists or as collectibles, often in colored or limited editions. Record Store Day, started in 2008, celebrates independent shops. The revival has led to new pressing plants opening, though demand sometimes outstrips supply.",
s:[["How records work","A record has a single spiral groove with tiny wiggles that match the sound waves. As the record spins, usually at 33⅓ or 45 revolutions per minute, a needle, or stylus, follows the groove, and its vibrations are turned into an electrical signal and amplified."],["Record Store Day","Founded in the United States in 2008, Record Store Day is held each April in countries around the world, with special releases sold only in independent record shops."],["Big sellers","Pop stars such as Taylor Swift have sold huge numbers of vinyl records, often in multiple colored versions. Classic albums by artists such as The Beatles, Fleetwood Mac and Pink Floyd also remain strong sellers."],["Environmental concerns","Most records are made from PVC plastic, and pressing them uses energy and chemicals. Some pressing plants are experimenting with recycled and bio-based materials."]],
k:[["Format","Vinyl records played on turntables"],["Revival began","About 2007"],["US growth","19 years in a row (to 2025)"],["US sales 2025","Over $1 billion; about 46.8 million records"],["Common speeds","33⅓ and 45 rpm"],["Record Store Day","Since 2008"]],
d:[["1948","Columbia introduces the long-playing (LP) record"],["1982","Compact disc launched"],["2007","Vinyl sales begin rising again"],["2008","First Record Store Day"],["2025","US vinyl sales pass $1 billion"]],
s2:[["Cassettes too","Cassette tapes have also seen a small comeback, popular with some artists and collectors."],["Turntablism","DJs in hip hop and dance music use turntables as instruments, scratching records to create new sounds."]],
g:[["Vinyl record","A plastic disc with a groove that stores sound"],["Turntable","A device that spins records"],["Stylus","The needle that reads a record’s groove"],["LP","Long-playing record"]],
f:["Vinyl sales have grown since about 2007.","US vinyl sales passed $1 billion in 2025.","Record Store Day began in 2008."],
fun:"Each side of a record has just one groove, a single spiral running from the edge to the center."});

ADDART({id:"escaperooms",t:"Escape Rooms",c:"Economics and Society",i:"🔐",w:"Escape room",r:["boardgames","videogames","fandom","themeparks","ciphers","cosplay"],
p:[["Solve puzzles to escape","Teams find clues and unlock locks against the clock."],["A worldwide craze","Thousands of rooms opened in the 2010s."],["Teamwork","Popular for friends, families and coworkers."]],
l:"An escape room is a live game in which a small group of players, usually between two and eight, are locked in a themed room and must work together to solve puzzles, find hidden clues and open locks to ‘escape’ or complete a mission within a time limit, typically 60 minutes. Rooms are designed around stories, such as breaking out of a prison cell, solving a murder mystery, stopping a bomb, exploring an ancient tomb or escaping a spaceship, and use props, codes, riddles, padlocks, hidden compartments and sometimes electronic gadgets. A game master watches through cameras and can give hints. Escape rooms were inspired by ‘escape the room’ video games of the early 2000s, interactive theater and puzzle hunts. One of the first commercial escape rooms, Real Escape Game, was created in Kyoto, Japan, in 2007, and the idea spread rapidly through Asia and Europe and then worldwide in the 2010s. By the late 2010s there were thousands of escape rooms in many countries. They are popular for parties and team-building exercises, and many people also play escape room board games and online versions at home.",
s:[["Origins","The Japanese company SCRAP launched Real Escape Game in 2007. In Budapest, Hungary, the company Parapark opened rooms in 2011 inspired by psychologist Mihály Csíkszentmihályi’s ideas about ‘flow’, and the city became a center of the craze."],["Types of puzzles","Puzzles include codes, ciphers, jigsaw puzzles, searching for hidden objects, using UV light to reveal messages, matching patterns and physical challenges. Good rooms require players to share information and split up tasks."],["Safety","After a tragic fire at an escape room in Poland in 2019 killed five teenagers, many countries tightened safety rules. Modern rooms are designed so players can always leave in an emergency."],["At home","Boxed escape games and printable puzzle kits let families play at home, and during the COVID-19 pandemic, many companies created online escape rooms played over video calls."]],
k:[["Players","Usually 2–8"],["Typical time limit","60 minutes"],["Early commercial room","Real Escape Game, Kyoto, 2007"],["Spread","Asia and Europe, then worldwide (2010s)"],["Popular for","Parties and team-building"],["Help","Hints from a game master"]],
d:[["2004","Video game Crimson Room popularizes ‘escape the room’ games"],["2007","Real Escape Game in Kyoto"],["2011","Parapark opens in Budapest"],["2019","Escape room fire in Poland leads to safety reforms"]],
s2:[["Success rates","Many rooms are designed so that only about a third to half of teams escape in time."],["Inspiration","Escape rooms borrow ideas from mystery novels, adventure films and classic puzzle books."]],
g:[["Escape room","A live puzzle game in a themed room"],["Game master","The person running an escape room"],["Cipher","A secret code"],["Team-building","Activities that help groups work together"]],
f:["Escape rooms are solved by teams.","An early escape room opened in Kyoto in 2007.","Players usually have about an hour."],
fun:"Escape rooms grew out of online ‘escape the room’ video games."});

ADDART({id:"truecrime",t:"True Crime",c:"Economics and Society",i:"🔎",w:"True crime",r:["podcasts","documentary","police","newspapers","streaming"],
p:[["Real crimes as stories","Books, podcasts and shows about actual crimes."],["A huge audience","One of the most popular genres today."],["Ethical questions","Concerns about victims, accuracy and justice."]],
l:"True crime is a genre of nonfiction books, articles, podcasts, documentaries, films and television series that examine real crimes and the people involved, often murders, disappearances, frauds or miscarriages of justice. Interest in real crime stories is old: in the 1500s to 1800s, printed pamphlets and broadsides described crimes and executions, and newspapers covered sensational trials. A landmark of the modern genre was In Cold Blood (1966) by the American writer Truman Capote, about the murder of a farming family in Kansas, which read like a novel. True crime became hugely popular in the 21st century, especially with the podcast Serial in 2014, which reexamined a 1999 murder case in Baltimore and was downloaded hundreds of millions of times, and streaming documentaries such as Making a Murderer (2015). Fans are drawn to the puzzle of solving a mystery, curiosity about human behavior and a desire to understand danger. Some investigations have helped reopen cases and free wrongly convicted people. Critics, however, worry about sensationalizing violence, causing distress to victims’ families, spreading inaccurate information and turning real tragedies into entertainment.",
s:[["Serial","Serial, hosted by journalist Sarah Koenig, examined the conviction of Adnan Syed for the 1999 murder of Hae Min Lee. It helped make podcasts mainstream. Syed was freed in 2022 when his conviction was overturned; it was reinstated on appeal in 2023, but in 2025 a judge reduced his sentence to time already served, so he remained free."],["Making a difference","Investigations by journalists and podcasters have sometimes found new evidence. Citizen investigators online have also helped identify unknown victims, often working with organizations such as the DNA Doe Project."],["Concerns","Families of victims have criticized some shows for reopening painful wounds without their consent. Amateur sleuths have sometimes wrongly accused innocent people online."],["Who listens","Surveys find true crime is one of the most popular podcast genres, with a large audience of women."]],
k:[["Formats","Books, podcasts, documentaries, TV series"],["Classic book","In Cold Blood (1966)"],["Podcast landmark","Serial (2014)"],["Streaming landmark","Making a Murderer (2015)"],["Positive impact","Helping reopen cases"],["Concerns","Sensationalism, victims’ families, accuracy"]],
d:[["1966","Truman Capote’s In Cold Blood published"],["1988","The Thin Blue Line documentary helps free a wrongly convicted man"],["2014","Serial podcast launches"],["2015","Making a Murderer released"]],
s2:[["The Thin Blue Line","Errol Morris’s 1988 documentary The Thin Blue Line uncovered evidence that led to Randall Dale Adams being freed from a Texas prison."],["Forensic science","Interest in true crime has also boosted interest in forensic science, the use of science to investigate crimes."]],
g:[["True crime","Nonfiction stories about real crimes"],["Miscarriage of justice","When an innocent person is convicted"],["Forensics","Science used to investigate crimes"],["Sensationalism","Exaggerating to shock or excite"]],
f:["True crime examines real crimes.","Serial was a landmark podcast.","True crime raises ethical concerns."],
fun:"Before podcasts and TV, people bought cheap printed pamphlets telling the stories of famous crimes."});

ADDART({id:"oceanacidification",t:"Ocean Acidification",c:"Economics and Society",i:"🌊",w:"Ocean acidification",r:["climchange","coral","acid","oceancurrents","mollusc","carbonfootprint"],
p:[["The other CO₂ problem","Oceans absorb carbon dioxide, becoming more acidic."],["Shell builders at risk","Corals, oysters and plankton struggle."],["A boundary crossed","Scientists said in 2025 it had passed a safe limit."]],
l:"Ocean acidification is the ongoing decrease in the pH of the world’s oceans, caused mainly by the absorption of carbon dioxide from the atmosphere. Since the Industrial Revolution, people have released huge amounts of carbon dioxide by burning fossil fuels and clearing forests, and the oceans have absorbed roughly a quarter to a third of it. When carbon dioxide dissolves in seawater, it forms carbonic acid, which lowers the water’s pH and reduces the amount of carbonate ions available. Since the 1800s, the average pH of surface ocean water has fallen from about 8.2 to about 8.1. That may seem small, but because pH is a logarithmic scale, it means the water has become about 30 percent more acidic. Seawater is still slightly alkaline, but the change makes it harder for creatures that build shells and skeletons from calcium carbonate, such as corals, oysters, clams, sea urchins and tiny plankton called pteropods, to grow and survive. In 2025, scientists reported that ocean acidification had crossed its ‘planetary boundary’, the seventh of nine Earth system limits to be passed.",
s:[["The chemistry","Carbon dioxide plus water forms carbonic acid, which releases hydrogen ions. These ions make the water more acidic and combine with carbonate ions, leaving fewer for shell-building animals. In some cold and deep waters, shells can even begin to dissolve."],["Who is affected","Coral reefs, already stressed by warming and bleaching, grow more slowly in more acidic water. Oyster hatcheries in the Pacific Northwest of the United States lost billions of larvae in the late 2000s when acidic deep water reached the coast."],["Planetary boundaries","The planetary boundaries framework, developed by scientists led by Johan Rockström in 2009, describes nine limits for a stable Earth. A 2025 ‘Planetary Health Check’ found that ocean acidification had moved beyond its safe limit."],["Solutions","The main solution is cutting carbon dioxide emissions. Protecting seagrass and kelp, which absorb carbon dioxide locally, reducing other pollution and monitoring ocean chemistry can help vulnerable areas."]],
k:[["Cause","Oceans absorbing carbon dioxide"],["Share of CO₂ absorbed","About a quarter to a third"],["Surface pH change","About 8.2 to 8.1 since the 1800s"],["Increase in acidity","About 30 percent"],["Most affected","Corals, shellfish, plankton"],["Planetary boundary","Reported crossed in 2025"]],
d:[["1958","Charles Keeling begins measuring atmospheric CO₂"],["1988","Long-term ocean pH measurements begin near Hawaii"],["2009","Planetary boundaries framework published"],["2025","Ocean acidification reported to have crossed its planetary boundary"]],
s2:[["Not acid yet","Even after acidification, seawater is still slightly basic; ‘acidification’ means it is moving toward the acid end of the scale."],["Fish senses","Experiments suggest more acidic water may affect some fish’s sense of smell and hearing, though results vary."]],
g:[["pH","A scale measuring acidity"],["Carbonic acid","An acid formed when CO₂ dissolves in water"],["Calcium carbonate","The material of shells and coral skeletons"],["Planetary boundary","A safe limit for an Earth system"]],
f:["The oceans absorb carbon dioxide.","Ocean acidity has risen about 30 percent.","Acidification harms shell-building animals."],
fun:"Since the 1800s, the oceans have become about 30 percent more acidic, even though seawater is still slightly basic."});

ADDART({id:"noisepollution",t:"Noise Pollution",c:"Economics and Society",i:"🔊",w:"Noise pollution",r:["sound","urban","airpollution","ear","sleep","lightpollution"],
p:[["Unwanted sound","Traffic, aircraft, construction and more."],["A health problem","Noise disturbs sleep and raises stress."],["Wildlife affected","Animals struggle to communicate."]],
l:"Noise pollution is harmful or annoying levels of unwanted sound, mostly from human activities such as road traffic, railways, aircraft, construction, factories, loud music and crowded city life. Sound is measured in decibels (dB) on a logarithmic scale, so every increase of 10 decibels means ten times more sound energy and sounds roughly twice as loud. Quiet conversation is about 60 dB, heavy traffic about 85 dB, and a nearby jet engine over 120 dB. The World Health Organization considers noise one of the most important environmental threats to health in Europe after air pollution. Long-term exposure to traffic noise can disturb sleep, raise stress hormones, increase blood pressure and the risk of heart disease, and affect children’s learning and concentration. Loud sounds, from machinery or headphones, can permanently damage hearing. Noise also harms wildlife: birds in cities sing louder or at different times, and underwater noise from ships, sonar and construction interferes with how whales and dolphins communicate and navigate. Cities fight noise with quieter road surfaces, noise barriers, flight-path rules, quieter vehicles and green spaces.",
s:[["Decibels","Prolonged exposure to sounds above about 85 dB can damage hearing. The WHO recommends that average road traffic noise be kept below 53 dB during the day and 45 dB at night."],["Health effects","The European Environment Agency estimates that long-term exposure to transportation noise contributes to many thousands of premature deaths and many more cases of heart disease each year in Europe."],["Ocean noise","Ship traffic has made the oceans much louder since the 1950s. When shipping slowed briefly in 2001 and 2020, scientists measured lower stress hormones in whales."],["Quiet places","Some places protect natural quiet. Organizations certify ‘quiet parks’, and some cities map noise and create quiet zones."]],
k:[["Main sources","Traffic, aircraft, rail, construction, industry"],["Measured in","Decibels (dB)"],["Conversation","About 60 dB"],["Hearing damage risk","Above about 85 dB over time"],["Health effects","Poor sleep, stress, high blood pressure"],["Wildlife effects","Disrupted communication"]],
d:[["1972","US Noise Control Act passed"],["2002","EU Environmental Noise Directive adopted"],["2018","WHO publishes environmental noise guidelines for Europe"]],
s2:[["Electric cars","Electric cars are much quieter at low speeds, so many countries require them to make an artificial sound to warn pedestrians."],["Urban birds","Some city birds, such as European robins, sing at night when it is quieter, partly to avoid daytime noise."]],
g:[["Noise pollution","Harmful or annoying unwanted sound"],["Decibel","A unit measuring sound level"],["Noise barrier","A wall that blocks sound"],["Sonar","Using sound waves to detect objects underwater"]],
f:["Noise is measured in decibels.","Noise can disturb sleep and raise blood pressure.","Ship noise affects whales."],
fun:"Some city robins have started singing at night, when it is quieter."});

