// New articles, 18 November 2026 (part 16): space.
ADDART({id:"lunarlanders",t:"Commercial Moon Landers",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🌖",w:"Commercial Lunar Payload Services",r:["moon","commspace","artemis","changeprogram","moonbases"],
p:[["Companies go to the Moon","Private companies are now building spacecraft to land on the Moon."],["Hard to land","Several early attempts crashed or tipped over."],["A perfect landing","Firefly’s Blue Ghost made the first fully successful commercial Moon landing in 2025."]],
l:"Commercial Moon landers are robotic spacecraft built by private companies to deliver scientific instruments and cargo to the lunar surface, often for NASA and other space agencies. In 2018, NASA started the Commercial Lunar Payload Services (CLPS) program, buying delivery services from companies rather than building its own landers, to prepare for Artemis astronaut missions. Landing on the Moon is difficult because it has no atmosphere for parachutes, so landers must use rocket engines and accurate navigation to touch down gently. Israel’s privately funded Beresheet crashed in April 2019, and Japan’s ispace crashed in April 2023 and again in June 2025. Astrobotic’s Peregrine suffered a fuel leak in January 2024 and never attempted a landing. On 22 February 2024, Intuitive Machines’ Odysseus became the first commercial spacecraft to land on the Moon and the first American lander since 1972, though it tipped onto its side. On 2 March 2025, Firefly Aerospace’s Blue Ghost landed upright in Mare Crisium and completed its full two-week mission, the first fully successful commercial landing. Days later, Intuitive Machines’ Athena also tipped over near the south pole. More landers from Firefly, Intuitive Machines, Astrobotic, Blue Origin and others are planned.",
s:[["Why use companies?","NASA hopes that buying rides, like buying cargo deliveries to the space station, will be cheaper and faster than building its own landers, and that companies will also find other customers, from universities to other countries."],["Why landings fail","Landers must measure their height and speed precisely in the final minutes. Odysseus came down with a disabled laser rangefinder, ispace’s first lander misjudged its altitude, and uneven ground can cause landers to tip over."],["Blue Ghost’s mission","Blue Ghost carried ten NASA instruments, drilled into the soil, tested a lunar dust-repelling system and recorded high-definition video of a total solar eclipse as Earth passed in front of the Sun, seen from the Moon, on 14 March 2025."],["Toward Artemis","Commercial landers scout the lunar south pole, where astronauts may land, studying water ice, radiation and the ground. Larger landers are being built to carry rovers and heavy cargo."]],
k:[["NASA program","Commercial Lunar Payload Services (2018)"],["First commercial landing","Odysseus, 22 February 2024"],["First fully successful commercial landing","Blue Ghost, 2 March 2025"],["Main challenge","Precise, gentle touchdown"],["Key target","Lunar south pole"]],
d:[["2018","NASA starts the CLPS program"],["2019","Beresheet crashes on the Moon"],["2023","ispace’s first lander crashes"],["2024","Odysseus lands on its side"],["2025","Blue Ghost lands successfully; Athena tips over"],["2025","ispace’s second lander crashes"]],
s2:[["Eclipse from the Moon","On 14 March 2025, Blue Ghost filmed Earth blocking the Sun, a solar eclipse seen from the Moon, while people on Earth saw a lunar eclipse at the same time."],["Lunar laws","Commercial landers carry items from around the world, from art to time capsules. The Artemis Accords and international discussions aim to set rules for activities on the Moon."]],
sp:["‘Odie’ the lander","Odysseus, nicknamed ‘Odie’, carried a camera system built by university students that was meant to photograph the lander as it descended, but it was not released during the descent as planned."],
g:[["Lander","A spacecraft that touches down on a surface"],["CLPS","NASA’s Commercial Lunar Payload Services program"],["Payload","Cargo carried by a spacecraft"],["Rangefinder","A device that measures distance"]],
f:["Odysseus was the first commercial Moon lander, in 2024.","Blue Ghost made the first fully successful commercial landing.","Several private landers have crashed."],
fun:"In 2025, a commercial lander filmed a solar eclipse from the Moon as Earth passed in front of the Sun."});

ADDART({id:"shuttledisasters",t:"The Challenger and Columbia Disasters",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🕯️",w:"Space Shuttle Columbia disaster",r:["spaceshuttle","spaceagencies","astronauts","womeninspace","iss"],
p:[["Two tragedies","Two Space Shuttles were lost with their crews, in 1986 and 2003."],["Challenger","Challenger broke apart 73 seconds after launch."],["Columbia","Columbia broke apart while returning to Earth."]],
l:"Two of NASA’s five Space Shuttle orbiters were destroyed in accidents that killed their seven-person crews. On 28 January 1986, the Space Shuttle Challenger broke apart 73 seconds after launch from Florida, watched live by millions, including schoolchildren following teacher Christa McAuliffe, who was to be the first teacher in space. An investigation by the Rogers Commission found that a rubber O-ring seal in one of the solid rocket boosters had failed in unusually cold weather, letting hot gas escape and burn through to the external fuel tank. Engineers had warned of the danger the night before, but managers approved the launch. Shuttle flights stopped for nearly three years. On 1 February 2003, the Space Shuttle Columbia broke apart over Texas as it returned from a 16-day science mission. During launch, a piece of insulating foam from the external tank had struck the left wing, damaging the heat shield; hot gases entered during reentry and destroyed the wing. The crew included Ilan Ramon, Israel’s first astronaut, and Indian-born Kalpana Chawla. Both investigations criticized NASA’s safety culture. The disasters led NASA to retire the shuttle in 2011 and design safer spacecraft with escape systems.",
s:[["Challenger’s crew","Challenger’s crew were Francis Scobee, Michael Smith, Judith Resnik, Ellison Onizuka, Ronald McNair, Gregory Jarvis and Christa McAuliffe. McAuliffe’s planned lessons from space were later taught by astronauts on the space station in 2018."],["Feynman’s demonstration","During a televised hearing, physicist Richard Feynman, a commission member, dipped a piece of O-ring material into a glass of ice water and showed that it lost its springiness in the cold, a simple demonstration of the cause."],["Columbia’s crew","Columbia’s crew were Rick Husband, William McCool, Michael Anderson, Kalpana Chawla, David Brown, Laurel Clark and Ilan Ramon. Debris fell across Texas and Louisiana and was gathered by thousands of searchers."],["Lessons","Investigators said NASA had become used to warning signs, such as earlier foam strikes and O-ring damage, treating them as normal. The term ‘normalization of deviance’ is now taught in engineering and management courses."]],
k:[["Challenger disaster","28 January 1986"],["Cause (Challenger)","Failed O-ring seal in cold weather"],["Columbia disaster","1 February 2003"],["Cause (Columbia)","Foam strike damaged the wing’s heat shield"],["Lives lost","14 astronauts"]],
d:[["1981","First Space Shuttle flight"],["1986","Challenger disaster"],["1988","Shuttle flights resume"],["2003","Columbia disaster"],["2005","Shuttle flights resume"],["2011","Space Shuttle retired"]],
s2:[["Memorials","The Space Mirror Memorial at Kennedy Space Center honors astronauts who died in the line of duty. Craters on the Moon and hills on Mars are named after the Challenger and Columbia crews."],["Kalpana Chawla","Kalpana Chawla, born in Karnal, India, was the first woman of Indian origin to fly in space, in 1997. Schools, scholarships and a satellite have been named in her honor."]],
sp:["Worms survived","Tiny worms called C. elegans, part of an experiment aboard Columbia, were found alive in their containers among the debris, an extraordinary survival story."],
g:[["O-ring","A rubber ring that seals a joint"],["Heat shield","Protection against the heat of reentry"],["Reentry","Returning into Earth’s atmosphere"],["Safety culture","An organization’s attitudes toward safety"]],
f:["Challenger was lost in 1986.","Columbia was lost in 2003.","Both disasters killed seven astronauts."],
fun:"Tiny worms from a science experiment aboard Columbia survived the disaster and were found alive in the debris."});

ADDART({id:"apollo8",t:"Apollo 8",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🌍",w:"Apollo 8",r:["apollo","moon","saturnv","space","earthplanet"],
p:[["First humans around the Moon","Apollo 8 carried the first people to orbit the Moon in December 1968."],["Earthrise","The crew took the famous photograph of Earth rising over the Moon."],["Christmas Eve","They read from the Book of Genesis on live television."]],
l:"Apollo 8 was the first crewed spacecraft to leave Earth’s orbit, reach the Moon and orbit it. Launched on 21 December 1968 on the first crewed flight of the giant Saturn V rocket, it carried astronauts Frank Borman, Jim Lovell and William Anders. NASA had planned the mission to test the lunar module in Earth orbit, but because the lunar module was delayed and there were fears the Soviet Union might send cosmonauts around the Moon first, managers boldly changed it to a lunar mission with just four months’ notice. On 24 December, Apollo 8 entered orbit around the Moon, becoming the first time humans saw the Moon’s far side with their own eyes. During its fourth orbit, Anders photographed Earth rising above the lunar horizon, an image called Earthrise that became one of the most famous photographs ever taken and helped inspire the environmental movement. On Christmas Eve, the crew read the first verses of the Book of Genesis during a television broadcast watched by an estimated one billion people. After ten orbits, they fired their engine to return home, splashing down in the Pacific on 27 December. Apollo 8 paved the way for the Apollo 11 landing seven months later.",
s:[["A bold decision","Sending Apollo 8 to the Moon was a big risk: the Saturn V had flown only twice without crew, and the second flight had problems. Success proved that the spacecraft and navigation could carry humans to the Moon and back."],["Earthrise","Anders took the photo with a handheld camera after the crew suddenly saw Earth appearing over the Moon’s edge. The image of a small blue planet in the blackness of space changed how many people thought about Earth."],["Firsts","The Apollo 8 astronauts were the first humans to travel beyond low Earth orbit, to experience the Moon’s gravity, to see the whole Earth as a sphere and to see the far side of the Moon."],["‘The best Christmas present’","After the mission, a telegram to Borman reportedly said ‘Thank you Apollo 8. You saved 1968’, a year marked by war, assassinations and unrest. The crew were named Time magazine’s Men of the Year."]],
k:[["Launched","21 December 1968"],["Crew","Frank Borman, Jim Lovell, William Anders"],["Lunar orbits","10"],["Famous photo","Earthrise, 24 December 1968"],["Splashdown","27 December 1968"]],
d:[["1968","Apollo 8 launched on the first crewed Saturn V"],["1968","Enters orbit around the Moon on 24 December"],["1968","Earthrise photographed; Genesis reading broadcast"],["1968","Splashes down in the Pacific"],["1969","Apollo 11 lands on the Moon"]],
s2:[["Jim Lovell","Lovell later commanded Apollo 13, which suffered an explosion on the way to the Moon in 1970, so he traveled to the Moon twice without ever landing on it."],["Earthrise stamp","The Earthrise photograph appeared on a US postage stamp in 1969, and the first Earth Day in 1970 was inspired partly by images of Earth from space."]],
sp:["Santa Claus","When Apollo 8 emerged from behind the Moon after its engine burn to head home on Christmas Day, Jim Lovell radioed, ‘Please be informed, there is a Santa Claus.’"],
g:[["Lunar orbit","Circling the Moon"],["Far side","The side of the Moon that always faces away from Earth"],["Splashdown","Landing a spacecraft in the ocean"],["Earthrise","Earth appearing over the Moon’s horizon"]],
f:["Apollo 8 was the first crewed flight to the Moon.","Its crew took the Earthrise photo.","It orbited the Moon ten times."],
fun:"On Christmas Day 1968, Jim Lovell told Mission Control from lunar orbit, ‘Please be informed, there is a Santa Claus.’"});

ADDART({id:"rangersurveyor",t:"Ranger, Surveyor and Lunar Orbiter",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🌕",w:"Surveyor program",r:["moon","apollo","lunaprogram","lro","space"],
p:[["Scouting the Moon","Robot spacecraft prepared the way for Apollo astronauts."],["Crash and photograph","Ranger probes sent close-up pictures before crashing."],["Soft landings","Surveyor landers showed the Moon’s surface could support a spacecraft."]],
l:"Before astronauts could land on the Moon, NASA sent three series of robotic spacecraft to study it. The Ranger program (1961–1965) aimed spacecraft straight at the Moon, taking pictures until they crashed. After six failures, Ranger 7 succeeded on 31 July 1964, sending back more than 4,300 images showing craters as small as a meter across; Ranger 9’s pictures in March 1965 were broadcast live on television. Lunar Orbiter spacecraft (1966–1967) circled the Moon and mapped about 99 percent of its surface, helping choose Apollo landing sites; Lunar Orbiter 1 also took the first picture of Earth from the Moon’s vicinity. The Surveyor program (1966–1968) made soft landings to test the surface. Surveyor 1 landed on 2 June 1966, a few months after the Soviet Luna 9 made the first soft landing, and showed that the ground was firm enough to support a lander, ending fears that spacecraft might sink into deep dust. Five of the seven Surveyors succeeded, testing soil with scoops and chemical analyzers. In November 1969, Apollo 12 astronauts landed near Surveyor 3 and brought parts of it back to Earth, allowing scientists to study how materials aged on the Moon.",
s:[["Ranger’s struggles","The first six Ranger missions failed because of rocket, camera and electronics problems, nearly ending the program. Ranger 7’s success restored confidence and gave the first close-up views of the Moon."],["Lunar Orbiter","The Lunar Orbiter cameras used film that was developed on board, scanned and transmitted to Earth. In 2008, a project recovered and restored the original data, producing much sharper versions of the 1966 pictures."],["Surveyor","Surveyor landers carried television cameras that sent back thousands of images. Some had robotic scoops that dug trenches, and later ones measured the chemistry of the soil."],["Surveyor 3 and Apollo 12","Apollo 12 landed within about 200 meters of Surveyor 3. Astronauts Pete Conrad and Alan Bean walked to it and removed its camera and other parts, now displayed in museums."]],
k:[["Ranger program","1961–1965"],["First successful Ranger","Ranger 7, 31 July 1964"],["Lunar Orbiter program","1966–1967"],["Surveyor 1 landing","2 June 1966"],["Successful Surveyors","5 of 7"]],
d:[["1964","Ranger 7 sends close-up images before impact"],["1965","Ranger 9 images broadcast live"],["1966","Surveyor 1 soft-lands on the Moon"],["1966","Lunar Orbiter 1 photographs Earth from near the Moon"],["1967","Surveyor 3 lands"],["1969","Apollo 12 visits Surveyor 3"]],
s2:[["The germ controversy","Scientists reported finding bacteria inside Surveyor 3’s camera after its return, suggesting microbes had survived on the Moon. Later studies concluded the bacteria probably came from contamination after the camera returned to Earth."],["Luna 9","The Soviet Luna 9 made the first soft landing on the Moon on 3 February 1966 and sent the first photographs from the lunar surface."]],
sp:["Live from the Moon","Ranger 9’s final pictures as it plunged into the crater Alphonsus in 1965 were shown live on US television with the caption ‘Live from the Moon’."],
g:[["Soft landing","Touching down gently"],["Impactor","A spacecraft designed to crash into a target"],["Regolith","The loose dust and rock on the Moon’s surface"],["Landing site","A chosen place to touch down"]],
f:["Ranger 7 sent the first close-up Moon images in 1964.","Surveyor 1 soft-landed in 1966.","Apollo 12 astronauts visited Surveyor 3."],
fun:"Apollo 12 astronauts landed next to the robotic Surveyor 3 and took parts of it home."});

ADDART({id:"lunokhod",t:"The Lunokhod Moon Rovers",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🛞",w:"Lunokhod programme",r:["lunaprogram","moon","marsrovers","lro","space"],
p:[["First rovers on another world","Soviet Lunokhod rovers explored the Moon in the 1970s."],["Driven from Earth","Teams on Earth steered them by remote control."],["Still useful","Their reflectors are still used to measure the distance to the Moon."]],
l:"Lunokhod (meaning ‘Moonwalker’ in Russian) was a series of Soviet robotic rovers that explored the Moon, the first remote-controlled vehicles to travel on another world. Lunokhod 1 was carried by the Luna 17 lander, which touched down in the Sea of Rains on 17 November 1970. Looking like a bathtub on eight wheels, with a lid covered in solar cells, the rover was driven by a team of five operators in the Soviet Union, who watched slow television images and dealt with a delay of several seconds. Lunokhod 1 worked for about ten months, far longer than planned, traveled about 10.5 kilometers, took more than 20,000 pictures and tested the soil. Lunokhod 2, landed by Luna 21 in January 1973, traveled about 39 kilometers in four months, a record for distance on another world that stood until NASA’s Opportunity rover passed it on Mars in 2014. Both rovers carried French-made laser reflectors. After Lunokhod 1’s location was lost for decades, NASA’s Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter photographed it in 2010, and scientists bounced lasers off its reflector, which still helps measure the Moon’s distance precisely.",
s:[["How it worked","Lunokhod’s lid opened during the day to collect sunlight with solar cells and closed at night to keep in heat. A small radioactive heater kept its electronics warm during the two-week lunar nights, when temperatures fall below −150°C."],["Driving on the Moon","Operators sat at consoles in Crimea, steering the rover in short moves. Because the TV images arrived slowly, they had to anticipate obstacles, a difficult and tiring job. The team was kept secret for years."],["Lunokhod 2’s end","Lunokhod 2 ended its mission in May 1973, apparently after it brushed against a crater wall and soil covered its radiator, causing it to overheat."],["A private owner","In 1993, Lunokhod 2 and the Luna 21 lander were sold at a Sotheby’s auction to American video game developer Richard Garriott, who became the owner of an object on the Moon."]],
k:[["First rover on another world","Lunokhod 1, landed 17 November 1970"],["Lunokhod 1 distance","About 10.5 km"],["Lunokhod 2 distance","About 39 km"],["Control","Remote control from Earth"],["Still in use","Laser reflectors"]],
d:[["1970","Lunokhod 1 lands on the Moon"],["1971","Lunokhod 1 mission ends"],["1973","Lunokhod 2 lands and travels about 39 km"],["1993","Lunokhod 2 sold at auction"],["2010","LRO finds Lunokhod 1"],["2014","Opportunity breaks Lunokhod 2’s distance record"]],
s2:[["Chinese and Indian rovers","After Lunokhod, no rover drove on the Moon until China’s Yutu in 2013. China’s Yutu-2 became the first rover on the Moon’s far side in 2019, and India’s Pragyan rover explored near the south pole in 2023."],["Lunar Roving Vehicle","Unlike the robotic Lunokhods, the Lunar Roving Vehicles of Apollo 15, 16 and 17 were driven by astronauts sitting in them."]],
sp:["A rover’s chair","Lunokhod operators worked in teams, with a commander, driver, navigator, engineer and antenna operator, like the crew of a ship."],
g:[["Rover","A vehicle that travels over a surface"],["Remote control","Operating a machine from a distance"],["Laser reflector","A device that bounces laser light back"],["Lunar night","The two-week dark period on the Moon"]],
f:["Lunokhod 1 was the first rover on another world.","Lunokhod 2 traveled about 39 kilometers.","Their laser reflectors are still used."],
fun:"An American video game developer bought Lunokhod 2 at auction in 1993, so he owns a robot on the Moon."});

ADDART({id:"marslanders",t:"Mars Landers",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🔴",w:"Phoenix (spacecraft)",r:["mars","marsrovers","vikinglanders","insight","marsorbiters"],
p:[["Touching down on Mars","Landers are spacecraft that set down on Mars’s surface."],["Bouncing airbags","Mars Pathfinder landed in 1997 by bouncing on airbags."],["Ice at the pole","Phoenix dug up water ice near Mars’s north pole in 2008."]],
l:"Mars landers are spacecraft that descend through the Martian atmosphere and touch down on its surface to study the planet up close. Landing on Mars is notoriously hard: the atmosphere is thick enough to heat a spacecraft but too thin for parachutes alone to slow it, so landers combine heat shields, parachutes and rockets or airbags in about seven minutes of descent. The Soviet Mars 3 made the first soft landing on 2 December 1971 but stopped transmitting after about 20 seconds. NASA’s Viking 1 and 2 landed successfully in 1976 and worked for years. On 4 July 1997, Mars Pathfinder bounced to a stop wrapped in airbags and released Sojourner, the first rover on Mars. Several missions failed, including NASA’s Mars Polar Lander in 1999, Britain’s Beagle 2 in 2003 and Europe’s Schiaparelli in 2016. On 25 May 2008, NASA’s Phoenix landed in the Martian Arctic and used its robotic arm to dig up water ice and discover perchlorate salts in the soil. Later landers include InSight (2018), which listened for marsquakes, and the rover-carrying landers of Curiosity, Perseverance and China’s Zhurong. About half of all attempts to land on Mars have failed.",
s:[["Seven minutes of terror","Engineers call a Mars landing ‘seven minutes of terror’ because the spacecraft must slow from about 20,000 kilometers per hour to a safe landing on its own; radio signals take several minutes to reach Earth, so controllers cannot intervene."],["Pathfinder’s airbags","Pathfinder used a cluster of airbags that inflated around it, bounced up to about 15 meters high and rolled before stopping. The same method landed the Spirit and Opportunity rovers in 2004."],["Phoenix","Phoenix scraped soil and watched white chunks of ice vanish over a few days as they turned directly into gas. It also saw snow falling from clouds, though it evaporated before reaching the ground."],["Beagle 2","Beagle 2, carried by Europe’s Mars Express, disappeared during landing on Christmas Day 2003. In 2015, the Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter found it on the surface, partly unfolded, showing it had landed but its solar panels had not fully opened."]],
k:[["First soft landing","Mars 3, 2 December 1971"],["First successful long-term landers","Viking 1 and 2, 1976"],["First rover","Sojourner, 1997"],["Phoenix landing","25 May 2008"],["Failure rate","About half of attempts"]],
d:[["1971","Mars 3 makes the first soft landing"],["1976","Viking 1 and 2 land"],["1997","Mars Pathfinder lands with Sojourner"],["1999","Mars Polar Lander lost"],["2003","Beagle 2 lost during landing"],["2008","Phoenix lands near the north pole"],["2016","Schiaparelli crashes"]],
s2:[["Perchlorates","Phoenix discovered perchlorate salts, which are toxic to humans but could lower the freezing point of water, allowing liquid brines to exist briefly. Some microbes on Earth can use perchlorate for energy."],["Sky crane","For heavier rovers, NASA developed the sky crane, a rocket-powered platform that lowers the rover on cables and then flies away, used for Curiosity in 2012 and Perseverance in 2021."]],
sp:["A DVD on Mars","Phoenix carried a DVD called ‘Visions of Mars’ with stories, art and the names of a quarter of a million people, intended as a library for future human explorers."],
g:[["Lander","A spacecraft that touches down on a surface"],["Heat shield","Protection against heat during atmospheric entry"],["Perchlorate","A salt containing chlorine and oxygen"],["Sky crane","A system that lowers a rover on cables"]],
f:["Mars 3 made the first soft landing in 1971.","Pathfinder bounced to a stop on airbags.","Phoenix dug up water ice in 2008."],
fun:"Mars Pathfinder bounced on airbags about 15 meters high before rolling to a stop on Mars."});

ADDART({id:"dawnmission",t:"The Dawn Mission",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🌄",w:"Dawn (spacecraft)",r:["ceres","vesta","asteroidbelt","rocketengines","dwarfplanets"],
p:[["Two worlds in one mission","Dawn orbited the giant asteroid Vesta and the dwarf planet Ceres."],["Ion engines","It used gentle but efficient ion engines."],["Bright spots","It solved the mystery of Ceres’s bright spots."]],
l:"Dawn was a NASA spacecraft that explored the two largest objects in the asteroid belt between Mars and Jupiter: the giant asteroid Vesta and the dwarf planet Ceres. Launched on 27 September 2007, it became the first spacecraft to orbit two different bodies beyond Earth and the first to orbit a dwarf planet. Dawn used ion engines, which accelerate charged xenon atoms with electricity from large solar panels. Their push was tiny, about the weight of a sheet of paper resting in your hand, but they could run for years, giving Dawn a total change in speed greater than any earlier spacecraft’s own engines. Dawn orbited Vesta from July 2011 to September 2012, revealing a world with a huge impact basin at its south pole and a mountain more than twice as tall as Mount Everest. It then traveled to Ceres, arriving on 6 March 2015. Ceres turned out to have bright spots in Occator crater, which Dawn found were deposits of salts, mainly sodium carbonate, left by salty water rising from below, suggesting Ceres may still have brine underground. Dawn ran out of fuel for steering in November 2018 and remains in orbit around Ceres.",
s:[["Ion propulsion","Chemical rockets give a big push for a few minutes, while ion engines give a gentle push for months. Dawn’s engines took four days to accelerate from 0 to 100 kilometers per hour, but over years they changed its speed by about 11 kilometers per second."],["Vesta","Vesta’s southern Rheasilvia basin is about 500 kilometers wide, with a central peak about 22 kilometers high. The impact that made it threw out rocks, some of which fell to Earth as meteorites, linking Vesta to about 6 percent of meteorites found on Earth."],["Ceres","Ceres, about 940 kilometers across, contains much water ice and clay minerals. Ahuna Mons, a lonely mountain about 4 kilometers tall, appears to be an ice volcano that erupted salty mud."],["The end","Dawn’s mission ended on 1 November 2018 when it ran out of hydrazine for pointing. It was left in a stable orbit to avoid crashing onto Ceres, which could contaminate a world that might hold clues to life’s chemistry."]],
k:[["Launched","27 September 2007"],["Vesta orbit","July 2011–September 2012"],["Ceres arrival","6 March 2015"],["Propulsion","Ion engines using xenon"],["Mission ended","1 November 2018"]],
d:[["2007","Dawn launched"],["2009","Mars gravity assist"],["2011","Enters orbit around Vesta"],["2012","Departs Vesta"],["2015","Enters orbit around Ceres"],["2018","Mission ends"]],
s2:[["Why ‘Dawn’?","The mission was named Dawn because Vesta and Ceres are relics from the dawn of the solar system, preserving information about how planets formed."],["Ceres in history","Ceres was discovered in 1801 by Giuseppe Piazzi and was first called a planet, then an asteroid, and since 2006 a dwarf planet."]],
sp:["Gentle push","Dawn’s ion engine pushed with about the same force as the weight of a single sheet of paper, yet it carried the spacecraft between worlds."],
g:[["Ion engine","An engine that accelerates charged atoms with electricity"],["Xenon","A heavy gas used as ion engine fuel"],["Dwarf planet","A small planet-like body"],["Cryovolcano","An ice volcano"]],
f:["Dawn orbited Vesta and Ceres.","It used ion engines.","Ceres’s bright spots are salt deposits."],
fun:"Dawn’s ion engine pushed only as hard as the weight of a sheet of paper, but it ran for years."});

ADDART({id:"ulysses",t:"Ulysses: Over the Sun’s Poles",c:"Space Exploration",i:"☀️",w:"Ulysses (spacecraft)",r:["sun","solarwind","jupiter","solarobservatories","spaceweather"],
p:[["Flying over the Sun","Ulysses was the first spacecraft to fly over the Sun’s poles."],["A boost from Jupiter","It used Jupiter’s gravity to swing out of the planets’ plane."],["Studying the solar wind","It mapped the solar wind in three dimensions."]],
l:"Ulysses was a joint mission of the European Space Agency and NASA that became the first spacecraft to study the Sun from high above and below its poles. Most spacecraft travel close to the ecliptic, the flat plane in which the planets orbit, so the Sun’s polar regions had never been observed from overhead. Leaving that plane takes enormous energy, so after its launch from the Space Shuttle Discovery on 6 October 1990, Ulysses first flew to Jupiter. In February 1992, Jupiter’s gravity flung it into a steeply tilted orbit around the Sun, passing over the Sun’s poles. Ulysses flew over the south pole in 1994 and the north pole in 1995, and repeated the passes in 2000–2001 and 2007–2008, observing the Sun at both quiet and active times in its 11-year cycle. It found that fast solar wind streams from the poles and that the Sun’s magnetic field reaches into space differently than expected. It also measured interstellar dust and gas flowing into the solar system and unexpectedly flew through the tails of several comets. Planned for five years, Ulysses operated for more than 18 years until 30 June 2009, when its power supply weakened.",
s:[["Why the poles?","The Sun’s magnetic field and solar wind behave differently at its poles than near its equator. Ulysses carried instruments to measure particles and magnetic fields rather than cameras, sampling the space around the Sun directly."],["Comet tails","In 1996, Ulysses passed through the ion tail of Comet Hyakutake, more than 500 million kilometers from the comet’s head, showing that comet tails are far longer than previously known."],["Solar cycle","Ulysses saw that during solar minimum, fast wind flows steadily from the poles, but during solar maximum, the wind is mixed and chaotic at all latitudes."],["Powered by plutonium","Ulysses was too far from the Sun at times for solar panels, so it used a radioisotope generator. As the generator weakened, its fuel lines began to freeze, ending the mission."]],
k:[["Agencies","ESA and NASA"],["Launched","6 October 1990"],["Jupiter gravity assist","February 1992"],["First polar pass","Sun’s south pole, 1994"],["Mission ended","30 June 2009"]],
d:[["1990","Ulysses launched from the Space Shuttle"],["1992","Jupiter flyby sends it out of the ecliptic"],["1994","First pass over the Sun’s south pole"],["1995","First pass over the Sun’s north pole"],["1996","Flies through Comet Hyakutake’s tail"],["2009","Mission ends"]],
s2:[["The name","Ulysses is named after the Latin name of Odysseus, the hero of Homer’s Odyssey, and the character in Dante’s Inferno who sails into unknown regions."],["Solar Orbiter","ESA’s Solar Orbiter, launched in 2020, carries cameras and is gradually tilting its orbit to photograph the Sun’s poles, building on Ulysses’s discoveries."]],
sp:["Long-distance flight","To reach the Sun’s poles, Ulysses traveled out to Jupiter, more than five times Earth’s distance from the Sun, showing that sometimes the best route to the Sun is to fly away from it first."],
g:[["Ecliptic","The plane in which the planets orbit"],["Gravity assist","Using a planet’s gravity to change a spacecraft’s path"],["Solar cycle","The Sun’s 11-year activity cycle"],["Solar wind","Charged particles flowing from the Sun"]],
f:["Ulysses was the first to fly over the Sun’s poles.","It used Jupiter’s gravity.","It operated for more than 18 years."],
fun:"To fly over the Sun’s poles, Ulysses first had to travel all the way out to Jupiter."});

ADDART({id:"fermitelescope",t:"Gamma-Ray Space Telescopes",c:"Space Exploration",i:"⚡",w:"Fermi Gamma-ray Space Telescope",r:["gammaraybursts","bh","pulsar","gravwaves","spitzer"],
p:[["The most energetic light","Gamma rays are light with the highest energy."],["Compton and Fermi","NASA’s Compton and Fermi observatories mapped the gamma-ray sky."],["Giant bubbles","Fermi discovered huge gamma-ray bubbles above and below our galaxy."]],
l:"Gamma rays are the most energetic form of light, produced by the most violent events in the universe, such as exploding stars, black holes devouring matter and colliding neutron stars. Earth’s atmosphere blocks gamma rays, so they must be observed from space. NASA’s Compton Gamma Ray Observatory, launched by the Space Shuttle Atlantis on 5 April 1991, was one of the four Great Observatories and, at about 17 metric tons, among the heaviest science satellites ever flown. It showed that gamma-ray bursts come from all directions in the sky, evidence that they originate in distant galaxies. Compton was deliberately brought down into the Pacific Ocean in June 2000. NASA’s Fermi Gamma-ray Space Telescope, launched on 11 June 2008 and named after physicist Enrico Fermi, scans the entire sky every three hours. In 2010, Fermi scientists discovered two enormous bubbles of gamma rays extending tens of thousands of light-years above and below the center of the Milky Way, probably blown out by past activity of its central black hole. On 17 August 2017, Fermi detected a burst of gamma rays two seconds after gravitational wave detectors recorded colliding neutron stars, a landmark in multi-messenger astronomy.",
s:[["Detecting gamma rays","Gamma rays cannot be focused with mirrors like visible light. Fermi’s main instrument detects them when they turn into pairs of electrons and positrons inside layers of tungsten, tracking the particles to find each gamma ray’s direction and energy."],["Gamma-ray bursts","Gamma-ray bursts are brief flashes, lasting from milliseconds to minutes, that can outshine whole galaxies. Long bursts come from collapsing massive stars, and short bursts from merging neutron stars. Fermi detects about 240 bursts a year."],["Thunderstorms","Fermi has detected terrestrial gamma-ray flashes from thunderstorms on Earth and even beams of antimatter produced by lightning, a surprising discovery."],["Swift","NASA’s Swift observatory, launched in 2004, quickly swings around to observe gamma-ray bursts with X-ray and ultraviolet telescopes, helping pinpoint their locations within minutes."]],
k:[["Compton launched","5 April 1991"],["Compton mass","About 17 metric tons"],["Fermi launched","11 June 2008"],["Fermi bubbles discovered","2010"],["Neutron star merger burst","17 August 2017"]],
d:[["1967","Spy satellites detect the first gamma-ray bursts"],["1991","Compton Gamma Ray Observatory launched"],["2000","Compton deorbited"],["2004","Swift launched"],["2008","Fermi launched"],["2010","Fermi bubbles discovered"],["2017","Gamma rays detected from merging neutron stars"]],
s2:[["Spy satellite discovery","Gamma-ray bursts were discovered in 1967 by US Vela satellites built to watch for secret nuclear weapons tests. The flashes came from space, not bombs, and the discovery was kept secret until 1973."],["Enrico Fermi","Enrico Fermi, an Italian-American physicist, built the first nuclear reactor in 1942 and proposed how cosmic particles could be accelerated to high energies, a process now named after him."]],
sp:["Antimatter from lightning","Fermi found that thunderstorms can produce beams of positrons, the antimatter version of electrons, which shoot into space along Earth’s magnetic field."],
g:[["Gamma ray","The highest-energy form of light"],["Gamma-ray burst","A brief, intense flash of gamma rays"],["Multi-messenger astronomy","Studying events with light and other signals"],["Positron","The antimatter version of an electron"]],
f:["Gamma rays are the most energetic light.","Fermi scans the whole sky every three hours.","Fermi discovered giant bubbles around our galaxy."],
fun:"Gamma-ray bursts were discovered by spy satellites looking for secret nuclear bomb tests."});

ADDART({id:"fastradiobursts",t:"Fast Radio Bursts",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"📻",w:"Fast radio burst",r:["radioastronomy","magnetars","pulsar","seti","galaxies"],
p:[["Mysterious flashes","Fast radio bursts are brief, powerful flashes of radio waves from space."],["Over in milliseconds","Each lasts only a few thousandths of a second."],["Magnetars","At least some come from highly magnetic dead stars."]],
l:"Fast radio bursts (FRBs) are extremely brief and powerful flashes of radio waves from distant space, each lasting only milliseconds yet releasing as much energy as the Sun does over days or even years. The first was found in 2007 by astronomers Duncan Lorimer and his student David Narkevic, who discovered a strange signal in archived data recorded in 2001 by the Parkes radio telescope in Australia, now called the Lorimer burst. At first some astronomers doubted FRBs were real, especially after other strange signals at Parkes, called perytons, turned out to come from staff opening a microwave oven door too early. However, more bursts were found, and in 2016 astronomers discovered one source that repeats, allowing them to trace it to a small galaxy about 3 billion light-years away. Most FRBs come from far beyond the Milky Way. On 28 April 2020, telescopes detected an FRB-like burst from a magnetar, a highly magnetic neutron star, in our own galaxy, showing that magnetars can produce them. Canada’s CHIME radio telescope now detects several bursts a day, and thousands are known. Astronomers also use FRBs to measure the gas between galaxies, helping find ‘missing’ ordinary matter.",
s:[["How they are found","Radio telescopes search for short pulses whose lower frequencies arrive slightly later than higher frequencies, a delay caused by traveling through clouds of electrons in space. The bigger the delay, the farther the burst has traveled."],["Repeaters","Most FRBs have been seen only once, but some repeat, sometimes in irregular bursts and sometimes in regular cycles. One repeater flares in a 16-day pattern, possibly because its source orbits a companion star."],["Perytons","In 2015, astronomers discovered that signals called perytons, which had puzzled them for years, came from a microwave oven at the Parkes observatory when someone opened its door before it finished heating."],["Not aliens","Because FRBs are bright and mysterious, people have asked whether they could be signals from aliens. Astronomers think natural explanations, especially magnetars, are far more likely."]],
k:[["First discovered","2007 (in 2001 data)"],["Duration","Milliseconds"],["First repeating source","Found 2016"],["Galactic burst from a magnetar","28 April 2020"],["Main detector","CHIME telescope, Canada"]],
d:[["2001","Parkes records the first known burst"],["2007","Lorimer burst discovered"],["2015","Perytons traced to a microwave oven"],["2016","First repeating FRB identified"],["2018","CHIME telescope begins detecting bursts"],["2020","FRB-like burst from a magnetar in the Milky Way"]],
s2:[["CHIME","The Canadian Hydrogen Intensity Mapping Experiment (CHIME), in British Columbia, looks like four giant half-pipes and has no moving parts, scanning the sky as Earth rotates. It has found thousands of FRBs."],["Missing matter","The delays in FRB signals revealed that much of the universe’s ordinary matter is spread thinly between galaxies, solving a long-standing puzzle about where it was hiding."]],
sp:["A microwave mystery","For years, astronomers puzzled over radio signals at Parkes that turned out to come from impatient staff opening a microwave oven at lunchtime."],
g:[["Fast radio burst","A millisecond flash of radio waves from space"],["Magnetar","A neutron star with an extremely strong magnetic field"],["Repeater","An FRB source that flashes more than once"],["Dispersion","Spreading of a signal by frequency"]],
f:["The first FRB was discovered in 2007.","FRBs last only milliseconds.","Some FRBs come from magnetars."],
fun:"Some strange radio signals once thought to be from space turned out to come from a microwave oven at the observatory."});

ADDART({id:"verarubin",t:"The Vera C. Rubin Observatory",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🎥",w:"Vera C. Rubin Observatory",r:["groundtelescopes","darkmatter","planetnine","chilecountry","asteroidbelt"],
p:[["A movie of the sky","Rubin Observatory photographs the entire southern sky every few nights."],["The biggest camera","It has the largest digital camera ever built for astronomy."],["Survey begins","Its 10-year survey began in 2025–2026."]],
l:"The Vera C. Rubin Observatory is an astronomical observatory on Cerro Pachón, a mountain in the Andes of northern Chile, designed to make a 10-year time-lapse movie of the southern sky called the Legacy Survey of Space and Time (LSST). Funded by the US National Science Foundation and Department of Energy, it is named after American astronomer Vera Rubin, whose studies of how galaxies rotate provided key evidence for dark matter. Its 8.4-meter telescope can swing quickly across the sky, and its camera, with 3,200 megapixels, is the largest digital camera ever built for astronomy; each image covers an area of sky about 45 times that of the full Moon. Rubin will photograph the whole visible sky every few nights, detecting anything that moves or changes, such as asteroids, comets, exploding stars and variable stars, and sending millions of alerts to astronomers each night. Its first images, released on 23 June 2025, showed glowing nebulae and millions of galaxies, and included more than 2,000 newly discovered asteroids. The 10-year survey officially began in mid-2026. Rubin is expected to catalog about 20 billion galaxies, find millions of asteroids and help study dark energy and dark matter, and might even find a distant ‘Planet Nine’.",
s:[["The camera","The LSST Camera is about the size of a small car and weighs about 3 metric tons. Its detectors are cooled to about −100°C. A single image would need hundreds of ultra-high-definition televisions to display at full resolution."],["Data","Rubin will produce about 20 terabytes of data every night. Computers compare each new image with earlier ones and send alerts within about a minute of spotting changes."],["Protecting Earth","Rubin is expected to discover most near-Earth asteroids larger than 140 meters, helping planetary defense experts identify any that might threaten Earth."],["Satellite streaks","Growing numbers of bright satellites in low orbit leave streaks across telescope images. Rubin’s wide, deep images are especially affected, and astronomers are working with satellite companies to reduce brightness."]],
k:[["Location","Cerro Pachón, Chile"],["Telescope mirror","8.4 meters"],["Camera","3,200 megapixels"],["First images","23 June 2025"],["Survey length","10 years"]],
d:[["1970s","Vera Rubin’s galaxy rotation studies point to dark matter"],["2015","Construction begins"],["2019","Observatory renamed for Vera Rubin"],["2025","LSST Camera installed on the telescope; first images released"],["2026","10-year survey officially begins"]],
s2:[["Vera Rubin","Vera Rubin (1928–2016) faced discrimination as a woman in astronomy; Princeton would not admit women to its graduate astronomy program when she applied. Her work on galaxy rotation became some of the strongest evidence for dark matter."],["Simonyi Survey Telescope","The telescope is named after software pioneer Charles Simonyi, whose family foundation helped fund it. Its primary and tertiary mirrors were cast as a single piece of glass."]],
sp:["Asteroid haul","In just ten hours of test observations, Rubin discovered more than 2,000 new asteroids, a taste of the millions it is expected to find."],
g:[["Survey","A systematic observation of a large area"],["Megapixel","One million pixels"],["Alert","A notice sent when something changes in the sky"],["Near-Earth asteroid","An asteroid whose orbit comes close to Earth’s"]],
f:["Rubin has the largest astronomy camera ever built.","Its first images were released in June 2025.","It will survey the sky for 10 years."],
fun:"Rubin found more than 2,000 new asteroids in its first ten hours of test observations."});

ADDART({id:"alma",t:"ALMA: The Atacama Large Millimeter Array",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"📡",w:"Atacama Large Millimeter Array",r:["radioastronomy","starformation","eht","chilecountry","solarform","protoplanetarydisks"],
p:[["Sixty-six dishes in the desert","ALMA is a radio telescope made of 66 antennas in Chile."],["High and dry","It sits at 5,000 meters, one of the driest places on Earth."],["Baby planets","It has photographed planets forming around young stars."]],
l:"The Atacama Large Millimeter/submillimeter Array (ALMA) is a powerful radio telescope made up of 66 high-precision antennas on the Chajnantor plateau in Chile’s Atacama Desert, at an altitude of about 5,000 meters. It observes light with wavelengths of about a millimeter, between infrared and radio waves, which comes from some of the coldest objects in the universe, such as clouds of gas and dust where stars and planets form, and from very distant galaxies. Water vapor in the air absorbs this light, which is why ALMA was built in one of the highest and driest places on Earth. Its antennas, 12 or 7 meters across, can be moved by giant transporter vehicles to spread them up to 16 kilometers apart, working together as one huge telescope through interferometry. ALMA is an international partnership of Europe, North America and East Asia with Chile, and was inaugurated on 13 March 2013. In 2014, it produced a stunning image of HL Tauri, a young star surrounded by a disk with gaps likely carved by forming planets. ALMA has also detected complex organic molecules around young stars, studied the earliest galaxies and served as a key part of the Event Horizon Telescope that imaged black holes.",
s:[["Working at altitude","At 5,000 meters, the air has about half the oxygen of sea level. Most staff work at a base camp at 2,900 meters and control the telescope remotely; those who go up carry oxygen and undergo health checks."],["Moving antennas","Two giant transporters, named Otto and Lore, each with 28 tires, carry the 100-ton antennas between pads, changing the array’s layout to zoom in or see wider areas."],["Planet formation","ALMA’s images of disks around young stars show rings, gaps and spirals, giving astronomers their first detailed views of solar systems in the making."],["The early universe","ALMA detects light from galaxies that existed less than a billion years after the Big Bang, revealing dust and gas in some of the first galaxies."]],
k:[["Antennas","66"],["Altitude","About 5,000 meters"],["Inaugurated","13 March 2013"],["Maximum spread","About 16 kilometers"],["Famous image","HL Tauri disk (2014)"]],
d:[["2003","Construction begins"],["2011","Early science observations start"],["2013","ALMA inaugurated"],["2014","HL Tauri image released"],["2017","Takes part in Event Horizon Telescope observations"]],
s2:[["Chajnantor","Chajnantor means ‘place of departure’ in the Kunza language of the local Atacameño people. The plateau also hosts other telescopes because of its dry, clear skies."],["Mars on Earth","The Atacama Desert is so dry that some parts have gone years without rain, and NASA has tested Mars rovers there."]],
sp:["Giant trucks","Each of ALMA’s antenna transporters is about 20 meters long and 10 meters wide and rolls on 28 tires."],
g:[["Interferometry","Combining signals from several antennas"],["Millimeter wave","Light with wavelengths around a millimeter"],["Protoplanetary disk","A disk of gas and dust where planets form"],["Plateau","A high, flat area of land"]],
f:["ALMA has 66 antennas.","It sits at about 5,000 meters.","It photographed a disk forming planets in 2014."],
fun:"ALMA’s 100-ton antennas are moved around the desert by giant trucks named Otto and Lore."});

ADDART({id:"arecibo",t:"Arecibo and FAST",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"📡",w:"Arecibo Telescope",r:["radioastronomy","seti","pulsar","chinacountry","exoplanet"],
p:[["Giant dishes","Arecibo and FAST are the largest single-dish radio telescopes ever built."],["Arecibo’s fall","Puerto Rico’s Arecibo telescope collapsed in 2020."],["China’s giant","China’s FAST, 500 meters wide, is now the largest."]],
l:"The Arecibo Telescope in Puerto Rico and the Five-hundred-meter Aperture Spherical radio Telescope (FAST) in China are the largest single-dish radio telescopes ever built, each set into a natural bowl in the landscape. Arecibo, completed in 1963, had a dish 305 meters across built into a sinkhole, with a 900-ton platform of instruments hanging above it from cables. For 53 years it was the world’s largest single-dish telescope. It discovered that Mercury rotates in 59 days rather than 88, found the first binary pulsar in 1974, a discovery that earned Russell Hulse and Joseph Taylor the 1993 Nobel Prize in Physics, and was used by Aleksander Wolszczan and Dale Frail to find the first confirmed planets beyond the solar system, orbiting a pulsar, in 1992. It also beamed the famous Arecibo message toward a star cluster in 1974 and tracked asteroids with radar. After cables failed in 2020, the platform crashed into the dish on 1 December 2020. FAST, in Guizhou province in southern China, was completed in September 2016. Its 500-meter dish, made of 4,450 panels that can change shape, has discovered more than a thousand new pulsars.",
s:[["Building in a sinkhole","Arecibo’s builders used a natural limestone sinkhole as a ready-made bowl, saving the cost of building a support structure. FAST also sits in a natural depression in karst terrain, and thousands of residents were relocated to keep the area radio-quiet."],["Radar astronomy","Arecibo’s powerful radar bounced signals off planets and asteroids, mapping the surface of Venus through its clouds and measuring the shapes and spins of near-Earth asteroids."],["The collapse","In August and November 2020, cables supporting Arecibo’s platform broke. Engineers decided the telescope could not be safely repaired, and before a planned controlled demolition, the platform fell on its own, destroying the dish."],["FAST","FAST’s surface is made of panels moved by actuators, forming a 300-meter-wide reflecting area aimed at different parts of the sky. A radio-quiet zone surrounds it, and visitors must leave phones and cameras behind."]],
k:[["Arecibo dish","305 meters"],["Arecibo completed","1963"],["Arecibo collapsed","1 December 2020"],["FAST dish","500 meters"],["FAST completed","September 2016"]],
d:[["1963","Arecibo Telescope begins operating"],["1965","Arecibo measures Mercury’s rotation"],["1974","Arecibo message sent; first binary pulsar found"],["1992","First exoplanets found using Arecibo"],["2016","FAST completed"],["2020","Arecibo collapses"]],
s2:[["Arecibo in films","Arecibo appeared in the James Bond film GoldenEye (1995) and in Contact (1997), making it one of the world’s most famous telescopes."],["Puerto Rico’s pride","Arecibo trained generations of Puerto Rican scientists and drew many visitors. After the collapse, plans focused on education and science activities at the site."]],
sp:["Planets around a dead star","The first confirmed planets beyond the solar system, found with Arecibo in 1992, orbit a pulsar, a spinning dead star, an unexpected place for planets."],
g:[["Radio telescope","A telescope that detects radio waves"],["Sinkhole","A natural hole formed by dissolving rock"],["Radar","Bouncing radio waves off objects to study them"],["Pulsar","A rapidly spinning neutron star"]],
f:["Arecibo’s dish was 305 meters wide.","Arecibo collapsed in 2020.","FAST’s dish is 500 meters wide."],
fun:"The first planets found beyond our solar system, discovered with Arecibo, orbit a dead star called a pulsar."});

ADDART({id:"cargoships",t:"Space Station Cargo Ships",c:"Space Exploration",i:"📦",w:"Cargo spacecraft",r:["iss","spacex","soyuz","tiangong","salyut"],
p:[["Deliveries to orbit","Uncrewed cargo ships carry food, water, fuel and equipment to space stations."],["Progress first","The Soviet Progress began deliveries in 1978."],["Trash on the way back","Most cargo ships burn up on reentry, carrying away garbage."]],
l:"Cargo spacecraft are uncrewed vehicles that deliver supplies, such as food, water, air, fuel, clothing, spare parts and science experiments, to space stations, allowing crews to stay in orbit for months. The Soviet Union launched the first, Progress 1, on 20 January 1978 to resupply the Salyut 6 station, and improved Progress ships still serve the International Space Station. Europe’s Automated Transfer Vehicle (ATV), the size of a London double-decker bus, made five flights from 2008 to 2014, and Japan’s H-II Transfer Vehicle (HTV) made nine flights from 2009 to 2020, followed by the upgraded HTV-X, first launched in October 2025. In 2012, SpaceX’s Dragon became the first commercial spacecraft to deliver cargo to the space station and remains the only cargo ship able to bring large amounts of material back to Earth. Northrop Grumman’s Cygnus has flown since 2013, and China’s Tianzhou ships, first flown in 2017, supply the Tiangong station. Sierra Space’s Dream Chaser spaceplane has faced long delays. Most cargo ships are filled with trash after unloading and then burn up in Earth’s atmosphere, while some also use their engines to raise the station’s orbit.",
s:[["What gets delivered","A typical delivery carries several metric tons, including freeze-dried and fresh food, clothes, oxygen tanks, water, scientific experiments, spare equipment and sometimes treats such as ice cream or fresh fruit."],["Docking and berthing","Progress and Tianzhou dock automatically, while Cygnus and HTV approached the station and were captured by astronauts using the robotic arm Canadarm2, then attached to a port, a process called berthing. Dragon now docks automatically."],["Returning science","Because Dragon survives reentry and splashes down, it returns experiments, such as biological samples kept frozen, to scientists on Earth."],["Taking out the trash","The space station has no garbage truck, so crews pack waste into departing cargo ships, which burn up over the remote South Pacific Ocean."]],
k:[["First cargo ship","Progress 1, 20 January 1978"],["First commercial cargo delivery","SpaceX Dragon, 2012"],["Returns cargo to Earth","Dragon"],["Japan’s new ship","HTV-X (first flight October 2025)"],["China’s cargo ship","Tianzhou (since 2017)"]],
d:[["1978","Progress 1 resupplies Salyut 6"],["2008","Europe’s first ATV, Jules Verne"],["2009","Japan’s first HTV"],["2012","Dragon delivers cargo to the ISS"],["2013","First Cygnus mission"],["2017","China’s first Tianzhou"],["2025","Japan’s first HTV-X"]],
s2:[["Named after people","Northrop Grumman names each Cygnus after a person who contributed to spaceflight, and Europe named its ATVs after Jules Verne, Johannes Kepler, Edoardo Amaldi, Albert Einstein and Georges Lemaître."],["Fresh food","Astronauts look forward to cargo deliveries that bring fresh apples, oranges and onions, often sent by space agencies as a morale boost."]],
sp:["Garbage fireworks","Cargo ships filled with trash make a fiery reentry, sometimes photographed by astronauts from the station as streaks of light in the atmosphere."],
g:[["Cargo spacecraft","An uncrewed supply vehicle"],["Berthing","Attaching a spacecraft with a robotic arm"],["Reboost","Raising a station’s orbit with engine burns"],["Uncrewed","Without people aboard"]],
f:["Progress 1 made the first cargo delivery in 1978.","Dragon can bring cargo back to Earth.","Most cargo ships burn up carrying trash."],
fun:"Space station crews pack their trash into cargo ships, which then burn up in the atmosphere."});

ADDART({id:"shenzhou",t:"Shenzhou: China’s Crewed Spacecraft",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🇨🇳",w:"Shenzhou (spacecraft)",r:["tiangong","longmarch","chinacountry","womeninspace","soyuz"],
p:[["China’s space capsule","Shenzhou spacecraft carry Chinese astronauts to orbit."],["Yang Liwei, 2003","China became the third country to launch a person into space."],["Crews to Tiangong","Shenzhou crews live aboard the Tiangong space station."]],
l:"Shenzhou, meaning ‘divine vessel’, is China’s crewed spacecraft, which carries astronauts, often called taikonauts, to orbit and to the Tiangong space station. Based partly on the Russian Soyuz design but larger, it has an orbital module, a reentry capsule and a service module with solar panels. After four uncrewed test flights beginning in 1999, Shenzhou 5 carried Yang Liwei into orbit on 15 October 2003, making China the third country, after the Soviet Union and the United States, to launch a person into space independently. Zhai Zhigang made China’s first spacewalk in 2008, and in 2012 Liu Yang became the first Chinese woman in space. Since 2021, Shenzhou crews have lived aboard Tiangong for about six months at a time. In November 2025, a crack caused by a tiny piece of space debris was found in a window of Shenzhou 20, so its crew returned on Shenzhou 21, and China launched the uncrewed Shenzhou 22 as a replacement lifeboat, an unprecedented emergency response. In May 2026, Shenzhou 23 carried China’s first astronaut from Hong Kong and began a planned year-long stay. China is developing a new spacecraft, Mengzhou, for Moon missions.",
s:[["The spacecraft","Shenzhou can carry three astronauts. Its orbital module, which can stay in orbit after the crew returns, gives extra living space. The bell-shaped reentry capsule lands under a parachute in the Gobi Desert or Inner Mongolia."],["Firsts","Wang Yaping became the first Chinese woman to walk in space in 2021. In 2023, Gui Haichao became China’s first civilian in space, and China has trained astronauts from other countries, including Pakistan."],["The debris scare","Before Shenzhou 20’s planned return in November 2025, the crew found cracks in a window, likely caused by space debris. Officials kept the crew safe aboard Tiangong and brought them home on Shenzhou 21, showing the value of having backup spacecraft."],["Mengzhou","China’s next-generation crew spacecraft, Mengzhou (‘dream vessel’), is designed to carry astronauts to the Moon on the Long March 10 rocket, with a crewed lunar landing planned before 2030."]],
k:[["Meaning","Divine vessel"],["First crewed flight","Shenzhou 5, 15 October 2003"],["First Chinese astronaut","Yang Liwei"],["Crew","Up to 3"],["Destination","Tiangong space station"]],
d:[["1999","Shenzhou 1 uncrewed test flight"],["2003","Yang Liwei flies on Shenzhou 5"],["2008","First Chinese spacewalk"],["2012","Liu Yang becomes the first Chinese woman in space"],["2021","Crews begin living on Tiangong"],["2025","Shenzhou 20 damaged by debris; Shenzhou 22 launched as replacement"],["2026","Shenzhou 23 begins a year-long mission"]],
s2:[["Yang Liwei","Yang Liwei, a former fighter pilot, orbited Earth 14 times in about 21 hours. He later said he heard a mysterious knocking sound during the flight, which has never been fully explained."],["Taikonaut","The word ‘taikonaut’ combines the Chinese word taikong, meaning ‘space’, with ‘astronaut’. In Chinese, astronauts are called hangtianyuan."]],
sp:["A mysterious knock","During Shenzhou 5, Yang Liwei reported hearing a sound like someone knocking on the spacecraft with a wooden hammer; later astronauts reported similar noises, perhaps caused by the spacecraft expanding and contracting."],
g:[["Taikonaut","A Chinese astronaut"],["Reentry capsule","The part of a spacecraft that returns to Earth"],["Orbital module","A living compartment used in orbit"],["Lifeboat","A spacecraft kept ready for emergency return"]],
f:["Yang Liwei flew on Shenzhou 5 in 2003.","Liu Yang was the first Chinese woman in space.","Shenzhou crews live on Tiangong."],
fun:"China’s first astronaut reported hearing a mysterious knocking sound outside his spacecraft."});

ADDART({id:"hera",t:"Hera: Revisiting DART’s Asteroid",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🎯",w:"Hera (space mission)",r:["planetdefense","asteroidbelt","spaceagencies","mars","phobosdeimos"],
p:[["Checking the crash","Hera is a European mission to study the asteroid NASA’s DART spacecraft hit."],["Planetary defense","It helps learn how to push dangerous asteroids off course."],["Arriving in 2026","Hera was due to reach the Didymos asteroids in November 2026."]],
l:"Hera is a European Space Agency mission to visit the double asteroid system Didymos and its small moon Dimorphos, the target of NASA’s Double Asteroid Redirection Test (DART). On 26 September 2022, DART deliberately crashed into Dimorphos, about 160 meters wide, at about 22,000 kilometers per hour, shortening its orbit around Didymos by about 32 minutes. It was the first test of the ‘kinetic impactor’ method of planetary defense, which could one day push an asteroid on a collision course with Earth onto a safer path. Hera, launched on a SpaceX Falcon 9 on 7 October 2024, is designed to perform a detailed ‘crime scene investigation’, measuring the crater, the mass and makeup of Dimorphos and how much the impact changed it. This will let scientists calculate exactly how much push the impact delivered, so the method can be used reliably in the future. On its way, Hera flew past Mars in March 2025, taking images of the small moon Deimos. It carries two small CubeSats, Milani, which will map the asteroids’ composition, and Juventas, which will use radar to look inside Dimorphos, the first radar probe of an asteroid’s interior. Hera was due to reach the Didymos system in November 2026, about a month earlier than first planned.",
s:[["DART’s impact","DART’s crash threw out a huge plume of rock and dust that formed a tail thousands of kilometers long. The recoil from the ejected material gave Dimorphos an extra push, making the impact more effective than expected."],["Why Hera matters","From Earth, scientists could measure the change in Dimorphos’s orbit but not its mass or the shape of the crater. Hera will provide these details, turning a one-off experiment into a well-understood technique."],["The CubeSats","Milani and Juventas, each about the size of a shoebox, will fly closer than Hera and eventually try to land on the asteroids, gathering data on their surfaces."],["Not a threat","Didymos and Dimorphos pose no danger to Earth; they were chosen because a double asteroid lets scientists measure orbit changes precisely."]],
k:[["Agency","European Space Agency"],["Launched","7 October 2024"],["Target","Didymos and its moon Dimorphos"],["DART impact","26 September 2022"],["Orbit change from DART","About 32 minutes shorter"]],
d:[["2021","NASA launches DART"],["2022","DART strikes Dimorphos"],["2024","Hera launched"],["2025","Hera flies past Mars and Deimos"],["2026","Hera due to arrive at Didymos"]],
s2:[["Hera and Juno","Hera is named after the Greek goddess of marriage. Didymos means ‘twin’ in Greek, and Dimorphos means ‘having two forms’, referring to how DART changed it."],["Apophis","Another asteroid, Apophis, will pass very close to Earth on 13 April 2029, closer than some satellites. ESA’s RAMSES mission and NASA’s OSIRIS-APEX plan to study it."]],
sp:["A boulder-covered rubble pile","DART’s final images showed that Dimorphos is a ‘rubble pile’ covered in boulders, loosely held together by weak gravity."],
g:[["Kinetic impactor","A spacecraft that hits an asteroid to change its path"],["Planetary defense","Protecting Earth from asteroid impacts"],["Binary asteroid","Two asteroids orbiting each other"],["CubeSat","A small standardized satellite"]],
f:["DART hit Dimorphos in 2022.","Hera launched in 2024.","Hera carries two CubeSats."],
fun:"NASA’s DART crash shortened the orbit of the asteroid moon Dimorphos by about 32 minutes."});

ADDART({id:"zerogravityflights",t:"Zero-Gravity Flights",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🛫",w:"Reduced-gravity aircraft",r:["grav","astronauts","spacebody","flight","apollo13"],
p:[["Weightless in an airplane","Special airplane flights create about 20 seconds of weightlessness."],["The Vomit Comet","NASA’s training plane earned a famous nickname."],["Training and research","Astronauts train and scientists test experiments on these flights."]],
l:"Zero-gravity flights, also called parabolic or reduced-gravity flights, use specially modified airplanes to give passengers short periods of weightlessness without going to space. The plane climbs steeply, then the pilots reduce thrust so that the aircraft follows the same curved path, called a parabola, as a thrown ball. During the top of the arc, everything inside falls together with the plane, so passengers float freely for about 20 to 25 seconds. The plane then pulls out of its dive, pressing passengers down with nearly twice normal gravity, and repeats the maneuver dozens of times per flight. NASA began such flights in the late 1950s to prepare astronauts for spaceflight, and its KC-135 aircraft became known as the ‘Vomit Comet’ because many passengers became sick. Scientists use parabolic flights to test equipment and study how fluids, flames and living things behave without gravity. The weightless scenes in the 1995 film Apollo 13 were filmed aboard NASA’s plane. Today, companies in the United States and Europe offer flights to the public, and on 26 April 2007, physicist Stephen Hawking, who used a wheelchair because of motor neurone disease, floated freely on a zero-gravity flight.",
s:[["How it works","Weightlessness happens whenever objects are in free fall. Astronauts in orbit are also falling, continuously around Earth. A parabolic flight creates the same free fall for a short time by flying a ballistic arc."],["Partial gravity","By flying shallower arcs, pilots can simulate the Moon’s gravity, about one-sixth of Earth’s, or Mars’s, about three-eighths, letting astronauts practice moving in spacesuits on other worlds."],["Research","Europe’s Airbus A310 Zero-G, operated by the French company Novespace, carries dozens of experiments a year, from medical tests to new spacecraft technologies. Drop towers, such as one 146 meters tall in Bremen, Germany, give a few seconds of weightlessness for experiments."],["Motion sickness","Many people feel sick because the inner ear’s balance sense gets confusing signals. Passengers are often given medicine and told to keep their heads still during transitions."]],
k:[["Weightless time per parabola","About 20–25 seconds"],["Parabolas per flight","Often 15–40"],["NASA nickname","Vomit Comet"],["Film shot aboard","Apollo 13 (1995)"],["Stephen Hawking’s flight","26 April 2007"]],
d:[["1950s","Early parabolic flights for research"],["1959","NASA begins astronaut training flights"],["1973","KC-135 becomes NASA’s main reduced-gravity plane"],["1995","Apollo 13 film scenes shot in weightlessness"],["2007","Stephen Hawking floats on a zero-gravity flight"]],
s2:[["Hawking’s float","Stephen Hawking made eight parabolas during his 2007 flight and said he hoped it would encourage public interest in space. He called the experience amazing."],["Weddings and art","People have held weddings, performed dances and made art in weightlessness on commercial zero-gravity flights."]],
sp:["Not really zero","Gravity is never actually turned off on these flights; passengers float because they and the plane fall at exactly the same rate."],
g:[["Parabola","A U-shaped curve like the path of a thrown ball"],["Free fall","Falling under gravity alone"],["Microgravity","Very weak apparent gravity"],["Motion sickness","Nausea caused by confusing motion signals"]],
f:["Parabolic flights give about 20 seconds of weightlessness.","NASA’s plane was called the Vomit Comet.","Stephen Hawking floated on a flight in 2007."],
fun:"The floating scenes in the film Apollo 13 were filmed in real weightlessness aboard NASA’s ‘Vomit Comet’."});

ADDART({id:"spaceradiation",t:"Radiation in Space",c:"Space Exploration",i:"☢️",w:"Health threat from cosmic rays",r:["cosmicrays","spacebody","magnet","solarwind","astronauts"],
p:[["An invisible danger","Space is filled with radiation that Earth’s atmosphere and magnetic field block."],["Van Allen belts","Belts of trapped radiation surround Earth."],["A challenge for Mars","Radiation is one of the biggest risks for astronauts going to Mars."]],
l:"Radiation in space is one of the main dangers for astronauts and spacecraft. On Earth, our thick atmosphere and magnetic field shield us from most of it, but in space there are three main sources: galactic cosmic rays, high-energy particles from exploding stars far away; solar particle events, bursts of protons from the Sun during solar storms; and particles trapped in the Van Allen radiation belts around Earth, discovered in 1958 by James Van Allen using instruments on Explorer 1, the first US satellite. Radiation can damage DNA, raising the long-term risk of cancer, and very large doses can cause radiation sickness. Astronauts on the International Space Station, inside Earth’s magnetic field, receive in a single day about as much radiation as people on Earth get from natural sources in one to three months. A trip to Mars would expose astronauts to much more: NASA’s Curiosity rover measured that a round trip would give a dose about equal to the maximum allowed for an astronaut’s entire career under some guidelines. Engineers protect crews with shielding made of materials rich in hydrogen, such as water and plastic, and storm shelters, while spacecraft electronics are hardened against radiation.",
s:[["Light flashes","Since Apollo, astronauts have reported seeing flashes of light with their eyes closed. These are caused by cosmic ray particles passing through their eyes or brains."],["Solar storms","A large solar storm in August 1972, between the Apollo 16 and 17 missions, would have been dangerous for astronauts on the Moon. Future crews will rely on space weather forecasts and shelters."],["Shielding","Heavy metals can create showers of secondary radiation when hit by cosmic rays, so lighter, hydrogen-rich materials work better. Spacecraft designers may place water tanks and supplies around crew quarters."],["Testing on Artemis I","Two mannequin torsos, Helga and Zohar, flew around the Moon on Artemis I in 2022 with radiation detectors; one wore a protective vest to test how well it reduced exposure."]],
k:[["Main sources","Cosmic rays, solar particles, Van Allen belts"],["Van Allen belts discovered","1958, Explorer 1"],["Main long-term risk","Cancer"],["Best shielding","Hydrogen-rich materials such as water"],["Measured on the way to Mars","Curiosity rover, 2011–2012"]],
d:[["1912","Victor Hess discovers cosmic rays using balloons"],["1958","Van Allen belts discovered"],["1969","Apollo astronauts report light flashes"],["1972","Major solar storm between Apollo missions"],["2013","Curiosity’s radiation measurements on the way to Mars published"],["2022","Artemis I mannequins measure radiation around the Moon"]],
s2:[["Through the belts","Apollo astronauts crossed the Van Allen belts quickly on paths that avoided the most intense regions, receiving modest doses."],["Electronics","Radiation can flip bits in computer memory or damage chips. Spacecraft use radiation-hardened processors and error-checking software to keep working."]],
sp:["Cosmic ray sparkles","Astronauts describe the light flashes caused by cosmic rays as streaks, dots or starbursts, seen most often when they are trying to sleep."],
g:[["Cosmic rays","High-energy particles from space"],["Van Allen belts","Zones of trapped radiation around Earth"],["Shielding","Material that blocks radiation"],["Dose","An amount of radiation absorbed"]],
f:["Earth’s magnetic field protects us from much space radiation.","The Van Allen belts were found in 1958.","Water makes good radiation shielding."],
fun:"Astronauts sometimes see flashes of light with their eyes closed, caused by cosmic ray particles passing through their eyes."});

ADDART({id:"hubbleservicing",t:"Hubble’s Servicing Missions",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🔧",w:"Hubble Space Telescope servicing missions",r:["hubblest","spaceshuttle","spacewalks","astronauts","shuttledisasters"],
p:[["Repairing a telescope in space","Astronauts visited Hubble five times to fix and upgrade it."],["Fixing blurry vision","The first mission in 1993 corrected Hubble’s flawed mirror."],["Spacewalking mechanics","Astronauts used special tools on long spacewalks."]],
l:"The Hubble Space Telescope, launched on 24 April 1990, was designed to be visited and upgraded by Space Shuttle astronauts, and five servicing missions between 1993 and 2009 kept it working and improved it. Soon after launch, scientists discovered that Hubble’s 2.4-meter main mirror had been ground to slightly the wrong shape, an error of about 2 micrometers, a fiftieth of the width of a human hair, which made its images blurry. In December 1993, the crew of the Space Shuttle Endeavour, on mission STS-61, installed a device called COSTAR containing corrective mirrors, a bit like eyeglasses, plus a new camera with built-in correction, during five spacewalks. The repair was a triumph, and Hubble’s sharp images soon amazed the world. Later missions in 1997, 1999, 2002 and 2009 replaced gyroscopes, batteries, solar panels and computers and installed new cameras and spectrometers, making Hubble far more powerful than at launch. After the Columbia disaster in 2003, NASA canceled the final servicing mission as too risky, but public and scientific pressure led NASA to restore it, and Space Shuttle Atlantis visited Hubble in May 2009. No further visits have been made, and since 2024 Hubble has operated using a single gyroscope to save its remaining ones.",
s:[["The flawed mirror","A measuring device used during mirror manufacturing had been assembled with a lens 1.3 millimeters out of place, causing the mirror to be polished with a tiny error. Because the flaw was precisely known, engineers could design corrective optics."],["Spacewalk work","Servicing missions involved some of the most complex spacewalks ever. Astronauts used more than 100 specially designed tools, some of them developed just to remove tiny screws while wearing bulky gloves."],["The final mission","During the 2009 mission, astronauts repaired two instruments never designed to be fixed in space, removing more than a hundred screws. The mission left Hubble in its best condition ever."],["Hubble’s future","Hubble has no means of being serviced now that the shuttle has retired. Its orbit is slowly decaying, and NASA studied but did not proceed with a 2022 private proposal to boost it higher."]],
k:[["Hubble launched","24 April 1990"],["Mirror flaw","About 2 micrometers"],["First servicing mission","STS-61, December 1993"],["Servicing missions","Five (1993–2009)"],["Final visit","Atlantis, May 2009"]],
d:[["1990","Hubble launched; mirror flaw discovered"],["1993","First servicing mission fixes Hubble’s vision"],["1997","Second servicing mission"],["1999","Third servicing mission (part A)"],["2002","Servicing mission 3B"],["2009","Final servicing mission"],["2024","Hubble switches to one-gyroscope mode"]],
s2:[["The scientist-astronaut","Astronaut John Grunsfeld, an astronomer, flew on three Hubble servicing missions and later led NASA’s science programs."],["Celebrated images","After the 1993 repair, Hubble’s images, such as the ‘Pillars of Creation’ in 1995, became some of the most famous scientific pictures ever."]],
sp:["Glasses for a telescope","The corrective device COSTAR worked like a pair of glasses for Hubble’s instruments. It was removed in 2009 because all newer instruments had built-in correction."],
g:[["Servicing mission","A mission to repair or upgrade a spacecraft"],["Spherical aberration","A blur caused by a wrongly shaped mirror or lens"],["Gyroscope","A device that helps a spacecraft point accurately"],["COSTAR","Hubble’s corrective optics device"]],
f:["Astronauts serviced Hubble five times.","The 1993 mission fixed Hubble’s blurry vision.","The last servicing mission was in 2009."],
fun:"Hubble’s main mirror was off by only about a fiftieth of the width of a human hair, but that was enough to blur its images."});

ADDART({id:"artemisaccords",t:"The Artemis Accords",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🤝",w:"Artemis Accords",r:["artemis","spacelaw","moon","spaceagencies","asteroidmining","spacemining"],
p:[["Rules for the Moon","The Artemis Accords are principles for peaceful exploration of the Moon and beyond."],["Dozens of countries","Seventy-six countries had signed by September 2026."],["Not everyone","China and Russia have not signed and lead a separate lunar program."]],
l:"The Artemis Accords are a set of non-binding principles for the peaceful, safe and cooperative exploration of the Moon, Mars, comets and asteroids, created by the United States in connection with NASA’s Artemis program. The accords were first signed on 13 October 2020 by eight countries: Australia, Canada, Italy, Japan, Luxembourg, the United Arab Emirates, the United Kingdom and the United States. They build on the 1967 Outer Space Treaty and commit signatories to principles including peaceful purposes, transparency about their activities, making their systems compatible, helping astronauts in distress, registering space objects, sharing scientific data, protecting historic sites such as the Apollo landing sites, using space resources in line with international law, avoiding harmful interference through ‘safety zones’ and limiting space debris. Many countries joined in the following years, including the Philippines, Hungary and Malaysia in December 2025, and by September 2026, when San Marino signed, the accords had 76 signatories. China and Russia have not signed; they are developing their own International Lunar Research Station and have invited other countries to join. Supporters see the accords as practical rules for a busy Moon, while critics question whether resource rules favor wealthy nations.",
s:[["What the principles say","The accords stress that space activities should be peaceful and transparent. Signatories agree to share information about where they operate, to coordinate to avoid accidents and to release scientific data publicly."],["Space resources","The accords state that extracting resources such as water ice from the Moon does not in itself amount to claiming territory, which is banned by the Outer Space Treaty. Some legal experts and countries dispute this interpretation."],["Heritage sites","Signatories agree to preserve historically significant sites, such as the Apollo 11 landing site with Neil Armstrong’s footprints and the remains of early landers, from damage by future missions."],["A rival vision","China and Russia’s International Lunar Research Station plans a base near the lunar south pole in the 2030s, with partners including several countries. The two groups reflect wider geopolitical competition in space."]],
k:[["First signed","13 October 2020"],["Founding signatories","8"],["Signatories (September 2026)","76"],["Based on","Outer Space Treaty (1967)"],["Not signed","China and Russia"]],
d:[["1967","Outer Space Treaty signed"],["2020","Artemis Accords signed by eight countries"],["2022","Accords reach 20 signatories"],["2025","Philippines, Hungary and Malaysia sign"],["2026","San Marino becomes the 76th signatory"]],
s2:[["Non-binding","The accords are political commitments rather than a treaty, so they do not need approval by national legislatures and carry no legal penalties, which made it easier for many countries to join."],["Annual meetings","Signatories meet regularly to discuss how to put the principles into practice, such as how to share information about lunar activities and handle debris."]],
sp:["Named after a goddess","Artemis is the Greek goddess of the Moon and twin sister of Apollo, linking NASA’s new program to the Apollo missions of the 1960s and 1970s."],
g:[["Accords","Agreements"],["Non-binding","Not legally enforceable"],["Signatory","A country that has signed an agreement"],["Safety zone","An area where operators coordinate to avoid interference"]],
f:["The Artemis Accords were first signed in 2020.","76 countries had signed by September 2026.","China and Russia have not signed."],
fun:"The Artemis Accords include a promise to protect historic sites on the Moon, such as Neil Armstrong’s footprints."});

