/* data/body8.js */
// The Human Body: hair and nails, vitamins, fever, and hiccups, sneezes and yawns.
ADDART({id:"hair",t:"Hair and Nails",c:"Human Body",i:"💇",w:"Hair",r:["skin","nutri","hygiene","dna","aging"],
p:[["Made of keratin","Hair and nails are made of a tough protein called keratin."],["Always growing","Scalp hair grows about a centimeter a month."],["Color from melanin","Hair color comes from the pigment melanin."]],
l:"Hair and nails are structures that grow from the skin and are made mostly of keratin, a tough protein also found in animal horns, hooves and feathers. Hair covers almost the entire human body except the palms, soles and lips, though most body hair is fine and short. A typical scalp has around 100,000 hairs, each growing from a follicle in the skin. Hair protects the scalp from sunlight, helps keep the body warm and senses touch, while eyebrows and eyelashes protect the eyes. Nails protect the tips of fingers and toes and help us pick up small objects. Hair color, texture and growth depend on genes, age, hormones and health. Hair and nails can also reveal signs of illness or poor nutrition.",
s:[["How hair grows","Each hair grows from a follicle, a tiny pocket in the skin. At its base, cells divide quickly and push older cells upward, where they harden into the hair shaft. Scalp hair grows about 1 centimeter a month in cycles: a growing phase that can last years, a short resting phase, and a shedding phase. Losing about 50 to 100 hairs a day is normal."],["Hair color and texture","Hair color comes from two forms of the pigment melanin: one producing brown and black, the other red and yellow. As people age, follicles make less pigment, and hair turns gray or white. The shape of the follicle affects texture: round follicles tend to produce straight hair, while oval or flat ones produce wavy or curly hair."],["Nails","Fingernails grow about 3 millimeters a month, faster than toenails. The visible nail plate is dead keratin, growing from living tissue under the skin at its base. A healthy nail is smooth and pink. Changes in color, shape or texture can sometimes signal health problems, such as anemia or fungal infections."],["What hair does","Scalp hair shields against sunlight and helps keep heat in. Eyelashes and eyebrows keep dust, sweat and rain out of the eyes. Hairs in the nose and ears trap dust. Tiny muscles attached to body hairs make them stand up when we are cold or frightened, causing goosebumps."]],
k:[["Made of","Keratin"],["Scalp hairs","About 100,000"],["Hair growth","About 1 cm per month"],["Fingernail growth","About 3 mm per month"],["Color pigment","Melanin"]],
d:[["c. 3000 BC","Ancient Egyptians wear wigs and use henna"],["1905","The permanent wave for curling hair is demonstrated in London"],["1907","Synthetic hair dye is sold commercially in France"],["1950s","Scientists study the structure of keratin"],["1980s","Hair analysis is used in forensic science"],["2020s","Researchers study why hair turns gray"]],
s2:[["Head lice","Head lice, called kuto in Filipino, are tiny insects that live on the scalp and lay eggs, called nits, on hair shafts. They are common among schoolchildren worldwide and spread by head-to-head contact, not because of poor hygiene. Regular combing with a fine-toothed comb and appropriate treatments can remove them. Parents in the Philippines traditionally check children’s hair with a suyod, a fine comb."],["Hair in culture","Hairstyles carry cultural meanings in many societies, from braids and headwraps to shaved heads in religious traditions. In precolonial Philippines, both men and women often wore long hair, and some groups tied it in elaborate styles. Hair is cut or kept as part of ceremonies around the world, from first haircuts to mourning rituals."],["Hair loss","Hair loss can be caused by genes, hormones, stress, illness, certain medicines or nutritional problems. Male-pattern baldness is common as men age and is mainly inherited. Some hair loss after illness or childbirth is temporary. Doctors can help identify causes and treatments."],["Hair as evidence","Hair can reveal information about a person. Strands with roots contain DNA that can identify individuals. Hair also records traces of substances taken over months, such as medicines and drugs, and even long-ago diets, which helps forensic scientists and archaeologists."],["Why does hair turn gray?","Gray hair happens when the pigment-making cells in follicles stop working. Genes largely determine when it begins. Studies in mice have shown that intense stress can speed up graying by affecting the stem cells that produce pigment cells, which may help explain stories of hair turning gray during hard times."],["Nails and health","Doctors sometimes look at nails for clues. Very pale nails may suggest anemia, spoon-shaped nails can indicate iron deficiency, and dark lines may need checking. Biting nails can damage them and spread germs. Keeping nails clean and trimmed is part of good hygiene."]],
sp:["The comb and the suyod","On a sunny afternoon in a Philippine province, a grandmother sits on the steps of her house with her granddaughter leaning on her lap. Using a suyod, a comb with very fine teeth, she carefully works through the child’s long hair, section by section, looking for kuto and their tiny eggs. She chats and hums as she works. The ritual is familiar to many Filipino families, a mix of care and conversation passed down through generations. Health workers say such regular combing really does help control head lice, which are common among schoolchildren everywhere and nothing to be ashamed of."],
g:[["Keratin","A tough protein in hair and nails"],["Follicle","A tiny pocket in the skin where hair grows"],["Melanin","The pigment that colors hair and skin"],["Kuto","The Filipino word for head lice"]],
f:["Hair and nails are made of keratin.","Losing 50 to 100 hairs a day is normal.","Fingernails grow faster than toenails."],
fun:"Hair, fingernails, animal horns and bird feathers are all made from forms of the same protein, keratin."});

ADDART({id:"vitamins",t:"Vitamins and Minerals",c:"Health",i:"🥕",w:"Vitamin",r:["nutri","anemia","bones","phfood","fruits","bloodclotting"],
p:[["Tiny but vital","The body needs small amounts of vitamins and minerals to work properly."],["Diseases of lack","Missing vitamins can cause diseases such as scurvy and rickets."],["From food","Most people get them from a varied diet."]],
l:"Vitamins are organic substances that the body needs in small amounts to grow, function and stay healthy, but cannot make enough of on its own, so they must come from food. There are 13 vitamins: A, C, D, E, K and eight B vitamins. Minerals such as iron, calcium, iodine and zinc are elements the body also needs. Together they are called micronutrients. They help build bones, make blood, fight infections, turn food into energy and keep eyes, skin and nerves healthy. A lack of particular vitamins or minerals causes deficiency diseases, such as scurvy, rickets, beriberi, anemia and goiter. Most people can get enough from a varied diet with fruits, vegetables, whole grains, beans, fish and other foods. In the Philippines, programs add vitamins and minerals to foods and give supplements to children.",
s:[["Two kinds of vitamins","Fat-soluble vitamins, A, D, E and K, are stored in the body’s fat and liver and can build up to harmful levels if taken in large doses. Water-soluble vitamins, C and the B vitamins, are not stored for long and need to be eaten regularly; excess amounts usually leave the body in urine."],["What vitamins do","Vitamin A helps vision and immunity. B vitamins help turn food into energy and keep nerves healthy. Vitamin C helps heal wounds and absorb iron. Vitamin D helps the body absorb calcium for strong bones and is made in the skin with sunlight. Vitamin K helps blood clot."],["Important minerals","Calcium builds bones and teeth. Iron is needed to make hemoglobin, which carries oxygen in blood. Iodine is needed by the thyroid gland; lack of it can cause goiter and harm brain development. Zinc supports growth and immunity."],["Food sources","Green leafy vegetables like malunggay and kangkong, orange fruits and vegetables like mango, papaya and squash, citrus such as calamansi, fish, eggs, beans, nuts and whole grains are rich in vitamins and minerals. Iodized salt provides iodine, and fortified rice and flour add nutrients to everyday foods."]],
k:[["Number of vitamins","13"],["Fat-soluble vitamins","A, D, E, K"],["Word coined","1912, by Casimir Funk"],["Philippine law","Food Fortification Act of 2000"],["Common deficiency","Iron, causing anemia"]],
d:[["1747","James Lind tests citrus fruit against scurvy"],["1897","Christiaan Eijkman links beriberi to polished rice"],["1912","Casimir Funk coins the word vitamine"],["1920s","Vitamin D is linked to preventing rickets"],["2000","The Philippines passes the Food Fortification Act"],["2021","The Philippines approves Golden Rice for planting"]],
s2:[["Scurvy and the sailors","On long sea voyages in the 1500s to 1700s, many sailors suffered from scurvy, with bleeding gums, weakness and slow-healing wounds, and many died. In 1747 the Scottish naval surgeon James Lind gave different treatments to sick sailors and found that those given oranges and lemons recovered quickly. Scurvy is caused by a lack of vitamin C."],["Beriberi and rice","In the late 1800s, beriberi, a disease causing weakness and heart problems, was common in Asia, including the Philippines, where people ate white, polished rice. The Dutch doctor Christiaan Eijkman, working in Java, found that chickens fed polished rice developed similar symptoms, which were cured by rice with its husk. The cause was later identified as a lack of vitamin B1, or thiamine."],["Micronutrient programs in the Philippines","Philippine health programs have given vitamin A capsules to young children, iron supplements to pregnant women and promoted iodized salt to prevent goiter. The Food Fortification Act of 2000 requires adding nutrients such as vitamin A, iron and iodine to some foods, including flour, cooking oil and sugar. The national nutrition council monitors malnutrition and anemia rates."],["The Golden Rice debate","Golden Rice is a genetically modified rice that produces beta-carotene, which the body turns into vitamin A. It was developed to help prevent vitamin A deficiency, which can cause blindness. In 2021 the Philippines became the first country to approve it for commercial planting. Supporters say it can save sight and lives, while opponents raise concerns about safety, farmers’ independence and whether better diets would be more effective. In 2024 a Philippine court halted its commercial planting pending further review."],["Do we need supplements?","Many people take vitamin pills. Doctors recommend supplements in specific situations, such as folic acid before and during pregnancy, vitamin D for people with little sun exposure, or iron for those with anemia. For most healthy people, a balanced diet provides enough. Very high doses of some vitamins can be harmful, so experts advise following medical advice."],["Hidden hunger","A person can eat enough calories but still lack vitamins and minerals, a condition sometimes called hidden hunger. It affects many people worldwide, especially children and women, and can harm growth, learning and immunity. Diverse diets, fortified foods and supplements help address it."]],
sp:["The chickens of Batavia","In the 1880s the Dutch army doctor Christiaan Eijkman was sent to Batavia, now Jakarta, to find the cause of beriberi, which was killing soldiers and prisoners. He believed it was caused by germs. Then he noticed something odd: chickens kept at his laboratory suddenly developed weakness and paralysis similar to beriberi. He discovered that a cook had been feeding them leftover polished white rice from the hospital. When a new cook switched back to cheaper unpolished rice, the chickens recovered. Eijkman realized that something in the rice husk prevented the disease. His work helped lead to the discovery of vitamins, and in 1929 he shared the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine."],
g:[["Vitamin","A substance the body needs in small amounts"],["Mineral","An element such as iron or calcium needed by the body"],["Deficiency","A lack of something needed"],["Fortification","Adding nutrients to food"]],
f:["There are 13 vitamins.","Scurvy is caused by lack of vitamin C.","Vitamin D can be made in the skin with sunlight."],
fun:"The discovery of a vitamin began with chickens that got sick from eating polished rice."});

ADDART({id:"fever",t:"Fever",c:"Health",i:"🌡️",w:"Fever",r:["immune","flu","dengue","firstaid","measles","temperature","bodytemperature"],
p:[["A raised temperature","A fever is a body temperature higher than normal."],["Fighting infection","Fever is often a sign the body is fighting germs."],["When to worry","Some fevers need urgent medical attention."]],
l:"A fever is a body temperature higher than the normal range, usually 38 degrees Celsius or above when measured with a thermometer. Normal body temperature is around 37 degrees Celsius, though it varies slightly between people and during the day. Fever is not a disease itself but a sign that the body is responding to something, most often an infection by viruses or bacteria, such as the common cold, influenza, dengue or COVID-19. A part of the brain called the hypothalamus acts as the body’s thermostat and raises the set temperature to help the immune system fight germs. Most fevers go away on their own in a few days, but very high fevers, fevers in young babies, and fevers with warning signs need prompt medical care.",
s:[["How fever happens","When the immune system detects germs, cells release chemicals called pyrogens that travel to the hypothalamus in the brain. The hypothalamus raises the body’s temperature set point. The body then shivers, narrows blood vessels in the skin and feels cold, which raises temperature. When the fever breaks, people often sweat as the body cools down."],["Why fever may help","Many germs grow best at normal body temperature, and a fever can slow them down. Higher temperatures also speed up some immune responses. Animals, including lizards that move to warm places when infected, also develop fevers, suggesting fever evolved as a defense."],["Measuring temperature","Thermometers can measure temperature in the mouth, armpit, ear, forehead or rectum, and readings differ slightly by site. Digital thermometers are common; old glass mercury thermometers are being phased out because mercury is toxic if they break."],["Caring for a fever","Rest, drinking plenty of fluids and wearing light clothing help a person with a fever feel better. Medicines such as paracetamol can lower fever and ease discomfort. Doses must follow the product label or a doctor’s advice, especially for children. Aspirin should not be given to children and teenagers because of the risk of a rare but serious condition."]],
k:[["Fever threshold","About 38 °C"],["Normal temperature","About 37 °C"],["Body thermostat","Hypothalamus"],["Common causes","Viral and bacterial infections"],["Common medicine","Paracetamol"]],
d:[["c. 400 BC","Hippocrates describes fever as a sign of illness"],["1593","Galileo builds an early thermoscope"],["1612","Santorio Santorio measures human temperature with an instrument"],["1868","Carl Wunderlich publishes millions of temperature readings"],["1970s","Scientists show that fever helps animals fight infection"],["2020","A study suggests average body temperature has fallen slightly over time"]],
s2:[["Warning signs","Health experts advise seeking urgent medical help for a fever in a baby younger than three months, a very high fever, a fever lasting more than a few days, or a fever with warning signs such as difficulty breathing, stiff neck, confusion, seizures, a rash that does not fade, severe headache, repeated vomiting or signs of dehydration. In dengue, warning signs include severe stomach pain, bleeding and extreme tiredness."],["Fever and dengue","In the Philippines, fever during the rainy season can be a sign of dengue, spread by mosquitoes. Dengue fever often comes with headache, body pain and rash. Some cases become severe, with bleeding and shock, often as the fever drops. Doctors advise people with fever during dengue season to see a health worker, drink fluids and avoid certain painkillers such as ibuprofen and aspirin unless advised."],["Febrile seizures","Some young children, usually between six months and five years old, have seizures when they have a fever. These febrile seizures can be frightening but are usually brief and do not cause lasting harm. Parents should place the child on their side, not put anything in the mouth, and seek medical advice."],["Is 37 still normal?","In 1868 the German doctor Carl Wunderlich analyzed huge numbers of temperature readings and declared 37 degrees Celsius the normal human temperature. Recent studies in the United States and elsewhere suggest that average body temperatures may now be slightly lower, possibly because people today have fewer chronic infections. Doctors note that normal temperature varies from person to person."],["Fever folk remedies","Many cultures have traditional remedies for fever. In the Philippines, families may sponge the patient with water, place damp cloths on the forehead or give herbal drinks. Lukewarm sponging can help comfort, but experts warn against using alcohol rubs or ice-cold water, which can cause shivering or other harm."],["Fever in history","Before modern medicine, fevers from diseases such as malaria, typhoid and yellow fever killed millions. Doctors once tried treating fevers by bloodletting, which did more harm than good. In the 1800s, the discovery of germs and later of antibiotics and vaccines greatly reduced deaths from fever-causing diseases."]],
sp:["The doctor with a million readings","In the mid-1800s, doctors had no agreed idea of what a normal human temperature was. The German physician Carl Wunderlich decided to find out. Over many years at his hospital in Leipzig, he and his staff took temperature readings from about 25,000 patients, using large thermometers held in the armpit for up to 20 minutes. In 1868 he published his findings, based on more than a million measurements, concluding that normal temperature averaged about 37 degrees Celsius and that fever was a sign of disease rather than a disease itself. His work made the thermometer a standard tool of medicine."],
g:[["Fever","A body temperature above normal"],["Hypothalamus","The part of the brain that controls body temperature"],["Pyrogen","A substance that causes fever"],["Febrile seizure","A seizure in a child caused by fever"]],
f:["Fever is usually a sign of infection.","Normal body temperature is about 37 °C.","Babies under three months with fever need urgent care."],
fun:"Some cold-blooded lizards give themselves a fever by moving to warm spots when they are infected."});

ADDART({id:"hiccups",t:"Hiccups, Sneezes and Yawns",c:"Human Body",i:"🤧",w:"Hiccup",r:["reflexes","lungs","nervous","brain","allergy"],
p:[["Automatic actions","Hiccups, sneezes and yawns happen without us choosing them."],["Contagious yawns","Seeing someone yawn can make you yawn too."],["Sun sneezes","Some people sneeze when they look at bright light."]],
l:"Hiccups, sneezes and yawns are reflexes, automatic actions controlled by the nervous system. A hiccup is a sudden contraction of the diaphragm, the muscle below the lungs, followed by the quick closing of the vocal cords, producing the familiar hic sound. A sneeze is a powerful burst of air through the nose and mouth that clears irritants such as dust, pollen or germs from the nose. A yawn is a deep, slow breath with the mouth wide open, often when tired or bored, and is famously contagious. Although these actions are common, scientists still debate some of their purposes. Most episodes are harmless and brief, but hiccups that last for days or frequent sneezing may need medical attention.",
s:[["Hiccups","Hiccups can be triggered by eating too fast, swallowing air, fizzy drinks, spicy food, excitement or sudden temperature changes. The diaphragm contracts suddenly, pulling air in, and the vocal cords snap shut about a third of a second later, causing the sound. Most hiccups stop within minutes. Babies hiccup often, even before birth."],["Sneezes","When nerves in the nose sense an irritant, they send signals to the brain stem, which coordinates a sneeze. The eyes close, chest muscles tighten and air bursts out, carrying droplets that can spread germs several meters. Covering sneezes with a tissue or elbow helps prevent the spread of colds and flu."],["Yawns","People yawn when tired, bored, waking up or before sleep. Some scientists suggest yawning helps cool the brain or increases alertness by stretching muscles and raising heart rate. The old idea that yawning brings more oxygen to the body has not been supported by studies."],["Contagious yawning","Watching, hearing or even reading about yawning can make people yawn. Contagious yawning is common in humans and has been seen in chimpanzees and dogs. Some researchers link it to empathy and social bonding, since people are more likely to catch yawns from family and friends."]],
k:[["Type of action","Reflexes"],["Hiccup muscle","Diaphragm"],["Purpose of sneezing","Clearing the nose"],["Contagious","Yawning"],["Longest hiccups","About 68 years"]],
d:[["c. 400 BC","Hippocrates notes that sneezing can be a sign in illness"],["1922","Charles Osborne begins hiccupping for 68 years"],["1964","Scientists describe the photic sneeze reflex"],["1990","Osborne’s hiccups finally stop"],["2000s","Studies link contagious yawning with empathy"],["2010s","Research suggests yawning may help cool the brain"]],
s2:[["Cures for hiccups","People try many remedies for hiccups: holding their breath, drinking water quickly or upside down, swallowing sugar, being startled or pulling on the tongue. Some may work by stimulating nerves involved in the reflex, though scientific evidence is limited. In the Philippines, a common folk remedy for babies with hiccups, called sinok, is to place a small piece of thread moistened with saliva on the baby’s forehead."],["The man who hiccupped for 68 years","In 1922 Charles Osborne, a farmer in the United States, began hiccupping while lifting a pig, and he continued for about 68 years, until 1990. At first he hiccupped dozens of times a minute. Despite this, he married twice and had eight children. He holds the record for the longest attack of hiccups."],["Sun sneezing","Some people sneeze when they suddenly step into bright sunlight. This photic sneeze reflex affects a significant minority of people and tends to run in families. Scientists think nerves carrying signals from the eyes and nose may cross-talk in the brain, triggering a sneeze."],["Bless you","Many cultures have words to say after someone sneezes. English speakers say bless you, Spanish speakers say salud, meaning health, and Filipinos often say God bless or Kaawaan ka ng Diyos. Some of these customs may come from old beliefs that sneezing could release the soul or was linked to illness."],["Should you hold a sneeze?","Doctors advise against holding in a sneeze by pinching the nose and closing the mouth, because the pressure can occasionally injure the ears, throat or blood vessels. It is better to sneeze into a tissue or the elbow."],["When to see a doctor","Hiccups lasting more than two days, or that interfere with eating or sleeping, can sometimes be a sign of other medical problems and should be checked. Frequent sneezing with a runny nose may be due to allergies. Excessive yawning without tiredness can occasionally be linked to medical conditions."]],
sp:["The thread on the forehead","When a Filipino baby starts hiccupping after feeding, a grandmother may reach for a sewing basket. She pulls out a short piece of thread, moistens it with a little saliva and gently places it on the baby’s forehead. Sometimes, she says, the hiccups stop soon after. Doctors explain that baby hiccups usually stop on their own within minutes and are harmless, and that burping a baby during and after feeding can help prevent them. Still, the thread remedy remains a familiar part of Filipino family tradition, passed from generation to generation along with many other home remedies."],
g:[["Reflex","An automatic action controlled by the nervous system"],["Diaphragm","The dome-shaped breathing muscle below the lungs"],["Photic sneeze","Sneezing triggered by bright light"],["Contagious","Spreading from one person to another"]],
f:["Hiccups are caused by sudden contractions of the diaphragm.","Yawning is contagious.","Some people sneeze in bright light."],
fun:"One man hiccupped for about 68 years."});

;
